5","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * ChangeLog started..."}},"dependencies":[]},"employment-hero":{"name":"Employment Hero","version":"1.0.0","description":"Connect to Employment Hero API for integration with themes.","description_html":"<p>Connect to Employment Hero API for integration with themes.<\/p>","description_plain":"Connect to Employment Hero API for integration with themes.","icon":"plug","author":{"name":"Benjamin Currey","email":"benjamin@currey.com.au"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=1.6.0"}],"homepage":"https:\/\/github.com\/Currey\/grav-plugin-employment-hero","docs":"https:\/\/github.com\/Currey\/grav-plugin-employment-hero\/blob\/develop\/README.md","demo":"http:\/\/demo.yoursite.com","keywords":"grav, plugin, api, employment, careers","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/Currey\/grav-plugin-employment-hero\/issues","license":"MIT","tag_name":"1.0.0","date":"2026-07-21T16:43:38+10:00","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/employment-hero\/1.0.0","repository":"https:\/\/github.com\/Currey\/grav-plugin-employment-hero","slug":"employment-hero","install_path":"user\/plugins\/employment-hero","content_sha256":"7c70fc6dc0ec1e79a0a0bfa84ab33ad5aa27f8dbb1ca9eb0bf30eb0d158dd107","changelog":{"1.0.0":{"date":"2026-07-21","content":"1. [](#launch)\n    * Plugin launched.\n    * Basic functionality\n    * Base template returns simple results: `title`, `city`, `application_url`.\n"},"0.1.0":{"date":" 2026-07-07","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * ChangeLog started..."}}},"kahunacart":{"name":"KahunaCart","version":"1.3.10","description":"Native ecommerce for Grav 2.0 \u2014 products, cart, checkout, orders, and a provider ecosystem","description_html":"<p>Native ecommerce for Grav 2.0 \u2014 products, cart, checkout, orders, and a provider ecosystem<\/p>","description_plain":"Native ecommerce for Grav 2.0 \u2014 products, cart, checkout, orders, and a provider ecosystem","icon":"shopping-cart","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.1.0"},{"name":"api","version":">=1.0.31"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, shop, cart, checkout, orders, payments","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart","license":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/premium\/license","tag_name":"1.3.10","date":"2026-10-02T01:06:25Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart\/1.3.10","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart"},"slug":"kahunacart","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart","content_sha256":"4f641bd31ee9bb65b60c6a9d07dcb24198df425a07cf0719d58cc75a66c401d2","changelog":{"1.3.10":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The catalog file carries custom fields.** `bin\/plugin kahunacart export` now writes the custom field definitions for products and variants and the values stored on them, and `import` reads them back, so an add-on can write one catalog file that creates the products and sets its own fields on them in a single import. A field the store lacks is created from the file and one it has is left alone. A name nobody declared, a type that disagrees with the store's or a value its field refuses stops the import before anything is written. A store with no custom fields exports the same file it always did\n    * **Text message channels for store-owner notifications.** **Text message (Textbelt)** and **Text message (Twilio)** join Email, Slack, Discord, Telegram, Pushover and ntfy on the Channels page. Textbelt is the quick one: buy a pack of texts, paste the key and the phone that should get them, and Send test. There is no number to buy and nothing to register with carriers. The text comes from a number Textbelt owns, and Textbelt has to allow links for your key first, because our text ends with one. Twilio is for your own number: paste the Account SID and Auth Token from the Twilio console, a number or Messaging Service SID to send from and the phone that should get the texts, and Send test. Textbelt answers 200 even when it refuses a text, so its own reason is read and shown under Send test, with your key taken out of it. The text is the headline, the facts and the admin link, folded to plain characters so a message stays in full-length segments (160 characters on Twilio), and cut at 480 characters with the link whole. Phone numbers can be typed the way people type them, `+1 (555) 123-4567`. Priority is ignored. The setup steps, including the link approval Textbelt needs and the approval a US or Canadian number needs before carriers deliver from it with Twilio, are in the Notifications docs\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **Help text follows the admin's Help text setting.** Help on the plugin's own admin screens (customer groups, shipping, pickup locations, notification channels, product options and more) now shows below the label or in a tooltip behind an info icon, whichever the person has chosen\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Reviews switched off still showed on the product page.** With **Reviews** off in the settings, the product page still drew the Reviews heading and \"no reviews yet\". The plugin's own template and the Kahuna theme are both fixed by this, with no theme update needed\n    * **A catalog file lost a backslash that sat before an `n`.** A quoted value holding a backslash followed by the letter n, with a line break elsewhere in it, was read back with the backslash gone\n"},"1.3.9":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The download no longer carries the plugin's tests.** `tests\/`, `phpunit.xml`, `.github\/` and `crowdin.yml` stay in the repository and out of the release zip, 641 files fewer on every install. Nothing the plugin runs is affected\n"},"1.3.8":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Articles with a teaser.** Put `paywall: premium` in a page's header (or pick customer groups in the new **Paywall** field beside **Access** in the page editor) and everyone can open the article, but a visitor who isn't in the group sees the headline, the opening paragraphs up to the `===` line (or the first two blocks) and a subscribe panel where the rest would be. The rest of the article is never sent to them: it isn't in the page, the feed or a search snippet, and anything else that prints the page's content for them (a listing that prints each child's content, a summary, an SEO plugin's description) gets the teaser too. A subscriber sees the whole article at the same address the moment their subscription starts, and loses it on the next click after it stops. The panel lists the plans that sell the group (Subscriptions answers the new `onKahunaCartGroupOffers` event) with a Subscribe button and price each, and **Already a subscriber? Sign in**; a page's own `paywall.subscribe` or the store's `paywall.subscribe_url` replaces the list. After paying, the receipt says **Continue reading: the article's title**. Every paywalled page goes out `Cache-Control: private, no-cache`, carries `isAccessibleForFree: false` structured data, and hands the feed plugin (1.10.4) and YetiSearch Pro (2.1.2, so a page saved in the admin counts too) its teaser instead of its body. `kahunacart_paywall(page)` gives a theme the same answer to branch on, and `kahunacart-paywall.html.twig` is the page a visitor without access gets in any theme. It is switched on for a page by the header and nothing else, so pages without `paywall:` are served exactly as before. A store can also let visitors who aren't subscribers read a few paywalled articles in full each month or week before the teaser applies: new settings under **Accounts \u2192 Articles with a teaser** (`paywall.meter.free`, up to 50, `.period` and `.who`, off by default). A repeat of the same article costs nothing, a signed-in customer's reads follow them to another device, a guest's are kept in a signed cookie (a cookie that has been edited counts as used up), search crawlers always get the teaser, and **Signed-in customers only** gives guests none and offers **Create a free account**. A slim bar above the article says how many are left. With the meter off nothing is written and no cookie is set. Adds migration `0098_paywall_reads`; the worker sweeps reads older than two periods.\n    * **Every admin table sorts by its column headings.** Press a heading to sort by it and press it again to turn it round; an arrow shows which column the table is sorted by and which way. A table that holds all its rows sorts in the browser. A table that pages (orders, customers, products, coupons and campaigns, reviews, deposits, store credit, abandoned carts, the email log, the notice queue, jobs, webhook deliveries, agent sessions, and the Products, Customers and Refunds reports) sorts on the server across the whole list and goes back to page one, so a heading never just shuffles the page on screen. Empty values go last whichever way a column runs, and order numbers sort as numbers. The list endpoints behind them take `?sort=<column>&dir=asc|desc` (see the admin API docs and the MCP tools); with no `sort`, every list keeps the order it always had, and the existing sort select on products, By value \/ By age on abandoned carts and `sort=cf:<name>` on orders, customers and products keep working alongside the headings. Tables whose order is the merchant's own (sale priority, anything with Move up and Move down, hook handlers, tax rates in a zone) are left in that order. Add-on screens get the same headings through `ctx.ui.sortHead()`, `sortRows()`, `sortQuery()` and `bindSortHeads()`, and server lists use `Support\\ListSort`\n    * **Reports have 6 months and 1 year quick ranges** beside 7, 30 and 90 days. They count calendar months, so 1 year up to Sep 29 starts on Sep 30 the year before\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **Coupons and campaigns page.** The codes list stopped at the first 100 with no way past them, and the campaign filter only looked at those. Both lists now page, the campaign filter runs on the server (`group_id`, a campaign id or `none`), and the counts are for the whole filtered list\n    * **Merchandising reaches the whole catalogue.** Bulk edit and the related-product pickers only ever held the first 50 products; they now page and sort on the server, and their search box filters (it sent a parameter the products endpoint never read)\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The Deposits Open \/ Settled filter did nothing.** The list ignored `?status=`, so choosing a filter listed and counted every collection\n"},"1.3.7":{"date":"09\/29\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **\"Still needed\" named the state field and then printed every country's state list.** At checkout the field partial wraps the whole region field in its label, and that includes the `<script>` holding the published state lists, so a customer who hadn't picked a state read \"Still needed: State \/ Region {\"lists\":{\"US\":{\"AL\":\"Alabama\",\u2026\" under Continue to payment. The line now reads a copy of the label with its controls, scripts and styles taken out at any depth, so it says \"Still needed: State \/ Region\", and a theme that wraps a control in a span of its own is read the same way\n"},"1.3.6":{"date":"09\/29\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A sender of the store's own.** Every store email took its From from the Email plugin's global `from` and `from_name`, so on a site where the store is one part of a bigger site (a forum that owns the site's sender, say) order confirmations and sign-in links went out under the forum's name. The store's **Notifications** tab now has **Send Store Emails From** and **Sender Name** (`emails.from`, `emails.from_name`), used by every store email: order emails, sign-in links, set-password invitations, shared carts, abandoned-cart and review requests, stock alerts and emailed owner notifications, and by the add-ons that send through core's sender (Bookings, Shipping). Each field falls back to the Email plugin's on its own, so a store that sets nothing sends exactly as before. The set-password and shared-cart emails used to read the Email plugin's settings themselves and now go through the same place as the rest, which also stops a name typed into the address field being wrapped twice. The **Mail transport** health check and `bin\/plugin kahunacart check` print the sender actually in use and say whether it is the store's or the Email plugin's.\n    * **A shorter checkout for downloads.** New setting **Billing Address for Downloads** (`checkout.digital_billing`). `full`, the default, changes nothing. `country` asks a cart with nothing to deliver for the country and the state or province only, and leaves out the street, city and postcode and the \"ship to the same address\" box; a cart with anything physical in it draws the full address as before. Any address column an enabled payment provider declares it needs stays on the form, in every country, because the provider is not chosen until the payment step. It is enough for tax zones by country or state and for a merchant of record like Polar; keep `full` for postcode zones or invoices that must print a street. See the checkout fields docs for what the billing address is used for.\n    * **Store pages titled with the store's name.** New setting **Title Store Pages With the Store's Name** (`seo.store_titles`, off by default). On, every store page has its own title (Your Cart, Checkout, the product's name, the account pages), written to the page header as well as the page, and on those pages the store's name (`seller.name`, then `invoice.store_name`, then the site title) takes the site title's place in the theme's `<title>`. The theme's own pattern stays, so a store inside a bigger site reads \"Your Cart | Grav Premium\" instead of \"Shop | Grav CMS\". Themes that build the title from the page header (event-horizon) used to print the stub's \"Shop\" on every store page; with the setting off they still do.\n    * **Store emails can name the store rather than the site.** New setting **Name the Store in Emails** (`emails.use_store_name`, off by default) on the **Notifications** tab. Store emails called the shop by the site's title, so a store inside a bigger site sent \"Order #1000 from Grav CMS is confirmed\" and \"Your sign-in link for Grav CMS\". On, every store email that names the shop (the order emails and the confirmation, sign-in links, set-password invitations, shared carts, abandoned-cart and review requests, and stock alerts), in its subject, text, card and footer, uses the store's name the way store page titles do: `seller.name`, then `invoice.store_name`, then the site title. Off, nothing changes. See [Naming the store, not the site](emails.md#naming-the-store-not-the-site)\n    * **A sale can give the customer the sale or a discount code, whichever saves more.** A sale's **Discount codes** setting (`stack_with_coupons`) gains a third answer beside stack and refuse: **Best of** (`2`, or `best` over the API, in the catalog file and in the seed). It is one discount per order: once for the whole cart, everything the best-of sales take off is weighed against everything the code would take off on its own, and the bigger one applies while the other gives nothing on any line, so a cart holding something the sale leaves out (a bundle, say) never gets the code there and the sale on the rest. The code is worked out on the list price for the comparison, a tie goes to the code (so its use is recorded and an affiliate's code gets the credit), and the one that loses writes no row, so the cart and receipt show only the discount that applied. A code that beat the sales records the sales it replaced (`replaced_sale_ids`, `replaced_item_ids`) in its source. A code that loses is turned down with its own message, \"The discount already on your cart saves you more than that code, so the code wasn't used.\" (`COUPON_ERROR_SALE_BETTER`), unless it also pays the postage, in which case it stays for the free shipping and takes nothing off the goods. Sales set to stack or refuse are no part of the comparison and keep working as before. Product cards for a shopper holding a campaign-link code show the same answer. The column stays an INTEGER, so there is no migration and every existing sale stacks or refuses exactly as before. The admin's Sales editor has a **Discount codes** select with the three options, the sales list marks best-of sales, and the MCP sale tools take `stack`, `refuse` or `best`. See [Best of the sale or the code](sales.md#best-of-the-sale-or-the-code)\n    * **A sign-in lands where the customer was going.** Somebody sent to `{base}\/signin` from a page they could not open (`?return=\/account\/licenses`, a path under the store) comes back to that page once signed in. The page to come back to rides on the form and on the emailed link, so it works in whichever browser opens the mail, and the password route on the same page returns there too. Only storefront pages are accepted; anything else is ignored. With no page to come back to, the new setting **After signing in, go to** (`customers.signin_landing`, default `\/account`, the overview as before) decides, so a store selling licence keys can send customers to `\/account\/licenses`. See [Where the customer lands](customer-accounts.md#where-the-customer-lands)\n    * **Downloads can have a title and a description.** A downloadable file had only its file name, so a customer's downloads list read `grav-skeleton-typhoon-onepage-publisher+2.0.3.zip`. A file can now also have a **Title** (\"Typhoon One-Page (Publisher)\") and a **Description** (\"A one-page starter site with the Typhoon theme\"), set under **Products \u2192 a product \u2192 Files \u2192 Edit**. The receipt, the account's Downloads tab, the release lists and the order emails show the title as the link with the description under it, and the file still downloads under its file name. Both are optional: a file without them lists exactly as before. The product files API (`POST` and `PATCH \/kahunacart\/products\/{id}\/files`), the release publish route (`-F title=\u2026 -F description=\u2026`, where a `replace=1` call that leaves them out keeps the replaced file's) and the MCP tools `list_product_files` and `update_product_file` carry them, every file row the API answers with has `title` and `description` (null when unset), and a download row handed to a template has `file_title` (the title, or the file name) and `file_description`. The order admin's downloads table shows the title too. New CSS class `kahunacart-download-description`. Migration 0096. See [Digital products and downloads](digital-products.md#files)\n    * **Account tabs can have pages of their own below them.** An add-on's account section registered with `paths: true` also answers `{base}\/account\/{id}\/{\u2026}`, one or more segments deep, so a tab that lists things can give each one its own address (`{base}\/account\/licenses\/3f9a0c1d2e4b5a67`) instead of a query string. The segments after the id reach the section's renderer and POST handler as `account_path`, the renderer throws the new `AccountSectionNotFound` for an address it does not recognise (a 404, either the store's own page or the section's \"not found\" markup inside the account area), and a form on such a page comes back to it afterwards, success or refusal. A section registered without it never sees a deeper address: it is a 404, never the tab drawn at a second URL. A signed-out visit goes through sign-in and comes back to the whole address, on the emailed link too. See \"Sections from other plugins\" in docs\/customer-accounts.md. On those pages the tab stays marked as current and is a link back to its list\n    * **Persistent database connections, for stores on a database server.** New setting **Persistent Connections** (`database.persistent`, off by default) keeps the MySQL, MariaDB or PostgreSQL connection open in each PHP-FPM worker between requests instead of opening a new one on every request. On PostgreSQL that connect is the most expensive thing a request does before it reads anything (about 6.7 ms on PostgreSQL 16 on the same box, plus a TLS handshake over TCP when the server has `ssl` on), and MariaDB saves its handshake too. A transaction a request left open is rolled back before the connection is used again, a PostgreSQL connection gets `DISCARD ALL` so no session settings or locks carry over, and a connection the server closed (a restart, an idle timeout) is replaced on the next request rather than failing it. The trade-off is one open connection per worker, so the database's `max_connections` must cover every PHP-FPM worker. SQLite and named connections are unaffected; the performance docs say why SQLite is left alone. The configuration docs now also list `database.pgsql.sslmode` and `database.mysql.unix_socket`, which already worked from YAML.\n    * **Order numbers can have a prefix.** New setting **Order Number Format** (`order_number_format`, beside **First Order Number**) prints the number in a pattern with the invoice format's placeholders: `{number}` (the default, the count as it is), `{number:6}` padded with zeros, and `{year}`. `GP-{number}` gives `GP-1000`. It is for a store that imported its old orders under a prefix and wants its own to carry on from them: raise the sequence past the last imported number after the import (`Sequence::raiseTo('orders', \u2026)`) and the next order is the next one. A number another order already has is passed over rather than refused by the unique index, so a missed step never leaves a paid order unplaced.\n    * **The account side can go by its own name.** New setting **Account Name** (`account.name`, empty by default) on the **Customer accounts** tab. A site where customers sign in to the site itself but buy from the store read \"Your sign-in link for Grav Premium\" beside a forum account. With it set (`getgrav.org`, say), the sign-in link, the set-password invitation and the password reset email use it in their subject, text, card and footer, and with `seo.store_titles` on so do the sign-in card, the set-password page and the account pages' titles (\"Your orders | getgrav.org\"). The order emails and the shop's pages keep the store's name. Empty, everything is named as before. Every storefront page is also handed `kahunacart_account_side` (true on the sign-in card, the set-password page and the account pages), so a theme can word its breadcrumbs and headings to match. See [Customer accounts](customer-accounts.md#sign-in-links) and [Naming the store, not the site](emails.md#naming-the-store-not-the-site)\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **Prices print and sales work out several times faster.** Every price on a storefront page went through brick\/money's arbitrary-precision arithmetic just to put a decimal point into a whole number of cents, and every sale badge did the same to take a percentage off. `Money::format()` now writes the number with integer arithmetic and one cached ICU formatter per locale, and sale, tier, coupon preview, price list and rule percentages are worked out in integers with the same single HALF_UP rounding, so every formatted price and every cent is byte-for-byte what it was (an odd rate like `1\/3` or an amount too large for 64-bit arithmetic still goes through brick\/money). Measured per call on PHP 8.4: formatting a price went from 4.1 to 0.56 microseconds, a percentage from 12.1 to 0.17, resolving a variant's sale from 13.3 to 1.0, and the decimal prices in a product page's structured data from 3.5 to 0.10. On a profiled 5,000 product store that was around 10 ms of every product and catalog page. New helpers `Money::percentageMinor()` and `Money::decimal()` give add-ons the same fast answers.\n    * **A code that stops applying leaves the cart.** Adding a line, changing a quantity or removing one now takes off any code that no longer applies (it no longer beats a best-of sale, the cart dropped below its minimum, and so on) and says why, in the same words the code would get if typed in now. Before, the code stayed listed doing nothing until checkout or the next code was typed, so the cart could show a code chip that wasn't taking anything off.\n    * **A tidier cart.** The discount field waits behind a quiet **Apply discount** link (a `<details>`, so it opens without scripting too), and a code on the cart is a small chip with what it took off and a \u00d7 to take it off (labelled \"Remove code \u2026\" for screen readers), in place of a bulleted list and a big Remove button. The totals, the estimate box, **Checkout** and the express buttons now sit together in one summary column, in that order, so the shopper sees what they'll pay before the button that pays it. Express checkout has a block of its own under Checkout, after an \"or\" divider rather than \"or continue below\", with its buttons the column's width and, on the `auto` theme, a softer fill than the page's full ink. Checkout keeps the express buttons at the top with \"or continue below\", as before. `kahunacart-cart.html.twig`, `partials\/kahunacart-coupon.html.twig` and `partials\/kahunacart-express.html.twig` are now 1.3.6 for themes that override them; a theme that places `cart.express` after its own Checkout button (Kahuna does) gets the new divider with no change\n    * **A store of downloads doesn't show the extras it can't use.** The checkout's gift card box shows only when a gift card is on sale or one has been issued and not spent. The account area's **Addresses** tab and the landing page's addresses panel are left out when nothing in the store ships, checkout asks a download's buyer for the country only, and the customer has saved no address. The **Stock alerts** tab is left out of the navigation when nothing in the store is stock-managed and the customer has no alert open. An archived product counts for nothing and a draft counts. Each page still answers at its address, and a store that uses any of them sees no change. `kahunacart-account.html.twig`, `partials\/kahunacart-account-nav.html.twig` and `partials\/kahunacart-gift-card.html.twig` are now 1.3.6 for themes that override them.\n    * The cart's estimate box no longer offers to estimate shipping on a cart with nothing to deliver. It is titled **Estimate tax** there, and on a store where every payment method is a merchant of record (so the tax is worked out at payment) a cart of downloads shows no box at all. `kahunacart-checkout.html.twig` and `partials\/kahunacart-estimate.html.twig` are now 1.3.6 for themes that override them.\n    * **The emailed sign-in link signs you straight in.** It used to open a page saying \"Press the button to finish signing in\" with a **Sign me in** button. Now the page says **Signing you in\u2026** and posts itself at once (`assets\/kahunacart-signin-page.js`), so clicking the link is the whole sign-in. A mail scanner that fetches the link still can't use it up: fetching the page spends nothing, only the POST does, and a scanner runs no script. Browsers with scripting off, and ones that report themselves as automated, get the button. Somebody already signed in who opens the link or the sign-in page goes where the sign-in would have taken them.\n    * **A proper sign-in page.** `{base}\/signin` is one centred card in four states: the email box with its label above it and a full-width button; **Check your email**, showing the address that was typed, how long the link lasts, **Send it again** and **Use a different address**; **Signing you in\u2026**; and **That link has expired**, with the email box right there for a new one (a used, lapsed or forged link all get this one answer, as before). The \"sent\" and \"expired\" states are held in the session for one draw, so the typed address never goes into a URL. The card is the new `partials\/kahunacart-signin-card.html.twig`, which a theme can include with its own `signin_heading` and `signin_intro`; `kahunacart-signin.html.twig` is now 1.3.6 for themes that override it. New strings `SIGNIN_EMAIL_LABEL`, `SIGNIN_EMAIL_PLACEHOLDER`, `SIGNIN_SENT_TITLE`, `SIGNIN_SENT_TO`, `SIGNIN_SENT_LIFETIME`, `SIGNIN_SENT_HELP`, `SIGNIN_RESEND`, `SIGNIN_DIFFERENT_EMAIL`, `SIGNIN_SIGNING_IN`, `SIGNIN_SIGNING_IN_TEXT`, `SIGNIN_EXPIRED_TITLE` and `SIGNIN_EXPIRED_TEXT`; `SIGNIN_CONFIRM` is shorter and `SIGNIN_LINK_INVALID`, `SIGNIN_ACCOUNT_PANEL` and `SIGNIN_ACCOUNT_PANEL_LINK` are gone.\n    * **The account overview no longer tells a signed-in customer how to sign in.** Its \"Sign in with a link\" panel (\"Forgotten your password next time?\u2026\") is gone; the sign-in page itself offers both routes.\n    * **No empty Downloads tab.** **Your downloads** is left out of the account navigation for a customer with no download at all, like the Addresses and Stock alerts tabs, so a store selling licence keys (installed by a package manager, not downloaded) no longer has buyers looking for their product in an empty tab. Their first grant brings it back, and the page still answers at its address. `partials\/kahunacart-account-nav.html.twig` reads the new `account_downloads`.\n    * **Storefront pages do less before they start.** Three things every request paid for, whether or not the page used them. The plugin's autoloader declared the eleven hundred functions of thecodingmachine\/safe (brought in by dompdf's CSS parser) on every request, and now loads them the first time anything touches dompdf, which is an invoice PDF (the autoloader went from 7.6 ms to 1.5 ms with opcache's file cache). The notification registry was built at init to put add-ons' event listeners on the dispatcher, and is now built on the first event that needs it or the first time anything asks for it; the events add-ons answer are remembered between requests, an event fired before the registry exists is still answered, and a store where no add-on answers an event does nothing at all. Where the encrypted provider settings are in the plugins config was found by walking the whole config on every page; the list of keys (never a value) is now kept in Grav's cache until a config file changes, so a store with nothing encrypted does no walk and one with secrets goes straight to them, decrypting at init as before. On the demo store the home page went from about 62 ms to about 53 ms and a product page from about 70 ms to about 63 ms in a command-line harness.\n    * **The category nav's counts are kept between requests.** The numbers in the category tree a theme draws with `kahunacart_categories()` (the home page draws it twice) were counted on every page: on a catalog of 5,000 products that is 1.7 ms on SQLite and was 10.9 ms profiled on MySQL for the home page. They are now kept in Grav's cache under a catalog version that every product, variant, category, tag, sale, price list and member-only save moves (and a reindex), so the next page after a save shows the new numbers, and they are kept apart for each audience the way the other catalog caches are. A stock movement does not move the version, so a checkout never writes the shared row. `catalog.facet_cache` sets their lifetime too, as a backstop for writes that go around the plugin, and `0` counts them on every page as before. See [Performance \u2192 The category nav on every other page](performance.md#the-category-nav-on-every-other-page)\n    * **Category and search pages no longer count the whole catalog's attributes on every request.** Beside the numbers on its tick boxes, the filter panel asks how many products in the whole visible catalog answer each attribute value, to decide which values to offer at all. On a catalog of 5,000 products that was two grouped counts reading 38,141 and 25,692 rows, 67 to 70 ms of the 87 to 89 ms a category or search page spent in the database on MariaDB, and it held those pages at 85 to 92 requests a second on all three engines. The answer is now kept in Grav's cache under the catalog version and the visitor's audience, like the category nav's counts, so the next page after a change shows the new numbers: writing or removing a product's attribute values moves the version wherever the write comes from, as do publishing, unpublishing, hiding or deleting a product, creating or deleting an attribute or changing its type or whether it is filterable, and member-only saves. A stock movement does not. `catalog.facet_cache` sets the lifetime as a backstop and `0` counts on every page as before. See [Performance \u2192 The whole-catalog attribute counts](performance.md#the-whole-catalog-attribute-counts)\n    * **Cached facet counts are about a sixth of the size.** A listing's category memberships were cached as one row per product-category pair, nearly half a megabyte on a 5,000-product catalog, and reading that back cost more than the query it saved. The filter panel's counts are now cached already folded (a list of ids per category, a number per tag and per attribute value): 86 KB instead of 486 KB for the memberships, and 0.08 ms instead of 0.66 ms to read and fold. Entries written the old way are simply never read again.\n    * **A product page with options no longer reads every option mapping in the store.** Finding which values each of a product's variants carries joined on a column no index started with, so MariaDB read the whole `kahunacart_variant_option_values` table on every product page (6.9 ms on a store of 5,000 products with two axes each, 0.034 ms after) and SQLite scanned its unique key (4.2 ms, 0.003 ms after). Migration 0093 adds the index `ix_kc_variant_option_option` on `(option_id, variant_id, value_id)`; no rows change.\n    * **A cart recalculation writes only what changed.** Every cart add, cart view, checkout step and storefront API call recalculates the cart, and each one used to rewrite every line, delete and re-insert every adjustment and every parcel, and read the order three times, even when nothing had moved. It now compares what it computed with the rows it already read and writes only the differences, so a cart that has not changed costs reads only and a commit that writes nothing, which on MySQL and PostgreSQL is the log flush every one of those requests was paying for. On the benchmark's five-line cart the MariaDB log showed 26 statements, 9 of them writes, for one recalculation; the same recalculation is now 14 statements and no writes. On a cart with a discount, two parcels and tax on goods and shipping, a recalculation that changes nothing went from 20 statements (13 writes) to 5 (none), and one that changes a single quantity from 20 (13 writes) to 16 (9): that line, the adjustments and parcel it moved, and the totals. Unchanged adjustments and parcels now keep their ids. The order's `updated_at` is still refreshed whenever anything was written, when a line was edited since it was last stamped, and otherwise at most once a minute, so abandoned-cart reminders and the inactive-cart purge see a customer's activity exactly as before.\n    * **Faster catalog pages on MySQL, MariaDB and PostgreSQL.** A listing or search page answered from the catalog index joined every product row before it sorted, so MariaDB built a temporary table of whole products (descriptions and all) and sorted it on disk, and PostgreSQL hashed all of them on every request. The listing now picks the page of product ids from the index first and loads only those products, in the same order, with the same pages and the same count. On a copy of the 5,000-product benchmark store a category page went from about 16 ms to under 3 ms on MariaDB, and search from about 15 ms to 7 ms. SQLite gets the same statement.\n    * **Faster catalog search on PostgreSQL.** Ranking a search re-read every matched product's text on each request (460 ms for a common word on the benchmark store). Migration 0094 stores the search vector in a new `search_vector` column on the catalog index, filled by the database itself, and search now matches and ranks from it: about 3 ms, with the same results in the same order. `bin\/plugin kahunacart index` reports it as `pgsql_tsvector_stored`. A PostgreSQL older than 12 keeps the previous index.\n    * **Price low to high, name and manual order read straight off an index.** With featured products listed first, these three sorts sorted the whole catalog for every page. Migration 0094 adds an index for each that already holds that order, and replaces the name and manual ones it supersedes. A deep page sorted by price went from 8 ms to 0.5 ms on PostgreSQL.\n    * **Order search is fast on MySQL and MariaDB, and puts the order you named first.** A search's words that are in most of the store's orders (an email address's domain, an order number's prefix, found by sampling the store's own orders) are no longer handed to the full-text index to intersect, and are checked on the few rows the other words found instead, which gives exactly the same orders. On the benchmark store (250,000 orders) a search by email address or by order number spent 200 to 250 ms on MariaDB intersecting those words and now takes a few milliseconds. On every engine, an order whose number, invoice number or email address is exactly what was typed (in any case) now comes first, followed by the other matches newest first as before, so a search for order 1042 no longer lists 10420 to 10429 and 104200 upwards above it. The orders found and the count are unchanged. Migration 0095 indexes the orders' email address for that lookup.\n    * **Catalog search on MySQL and MariaDB no longer slows down with every sale.** A product is reindexed on every save and every stock movement, and the catalog index deleted and re-inserted all of its rows each time. InnoDB keeps every deleted row in a `FULLTEXT` index as a dead document until the table is optimized, so a store's search got slower with each sale: on the 5,000-product benchmark store one upgrade and reindex left 30,478 dead documents against about 15,000 live rows, and a search for \"everyday\" went from 21 ms to 57 ms and \"granite leather\" from 1.1 ms to 12.4 ms. A reindex now reads the product's rows and terms, compares them with what it computed, and updates, inserts or deletes only the ones that differ, so a reindex that changes nothing writes nothing, a changed row is updated in place and keeps its id, and a stock change never rewrites the search text. Measured on MariaDB with 2,000 products (6,000 rows): five rounds of one sale per product used to leave 30,000 dead documents and take \"everyday\" from 1.9 ms to 7.4 ms and \"granite leather\" from 0.9 ms to 5.9 ms; they now leave none and search stays where it was, and each round went from 6,000 writes in 690 ms to no writes in 320 ms. A full rebuild (`index --rebuild`, or the admin's rebuild) deletes every row by design, so on MySQL and MariaDB it now ends with `OPTIMIZE TABLE kahunacart_catalog_index`, which recreates the table in under a second for 15,000 rows; a failure is logged and the rebuild still completes. `indexed_at` on an index row is now when that row last changed. SQLite and PostgreSQL write less too (no FTS5 churn, fewer dead rows to vacuum). `orders:reindex --text` also skips orders whose search text is already right, which MySQL did by itself but PostgreSQL and SQLite did not.\n    * **The storefront search page does a fraction of the work.** Search asked the product index for every match with a highlighted snippet of each, and only ever read the ids, so a common word built several hundred snippets per request for a page of 24 cards. It now asks for the ids alone.\n    * **Faster report totals.** The refund total every report shows was a scan of the whole transactions table; migration 0095 adds an index that reads just the refunds in the range (70 ms on MariaDB on the benchmark store, now well under a millisecond). The top products list is now ranked by the database, which names the ten in the same query, rather than fetched for every product and sorted afterwards. The products, totals and order are the same.\n    * **Password sign-in in the store's own sign-in card.** **Sign in with a password instead** on `{base}\/signin` used to send the customer to the Login plugin's `\/login` page, which looks nothing like the store. The same card now has a `password` state at `{base}\/signin?password`: email and password fields, a **Sign in** button, **Forgot your password?** when the Login plugin has a reset page, and **Email me a link instead**. It posts the Login plugin's own `login.login` task, so the rate limit, two-factor and account checks are the Login plugin's as before; a customer with two-factor goes on to its code form. A wrong password comes back to the card with the address kept and the Login plugin's message on it. The site's own `\/login` page is unchanged. Themes can set `signin_password_heading`, `signin_password_intro` and `signin_icons` on the card. New strings `SIGNIN_PASSWORD_TITLE`, `SIGNIN_PASSWORD_INTRO`, `SIGNIN_PASSWORD_LABEL`, `SIGNIN_PASSWORD_BUTTON`, `SIGNIN_FORGOT_PASSWORD`, `SIGNIN_USE_LINK`. See [Signing in with a password](customer-accounts.md#signing-in-with-a-password)\n    * **\"Forgot your password?\" in the sign-in card.** The password state's reset link went to the Login plugin's own `\/forgot_password` page, which looks nothing like the store (white on white under some themes), its email linked to `\/reset_password`, and a reset ended on `\/login`. They are now three more states of the same card: `{base}\/signin?forgot` asks for the email address, \"Check your email\" follows, and the link in the email opens **Choose a new password** (a new password, the same again and the site's password rules) at `{base}\/signin`. A saved password lands on the password state with the address filled in. The Login plugin still does the work, posting its own `login.forgot` and `login.reset` tasks, so its rate limit, token and email are unchanged; KahunaCart only says where each step lands, for requests to `{base}\/signin` alone (`KahunaCartFrontend::preparePasswordReset()` on both tasks at priority 100, below the protected-customer guard). The new password must also match its second box and pass `system.pwd_regex` on the server, which the Login plugin's own page left to the browser. A theme can pass `signin_forgot_heading` and `signin_forgot_intro`. New strings `SIGNIN_FORGOT_TITLE`, `SIGNIN_FORGOT_INTRO`, `SIGNIN_FORGOT_BUTTON`, `SIGNIN_FORGOT_SENT_TO`, `SIGNIN_FORGOT_SENT`, `SIGNIN_FORGOT_HELP`, `SIGNIN_FORGOT_AGAIN`, `SIGNIN_BACK_TO_PASSWORD`, `SIGNIN_RESET_TITLE`, `SIGNIN_RESET_INTRO`, `SIGNIN_NEW_PASSWORD_LABEL` and `SIGNIN_RESET_BUTTON`. See [Forgetting a password](customer-accounts.md#forgetting-a-password)\n    * **An order with nothing to ship shows its payment alone in the account.** The account overview's recent orders and **Your orders** showed \"Paid\", \"Placed\" and \"Not shipped\" on every order, which on a licence or a download says one thing three times, and \"Not shipped\" is wrong for it. An order with nothing to ship now shows only its payment (\"Paid\", \"Refunded\", \"Awaiting payment\"), plus its order status when that is canceled or held by a merchant's own status. An order with something to ship keeps all three, and the admin is unchanged. New Twig function `kahunacart_order_status_axes(order)`; `partials\/kahunacart-order-status.html.twig` is now 1.3.6 for themes that override it.\n    * **The receipt's closing links are a row, and a theme can make them buttons.** View invoice, Download PDF, Your orders and Back to shop were bare links separated by spaces, so they read as one sentence. They now wrap as a spaced row, each carries `kahunacart-order-link` and a name (`--invoice`, `--pdf`, `--orders`, `--shop`, and `--signin` on \"Sign in to manage your purchases\"), and the arrow on Back to shop is its own element, so a theme can draw them as buttons with an icon each without copying the page.\n    * **Persistent connections now come from grav-db-kit 1.0.3.** `database.persistent` and the persistent key (`kahunacart`) are unchanged, and `ConnectionFactory::create()` reads the toggle itself; KahunaCart's own `PersistentConnection` is gone. Building the kit's fix also closes a latent bug the old code had: a second connection opened in the same request built a second PDO on the same persistent handle, and PHP rolling back a persistent handle's open transaction whenever any PDO object on it is freed meant that second PDO going out of scope could roll back the first connection's work in progress. The kit now hands back the same connection instead, so a second `connection()` call in one request can no longer undo the first.\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Storefront search could become very slow under load after a deploy or a restore.** When the product search index was missing (a deploy or restore without `user\/data`, a cleared data folder) or behind the store (a bulk import that wrote products directly), the search that noticed rebuilt the whole index inside the shopper's request, deleting the old file first. Every other search in the meantime saw the index missing and started a rebuild of its own, deleting the one in progress, and a search that opened a half-built file failed and rebuilt again. On the benchmark store that was 2,367 rebuilds of 13 seconds each over two days of load tests, and search throughput fell from 127 to 35 requests a second with a 4.5 second p95. Searches also wrote to the index file on every connection (YetiSearch's R-tree support check, and its first statistics run), which made other searches fail now and then with `vtable constructor failed: products_fts` and set off the same rebuilds on a store whose index was fine. Now only one rebuild runs at a time, under a lock file beside the index; a web request never rebuilds, it books a `search.reindex` job (run right after its response, or by the worker) and searches the portable way until the index is ready; the new index is built in a file of its own and switched in by the marker when complete, so searches keep using the old one meanwhile and never see a half-built file; a new index is built so that searches only read it, and a search that fails is asked once more before anything is rebuilt; and a rebuild that fails, or one caused by a failing search, is not retried for five minutes. An existing index keeps working as it is, with the retry; the next rebuild, or `bin\/plugin kahunacart search:reindex`, gives it the read-only build. Index files are now named `products.<build stamp>.db` and the old one is removed a couple of minutes after it is replaced. `tests\/search-rebuild-concurrency.php` checks all of this with real processes.\n    * **The admin's order list follows a custom-field filter in its link.** `GET \/kahunacart\/orders?cf[printful_status]=error` has narrowed the list since custom fields shipped, but the admin screen only read `status`, `payment`, `fulfillment` and `refund_request` from its route, so a dashboard alert routed to `\/orders?cf[printful_status]=error` opened the whole list. The screen now passes every `cf[\u2026]` in its route to the server and shows a **Filtered** chip with an **All orders** chip beside it. See [Custom fields](docs\/custom-fields.md)\n    * **A store selling only through a merchant of record quoted tax of its own before payment.** A merchant of record like Polar works out the tax and adds it on its own page, and the order it is handed carries none of the store's, but until a payment method was picked the cart and checkout ran the store's own tax anyway. A store with no tax set up showed an estimate of \"Tax $0.00\", which tells a buyer in Germany there is no VAT to pay, and one with a rate left in its settings showed a tax that was never charged. Where every payment method on offer is a merchant of record, the cart, the checkout summary and the pay page now leave out any local tax line and the total it added, and say \"Any tax is added when you pay.\" under the total (`TAX_AT_PAYMENT`). The cart offers no tax estimate there: a cart of downloads gets no estimate box, and one with a parcel gets **Estimate shipping** with no tax line. The price note under a product (`tax.price_note`, when on) says the same line instead of \"incl.\" or \"excl.\" anything. The order after payment shows what was charged, as before, and a store with any other method on offer is unchanged. \"On offer\" means the methods checkout would show for the cart, so store credit, which is offered only to a signed-in customer whose balance covers the order, no longer made a Polar store look like a mixed one to the estimate box. `partials\/kahunacart-totals.html.twig` and `partials\/kahunacart-estimate.html.twig` are now 1.3.6 for themes that override them. See [Tax \u2192 Merchant-of-record providers](tax.md#merchant-of-record-providers)\n    * **A guest's cart was empty after they signed in.** The Login plugin rotates the session id on every login and Grav starts the new id on an empty session, so a cart (with its coupon) that a guest filled before signing in was gone afterwards, by password and by the emailed sign-in link alike, and the \"a cart this session started is theirs now\" adoption never had a cart to adopt. KahunaCart now holds its own session keys across the rotation (`onUserLogin`, either side of the Login plugin's listener) and puts back whichever the new session lacks, so the cart, its coupon link and the visit's attribution survive signing in and the cart is put in the customer's name.\n    * **The plain-text sign-in email printed a `<strong>` tag** around the store's name. The HTML version keeps the bold; the text version now drops the tag.\n    * **The plain-text order emails ran an add-on's links together and lost their addresses.** An order panel's markup was flattened with its tags stripped, so two paragraphs with no line break between them in the source read as one (\"View this on your order pageSign in to manage your licenses\"), and every link lost its address. The plain-text part now gets each block on its own line and each link as its words and its address. The confirmation, payment received, payment failed, balance request, quote and pay link text templates are now 1.3.6 for themes that override them.\n    * **Imported orders were short a line a day in the reports.** The day's report figures were recomputed when an imported order was dated and before its line was added, so on a store whose history came in through the importer each day counted one line too few (a rehearsal of 2,287 imported orders read 1,105 items sold) until somebody ran `reports:rebuild`. The line now goes in first, and a line a later row adds to an order that is already dated recomputes its day.\n    * **Imported refunds read \"Refunded $0.00\" on the dashboard.** The reports count refunds from the store's refund records, and an order imported as refunded had none. The order importer now records one for it, dated `refunded_at` (or with the order), for the whole total, or for the amount in the new `refunded` column, which is how a partly refunded order gets one. It's recorded under the provider `import`, so no payment driver ever acts on it; importing the file again doesn't add a second, and an order with a refund made in the store is left alone. See [Order fields](importing.md#order-fields)\n    * **A customer with no name was greeted by their address twice** on the account overview (\"andy@example.com \u2014 andy@example.com\"), because the name fell back to the account's username, which for a store account is the address. The address now shows once.\n    * **A password sign-in lands where the customer was going.** Signing in with a password from `{base}\/signin?return=\/account\/licenses\/<ref>` landed on the sign-in page and then the default landing page, because the Login plugin's own listener overwrote the saved return address on the sign-in page. The return (or `customers.signin_landing` when there is none) is now written just before the Login plugin's login task reads it, with the same checks as the emailed link, so nothing off the store is ever a redirect target.\n    * **The order actions line up with their row.** On the account's order list the Receipt \/ Invoice \/ Download PDF cell was a flex box, which takes a table cell out of the table, so its bottom border sat apart from the rest of the row. It is a plain cell again, with the links wrapping along one line.\n    * **A discount code on several lines was listed once per line** on the receipt, the checkout, the pay page, the invoice and its PDF: \"Coupon SAVE \u2212$100.00\" and then \"Coupon SAVE \u2212$75.00\" for one code on two lines. The cart already showed one row. Every page that draws the totals is now handed `summary_adjustments` (each code and each sale on one row, from the new `Checkout\\AdjustmentSummary`, which the cart, the storefront API and the agentic totals use too), and the totals block and the PDF print it. The stored rows stay one per line, so refunds, reports, tax and the admin are unchanged.\n    * **A password reset asked for in the store's sign-in card came from the site, not the store.** The Login plugin sends that email from the Email plugin's sender and names the site by its title, so on getgrav.org it came from \"Grav Forum <notifications@getgrav.org>\", said the reset was asked for on \"Grav CMS\", was signed \"John Appleseed\" (Grav's placeholder author) and put \"Grav Forum\" on the customer's own address. For a reset asked for on `{base}\/signin`, and only for that request, it now goes out from the store's sender with the store's name and the customer's bare address, in the store's email layout like the sign-in link (\"Choose a new password\", one button, a quiet line for somebody who didn't ask), from the new `emails\/account\/password-reset.html.twig` and `.txt.twig`. Resets asked for anywhere else are unchanged.\n    * **\"Sign in to manage your purchases\" on the receipt went to the Login plugin's `\/login`.** It opens the store's own sign-in card at `{base}\/signin` now, like every other store sign-in link.\n    * **A replacement that left a column out kept the old value of it.** `OrderRepository::replaceAdjustments()` and `ShippingPackageRepository::replaceForOrder()` skip the write when the stored rows already match, but compared only the columns the caller named, so a row that left out `item_id` or `included` (or a parcel's method, weight or metadata) was judged unchanged and the old value stayed, where the delete and insert they replaced put it back to the column's default. An omitted column now gets its default (NULL, or 0) before the comparison and the insert. The calculator names every column and was not affected; this is for add-ons and scripts that call the repositories directly with sparser rows.\n    * **A product edit made while the search index was being rebuilt could stay out of the index for good.** A save that had to wait for a running rebuild gave up after thirty seconds and dropped its product ids, and because the rebuild had already read that product, nothing ever wrote the new title, price or visibility into the new index until the product was saved again (the high-water mark only notices new products). The ids are now kept in a `search.reindex` job of their own, which writes those products into whichever index is live once the rebuild has let go of the lock, and is tried again with the queue's backoff (up to eight times) while the lock is still held.\n    * **An order search on MySQL or MariaDB could lose an order whose word is in a long hyphenated name.** A word that is in most of a store's orders (`gmail`, the order number's prefix) is checked outside the full-text `MATCH` for speed, as a whole word between spaces. The order's search text stops at 500 bytes and can end on a token like `rare-gmail-last` stored whole without the parts written after it, so an order the full-text index finds by `gmail` was turned away by the check. A word now counts wherever the server's tokenizer would start or end it: at the start or end of the text, or beside a space, `@`, `.`, `+` or `-`. It is a `REGEXP` on the few rows the `MATCH` has already found, so the search costs what it did.\n    * **A customer with more than fifty orders had only their newest fifty listed first when the order search was their email address.** The orders whose number, invoice number or email address is exactly the search term go to the top of the admin's order list, but the promotion was a list of the newest fifty ids, so the fifty-first order at that address sat behind a newer look-alike such as `buyer@example.com.au`. The search now sorts by the lookup's own condition, so every order at the address goes first, however many there are. The set of orders and the count beside the page are what they were.\n    * **An order number in mixed case was not put first on SQLite and PostgreSQL.** The order search puts an order whose number or invoice number is exactly the search term at the top, and on SQLite and PostgreSQL it tried the term as typed, in capitals and in lower case, so `kc-1042` found `KC-1042` but not an imported `Kc-1042`, and a newer order that only began with the number came first. The lookup now compares without case (`COLLATE NOCASE` on SQLite, `LOWER()` on PostgreSQL), as the email address has since 1.3.6, and migration 0097 indexes the number and the invoice number that way so it stays a probe rather than a scan. Nothing changes on MySQL and MariaDB, whose columns already ignore case, and numbers stored in capitals (`GP-1304`, typed `gp-1304`) were found before and still are.\n    * **A percentage rate with a trailing newline was accepted.** The integer fast path for `Money::percentageMinor()` ended its pattern in `$`, which also matches before a final newline, so `percentageMinor(1999, \"5\\n\", \"USD\")` answered 100 where brick\/math refuses the rate. The pattern now ends at the end of the string, and a rate with a trailing newline or space goes to brick and is refused as it always was. Rates the store writes itself are plain numbers and were not affected.\n"},"1.3.5":{"date":"09\/27\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Pages only a customer group can see, and groups an add-on grants while something is live.** Every customer group a store account is in is now a permission, `site.customer_group.<code>`, so a page with `access: site.customer_group.members: true` in its header opens only for customers in `members`, and a Downloads Pro page restricted that way keeps its files from everybody else too. The permissions are registered, so a page's **Security \u2192 Access** picker in Admin Next lists them under **Customer groups**. They are worked out on every request from the groups the customer holds (never the store default, and never stored on the account), so a customer taken out of a group is refused on their next click, and nothing outside `site.` is ever reachable. Add-ons can now put a customer in a group for as long as they say so by answering the new `onKahunaCartCustomerGroupsGranted` event (`GroupService::EVENT_GRANTED`) with a group code and a reason; the Subscriptions add-on uses it for a plan's group while the subscription is live. A granted group counts everywhere a ticked one does (price lists, coupon and sale conditions, member-only products, the account page's badges and the new permission), is never written as a membership, and shows on the customer's **Customer groups** panel as a line with the add-on's reason rather than a ticked box. `GET \/kahunacart\/customers\/{id}` answers with `granted_groups`, and `effective_groups` includes them. Asked for by a merchant selling private downloads to subscribers. See [Pages only a group can see](customer-accounts.md#pages-only-a-group-can-see) and [Groups an add-on puts a customer in](customers.md#groups-an-add-on-puts-a-customer-in)\n    * **What a tax add-on needs to sell to an EU business under reverse charge, without any VAT rules in core.** A tax engine is now handed the buyer's VAT number as `TaxContext::$buyerTaxId` (the country, the number, and whether the last check said `valid`, `invalid` or `unchecked`, and when; never a name, an email or a customer id), and can answer `TaxQuoteResult::reverseCharge()`. The order is then treated exactly like one entered with **Don't charge tax**: nothing is charged, an inclusive price is charged net as a `tax` row, `tax_exempt` is set in `data_json` with the engine's answer beside it as `reverse_charge`, and the documents print the VAT number and the reverse-charge line. The answer is asked for again on every recalculation, so a shopper who removes their number or moves into the store's own country is charged VAT again, while a merchant's own **Don't charge tax** is never touched. A VAT number reaches the order through the new `onKahunaCartCheckoutTaxId` event, fired once per checkout attempt after the checkout fields are stored: the add-on hands over the number and core checks it through the registered tax-id validator itself, keeping the result as `vat_id_check` (country, status, `checked_at`, the validator, and the register's reference, name and address), so nothing a shopper sends can claim a check or an exemption. A register that is down leaves the number `unchecked` rather than invalid, the same number is checked at most once a day per order, and a number that cannot be one refuses the order against the add-on's field. `TaxIdValidationResult` gains `reference` (VIES's consultation number), and a valid check prints its date and reference under the VAT number on the receipt, invoice, invoice PDF and credit note, and on the admin's order view, which also names the engine that zero-rated the order. `KahunaCart::buyerTaxIds()` records a number on any cart and keeps a customer's number for next time on their own record, which erasure clears. Core still implements no validator and no engine, and VIES checks and reverse charge remain the tax add-on's. `partials\/kahunacart-customer-tax.html.twig` is stamped `@version` 1.3.5. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [Tax drivers \u2192 How a VAT number reaches the order](tax-drivers.md#how-a-vat-number-reaches-the-order)\n    * **Show the product safety details the EU asks for: the manufacturer, the EU responsible person and warnings.** A new **Catalogue \u2192 Manufacturers** screen keeps each maker's name, trade name, postal address, email and website, with the **EU responsible person** in a collapsed group for a manufacturer outside the EU, and counts the products that name each one. The product editor gets a **Product safety** panel with a **Manufacturer** select (\"Store default (name)\" first) and **Safety information** in Markdown, translated on the language tabs like the description. **Settings \u2192 General \u2192 Default Manufacturer** (`catalog.default_manufacturer`) names the manufacturer for every product that names none, so a shop that makes its own goods sets it once. The product page shows it all in a closed **Product safety** section under the specification table, and nothing on a product with no manufacturer and no safety text; the product's structured data names the manufacturer as a schema.org `Organization`. Deleting a manufacturer takes it off its products, which fall back to the store default. New table `kahunacart_manufacturers` and product columns `manufacturer_id` and `safety_info` (migration 0088); routes `GET`\/`POST \/kahunacart\/manufacturers` and `PATCH`\/`DELETE \/kahunacart\/manufacturers\/{id}` under `kahunacart.products.manage`, with MCP tools `list_manufacturers`, `create_manufacturer`, `update_manufacturer` and `delete_manufacturer`; `manufacturer_id` and `safety_info` on the products routes and `create_product`\/`update_product`. The catalog file carries a `manufacturers:` block and each product's manufacturer by name, creating one with just the name when an import names one the store hasn't got, and the admin's CSV importer reads a `manufacturer` column (by name, created the same way, a new product with none on the store default) and a `safety_info` column. A CSV column headed \"Manufacturer\" used to be read as the brand and is now the manufacturer; the brand is still found by \"Brand\", \"Brands\" or \"Vendor\". The demo catalog's tee and hoodie show a manufacturer outside the EU with its responsible person. Themes print it with the new `partials\/kahunacart-product-safety.html.twig` partial (include it with `ignore missing`) or the new `kahunacart_product_safety(product)` function; the shipped product page is stamped `@version` 1.3.5. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [Product safety (GPSR)](products-and-catalog.md#product-safety-gpsr)\n    * **Show the EU energy label, warranty badges and other labels near the price.** A product's new **Labels** panel adds two kinds. An **energy label** is the class and scale you pick, the full label as a picture and, if you have it, the product information sheet as a PDF: the shop draws the class arrow beside the price in the class's official colour with the scale's range beside it, a click opens the full label, and a \"Product information sheet\" link sits next to the arrow, which is the display the EU energy labelling rules ask of an online shop (Regulation (EU) 2017\/1369 and its product regulations). The seven colours are the regulations' own and live in one place, `Catalog\\Labels\\EnergyScale`, for the A to G scale and the older A+++ to D, A++ to E and A+ to F ones; a class off its scale is refused. A **label image** (a warranty badge, a certification) is a title, the full image and optionally a small one. Either can be limited to one variant, and the page swaps them as the shopper picks. Product cards and the hero cards draw the energy arrow under their price too. A label's files are product media with the new role `label`, never shown in the gallery. New table `kahunacart_product_labels` (migration 0089); routes `GET`\/`POST \/kahunacart\/products\/{id}\/labels` and `PATCH`\/`DELETE \/kahunacart\/products\/{id}\/labels\/{labelId}` under `kahunacart.products.manage`, with MCP tools `list_product_labels`, `create_product_label`, `update_product_label` and `delete_product_label`; labels travel in the catalog file, come across with Duplicate, and go with their product, their variant or their full image. Themes print them with the new `partials\/kahunacart-product-labels.html.twig` or `kahunacart_product_labels(product)`, and a theme drawing its own lead price passes `show_labels: false` to the buy button. The sample catalog gains a Coldwater Cool Box with a class C energy label and its sheet, and a warranty badge on the Dawn Patrol Hoodie. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [Product labels and the lightbox](product-labels.md)\n    * **A lightbox for labels and the product gallery.** A click on a label opens the full label over the page, and a click on a product page's gallery picture opens the product's pictures as a set at full size, with arrow keys, swipe and a \"2 of 5\" counter. It is the browser's own `<dialog>`, with nothing third-party: Esc, the close button and a click outside close it, focus comes back to what opened it, the large image loads only when opened, and it stays still for anyone who asked for reduced motion. With scripting off every opener is a plain link to the full image. Videos and embeds stay as they are. **Open Gallery Pictures in a Lightbox** (`catalog.lightbox`, on by default) turns it off for the gallery, which then shows its pictures as they were before, with no link to a larger one, for a store whose pictures aren't worth enlarging; labels always use it. A theme opens it on its own elements with `data-kc-lightbox` on a link and `{{ kahunacart_lightbox() }}`, which prints the dialog once per page (`partials\/kahunacart-lightbox.html.twig`). The buy button, product grid, gallery and hero section templates are stamped `@version` 1.3.5, so a theme overriding one of them shows in the template check until it draws the labels or the openers too. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [Product labels and the lightbox](product-labels.md#the-lightbox) and [Theming](theming.md#product-labels-and-the-lightbox)\n    * **Back-in-stock alerts: \"Notify me when it's back\".** Under the buy button, wherever the chosen variant can't be bought, a shopper can ask to be emailed when it is back. It follows the variant picker, so each size has its own state, and works without a page reload (and as a plain form without JavaScript). A signed-in customer gets one button that toggles: pressing it records the alert and the line becomes \"We'll email you when it's back \u00b7 Don't notify me\". A shopper who isn't signed in leaves an email address and gets a confirmation email, and nothing counts until they click its link (double opt-in). The job worker's sweep emails every confirmed alert once when its variant can be bought again, whichever way the stock came back, and marks it sent in the same transaction so it never sends twice; everyone waiting is told, not just as many as came in. The email shows the variant's own picture and the price a guest pays today (a sale or a price list's price, with the ordinary price struck beside it), and its link opens the product page on that variant. Unconfirmed requests expire after 48 hours, active ones after `stock_alerts.expire_days` (365), and alerts on a product taken off the shop expire rather than send. The confirmation email carries a \"Don't notify me\" link (the back-in-stock email doesn't, as its alert is finished by then), the confirm and cancel links open a page that confirms or cancels as soon as it opens in a browser, so one click in the email is enough while a mail scanner that only fetches links can't act on them, and a customer's account has a **Stock alerts** tab with a Remove button. Two new emails on the Notifications screen, `stock_alert.confirm` and `stock_alert.back_in_stock`, with templates `stock-alert-confirm` and `back-in-stock`. The product editor's variants table shows \"N waiting\", the customer record has a Stock alerts panel, and there are new routes `GET \/kahunacart\/products\/{id}\/stock-alerts`, `GET \/kahunacart\/customers\/{id}\/stock-alerts` and `POST \/kahunacart\/stock-alerts\/{id}\/cancel` with MCP tools `list_product_stock_alerts`, `list_customer_stock_alerts` and `cancel_stock_alert`. Guest requests are limited per address and per client (`security.rate_limits.stock_alert_request`), erasing a customer deletes their alerts including guest ones under the same address, and nothing here ever adds anybody to a mailing list. Settings `stock_alerts.enabled` (on) and `stock_alerts.expire_days`, next to the stock settings. Migrations 0090 and 0092. The offer is its own partial, `partials\/kahunacart-stock-alert-offer.html.twig`, which the buy button includes and a theme can include without one, and `stock_alert_text` changes its words. For a product that hasn't launched yet, pass `stock_alert_wording: 'launch'` to either partial: the choice is kept with the alert, so the button, the answer after pressing it (with or without JavaScript), the confirmation email and its page, and the email that goes out when it can be bought all say \"when it launches\" and \"is now available\" instead of \"back\", from their own `_LAUNCH` language strings. Asking again with the other wording rewords the alert. The store owner hears about each alert as it becomes active (a guest's when they click the confirmation link, a signed-in customer's when they press the button, and never for a repeat, a toggle off or a cancellation) through a new `owner.stock_alert_requested` row on the Notifications screen: one sentence such as \"Someone wants to know when Checkout.com launches (3 waiting)\" or \"Someone wants Sandbar Boardshort 32 \/ Slate back in stock (1 waiting)\", a link to the product in the admin, and never the shopper's email address. Like every store-owner row it starts on and ticked nowhere, so tick a channel on its row to receive it. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [Back-in-stock alerts](stock-alerts.md)\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart check` warns about an SMTP login on port 25.** Port 25 is for delivery between mail servers, and most of them offer no login there, so the mailer skips the username without an error and the server refuses the message with \"relay access denied\". The **Mail transport** check now warns when the transport is SMTP, the port is 25 and a username is set, and points at 587 with TLS or 465 with SSL. Port 25 with no username still passes, for a local relay. The environment probe records only whether a username is set (`smtp_login`), never the name. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [Going live \u2192 Mail transport](going-live.md#mail-transport)\n    * A product page link with `?variant={id}` now opens the page on that variant on every store, not only while agentic commerce is on, and on a variant that has sold out too, so the back-in-stock email lands the shopper on their size. The option picker now really starts there too: the buy button's plain select, which the picker starts from, was always set to the default variant, so on a product with option axes the picker went straight back to it. The id still has to be a variant of that product the picker can reach, and anything else is ignored. The structured data offer is the variant's own only while agentic commerce is on and the variant can be bought, as before\n    * The \"incl.\" in a tax line inside the prices, \"VAT (19% incl.)\", was English on every store. It is now a language string, `TAX_LABEL_INCLUDED`, written in the language the order was placed in like the rate's own name, so a German order reads \"MwSt. (19% inkl.)\" once the string is translated. The line carries a flag saying its label already says the tax is included, and the totals, estimate, wishlist and invoice PDF templates read that rather than looking for the English word, so they add \"(included)\" once in any language. An order already saved keeps its label\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A signed-in customer's cart was priced as a guest's until checkout.** A cart only got its customer when checkout's Continue wrote the email, and everything priced for a customer reads the customer off the cart, so a Trade customer saw the catalog's $12 in the cart and on the checkout page, and the first recalculation after Continue turned it into their price list's $9. The same went for a sale ruled on a customer group. A signed-in customer's cart is now in their name from its first line, a cart started as a guest is put in their name and priced again when they sign in (and on the cart and checkout pages for a session that was already signed in), and a cart with somebody else's name on it is never taken. No email is written, so none of this makes an abandoned-cart email\n    * A price list, sale or payment-method rule on the shopper's email or email domain only matched a signed-in customer from checkout's Continue, because that is when a cart gets an email, so a customer at acme.com saw the catalog price in the cart until then. A signed-in customer's cart is now judged against their account's email from its first line. The email only feeds the rules and is never written onto the cart, so it makes no abandoned-cart email. A guest still matches from checkout, once they have typed one\n    * Tax inside the prices read \"VAT (19% incl.) (included)\" in the cart, at checkout, in the shipping and tax estimate, on the wishlist estimate and on the invoice PDF, because the tax line's own label already says \"incl.\" and the templates added \"(included)\" after it. It now says it once. An included line whose label doesn't say so, such as one from a tax add-on, still gets \"(included)\". The totals, estimate, wishlist and invoice PDF templates are stamped 1.3.5\n    * A zone bound to a tax engine was never quoted by it at checkout, and a region or currency price preference never changed how checkout taxed a line, because the checkout's tax stage was handed the store's tax settings and never received them. Every zone was taxed from its own rates, with only the product's own setting and **Prices Include Tax** deciding whether a price included tax. Checkout now uses the bound engine and the price preferences, as the admin, the price note and the disqualification stage already did. A store with no engine bound and no price preferences is taxed exactly as before\n    * A search on a translated storefront with a filter ticked matched a product's translated buy button label, which the docs said it never did and the plain search already didn't. Both searches now match the translated title, summary and description only, which also keeps the new translated safety information out of search results\n    * On a MySQL or MariaDB server holding more than one KahunaCart database, migration 0036 could ask another database whether product attributes had been rebuilt for variants, and skip the rebuild on this one. The store then kept the old unique key without `variant_id`, so two variants giving an attribute the same value (two sizes that weigh the same) collided. The check now asks only about the database the store is connected to, and new migration 0091 repairs a store that already skipped the rebuild: on MySQL and MariaDB only, and only where the store's own key lacks `variant_id`, it gives the key `variant_id` and makes `variant_id` `NOT NULL DEFAULT 0` (a missing variant becomes 0, the product's own answer, as 0036 did), in place, without rebuilding the table or touching `store_id` or any other column. It does nothing on SQLite, PostgreSQL or a store 0036 rebuilt properly\n"},"1.3.4":{"date":"09\/26\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Edit a customer's address book from the admin.** A customer's **Address book** on **KahunaCart \u2192 Customers** now has **Add address**, **Edit**, **Make default billing**, **Make default shipping** and **Delete**, for the trade customer who orders by phone and never opens their account page. Addresses are checked by the same code the customer's own account page uses: a country from the list, a state that country has, stored as their codes so the tax and shipping zones match them. New routes `GET`\/`POST \/kahunacart\/customers\/{id}\/addresses`, `PATCH`\/`DELETE \/kahunacart\/customers\/{id}\/addresses\/{addressId}` and `POST \/kahunacart\/customers\/{id}\/addresses\/{addressId}\/default` (`customers.manage` to write, `customers.view` to read), `GET \/kahunacart\/countries` for the country and state lists, and MCP tools `list_customer_addresses`, `create_customer_address`, `update_customer_address`, `delete_customer_address`, `set_default_customer_address` and `list_countries`. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [A customer's address book](customers.md#a-customers-address-book)\n    * **Orders entered by hand take a billing and a shipping address, and are taxed and shipped from them.** **Orders \u2192 New order** has a billing address, **Ship to the billing address** ticked to start with, and a shipping address when it is unticked. Once the email matches a customer, both are filled from their default addresses, with a **Saved address** list to pick another. With EU tax zones set up, an order for a German address is charged German VAT, and a shipping zone prices the delivery, exactly as at checkout. **Save to the customer's address book as their defaults** is off unless ticked, and needs `kahunacart.customers.manage` as well as the orders permission: without it the tick is not shown and the API refuses `save_addresses` with a `403`. `POST \/kahunacart\/orders` and the `create_order` MCP tool take `billing`, `shipping`, `shipping_method_id` and `save_addresses`; a body without them builds the order it always did. `OrderBuilder::withAddresses()` now puts the addresses on the cart before the first line, so every add-on that builds orders with addresses gets them taxed too; before, they were stored on the order and priced nothing. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [Addresses on a hand-entered order](orders-and-admin.md#addresses-on-a-hand-entered-order)\n    * **Price list rows and quantity tiers pick their variant by product and SKU.** The **Variant id** box on a price list's rows and on the **Quantity tiers** tab is now the product-then-variant picker Sales and Coupons use, with each variant named by its option and SKU, and the tiers load as soon as a variant is picked. On a store with more than the 200 products the pickers list, each unpicked row and the tiers tab also take a variant id or a SKU typed into a box. The price list and tier routes, and their MCP tools, take `sku` in place of `variant_id` (one or the other, never both); a SKU no variant has, or one two variants share, is a `422` naming it. The product editor's variants table prints each variant's id under its name, for the API and MCP tools that still use it. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [Naming a variant by SKU](pricing.md#naming-a-variant-by-sku)\n    * **Say whether a price includes tax.** A new setting, **Settings \u2192 Tax \u2192 Say whether prices include tax** (`tax.price_note`), prints a small \"incl. VAT\" or \"excl. VAT\" line under product prices on the buy button, the product grid and the hero section cards. It asks the same rules checkout uses (the product's own setting, then the shopper's region, then the currency, then Prices Include Tax), for the place the shopper is known to be: the cart's address, the estimate box's country, or the store's default country. It uses the store's own name for the tax, the matched zone's rate label (\"VAT\", \"MwSt.\") or the flat `tax.label`, and prints nothing where checkout charges no tax: on a store that charges none, for a shopper whose country no zone covers, and for a product whose tax class has no rate above zero in the shopper's zone. A second switch, `tax.price_note_shipping`, adds \"plus shipping\" for products that ship. Both are off by default. Themes print it with the new `kahunacart_tax_note(product, variant)` function or the new `partials\/kahunacart-tax-note.html.twig` partial (include it with `ignore missing` to stay safe on older plugins), and a theme printing its own note beside a lead price passes `show_tax_note: false` to the buy button. The buy button, product grid and hero section templates are stamped `@version` 1.3.4. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [Tax \u2192 Saying so under the price](tax.md#saying-so-under-the-price)\n    * **Catch a background worker that sends email with other settings than the site.** On a store with a `user\/env\/<host>\/` folder, Admin Next saves settings into that folder, and cron runs `bin\/plugin kahunacart work` as the `cli` environment, which never reads it. The worker then sends order emails through the SMTP settings in `user\/config`, often with no login, and the mail server answers \"relay access denied\" while the admin's own test email works. A new **Worker environment** check in `bin\/plugin kahunacart check` and on the Health screen finds the store's host folders (from `security.site_urls` and `system.custom_base_url`) whose `email.yaml` or `kahunacart.yaml` differs from what the worker loads (`user\/config`, plus the worker's own environment folder when it runs as one), names the file and the settings (never their values), and says how to fix it: move them into `user\/config`, set `GRAV_ENVIRONMENT=<host>` in the root `.env`, or start the cron line with it. It fails for email settings and warns for KahunaCart's own, and the same thing shows as a new dashboard alert. The worker now records the environment it ran under on every tick, so the check knows rather than guesses. KahunaCart does not switch the worker into the host's environment on its own, because a staging copy whose config names the live address would then send its test emails through the live mail account. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [Going live \u2192 Worker environment](going-live.md#worker-environment) and [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md#relay-access-denied-but-test-emails-from-the-admin-work)\n    * **Sell to an EU business without VAT, under reverse charge, from Orders \u2192 New order.** A new **Tax** section has **Customer VAT ID** and **Don't charge tax**, off to start with. Ticked, the order is priced as if the customer were outside every tax zone: a price that excludes tax gets none added, and a price that includes tax is charged net, with the included tax taken off as a `tax` row (`subtype: disqualified_inclusive`) whatever `tax.adjust_price_when_disqualified` says. The store does not check the number; check it on VIES first. It is a bridge until a VIES tax add-on exists. The VAT number (spaces out, in capitals) and the exemption are kept in the order's `data_json` as `vat_id` and `tax_exempt`, which only `POST \/kahunacart\/orders` writes, so nothing a shopper sends can set them, and order changes and refunds keep reading the recorded tax. The order view shows the VAT ID and \"Not charged (reverse charge)\", and the receipt, the invoice, the invoice PDF and the credit note PDF print the VAT ID and, on an exempt order, a reverse-charge line: the new **Settings \u2192 Invoices \u2192 Reverse Charge Wording** (`invoice.reverse_charge_text`), or \"VAT reverse charge: the customer accounts for the VAT.\" when it is empty. `POST \/kahunacart\/orders` and the `create_order` MCP tool take `tax_exempt` and `vat_id` (a `vat_id` longer than 32 characters or holding anything but letters, digits and `. + * \/ -` is a `422`), and `GET \/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}` answers with `customer_tax`. The four templates print it through the new `partials\/kahunacart-customer-tax.html.twig` and are stamped `@version` 1.3.4, so a theme overriding one of them shows in the template check until it includes the partial. Asked for by a merchant in the EU. See [Tax \u2192 Orders entered with \"Don't charge tax\"](tax.md#orders-entered-with-dont-charge-tax)\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * An order entered on **Orders \u2192 New order** for a customer in a customer group was charged the catalogue price, because its lines were priced before the customer was put on the order. A customer on file is now on the order before its lines, so their group's price list and any sale for their group apply exactly as at their own checkout. An email nobody has ordered with yet is still priced as a guest. Renewals, bookings and rentals are priced as they always were. Asked for by a merchant in the EU\n"},"1.3.3":{"date":"09\/26\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Tell the shopper a variant is on backorder.** A tracked variant with nothing left and backorders allowed now says so: next to the buy button, on the option picker's status line, in `kahunacart_stock()` for a theme's stock line and card badge, and on its cart and checkout lines. Each variant gets an optional **Backorder note** under the Backorders select (\"Expected to ship late October\"), and the store gets a default under **Settings \u2192 Stock \u2192 Backorder Wording** (`stock.backorder_text`); with neither set it says \"On backorder\". A cart line with only part of its quantity on the shelf counts it, \"2 of 3 on backorder \u00b7 Expected to ship late October\". The button still says Add to cart. When the order is placed the wording is frozen onto the line, so the admin order view, the confirmation email and the receipt keep saying it after the stock arrives. `kahunacart_stock()` gains a `backorder` key (with `reason` `backorder` and the label in `label`). `label` and `reason` used to be empty whenever the product could be bought, so a theme that took a non-empty `label` to mean sold out should check `sold_out` instead. The lines handed to the cart, checkout, receipt, pay page and confirmation email carry a `backorder` key; a placed line whose quantity is changed afterwards shows just the label, since the frozen count no longer fits it. New column `kahunacart_variants.backorder_note` (migration 0087), taken by the products API, the `update_variant` MCP tool, the catalog file, the CSV importer, Duplicate and variants generated from options. The shipped cart, checkout, receipt, pay page, buy button and confirmation email templates print it and are stamped `@version` 1.3.3, so a theme overriding one of them shows in the template check until it prints the line too. Asked for by PalmTree. See [Telling the shopper it is on backorder](products-and-catalog.md#telling-the-shopper-it-is-on-backorder)\n    * **The sample catalog shows a backorder both ways.** `bin\/plugin kahunacart seed` now puts the Tide Line Beanie's Kelp colour at zero with backorders allowed and the note \"Knitted to order. Ships in about three weeks.\", and leaves the Saltwater Socks with one pair on the shelf and backorders allowed, so two in the cart read \"1 of 2 on backorder\" in the store's own wording. The demo store resets from this catalog every hour\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * The catalog index read a variant's backorders setting with a raw comparison against `deny`, so a hand-edited or imported row holding `no` or `0` was filed under \"backorder\" in the stock filter while the cart refused to sell it below zero. It now reads it the way the cart does\n    * The admin product list called a variant at zero with backorders allowed \"Out of stock\", though the store was still selling it. It now says \"On backorder\", with how many are owed once the count is below zero, and the product row's \"out\" count leaves those variants out. The structured data on product pages and the agentic product feeds also read the backorders setting the way the cart does now, so a row holding `no` or `0` is no longer marked as a backorder there either\n    * The CSV importer stored WooCommerce's `notify` backorders value as it came, and the admin's Backorders select showed those variants as Denied though the store sold them below zero. Both import paths now store `allow`, and the admin reads every spelling the cart accepts (`allowed`, `notify`, `yes`, `on`, `1` and so on)\n"},"1.3.2":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Site Addresses on Settings \u2192 Security (`security.site_urls`).** The addresses the store is served on. Links in emails use the one the customer was on, or the first one otherwise, and anything sent from the command line uses the first. While it and Grav's `custom_base_url` are both empty, it is filled in with the address a signed-in administrator is using (`admin.super` or `api.access`, never an API key with scopes, never from the command line) the first time they open the Admin Next dashboard or KahunaCart's admin page, or save KahunaCart's settings, as long as that is an HTTPS address and not a development one such as `localhost` or a `.test` name. It is written into the file the admin's settings save would write, never over a value that is there, and logged once. Sign-in, set-password and cart link jobs refused while there was no address are released by the worker at the start of its next run once it sees one, so they go out then rather than running out of retries. A new dashboard alert and a new **Site address** check in `bin\/plugin kahunacart check` say when neither is set. See [Hardening \u2192 Site address for emailed links](hardening.md#site-address-for-emailed-links)\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * Links in emails no longer take their address from the request when no site address is configured. A request's host is used only when it is one of the store's Site Addresses, or when a signed-in admin is asking on a store that has set neither `security.site_urls` nor `custom_base_url`. With no trusted address, sign-in, set-password and cart links are not sent (the visitor sees the same \"check your email\" page as always, and the reason is logged and kept on the job), and every other email carries site-relative links. The shipped email layout no longer falls back to Grav's own base URL when it is handed an empty `site_url`, and its `@version` is now 1.3.2\n"},"1.3.1":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Custom fields filter and sort the order and customer lists, not just products.** `GET \/kahunacart\/orders?cf[printful_status]=pending` answers the orders holding that value, and `sort=cf:<name>[:desc]` orders by a sortable field, the same two parameters the product list has taken since custom fields arrived; the customer list takes them too. Each list keeps its own rules while it narrows: the order list still leaves carts out and reads newest completed first, and the customer list still carries each customer's order count and lifetime total. The docs said fields sort and filter \"the admin list\" for every entity and only the product list did; now it is true. Asked for by a merchant tracking a fulfilment service's status on each order. See [Custom fields \u2192 Sort and filter the admin lists](custom-fields.md#sort-and-filter-the-admin-lists)\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * The admin side menu's chevron now turns sideways on a closed section, as it was meant to; the rule pointed at the heading's old place and never matched\n    * The plugin ships a production `vendor\/` again. Releases since August carried PHPUnit and its dev packages (about 1,100 files) because `vendor\/` was committed from a dev install. `psr\/log` and `psr\/simple-cache` are now declared in composer's `replace` so they come from Grav core. Before, the autoloader pointed at a `vendor\/psr\/log` that was never committed\n"},"1.3.0":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Move a Squarespace store in with one command.** Squarespace has no single export: it gives you a products CSV, an orders CSV, a customers CSV and a WordPress export of the site, and none of them fit an importer as they come. `bin\/plugin kahunacart squarespace <folder>` reads all four from one folder, recognising each by its columns rather than its file name, and brings in the store and the site. **Products** become a catalog file that is imported the way `import` imports one, so every variant keeps its option values, price, sale price, stock and weight, a product hidden on Squarespace arrives as a draft, several shop pages become categories, and descriptions are converted from HTML to Markdown. **Pictures** are downloaded off Squarespace's servers before the links stop working, and only real pictures are saved: http and https only, with the bytes checked the way an upload's are. **Customers and orders** go through the ordinary CSV importer (contacts who never placed an order are newsletter subscribers, so they are left out and counted) and show up under **Import \u2192 Runs**, with orders keeping their numbers, dates, totals and lines, and recorded under the provider `squarespace` so nothing tries to refund one through a driver that never took the money. **The site** becomes Grav pages, with the blog as a listing page and one folder per post, each with its pictures, date and taxonomy. **Redirects** from every old Squarespace address to its new one are written out ready to paste into `site.yaml`, covering 7.0 and 7.1 product addresses and old shop pages, and a product whose address Squarespace made up gets a slug from its title. `--tidy-titles` turns shouting and sentence-case titles into Title Case and, given the brand, takes it off the front of each (`HB TACK \"SAGA\" LEATHER HALTER` becomes `Saga Leather Halter`). Squarespace doesn't say what units its weights and sizes are in, so a file with any in it asks for `--weight-unit` and `--dimension-unit` rather than guessing. Gift cards are left out and digital products arrive without their files, and the command says so by name. Everything the importers already promise holds: `--dry-run` writes nothing, nothing is deleted, nobody is emailed, a page folder that exists is never overwritten, and running it again updates instead of duplicating. See [Importing \u2192 Moving from Squarespace](importing.md#moving-from-squarespace)\n    * **Agentic commerce: AI shopping agents can find and buy from the store.** Google AI Mode and Gemini can now put a product in a basket and pay with Google Pay without the buyer leaving the conversation, ChatGPT can find products through the OpenAI product feed, and any MCP client can search the catalog and build a cart. It comes in four parts that build on each other, and **everything that answers a request is off as shipped and stays off on upgrade**, so a store that never opens the new **Settings \u2192 Agentic Commerce** tab sells exactly as it did. The tab takes the seller (`seller.*`) and the store's policies (`policies.*`: terms, privacy, returns, shipping, FAQ, the return rules and a per-country shipping summary) that every part reads. An agent sees exactly what a logged-out visitor sees, the store's default customer group included, never a stock quantity or a cost, cannot sign in or set a price, and no customer record is created for an agent session until its checkout completes. See [Agentic commerce](agentic-commerce.md)\n    * **Richer structured data on product pages.** Each offer now states its condition, the seller, per-country shipping from `policies.shipping_summary` (with the free rate over a threshold, and nothing for a product that does not ship), the return policy once `policies.refund_url` is set (not permitted for a final sale), and how long a sale price holds. The product carries its brand, GTIN and MPN, and the shop front page names the store as an `OnlineStore` with its logo and support contact. `seo.variant_markup: group` emits a `ProductGroup` with one `Product` per variant. `seo.organization`, `seo.shipping_details` and `seo.return_policy` switch each block off, and a store that sets none of the new data emits the markup it always did, byte for byte. `{base}\/product\/{slug}?variant={id}` now opens the page on that variant, with its own price and offer and the bare product URL as the canonical. The shipped `kahunacart-products.html.twig` includes the JSON-LD partial for the front page and is stamped 1.3.0. See [Storefront \u2192 Search engines](storefront.md#search-engines)\n    * **Product feeds for Google Merchant Center and ChatGPT.** `{base}\/feeds\/google.tsv`, the supplemental `google-checkout.tsv` and `openai.jsonl.gz`, one row per variant a guest could buy today, in every language the site runs. The worker builds them in the background five minutes after a catalog change and again past `feeds.max_age`, a small catalog builds a missing file on the first request, and they are served with `ETag` and `304` behind an optional `feeds.access_key`. While UCP is on the Google feeds carry `native_commerce` for each variant. `bin\/plugin kahunacart feeds:build` builds them by hand and `--out` copies them out for an SFTP cron line to OpenAI. Switched on with `feeds.enabled`. See [Product feeds](product-feeds.md)\n    * **A public storefront MCP endpoint at `{base}\/mcp`.** Thirteen tools with UCP's names: `search_catalog`, `lookup_catalog`, `get_product`, `search_shop_policies_and_faqs`, and the cart and checkout tools from `create_cart` to `complete_checkout`. Stateless Streamable HTTP with JSON-RPC 2.0, no login, the Origin check, size and depth caps, schema-checked arguments and three new rate-limit buckets (`agent_read`, `agent_write`, `agent_complete`). A plain MCP client may browse, build carts and hand the buyer to the store's checkout while `storefront_mcp.allow_anonymous` is on. Switched on with `storefront_mcp.enabled`. See [Storefront MCP](storefront-mcp.md)\n    * **Native checkout through the Universal Commerce Protocol.** A business profile at `\/.well-known\/ucp`, checkout and cart sessions under `{base}\/ucp` over REST and over the MCP endpoint, capability negotiation against the platform's own profile, idempotency keys that fail closed, a `continue_url` that drops any session into the store's own checkout, Google Pay through a provider with the new `agent_payments` capability (Stripe first), and order webhooks signed with the store's ES256 key, with `GET {base}\/ucp\/orders\/{id}` for the platform that placed the order. Platform profiles are fetched through a guarded fetcher that refuses private addresses and redirects, and the Google Pay token is passed to the provider and never stored, logged or echoed. `bin\/plugin kahunacart agentic:keys` shows and rotates the signing key. Switched on with `ucp.enabled`; `\/.well-known\/ucp` answers `404` until the store is ready. Migration 0085. See [UCP](ucp.md)\n    * **Agent payments for provider plugins.** A provider that can charge a wallet token an agent platform collected declares `agent_payments` and implements `AgentPaymentInterface`; core advertises its Google Pay handler, checks the token, opens the ledger row and settles the order exactly as `initiate()` does. See [Provider contract \u2192 Agent payments](provider-contract.md#agent-payments-optional) and the new scenarios in [Testing a payment provider](provider-testing.md#agent-payments)\n    * **Brand, condition, final sale, agent checkout and Google category on a product, GTIN and MPN on a variant.** In the product editor's new **Product Data** fieldset, the products API and MCP tools, the catalog file, the seed file and the CSV importer, which knows the headers WooCommerce and Shopify write. A GTIN must have a valid check digit and is stored as given. **Agent checkout** is Automatic, Allow or Never, and the editor shows the automatic answer with its reasons: a required add-on, pay what you want, a deposit, a prerequisite, a restricted product, a subscription, a service, a backorder or a final sale is never checked out by an agent, and a digital or used product only when allowed. Migration 0084. See [Products and catalog \u2192 Product data for search engines and agents](products-and-catalog.md#product-data-for-search-engines-and-agents)\n    * **Mark a shipment delivered.** A **Mark delivered** button on each shipment row records when a parcel arrived, once, and fires the new `onKahunaCartShipmentDelivered`, which a carrier add-on can also fire through `FulfillmentService::markDelivered()`. For an agent order it sends the platform a `delivered` webhook. `POST \/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/shipments\/{sid}\/delivered`, MCP tool `mark_shipment_delivered`. See [Orders and admin \u2192 Mark a shipment delivered](orders-and-admin.md#mark-a-shipment-delivered)\n    * **An Agentic commerce screen, and the doctor checks it.** Under Setup in the store admin: whether UCP is ready and what is still missing, the endpoints, the signing key, each feed file with its build time and the agent sessions, with **Rebuild feeds** and **Rotate signing key**. Five admin routes and MCP tools (`get_agentic_status`, `rotate_ucp_signing_key`, `list_agent_sessions`, `list_feeds`, `rebuild_feeds`), and a new **Agentic readiness** check in `bin\/plugin kahunacart check` that runs whenever a switch is on. See [Admin API \u2192 Agentic commerce](admin-api.md#agentic-commerce) and [Going live \u2192 Agentic readiness](going-live.md#agentic-readiness)\n    * **Agent orders have their own attribution bucket.** `agent`, with the platform as the source and the protocol as the medium, in every report filter and breakdown and on the order. A basket an agent started and a person finished in the browser still counts as an agent sale, and an agent's basket is never sent a cart-recovery email. See [Reporting \u2192 Where an order came from](reporting.md#where-an-order-came-from)\n    * **One master switch for agentic commerce.** `agentic.enabled`, the first toggle on **Settings \u2192 Agentic Commerce**, ships off and reads as off when missing on a new install or an upgrade. While it is off the store behaves as it did before: `\/.well-known\/ucp`, `{base}\/feeds\/*`, `{base}\/mcp` and `{base}\/ucp\/*` answer `404`, no feed rebuild, order webhook or signing key work runs, the product and listing JSON-LD is byte for byte what 1.2.14 emitted, the product editor hides **Agent checkout**, the **Agentic commerce** screen only says how to turn it on, and the doctor reports nothing about it. `feeds.enabled`, `storefront_mcp.enabled`, `ucp.enabled` and the `seo.*` additions take effect only while it is on; the product fields work either way. See [Configuration \u2192 Agentic commerce](configuration.md#agentic-commerce)\n    * **3-D Secure on an agent's card becomes a handoff, not a decline.** When the card's bank wants the cardholder to authenticate, a UCP completion now answers `requires_escalation` with UCP's `requires_3ds` message, and the checkout's `continue_url` takes the buyer to the provider's challenge page. The provider's webhook settles the order as it settles a hosted checkout, the session reads `completed`, and the platform's retried completion (with a new `Idempotency-Key`) answers from the ledger and never charges twice. A provider signals a challenge by answering `chargeAgentCredential()` with `PaymentResult::redirect($challengeUrl, $reference)`; see the agent payments section of the provider contract.\n    * **Signed UCP responses.** The store now signs the answer that completes a checkout, over REST and over the MCP endpoint, with its UCP signing key, as UCP 2026-04-08 recommends: RFC 9421 over `\"@status\" \"content-digest\" \"content-type\"` with `Content-Digest` over the exact bytes, so a platform can check a confirmation came from this store unchanged against the key in `\/.well-known\/ucp`. `ucp.sign_responses` (**Settings \u2192 Agentic Commerce \u2192 Sign Responses**) is `complete` as shipped; `all` signs every UCP answer and `off` none, and nothing is signed while UCP is off. A store with no key answers unsigned and logs it.\n    * **Signed AI agents are recorded on the order.** An agent that signs its requests with Web Bot Auth (Visa's Trusted Agent Protocol, Mastercard Agent Pay, Cloudflare signed agents) has its `Signature-Agent` checked against the key directory at `\/.well-known\/http-message-signatures-directory` on its host, Ed25519 through sodium or EC P-256, with `created`, `expires` and the `web-bot-auth` tag checked and only the keys the directory signed for itself used. The directory is fetched through the same guarded fetcher as platform profiles, cached beside them with a negative cache and counted in the `agent_profile_fetch` budget. The outcome, verified with the agent's host or unverified with the reason, is kept on the order and shown in the agentic screen's session list and on a new **Agent** card on the order. A bad signature never refuses a request, and a request with no signature is not slowed by any of it.\n    * **Agents can choose pickup.** A store with click and collect answers a UCP `pickup` fulfillment method beside shipping, its destinations the offered pickup locations (`retail_location`: id, name and address, with the opening hours in the option's description). Choosing one goes through the checkout's own pickup choice, so no shipping is charged, the location's charge is the shipping line and tax is worked out at the location; a platform can switch between shipping and pickup on any update and at completion, and the order webhook expects `pickup` at the location's address.\n    * **Split shipments reach agents as one group per parcel.** A cart whose lines fall under different shipping profiles answers one UCP fulfillment group per parcel, each with its own line items and options priced for that parcel, to a platform whose profile sets `supports_multi_group`; any other platform gets the single group the spec requires, priced as every parcel together. A group selection maps onto the order's one shipping method, and a group that could not keep what was asked says so with a `fulfillment_changed` warning. The order webhook has one shipping expectation per parcel.\n    * **Semantic product search, optional.** With `search.products.semantic.enabled` on, the storefront search ranks by meaning as well as keywords, so `automobile` finds a product called a car. It uses YetiSearch 2.5.1's hybrid search with any OpenAI-compatible embeddings API (OpenAI `text-embedding-3-small` at 512 dimensions out of the box, OpenRouter, or Ollama on your own server). Embedding runs only on the job worker, through a `search.embed` job booked after a reindex or product saves and by the worker tick while anything is waiting, and stops at the first provider error; the term a shopper types is embedded with a five-second timeout and falls back to keywords on any error. The keys match YetiSearch Pro's `semantic` block, and empty ones take YetiSearch Pro's values when it has semantic search on. The API key is stored encrypted, or read from the environment with `env:NAME`. Off, search is exactly what it was, with nothing sent anywhere. See [Storefront \u2192 Semantic search](storefront.md#semantic-search).\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart search:embed [--reset]`** embeds the product index by hand until nothing is waiting, printing the provider's error if there is one. `bin\/plugin kahunacart status` and a new **Semantic search** doctor check report how many products are embedded and waiting, the model and the last run's error.\n    * **Semantic search measures its own noise gate on each catalog.** Nonsense scores higher against a large catalog than a small one, so no single `min_margin` works everywhere: on the 17-product demo catalog `asdf`, `qwerty` and `lorem ipsum` stood at most 0.06 above the median product and real searches at least 0.14, while on a 4,415-product catalog nonsense reached 0.20 and real one-word searches started at 0.20. With the new `search.products.semantic.calibration` on `auto` (the default), YetiSearch 2.5.1 searches for a fixed set of 88 nonsense strings at the end of an embedding run on the job worker, each embedded exactly as a shopper's search is, leaves out the ones keywords find in the catalog (five on each of those two), and sets `min_margin` at the mean of the rest's margins plus two standard deviations: `0.121` on the demo catalog and `0.200` on the large one, where every nonsense search tested finds nothing and real searches keep their meaning, apart from a one-word search that only just stands out on a large catalog (`pants` there), which is answered by keywords alone. A new `calibration_strictness` setting (2.0, inherited from YetiSearch Pro like the other tuning keys) sets how many standard deviations that is, and takes effect on the next search without measuring again. It measures again when the model, dimensions, prefixes or query frame change, or the catalog grows or shrinks by more than a quarter, and the probes are embedded once and cached in the index file, so measuring again costs no provider call. A search only ever reads the calibration. `off` uses `min_margin` exactly as set. `bin\/plugin kahunacart search:calibrate` prints the working, including the probes it left out, and `status`, the doctor and the settings screen show the value in use and when it was measured. See [Storefront \u2192 Tuning semantic search](storefront.md#tuning-semantic-search)\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **Product search finds products by their category, tags, brand and attributes.** The ranked index now holds each product's category paths (`Clothing > Mens > Tops`), tag labels, brand, text and select attribute values and option values beside its title, summary, description and SKUs, so `tops` finds a tee filed under Tops. With semantic search on, the same text, SKUs aside, is what gets embedded, which gives a short product (a title and one line of summary) something to be understood by. The index rebuilds itself once after the update, and a store with semantic search on re-embeds its products.\n    * **Semantic search is tuned for product catalogs.** Two new settings, `min_margin` (the noise gate: how far the best product must stand above the median before meaning adds results) and `relative_similarity` (how close to the best match a product must be), default to `0.10` and `0.85` rather than YetiSearch's `0.15` and `0.6`, which were measured on long pages. Short product text packs similarities together, so real one-word searches such as `top` and `music` were gated out as noise and loosely related products filled the list. `min_query_chars` now counts letters and digits, so spaces and punctuation no longer make a query long enough.\n    * **Short searches find what they mean: `hat` finds the cap and the beanie.** Three changes, measured on the demo catalog with `text-embedding-3-small`. The search term is embedded in a phrase, `a hat` rather than `hat` (the new `query_frame` setting, `a {query}` by default), because a single word on its own is read as a word: `hat` scored 0.20 against the Shorebreak Cap and ranked it fifth behind a wall rack, while `a hat` scored 0.39 and ranked it first. Each product also gets a short card beside its full text, `Product: Shorebreak Cap (Accessories)`, which a short search is compared with on its own. And the noise gate is decided on the cards alone, before the search runs, so a description that happens to be close to nonsense cannot let it in: `lorem ipsum` scored 0.34 against the Shaper's Handbook's description but no more than 0.24 against any card. On the cards the best match stood at most 0.06 above the median for `asdf`, `qwerty` and `lorem ipsum`, and at least 0.14 for real searches. `top`, `pants`, `music`, `haircut`, `hoodei`, `calendar`, `sweatshirt`, `swim trunks`, `surfboard storage`, `book` and `workshop` all find what they should, and `sweatshirt`, `book`, `workshop` and `surfboard storage` with shorter lists than before. Cards exist only while semantic search is on, and switching it on or off rebuilds the index once. Changing `query_frame` re-embeds nothing, since only the search is framed; the noise gate is measured again. The card is a YetiSearch meaning-only document, with no keyword entry at all, so the word `product` finds only the products whose own text says it. See [Storefront \u2192 Tuning semantic search](storefront.md#tuning-semantic-search)\n    * **A typo correction has to agree with the meaning.** With semantic search on, the typo-correction pass runs only when keywords and meaning together found nothing, and keeps a corrected product only when the query passes the noise gate and the product's similarity to it clears `min_similarity`. `hoodei` still finds the hoodie; `asdf` no longer finds the boardshort by way of a word it shares a few letters with, and `pants` finds the boardshort and the socks by meaning instead of the cap whose summary mentions \"panels\".\n    * **YetiSearch 2.5.1.** The bundled search library moves from 2.3.6 to 2.5.1, which brings the hybrid search, the per-catalog noise calibration, the query frame and meaning-only documents that semantic product search is built on. Without an embedding provider it searches exactly as before.\n    * **brick\/math, brick\/money and YetiSearch are bundled under KahunaCart's own namespace.** Grav loads plugin autoloaders in folder-name order, so the first plugin to ship one of these unprefixed supplied it to every other: with grav-ray installed, its brick\/math 0.14.1 answered for KahunaCart's brick\/money, which needs 0.15 or later, and KahunaCart's price calculations stopped with a fatal error; and yetisearch-pro ran on KahunaCart's copy of YetiSearch instead of its own. KahunaCart's copies are now `Grav\\Plugin\\KahunaCart\\Vendor\\Brick\\\u2026` and `Grav\\Plugin\\KahunaCart\\Vendor\\YetiSearch\\\u2026`, built with Strauss, so each plugin gets the version it was built for. Add-ons that imported `Brick\\\u2026` directly should use the `Money` helper instead. See [Money \u2192 Bundled under a prefix](money.md#bundled-under-a-prefix)\n    * **The database layer, migrator and job queue are grav-db-kit's.** KahunaCart now runs on [grav-db-kit](https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-db-kit) 1.0.1, the shared database package for Trilby Media's Grav plugins, bundled under KahunaCart's own prefix like brick\/money, and its in-tree copies of the connection, the three dialects, the migrator, the job queue and runner, the daemon, the inline drain and the rate limiter's counting are gone (about 4,100 lines). An existing store keeps its `kahunacart_*` tables and every tracking row, so no migration runs again. The catalog index drain and the refund lock hold the kit's `Lease`, which no longer depends on a failed insert and so also works inside an open PostgreSQL transaction, and the per-request schema check is the kit's `SchemaGuard`, still remembered in Grav's cache and still skipped before the database is opened.\n    * **Rate-limit counters start again once, on upgrade.** Migration `0086_rate_limits_buckets` rebuilds `kahunacart_rate_limits` in the kit's layout, with the bucket and the key in columns of their own. Every row is a counter for a window of a minute or an hour, so nothing is copied: callers get a fresh count, once.\n    * **Add-ons built for 1.2 keep working, and the old class names are deprecated.** `Grav\\Plugin\\KahunaCart\\Database\\Connection`, `Database\\Migration`, `Database\\Dialect\\*`, `Jobs\\JobHandler`, `Jobs\\JobQueue` and the rest are now aliases of the kit's classes, registered by KahunaCart's autoloader, and `Database\\Migrator` and `Security\\RateLimiter` keep their old constructors. New code uses the kit's names through KahunaCart's prefix, and a test builds a queue or migrator with `Database\\Kit::queue()` and `Kit::migrator()`. See [Database and job classes](docs\/provider-developer-guide.md#database-and-job-classes). The scaffolder's migration template uses the new names.\n    * **Agent endpoints in the hardening guide.** What to let through a CDN (no \"block AI bots\" rules or challenges on the four new paths, verified bots and signed agents allowed) and what stops what on each new public endpoint. See [Hardening \u2192 Agent endpoints](hardening.md#agent-endpoints)\n    * **A plugin's veto reaches an agent before the checkout reads ready.** `onKahunaCartCheckoutValidate` now runs (context `agent`, nothing charged) every time a UCP platform creates or updates a checkout, so a refused basket reads `requires_escalation` with `checkout_refused` and the plugin's sentence straight away rather than `ready_for_complete` until the charge. Completion asks again.\n    * With Grav 2.2's lazy sessions turned on, shoppers who are only browsing no longer get a session cookie from the store, so a proxy or CDN can cache those pages.\n    * With lazy sessions turned on, orders still record where the shopper came from and on what device, and signing in from a store page still returns the shopper to that page.\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **YetiSearch's query cache classes ship again.** An ignore rule meant for Grav's `cache\/` folder also matched YetiSearch's `src\/Cache\/` on case-insensitive filesystems, so `QueryCache` and `CacheManager` were never in the release. KahunaCart keeps YetiSearch's query cache off, but any code that turned it on hit a missing class.\n    * **A sign-in link no longer skips two-factor.** The emailed sign-in link and the set-password page handed the customer to the Login plugin without asking for the second factor, so a customer who had switched two-factor on was signed in by the link alone. Both now ask whenever the site has two-factor on, and send the customer to the code form before the account.\n    * **Custom fields on a variant can be filled in from the admin.** A field declared on `variant` was stored and read everywhere else, but the admin only drew the custom-fields panel on order, product and customer screens, so there was nowhere to type a value. Open a variant on the product screen and its fields are now under **Save variant**, in a new `variant.details` zone that add-ons can mount their own widgets into as well.\n    * **Filtered-out categories are dimmed again.** A comment in the storefront stylesheet was closed a line too early, so browsers skipped the rule that fades a category with nothing to show under the current filters, and Grav's CSS minifier gave up on the whole file.\n    * **A stuck agent completion is decided by what the provider did.** A checkout left in `complete_in_progress` (the process died mid-charge) used to be let go after fifteen minutes and canceled at expiry, releasing the stock of an order that might have been paid. The worker now reads the order's last agent charge in the ledger: a settled one completes the session, a failed or never-sent one puts it back to `ready_for_complete` (or cancels it when expired), and a pending one waits for the provider's webhook. A second agent charge on an order whose last one is still pending is refused before the provider is called.\n    * **A checkout switched from delivery to pickup no longer keeps the delivery address.** Choosing a pickup location left the address typed for delivery on the order, so the invoice and the packing slip printed it under \"Ship to\" beside the counter the order was collected from, and the admin and an agent's order showed it too. It is now removed as soon as a location is chosen, in the browser checkout and over UCP, and switching back to delivery takes the address again. See [Click and collect](pickup.md#a-collected-order-has-no-shipping-address)\n    * Hourly rate limits (magic link, account offer, review submit, cart restore and the rest) no longer reset early on a busy store. The limiter's occasional sweep of old rows worked out its cutoff from the calling bucket's window and then swept every bucket, so a one-minute bucket like cart writes deleted an hourly bucket's current count a couple of minutes into the hour. The sweep now stays inside the calling bucket (grav-db-kit 1.0.2)\n"},"1.2.14":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Buy now: a buy button that goes straight to checkout.** A store that sells one thing at a time, like a storage unit, an hour of consulting or a mobile top-up, had every shopper go through a cart they never needed. A product can now say **Buy now**: pressing it puts the product in the cart and opens checkout instead of the basket. **It is an ordinary add**, so stock, quantity limits, members-only products, coupon links and an open payment all behave exactly as they do for **Add to cart**, and a refusal still lands on the cart page. **Anything already in the cart stays there** and is on the checkout page beside it, because throwing away a basket without asking is worse than showing it. The store-wide switch is **Buy now goes straight to checkout** on **Settings \u2192 Commerce** (`cart.buy_now`, off as shipped), and each product can follow it, always go straight to checkout, or always use the cart, from a new **Buy button** fieldset in the product editor. The same fieldset takes a **Button label**, \"Rent now\" or \"Move in\" in place of \"Add to cart\" or \"Buy now\", and the label is translatable like the title (it is kept out of search). A kit or grouped product's button follows the parent. The form asks for checkout by posting `buy_now=1`, so a theme can put a second **Buy now** button beside **Add to cart** on any product. `buy_now` and `button_label` on the product API and MCP tools and in the catalog and seed files, `kahunacart_buy_now()` for a theme drawing its own button; migration 0083. See [Products and catalog \u2192 Buy now](products-and-catalog.md#buy-now)\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Media tiles in the product editor are no longer squashed.** Each gallery picture and sample under **Media** sat about 40px in from both sides of its grid cell because the browser's default margin on a `<figure>` was never reset. That left a narrow column where the badges, the description and the move buttons stacked one per line. The tiles now fill their cells.\n    * **Blue status pills line up with the pills beside them.** A pill in the info colour, like **PDF** on a media tile, also matched the admin's `.info` notice-box style, so it got a notice's 12px bottom margin and border and sat higher than the pill next to it. Pills now reset both, which fixes every info pill in the admin, not just the media ones.\n"},"1.2.13":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The deposits switch has moved to the Commerce tab.** **Deposits and part payment** sat under **Settings \u2192 Security**, which is the last place anybody deciding how customers pay would look. It is now on **Settings \u2192 Commerce**, right under **Payments**, with every field unchanged. The config keys are the same (`checkout.deposits.enabled` and `checkout.deposits.default_percent`), so an existing store keeps its setting and nothing needs migrating. The **Deposits** screen's \"Deposits are turned off\" note now links to the settings and says where the switch is. See [Deposits \u2192 Before you begin](deposits.md#before-you-begin)\n"},"1.2.12":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Pay what you want.** A product can let the shopper name the price, the way Bandcamp sells an album and itch.io sells a game. Tick **Let shoppers name their price** under **Price & stock** and each variant's price becomes the least a shopper may pay \u2014 0.00 is free, or pay what you like \u2014 with an optional **Suggested price** the box starts at and an optional **Maximum price** that catches 5000 typed for 50.00. The buy button grows a **Name your price** box with \"Minimum $12.00\" under it, and the hint follows the shopper to a variant with another minimum; the cart checks the amount whatever the page says, and a shopper below the minimum or above the maximum is told so with the product and the number in the sentence. An add that sends no amount \u2014 a grid's button, the API, a wishlist \u2014 pays the suggestion. Adding the same variant at a different amount replaces the line rather than sitting a second one beside it. **No sale, coupon or price list reaches a price the shopper chose**, and the catalog draws no sale badge or struck price on one: the amount is frozen onto the line as a line price marked not discountable, so tax, refunds, reports, receipts and invoices need nothing new. `pay_what_you_want`, `pwyw_suggested_minor` and `pwyw_max_minor` on the product API and MCP tools, `pwyw_suggested` and `pwyw_max` in the catalog and seed files, `kahunacart_pay_what_you_want()` for a theme drawing its own buy box; migration 0082. The demo store's **Offshore Ambient Album** is sold this way, from 12.00. See [Products and catalog \u2192 Pay what you want](products-and-catalog.md#pay-what-you-want)\n"},"1.2.11":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **An add-on can now set a cart line's price, and say what the price is made of.** Some things cost what they cost because of what the customer picked, and no column in the catalog can hold that number: five nights at 180.00, two at 220.00 and an 85.00 cleaning fee is one product at one price, and an hour with the senior stylist is not the same money as an hour with the junior. There was no supported way to say so \u2014 `onKahunaCartResolvePrice` only watches, and writing `unit_price_minor` inside `onKahunaCartCalculateAdjustments` worked by accident and was documented nowhere. **`onKahunaCartCartLinePrice` is the supported way.** It fires once, when a line is about to be created, after `onKahunaCartCartLineValidate` has passed and on an order the store built over the API as much as on a storefront cart, and a listener answers with a unit price and the rows that make it up: a stable `code`, a `label` a customer reads, an `amount_minor` per unit, and optionally `meta` and `discountable`. The rows have to add up to the price or the answer is refused with both numbers in the sentence, and **at most one add-on may price a line** \u2014 a second is refused by name, both names, because a price settled by plugin load order is not a price. Core then does for that answer exactly what it already does for a [priced add-on option](products-and-catalog.md#priced-options): the price goes into the line's own `unit_price_minor`, so it is taxed at the line's tax class, refunded with the line and counted as the line's revenue with no change to tax, refunds or reports; the rows are frozen into the line's snapshot and re-emitted every calculation as `included` adjustments of type `line_price`, so the cart, the checkout, the receipt, the PDF invoice, the order emails, the admin's order view and the storefront JSON all print \"5 nights at 180.00\" without a single template knowing what a night is. A sale discounts the line as it discounts any other, except the rows marked `discountable: false` \u2014 20% off the shop is 20% off the nights, and the cleaning still costs what cleaning costs. **A price list does not apply to a line priced this way**, because a list names a price for a variant and five nights in August are not the variant's price. `fixed_qty` on the answer says the quantity is the add-on's and not the customer's, and then the quantity box and a second identical add are both refused with a sentence naming the line, while removing it is always allowed. **A revisited cart keeps the price it froze**: nothing reprices a line an add-on priced, and an add-on that wants a different price removes the line and adds it again. The storefront line JSON gains `price_breakdown` and `fixed_qty`. **A store with no such add-on behaves exactly as it did**: no listener, no snapshot key, no adjustment row, and a line priced from the catalog the way it always was. See [Pricing \u2192 A price an add-on sets](pricing.md#a-price-an-add-on-sets) and [Developer guide \u2192 A price an add-on sets](provider-developer-guide.md#a-price-an-add-on-sets)\n    * **A product type can now say it sells a service, and the store stops treating it like a download.** `is_digital` on an order line has always meant \"needs no parcel\", and because a file was the only thing that needed none, three different questions grew up on that one flag: does this need postage, is this a digital good for tax, and may a digital-goods-only platform sell it. For a PDF all three answers are the same. For a haircut they are yes, no and no \u2014 so a store selling one was handing an hour in a room in Denver to its tax engine under the rules written about electronically supplied services, and offering it at checkout through a platform whose own terms cover files and nothing else. A type now declares its **nature** \u2014 `goods`, `digital` or `service` \u2014 and tax and payment read that instead. Shipping keeps reading the flag and keeps its answer, because a service needs no parcel any more than a download does, and so do downloads themselves, fulfilment, packing slips, the weight rules and the shipping apportionment inside the tax stage. **Nothing has to be declared for a store to behave exactly as it did**: a type that says nothing is `goods` when it ships and `digital` when it does not, which is precisely what the flag meant on its own, so core's four types and every add-on's type written before today answer what they answered yesterday. The nature is frozen onto each line as it enters the cart, beside the price and the title and for the same reason \u2014 retyping a product next month must not change what an order already taken is taxed as or may be paid with. A word outside the three is refused when the type registers, by name, the way a duplicate code is. `GET \/kahunacart\/product-types` answers `nature` per type and the storefront's line JSON answers it per line; the admin's type picker shows nothing new. **An add-on that implements `PurchasableType` directly has one method to add**, `nature()`; one declared through `CoreType::of()` needs no change at all. Migration 0078. See [Products and catalog \u2192 What a type sells](products-and-catalog.md#what-a-type-sells), [Tax \u2192 A service is not a download](tax.md#a-service-is-not-a-download) and [Payment providers \u2192 Digital-goods-only providers](payment-providers.md#digital-goods-only-providers)\n    * **An order entered over the API can carry extra facts on a line.** `POST \/kahunacart\/orders` read `variant_id` and `qty` and threw the rest of the line away, which meant the one thing a booking, a licence renewal or an engraving needs could not be recorded on an order taken over the phone \u2014 even though the builder underneath has accepted it all along. A line may now carry `line_data`, the same field a buy button's own fields fill in on the storefront, held to the same limits: at most ten keys of lowercase letters, digits and underscores, each value a string, a number or true\/false of at most 190 characters. **A key the limits refuse is a 422 naming the line and the key**, never a line quietly stored without it \u2014 a till that believes it booked three o'clock and did not is worse than one that is told no. An add-on listening on `onKahunaCartCartLineValidate` can refuse the line, and that refuses the whole order, which is the point: a phone booking for a slot somebody has just taken should fail while the customer is still on the phone. The `create_order` MCP tool takes it too. See [Orders and admin \u2192 Extra facts on a line](orders-and-admin.md#extra-facts-on-a-line)\n    * **A customer can now pay a deposit from their own side, at checkout and on a pay link, with the balance later.** Deposits shipped as something a merchant opened from the admin on an order that already existed, and two things were missing from the other half of it. Nothing could open a collection at checkout, so \"30% now, the rest fourteen days before you arrive\" had nowhere to be said \u2014 that figure depends on the dates the guest picked and no merchant can type it on an order that does not exist yet. And a customer-side payment always charged the order's **total** and always marked the order **paid**, whatever the collection said, because the only thing that told a collection money had arrived was a listener on `onKahunaCartPaymentCaptured` \u2014 an event an instant-capture provider never raises. So a store with deposits switched on and a shopper paying by card took the whole stay in one go and called the order settled. **`onKahunaCartOrderPaymentSchedule` is where an add-on says otherwise.** It fires once, when an order is about to be paid for the first time \u2014 from the storefront checkout after every other refusal has passed, and from `OrderBuilder` when a caller asks \u2014 and a listener answers a **payment schedule**: `PaymentSchedule::percent('30')` or `PaymentSchedule::amount(295_00)`, with an optional date the balance is due by and an optional earlier date to ask for it on. **At most one add-on may schedule an order**, and a second is refused by name, both names, because money settled by plugin load order is not money. Core then opens the collection with two sessions \u2014 what is due today and what is left, the balance carrying its own date \u2014 and **charges what is due rather than what the order comes to**, at checkout, on the pay link and on an off-session charge against a saved card. **Every path that settles a payment now asks the collection first**: an instant capture, a webhook from a hosted checkout, a capture against a held authorisation and the storefront's own repair of a stranded payment all end `partially_paid` or `paid` as the collection decides, and `onKahunaCartOrderPaid` and the payment-received email are kept for money that actually finishes the order. A part payment announces itself on the new **`onKahunaCartOrderPartPaid`**, carrying what landed, what has been captured, what is left and when it is due \u2014 which is the event an add-on confirms a booking on. `onKahunaCartPaymentCaptured` fires exactly where it always did and carries exactly what it always carried. The checkout page, the pay page and the receipt say it in words through the shipped totals block \u2014 **Due today**, **Paid so far**, and what is left with the date it is due by \u2014 and the pay-link email says what the link will charge when that is not the whole order. **With `checkout.deposits.enabled` off, an answer is ignored** with one line in the log naming the plugin, and every order is charged in full and settled exactly as it was; a store with no such add-on writes nothing, logs nothing and behaves as it always has. A merchant's own **Take a deposit** is untouched and is never rewritten by an add-on's answer, and neither is a schedule money has already moved against \u2014 though a shopper who goes back to the shop, adds a night and comes round again is re-priced against the basket they actually bought. See [Deposits \u2192 A deposit decided at checkout](deposits.md#a-deposit-decided-at-checkout) and [Developer guide \u2192 A deposit an add-on asks for](provider-developer-guide.md#a-deposit-an-add-on-asks-for)\n    * **The balance now asks for itself.** A schedule that named a due date puts two dates in the diary the moment the deposit lands. On the reminder date \u2014 the due date, unless the add-on asked for an earlier one \u2014 core queues the **same balance request** the admin's **Collect balance** button sends: same message, same `email.queued` entry on the order, same switch on the Notifications screen, so a merchant who has turned that message off is not sent one behind their back, and nothing goes out on an order that has been paid in the meantime or that nobody ever part paid. Once the due date has passed and the money is still owed, core fires **`onKahunaCartOrderBalanceOverdue`** and does nothing else \u2014 what a late balance costs a customer is not a decision a shop plugin gets to make for every store, since a holiday let releases the week and gives the deposit back, a workshop holds the place and rings the customer, and a furniture maker carries on building. Both are ordinary queue jobs with a run-after date and a dedupe key per order, so a balance chased once is booked once however many payments land against it, and a schedule that named no dates books nothing at all\n    * **A theme can ask whether a product is a service.** `kahunacart_product_type(product)` now answers `nature` beside `ships`: `goods`, `digital` or `service`, and empty for a type whose plugin is switched off. A theme that printed \"Digital download\" on everything that does not ship was calling a haircut a download, because `ships` is false for both. Badge on `nature` instead. See [Products and catalog \u2192 What a type sells](products-and-catalog.md#what-a-type-sells)\n    * **A customer can now be in more than one customer group.** A group lived in one column on the customer, so a founder who also bought wholesale had to be one or the other and the store forgot the other half of them. Memberships have their own table now, and a customer is in as many groups as the merchant puts them in. **What several groups mean is decided once**: a `customer_group` condition \u2014 on a price list, a sale, a coupon, a payment method or a checkout field \u2014 matches when any of the customer's groups satisfies it, so `is founders` matches a customer in Founders and Trade, `is not founders` does not, and `starts with found` does; a member-only product is visible when any of their groups may buy it; a group's `discount_percent` resolves to the highest among their groups rather than to their sum; and the store's default group still stands in for a customer in none, and only for them. **For a customer in one group every rule reads exactly as it did**, and every existing membership is copied across by migration 0079. `customers.group_map` now adds a customer to every mapped group at sign-in instead of stopping at the first, and a coupon that grants a group adds it beside the groups the customer already has instead of refusing when there was one \u2014 see [Coupons \u2192 The grant adds a group and takes none away](coupons.md#the-grant-adds-a-group-and-takes-none-away). `POST \/kahunacart\/customers\/{id}\/group` takes `group_ids`, the whole list; the older `group_id` body still means exactly that one group. The customer record answers `group_ids` and `effective_groups`, and the admin's customer record picks groups with a checklist. The reports' **Customer group** segment reads a customer's first group in the merchant's order, so an order is never counted once per group. The newsletters add-on reads the new table from its next release. See [Customers \u2192 Customer groups](customers.md#customer-groups)\n    * **The customer list shows each customer's groups and filters on group and sign-in status.** A **Groups** column beside the name, a group filter with **No group** for customers in none, and a sign-in filter for active, two-factor, invited, disabled and no account \u2014 the same five words the sign-in column prints. The count above the table is the filtered count. Over the API they are `group` and `account` on `GET \/kahunacart\/customers`, and each row carries `groups`; the `list_customers` MCP tool takes both\n    * **A customer group's page lists the customers in it**, each linked to their record, with their sign-in status, orders and spend, and the edit form above it has been laid out again so the help sits under each field rather than beside the label\n    * **A customer can see which groups they are in.** The storefront account page and the profile page show a badge per group, so a Founders member no longer has to email the store to ask whether they are one. A group carries a new **Show on customer account** switch, on by default, for the group a customer should not see \u2014 a watch list, a staff flag. The store default is never shown, because it is what a customer in no group is rather than something they joined. Themes read `account_groups` on every account page; the shipped templates include `partials\/kahunacart-account-groups.html.twig`\n    * **A price list can show its price in the catalog as a discount.** A price list was applied in the cart and nowhere else, so a Founders member whose renewal is $49 was shown $99 on the renewal page and only found the $49 in the cart. A list now carries **Show as a discount in the catalog** and **Badge text**: for a shopper the list applies to, every product page, card, buy button, option picker and `kahunacart_sale()` strikes the catalog price through and shows the list's price beside it with the badge, drawn through the same `sale` offer a sale and a coupon link already use, so no theme needs a change. The figure is the lowest one and what the cart will charge \u2014 the list's price less any sale running on the product \u2014 under the list's badge. It is off on every list until turned on, so a trade list keeps reading as the price. Only rules about the customer can be judged on a product page; a list with rules about the basket still prices the cart and is not shown in the catalog. It adds nothing to the **On sale** filter and does not trip a coupon's **Exclude sale items**, because in the cart a price list is the price rather than a sale. `show_in_catalog` and `catalog_label` on the price-list API and MCP tools; migration 0080. The product page's buy box now prices the product's default variant, the same one the price under the title names, rather than the first variant in the list. See [Pricing \u2192 Showing a list's price in the catalog](pricing.md#showing-a-lists-price-in-the-catalog)\n    * **A download that runs out no longer says \"Out of stock\".** A file is never really out of stock: a digital product sits at zero because it sold a limited run, or because the store holds it back until release. A new setting under **Settings \u2192 Stock**, `stock.digital_sold_out`, says what a product that needs no shipping reads at zero stock \u2014 **Not available** (the default, which says nothing about whether it comes back) or **Coming soon** \u2014 and `stock.digital_sold_out_text` takes the merchant's own words instead. Products that ship still say **Out of stock**. It reaches the disabled buy button on a one-variant product (which now says why instead of posting and coming back refused), the option picker's status line, the sentence the cart and the storefront API give when they refuse the line, and the restore page for an old cart. Themes read it through the new `kahunacart_stock(product, variant)`, which answers `sold_out`, `low` (on the store's low-stock threshold), `qty`, `reason` and a ready-worded `label`, so no theme works the rule out for itself. Asking for more than is left now says there are not enough left rather than calling the product out of stock\n    * **The cart can now offer the bundle when a shopper has its parts in the basket separately.** Someone who buys Bookings and Rentals only one at a time pays $298 for what Bookings + Rentals sells for $249, notices afterwards and asks for a refund. Give the bundle variant a **Bundle of** list in its variant editor (any variants, from any products, one of each) and turn on `cart.bundle_suggestions` in Settings, and the cart and checkout say \"Bookings and Rentals only come to $298.00 here. Bookings + Rentals is $249.00 and includes both.\" with **Swap to the bundle** and **Keep them separate**. It is only offered when the bundle can be bought and costs this shopper less, priced with their price lists, sales and coupons, and whatever the parts were bought with (the licence an add-on attaches to, say) moves to the bundle line when every part carries the same; parts aimed at different things are not offered. Keep them separate is remembered on the basket until it forms a new match. Themes include one partial, and the storefront JSON API carries the offers as `bundle_suggestions` with `POST` and `DELETE \/cart\/bundles`. Off as shipped; migration 0081 adds the table the lists live in. See docs\/storefront.md.\n    * **A storefront message can now carry a link.** The notice after Add to cart, a refusal at the cart or checkout and a cart line note may say \"[Sign in](\u2026) to the account that holds your license\" and the shopper gets a link rather than brackets. A message carries a tiny piece of Markdown \u2014 `[text](url)`, `**bold**`, `*emphasis*` \u2014 and nothing else: everything is escaped first, so a product called `<script>` prints as the word, and a link is drawn only for a path on the site or an `http`\/`https` URL, never `javascript:` or `data:`. Flash notices go into Grav's message queue as that escaped HTML, which is what Grav's own messages partial prints; templates use the new `|kahunacart_message` filter (and `|kahunacart_message_text` for plain text); the storefront JSON API keeps `detail` and `fields` as plain text and adds `detail_html` and `fields_html` when there is a link to draw; the admin reads plain text. The new `kahunacart_sign_in_url(return)` and `KahunaCartFrontend::signInUrl($returnTo)` give a sign-in link that comes back to the page it names, through `{base}\/account?return=\u2026`, since the Login plugin takes no return address of its own. Add-ons build their sentences with `Frontend\\StorefrontMessage::literal()` and `link()`. See [Storefront \u2192 Messages with a link in them](storefront.md#messages-with-a-link-in-them)\n\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **You are told when a problem you were warned about is over.** **Provider not answering** and **Background jobs stopped** cleared their stamp when the provider answered again or the queue drained, so the next outage would be news \u2014 but the recovery itself said nothing. An owner woken by \"Cannot reach PayPal\" had to log in to find out whether it was still broken, and a transient timeout read exactly like an outage nobody fixed. The same channels are now told \"PayPal is answering again\" or \"Background jobs are running again\", at normal priority rather than the alert's, and only when the alert actually went out \u2014 a problem that never alerted clears in silence. It goes out under the same notification, flagged `recovered: 1`, so it needs no switch of its own. See [Notifications](notifications.md)\n    * **Picking a size from a plain variant list now changes the price beside it.** A product with variants and no option axes \u2014 a tee in Small, Medium and Large \u2014 draws a plain select, and nothing listened to it: the price box, the struck price, the badge and the SKU kept the default size's figures whatever the shopper picked, which went unnoticed only while every size cost the same. Each option now carries what the price box says for it, and `kahunacart-options.js` puts it there on change, along with the size's quantity limits, the same way the option picker does for a product with axes. A theme that mirrors the buy box's price into its own heading, as Kahuna does, follows it too\n    * **A fixed-quantity cart line no longer offers a box the server will refuse.** A line an add-on priced and marked `fixed_qty` is one thing \u2014 five nights in August is one stay \u2014 and the cart has refused a change to it since the day it shipped, with a good sentence naming the line. What was wrong was the page: it drew a number box beside the stay, so a guest typed 2, pressed **Update cart**, and was told off for doing exactly what the page invited them to do. The shipped cart prints the number instead, and Remove is still there, because removing a line an add-on priced is always allowed. A theme that overrides the cart asks the same question with the new `kahunacart_line_fixed_qty(item)`, which answers false for every ordinary line \u2014 so a theme that adds the branch behaves exactly as it did on a store with no such add-on. See [Storefront \u2192 `kahunacart_line_fixed_qty(item)`](storefront.md#kahunacart_line_fixed_qtyitem)\n    * **The overdue balance job can now be asked to look again.** `onKahunaCartOrderBalanceOverdue` fired once, a second past the due date, and stopped \u2014 which meant the balance was always nought days late at the only moment anybody heard about it, so *leave the owner alone for three days and then say it plainly* could not be written without the add-on booking a job of its own. **The first firing is exactly what it always was.** What is new is that a listener may ask for another look: `$event['again']->inDays(3)`, or `at()` with a moment of its own, and core books an ordinary queue job for it. Every later firing carries the true `days_over`, the `look` it is, and the same guard the first one has, so a chase **goes silent the moment the order is cancelled, refunded or paid**. The chain is bounded at twelve further looks and a year out, because a listener that asks again every time is a loop and a loop in a queue is a store chasing a customer every four hours; past either bound the ask is dropped and the event stops firing, which is where it was before. Two add-ons both asking is one look at the earlier of the two \u2014 asking sooner covers asking later. See [Deposits \u2192 Chasing a late balance](deposits.md#chasing-a-late-balance)\n    * **A part-paid order says so in words on the receipt.** The **part paid** status shipped with one language string behind it and the storefront needed two: core's own pages resolve a status through the registry, and a theme that builds the key by hand \u2014 which is what both shipped Kahuna themes do on their order page \u2014 reached `PLUGIN_KAHUNACART.PAYMENT_PARTIALLY_PAID`, which nothing defined, so the receipt for a deposit printed the key. Both spellings are defined now and say the same thing, *Part paid, rest to come*, which is the customer's line and sits beside the block already telling them what they have paid and what is due when. The merchant's word for it is **Part paid**, and the admin says that rather than sentence-casing the stored value into *Partially paid*, with the same amber it gives an order awaiting payment\n    * **A refund request can now carry the amount it is asking for.** A cancellation policy that works out that $257.10 of a stay comes back had nowhere to put the number: `RefundRequestService::open()` wrote `null` into a column that has been there since requests shipped, and the admin's panel printed the message and nothing else \u2014 so the figure lived inside a sentence, where no report can add it up and no button can be opened on it. A caller may now pass it, and it is checked rather than trusted, because it is about to be printed in front of a merchant with a Refund button under it: an amount at or below zero, or one above what the order could actually give back, is refused by name with both numbers in the sentence. The panel says **asked for $257.10** beside the reason and the date, **Approve and refund\u2026** opens the refund form on that figure instead of on the whole balance with one line saying where it came from, the owner notification prints it as **Asked for**, and `onKahunaCartRefundRequested` carries `amount_minor`. **A request with no amount reads exactly as it did** \u2014 which is every request the shipped storefront form takes, because it asks for a reason and a sentence and no figure, and a request with no figure has always meant the order. See [Orders and admin \u2192 Asking for a figure](orders-and-admin.md#asking-for-a-figure)\n    * **A pay link on a part-paid order now charges the balance rather than the whole order again.** `docs\/deposits.md` has said since deposits shipped that the order's own pay link \"charges whatever is outstanding\", and the collect-balance endpoint's own docblock said the same. Neither was true: the payment service consulted no collection at all and opened its ledger row for `total_minor`, so a customer sent a balance request for \u00a3700 on a \u00a31000 order with \u00a3300 already paid was handed a page that would take the full \u00a31000 off them a second time. It charges what the collection says is due next now \u2014 the deposit before it has been paid, the balance afterwards \u2014 and the ledger row is opened for the same figure, so the money on the books is the money that moved\n    * **A deposit paid through a hosted checkout no longer holds the order for a person to look at.** The guard that catches a basket changing while the shopper is away paying for it compared the payment row's amount against the order's total, and on an order being paid in parts those differ on purpose. Every deposit taken through a redirect provider therefore landed as \"the cart moved under the payment\": the order stayed in the cart state, was marked paid, sent nothing, granted nothing and sat in the merchant's queue. The comparison is against what the order was being *charged* now, which on every ordinary order is still its total\n    * **A deposit refunded in full is owed again.** The payment status on a part-paid order netted refunds and the balance did not, so the two disagreed about the same order the moment any of a deposit went back. A guest whose \u00a3300 deposit was returned as goodwill was still quoted \u00a3700 on their \u00a31,000 order, on the pay link and in the balance-request email, whose own \"you have paid\" line did net the refund and therefore no longer added up to the order. Paying that \u00a3700 then announced the order **paid** \u2014 licences issued, downloads granted, subscriptions started, the payment-received email sent \u2014 with the shop \u00a3300 short. There is one arithmetic for what an order still owes now, captures less refunds, and everything that quotes a figure at a customer or decides a payment status reads it\n    * **Refunding part of a deposit now lowers the balance instead of adding to it.** A merchant who gave \u00a3100 of a \u00a3300 deposit back on a \u00a31,000 order saw the balance go from \u00a3700 to \u00a3800, so the customer was asked to pay back the money they had just been given, and the balance-request email said so. A part refund is now treated as money off the price: the order comes to \u00a3900, the customer has paid \u00a3200 and is asked for \u00a3700, and paying it marks the order **Paid**. The balance-request email prints the refund on a **Refunded to you** line so the figures add up. Handing back all of a deposit still starts the order over at **Pending**, owing its whole total, so Cancel works as before. A refund after the balance was paid no longer flips the order back to **Part paid** or leaves a balance for the reminders to chase; the payment status stays the **Partially refunded** or **Refunded** the refund wrote. A balance arriving by webhook after a part refund is no longer reported as a double payment. See [Deposits \u2192 What a refund does to the balance](deposits.md#what-a-refund-does-to-the-balance)\n    * **A deposit taken for a different figure than it was asked for no longer breaks the balance.** A merchant capturing \u00a3250 of a \u00a3300 hold, or a merchant-of-record platform settling at its own figure once it has added tax, leaves the deposit recording what actually moved \u2014 and the balance was still quoting the number the schedule first wrote. The two then stopped adding up to the order: the customer paid the balance they were quoted and the order was never finished by it, or was charged more than was left. The leg still waiting is re-priced to what the collection actually owes after any capture that landed at another figure, and nothing is ever charged more than the order still owes whatever a session says\n    * **A part-paid order is now worth what it actually took in the reports.** A 30% deposit on a \u00a31,000 booking put \u00a3300 in the till and the dashboard, the revenue breakdowns and every report said \u00a31,000. The revenue expression already measured the money rather than the total for an order the store had held an authorisation on, and a deposit taken through an instant-capture or redirect provider leaves a purchase row rather than a hold, so it fell through to the order's total. An order sitting on **part paid** is measured by what its collection is holding \u2014 captures less refunds \u2014 and the rest joins the figure when the balance is paid. **No other order moves by a penny**: an order nobody has part paid cannot be in that status, so a store that has never taken a deposit, and a merchant who opened a deposit and then marked the order paid by hand, both read exactly what they read before\n    * **Two payments landing on one order at the same moment both reach its collection.** Deciding which part of an order money belongs to was a read, and settling it was a write, and between the two another request could settle the same session. The conditional update correctly let only one of them win \u2014 and the loser's money then reached the collection nowhere at all, with nothing said about it, because the sum was still inside what the order owed. The decision and the write are one moment now, under the order row's own write lock, so the second payment reads a settled deposit and lands on the balance\n    * **An add-on confirming an order on its deposit is now seen by the confirmation email.** The cart branch placed the order and settled the collection afterwards, so `onKahunaCartOrderPartPaid` fired after the receipt had already been queued \u2014 and the queue runs jobs in the order they were booked, so an add-on that confirms a booking when the deposit lands queued its work behind the message describing it. The first email the customer kept said the booking was still pending. The deposit is announced inside placing the order now, after `onKahunaCartOrderCompleted` and before the receipt, which is the ordering the completion sequence already kept for download grants and licence keys\n    * **A store that trialled deposits and switched them off still settles the order it took one on.** The **part paid** status is contributed for the life of a request rather than seeded, and the listener that contributes it asked only whether deposits were switched on \u2014 so an order with a collection still on its books was written a status the store had never heard of, which nothing could read, which counted as unpaid for ever and which no screen could move. The status is contributed whenever the store has a collection, whatever the setting now says, and nothing that settles a payment will write a status the registry does not know: the order sits in **awaiting payment** instead, which is exactly what a completed order that has not been paid in full is, and reaches **paid** when the balance clears. A store that has never taken a deposit asks one cheap question and keeps the vocabulary it always had\n    * **A corrupt deposit record no longer takes the checkout down with it.** A payment schedule is read back off the order's own collection on the way into every payment, so that a repriced order is charged the right proportion of what it now comes to \u2014 and a percentage that could not be parsed threw from the middle of that read. A blob edited around the API, or left behind by an older version, therefore answered 500 to the customer's Pay button instead of charging them. Reading a schedule back off a row now answers \"no schedule\" on anything it cannot make sense of, the way a product's own stored percentage already did, and the payment path logs and carries on: the order is charged the figures the customer was quoted\n    * **A part-paid order can now be marked paid by hand.** A guest pays a 30% deposit by card and sends the balance by bank transfer, and **Mark paid** refused the order outright \u2014 \"order payment is not pending\" \u2014 so there was no way to settle it at all and it stayed part paid for ever. The button is offered on a part-paid order, reading **Mark balance paid**, and the balance is recorded against the collection in the same breath, so the order's payment status and its collection agree and nothing drags it back on the next write. The refusal for an order that really has been paid says so in words now instead of naming a status\n    * **A customer who paid a deposit can now ask for it back.** The storefront's refund request asked whether the order's payment status was one of two spelled-out words, and **part paid** deliberately is not one of them \u2014 so a guest who had just handed over \u00a3300 was told their order had not been paid, while the admin's own refundable list had that order on it. The question is whether the shop is holding any of the customer's money, which the ledger answers, with the registry's own paid flag in front of it: on a seeded store that is exactly the two words it replaced, and a merchant's own status flagged paid is asked about the same way instead of being refused by name\n    * **The pay-link email no longer promises a third payment on the last one.** \"You will be charged \u00a3300 now. We will ask for the rest later\" was printed whenever the link charged something other than the order's total, which includes the balance \u2014 so the message settling an order told the customer there was more to come. The balance leg says it is the rest of what is owed\n    * **A failed balance payment no longer tells the customer nothing has been charged.** \"Nothing has been charged\" is the one sentence in that message that stops a support call, and on an order with a \u00a3300 deposit already on it, it read as the store having lost the deposit. A part-paid order is told the attempt took nothing and that what they have already paid is still on their order\n    * **Download grants on completion read the paid flag rather than the word.** The fallback path for a store with the hooks runner switched off compared `payment_status` against `paid` outright, so an order that had been partly refunded \u2014 money that did arrive \u2014 was treated as unpaid, and a part-paid order would have been handed its files on the deposit. It asks the status registry's own flag now, which is what the hooks path has always asked\n    * **A balance payment no longer reports the order as paid twice.** Two successful payment rows against one order is what a deposit and a balance are, and the duplicate-charge check read it as the shopper having paid the same order twice \u2014 a line in the log, an entry in the order's history and an item in the merchant's queue, on every single part-paid order. On an order with a collection the check now adds the money up and reports only what is over what the order owes; on every other order it is exactly as it was\n    * **A product that needs an earlier purchase now links to it instead of printing HTML.** The refusal built its \"only available once you have bought Widget\" link as an `<a>` tag inside the sentence, and every page that escapes messages \u2014 KahunaCart's own messages partial among them \u2014 printed the tag as text. On a theme that prints messages raw, the product's own title went into the page unescaped. The sentence is now written with a storefront message link, so it is a link everywhere and the titles are always escaped\n    * **The bundle suggestion names things the way a shopper would.** It named each part by its whole cart line title, so it read \"Bookings \u2014 One site and Rentals \u2014 Rentals only come to $298.00 here. Rentals \u2014 Bookings + Rentals is $249.00\". A product that sells one variant is now named by its title and a variant of a product that sells several by the variant's own name: \"Bookings and Rentals only come to $298.00 here. Bookings + Rentals is $249.00 and includes both.\" The \"Swapped\" notice after the swap reads the same way. Cart lines keep their full titles, and the offer carries both, as `bundle_title` and `bundle_name`, with a `name` on each part\n"},"1.2.10":{"date":"09\/16\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A wallet button can now finish a purchase without reaching into the checkout controller.** Core drew the express region, handed every provider the style contract and then stopped: a driver that wanted its button to actually buy something had to call `KahunaCartFrontend::placeOrder()`, which is marked internal and belongs to the checkout form and the storefront JSON API, or assemble the whole thing out of services by hand and skip the country and region normalisation, the checkout fields, the basket limits, the provider re-check and the ledger row on the way past. There are two public methods for it now. `quoteExpressOrder($orderId, $post)` does what pressing Continue on the checkout form does \u2014 stores the wallet's contact and addresses on the cart, reprices shipping and tax against them \u2014 and answers with the delivery offers and the repriced order, so a sheet can show real methods at real prices, carrier rates included. `placeExpressOrder($orderId, $post)` places it: every gate the form asks, the site's plugins vetoing with `CheckoutValidation::CONTEXT_EXPRESS` on the event, the provider handed the order through `PaymentService::initiate()`, the cart forgotten, and a signed receipt link back. An order the platform held rather than took lands on the receipt as well, which is what a store on `capture_mode: manual` needs. **Nothing about a payment passes through either of them** \u2014 no card field, no token and no wallet payload \u2014 and that rule is held by a test. The test provider is the reference implementation: its Test express pay button opens a fake wallet sheet and buys the cart through exactly these two calls. See [Provider contract \u2192 Express pay](provider-contract.md#express-pay-optional)\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **An order notification now says what was ordered.** \"Items: 1 item\" was the one fact on a sale notification that told a merchant nothing \u2014 the total had already said something was bought, and the answer to \"which one\" was a login away, which for a phone on a Sunday morning means not until Monday. A sale, a payment received and a cancellation now name the products: `Items: 2 \u00d7 Widget Pro, Gadget (Blue)` \u2014 the line title as it was frozen onto the order, the variant's own name after it where the variant has one, the quantity in front where more than one was bought, and no quantity at all for one, the way a receipt leaves it off. **Three lines are named and the rest are counted** (`Anvil, Bellows, Chisel + 4 more`), a product named at length is cut at 48 characters, and the order screen is one tap away for the whole list \u2014 a notification has to fit on a lock screen. It reaches every channel, because they are all rendered from the same summary: Slack, Discord, Telegram, Pushover, ntfy and the owner email. **A refund names no products**, deliberately: the event knows the order's lines but not which of them the money came back for, and naming all of them beside a partial refund would say something untrue. The generic webhook gets the lines as data rather than as a sentence \u2014 `item_count` and an `items` list of `product_id`, `variant_id`, `sku`, `title`, `variant_title`, `qty`, `unit_price_minor` and `total_minor` per line, on `owner.order_completed`, `owner.payment_received` and `owner.order_cancelled`. **Never the line's whole snapshot**, which holds the product and the variant as they were bought, options and all, and is a page of JSON per line for the sake of a word in a chat message. The product names are the store's own catalogue text and not anything a shopper typed, so the privacy rule the chat channels are held to is unchanged \u2014 no email address and no postal address, and both still documented as the email channel's and your own endpoint's alone. An add-on recording an order event without the lines still gets the count it always got. See [Notifications](notifications.md#the-items-line-says-what-was-bought)\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A shipping method the carrier would not quote for no longer prints \"$0.00\" beside the refusal.** A carrier-bound method a courier declined kept whatever `cost_minor` was sitting beside the binding \u2014 nearly always zero, because that number was never the merchant's postage \u2014 and the checkout printed it anyway, so a shopper read \"FedEx Priority Overnight \u00b7 $0.00 \u00b7 No rate is available for this order right now\" next to a radio button nobody could press. A price of nothing beside a sentence saying there is no price reads as free delivery somebody broke. A refused method now carries its sentence and nothing else, and a method the cart can actually have still says what it costs. The offer still carries `price_minor` for anything that wants it; it is the shipped picker that has stopped printing it. Both Kahuna themes override the checkout templates and have had the same change\n    * **Saving a shipping zone no longer gives every method in it a new id.** `PATCH \/kahunacart\/shipping\/zones\/{id}` carrying `methods` deleted the zone's methods and inserted the set again, so a zone whose methods were 1, 11 and 12 came back as 31, 32 and 33, and the next save renumbered them to 34, 35 and 36 \u2014 two edits, one changed method, every id in the zone moved twice and nothing said so. A method id is not a private detail of a form post: `shipping.carrier_rates.fallback_method_id` in the plugin config names one, a checkout in progress keeps the shopper's choice as one, a parcel row points at one, and a placed order's shipping adjustment records the one it was charged under. All four were quietly left pointing at rows that no longer existed, which is how a merchant ends up with a nominated carrier fallback that does not exist and a checkout that has nothing to fall back to. A zone save now reconciles instead of replacing: a method sent back with its `id` is edited in place and keeps that id, a method sent without one is created, and a method left out of the payload is deleted along with the translated labels that named it. Ordering is unchanged \u2014 a client that sends its own `position` is still obeyed, and one that does not now gets the order it sent its methods in rather than relying on the ids being minted in that order on the way past\n    * **The Carriers panel no longer warns about services a driver simply has not listed yet.** A driver that implements `CarrierServiceCatalogInterface` and comes back with nothing \u2014 a test key that cannot list the courier account yet, a cached fetch that has not run, a list that threw \u2014 had its empty answer read as \"definitely not in the catalogue\", so every method bound to it was called out by name as naming a service the carrier does not have, while all of them were quoting perfectly well. An empty catalogue is not a short catalogue: a driver that names nothing is now treated exactly like a driver with no catalogue at all, `service_known` on its methods comes back `null` rather than `false`, and the warning stays quiet until there is a real list to check against. `GET \/kahunacart\/shipping\/carriers` says which of the three it is with a new `catalogue` per driver \u2014 `none` for a driver without the interface, `empty` for one that has it and cannot say right now, `listed` for one that named something \u2014 and `services` keeps the shape it always had, so nothing reading it has to change. A populated catalogue still calls out a service that is not in it, and it is still a warning and never a refusal\n    * **A custom field's help text now sits under the field it explains.** The Details panel beside an order, a product or a customer drew each field's caption and its control as one cell of the form grid and the sentence explaining the field as the next cell, so on a panel wide enough for two columns \"Gift Message\" and its input took the left half and \"What to write on the card packed with this order.\" floated at the top of the right half, level with the caption, attached to nothing, while the input it belonged to was squeezed into half a panel it had entirely to itself. A field is now one block \u2014 caption, control, then the help in small muted text under the control, all on one left edge \u2014 and the control names its help through `aria-describedby`, so a screen reader reads the two together instead of reading every sentence after the whole panel. Several fields still share the grid and each one keeps its own sentence beneath it; a single field fills the panel. **Save details** sits a field's gap under the last field rather than welded to its input. Every type the widget draws got the same treatment: text, number, textarea, select, the tick box, and a readonly field's printed value\n    * **The express-pay block no longer runs the width of the page above checkout.** A store with a wallet button installed opened its checkout with \"Express checkout\" centred over a black button stretched from one side of the page to the other and \"or continue below\" under it, all sitting above the two-column layout \u2014 so the first thing on the page read as a banner rather than as one of the two ways to pay, and even on a stock single-column store the block was wider than the 42rem form it stood in front of. The region now sits at the top of the form itself, above Contact and at the width of the fields: the caption in the same small muted voice the form uses for its own labels, the buttons in a row on the same left edge as the inputs, each capped at 20rem so a lone wallet button does not stretch across the column, and \"or continue below\" set into the middle of a divider line the way checkouts everywhere draw it. Two or three buttons sit side by side at that cap and wrap when the column runs out, the merchant's `express.min_width` is still the floor, and a theme that wants a different cap sets `--kahunacart-express-max-width`. **The contract is untouched**: the region mounts a provider's own button and nothing else, no card data goes near it, and a store where no provider mounted a button still renders nothing at all. A provider drawing a bare `<button>` should give it a type of its own now that the region is inside the form. Both Kahuna themes override the checkout template and have had the same change\n    * **An express-pay button no longer disappears on a dark storefront.** `express.theme` had three values and all three were absolute \u2014 dark drew a black button, light a white one, outline a transparent one with black text \u2014 so a merchant picked a colour once and got it on every page. That is fine until the store has a dark mode: on the Kahuna theme's dark side the default black button sat on a near-black page as a rectangle you could only find by tabbing to it, and outline was worse, since its black label had nothing behind it at all. **`auto` is the new default and follows the page**: a dark button with a light label on a light page, a light button with a dark label on a dark one, decided in CSS at render time rather than in the settings, so a shopper who flips the theme mid-checkout sees the button flip with it. It resolves from the storefront's own ink and ground, which means a theme publishing `--kahunacart-fg` and `--kahunacart-bg` gets its own palette on the button for nothing, and a theme that wants some other colour sets `--kahunacart-express-auto-bg`, `--kahunacart-express-auto-fg` and `--kahunacart-express-auto-border` on `.kahunacart-express`. **A merchant who picked dark or light keeps exactly what they picked** \u2014 both are unchanged and still absolute. **`outline` moves as well, in the one way it was broken**: it keeps its transparent fill and takes its border and its label from the page's own foreground instead of a fixed black, because picking an outlined button is a choice about the button's edge and never a choice to have it vanish on the dark side of a theme. A wallet SDK that takes a theme of its own at mount and has no `auto` to give it picks the nearest it can, which is black, so nothing a real wallet draws changes today. See [Provider contract \u2192 Express pay](provider-contract.md#express-pay-optional)\n    * **The Details panel no longer breaks outright on a field marked internal or readonly.** Those two badges were drawn by calling the view's own status helper, and a zone widget has no view under it \u2014 `this` inside a widget is the widget's registry entry \u2014 so the whole panel came back as \"Custom fields could not be read: this.status is not a function\" as soon as one field on the entity carried either mark. Products and customers wore it worst, since a supplier code that is never printed on the storefront and a field owned by the plugin that declared it are precisely what those marks are for. The badges come from the module-scope pill now, which is where every other zone widget already got its own\n"},"1.2.9":{"date":"09\/15\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A variant can now record how big it is, not just what it weighs.** `length_mm`, `width_mm` and `height_mm` sit beside the weight in the variant editor, under a **Size** caption, in whole millimetres. Leave them empty and nothing at all changes \u2014 null means \"not recorded\", which is what almost every variant in almost every catalogue says, and is a real answer rather than a gap to fill with a zero. A zero, a negative or a decimal is refused with a 422 naming the field, because a box with a zero side is not a box and a courier asked to quote for one answers nonsense. They come through the products API, the `update_variant` MCP tool, the CSV importer as three more columns (with **no centimetre alias** \u2014 WooCommerce heads its own columns `Length (cm)`, and reading a 24 into a millimetre column would hand a courier a parcel a tenth of its real size) and the catalogue file, and they are frozen onto the order line with the weight, so correcting a product's size never changes what a completed order was charged. **Nothing in KahunaCart prices from them.** A live carrier does: the [KahunaCart Shipping add-on](https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-shipping) reads them off the parcel's lines, and the packing, the box selection and the dimensional weight are its job, exactly as the carriers themselves are. Migration 0076\n    * **A carrier driver can say which services it has, so a merchant stops typing them.** `carrier_service_code` on a shipping method has always been free text filled in from a courier's documentation, and nothing checked it \u2014 a typo produced a method that quoted nothing and reported it as the carrier declining the parcel rather than as a misspelt word. A driver may now implement `Shipping\\Carrier\\CarrierServiceCatalogInterface`, one method answering a list of `CarrierService`, and the service box in the zone method editor becomes a select of that driver's own services, grouped by the underlying carrier for a driver that fronts an aggregator. It is optional the way a payment provider's connection check is optional: a driver that does not list its services gets exactly the free-text field it had before, and the select always carries a **Something else\u2026** option that gives the box back, so nobody is ever locked out of a service a driver forgot to list. A method naming a service outside its driver's list is called out by name on the Carriers panel, beside the warning about a carrier no add-on provides \u2014 and it is **a warning and never a refusal**: the method stays bound, stays offered and stays priced, because a driver's list can be out of date. `GET \/kahunacart\/shipping\/carriers` answers a `services` list per driver, empty for one that does not list them, and `service_known` per bound method\n    * **The zone editor finally has the carrier fields it was always saving.** `carrier_code` and `carrier_service_code` have been columns on a shipping method since 1.1 and have never been in the zone editor, which meant saving a zone rewrote every method without them and quietly unbound a carrier somebody had set over the API. Both are on the method now \u2014 a select of the installed drivers where there are any, the code as text where there are none \u2014 and both are sent on every save\n    * **An add-on can hang recurring work on the worker's own tick.** The worker's maintenance list \u2014 hold releases, the sweeps, the rollup catch-up \u2014 has always been core's own and has had no door into it, so a plugin with a sweep of its own had two bad choices: keep a clock in a table of its own, or ride the job-handler registration, which fires whenever anything builds the registry rather than once a run. There is now an `onKahunaCartWorkerTick` event, fired once per `bin\/plugin kahunacart work` run, carrying the queue and the time the tick believes it is. It fires **last**, after core's own sweeps, for the same reason add-ons register their job handlers after the built-ins \u2014 a slow listener must not cost the store its abandoned-cart mail \u2014 and **nothing a listener does can stop the tick**: a throw is caught, logged and stepped over, because the worker may still be holding somebody's receipt. Work enqueued on it is claimed by the next pass, which is a minute at most under cron and about a second under `work --daemon`. A store with no scheduler gets no tick and no recurring work, which is the honest answer rather than a sweep that happens only when somebody visits the shop\n    * **`JobQueue::enqueue()` takes a dedupe key.** A fifth argument, and while a job carrying that name is still waiting to run, enqueueing it again writes nothing and answers the id of the job already there. That is exactly what a sweep which re-books itself for the next interval needs: booked both by the tick above and by its own re-queue, one job an hour quietly becomes two and then four, and until now every add-on with a recurring job wrote the same guard by hand \u2014 a read of the queue looking for a pending copy. The key is free again the moment its job completes, is cancelled, or spends its last attempt, because a sweep that failed three times must not go silent for ever. It is **a read rather than a unique index**, and deliberately: the key has to be reusable once its job finishes, so the constraint would have to be a partial unique index, and SQLite, MySQL and Postgres do not spell one the same way \u2014 what it buys is turning a duplicate every tick into a duplicate only if two workers land inside the same millisecond, and a store running one worker never sees even that. A handler that must run *exactly* once still claims a `DedupeStore` key. A job queued with no key behaves precisely as it always has. `unrunJobFor()` answers the same question without queueing anything. Migration 0077\n    * **An add-on's own order email can join the Send email\u2026 menu.** A message about an order that comes from a plugin \u2014 the [Shipping add-on](https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-shipping)'s \"your parcel was delivered\", a subscription add-on's renewal notice \u2014 could be registered, switched off, re-pointed at another template and logged like any other, and could not be re-sent from the order screen, because the kind list was a `const` on a `final` class. A `NotificationDefinition` registered through `onKahunaCartRegisterNotifications` with both a `kind` and a `jobType` on it is now an order email like the store's own: it queues through `Orders\\OrderEmails`, earns its `email.queued` and `email.sent` entries on the order's history, appears on the **Send email\u2026** menu with the last time it went out beside it, and answers `POST \/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/emails\/{kind}\/resend` \u2014 which replays the payload the last one carried, so whatever the message needed comes back with it. The label on the menu comes from the definition's own language key, translated on the server because the admin's translator only reads core's namespace, and falls back to the kind sentence-cased. **Nothing about core's own messages moves.** The twelve keep their names, their order and their behaviour, the gift card stays queueable and deliberately not re-sendable, a plugin naming a kind core already owns is ignored, and a kind nobody registered is still a `404`\n    * **Any plugin can have a setting stored encrypted, not only a payment provider.** Encrypted settings shipped with the payment work and the writing half was wired to the payment registry, so a provider declared its secrets in `encryptedSettings()` and everybody else was stuck: a carrier driver holding a courier account token or an email provider holding an API key had no way to get it out of plaintext, even though the reading half never had that limit \u2014 an encrypted value carries its own prefix and is opened on the way in whoever wrote it. There is now an `onKahunaCartCollectEncryptedSettings` event carrying `keys`, and appending one line to it is the whole registration. A key that arrives through the event is treated exactly like a provider's: held to `plugins.<your-plugin>.<key>` so a listener cannot name another plugin's config or the rest of the site's, encrypted by the same file-wide sweep when the settings are saved, decrypted in place at the start of every request so the plugin reads its config as it always has, and keyed on the same database-held store secret. A key declared twice is one key, a malformed one is dropped and logged, and a listener that throws costs its own keys rather than the merchant's save. Nothing changes for a payment provider \u2014 `encryptedSettings()` is collected as before\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **Checkout has a button under each part of it, and the delivery options turn up on their own.** The page had one submit, beside the totals, and it changed its wording as checkout moved along \u2014 \"Continue to shipping\", then \"Continue to payment\", then \"Place order\" \u2014 so the button that took a shopper to the delivery options was the same button that spent their money, and it was nowhere near whatever they were filling in. Each part of the form now carries its own: **Continue to shipping** under the addresses (or **Continue to payment** when the order has nothing to deliver), **Continue to payment** under the delivery methods, and **Place order** beside the totals and nowhere else, saying the one thing it does. With the script running a button is greyed out until the step above it is finished \u2014 every required field answered, every list of choices settled on one \u2014 and the step and its button both carry `data-kc-step-ready` and `aria-disabled` so a theme can style either state. And the delivery methods no longer wait for a round trip nobody asked for: a moment after the address is complete, the storefront fetches the delivery step for the address as typed and drops it into place under the fields. **That preview stores nothing** \u2014 the cart keeps whatever address it already had, the address is saved by the ordinary submit, and the methods are priced again against the saved address, so the two-step property holds exactly as it did: the method on an order was always chosen against the address on the same order. A Continue is not a request to pay: the submit is read, stored and re-checked exactly as a Place order is, right down to the delivery gate, and then checkout re-renders at the next step with nothing flashed, because nothing went wrong. **None of it is needed to check out.** It is still one form and one POST, every button is a real submit, and with scripting off every button is live and the flow is the one it has always been. A theme that has not been updated keeps working: a form that posts no `checkout_step` is the whole checkout and places the order. `partials\/kahunacart-checkout-shipping-step.html.twig` is new and is what both the page and the fragment render; see [Storefront](storefront.md#a-button-under-each-part-of-the-form) and [Theming](theming.md#what-a-checkout-override-has-to-carry) for what an override must now carry\n    * **A Continue now lands on the step it names, instead of at the top of the page.** Each part of checkout carries its own button, and pressing one sent the browser back to the same URL \u2014 so **Continue to payment** scrolled *away* from payment, and the shopper had to go looking for the thing they had just asked for. Every step is now drawn inside an element with an id of its own \u2014 `kahunacart-checkout-address`, `-shipping` and `-payment` \u2014 and the redirect after a step submit ends in the id of the step the shopper should be on now: the delivery step after the address on an order that has one, payment after the address on an order that has nothing to deliver, and payment after the delivery method. A refused submit goes back to the step that could not be left, and the delivery gate \u2014 no method chosen, none on offer for this address \u2014 lands on the delivery step whichever Continue tripped it, because that is where the options are. **None of it is script**: it is an anchor on an ordinary redirect, so a browser with scripting off lands in exactly the same place. With the script running the step also takes the cursor \u2014 its first field, or the choice already picked \u2014 and the stylesheet keeps a theme's sticky header off the heading through `--kahunacart-sticky-offset`. Both Continue buttons grew a small down chevron, since the step they go to is the one below. **A theme that overrides the checkout template has to carry the three ids across**, or its Continue lands nowhere; see [Theming](theming.md#what-a-checkout-override-has-to-carry). A form that posts no `checkout_step` redirects exactly as it always did\n    * **Place order now waits for the whole checkout, not just the payment step.** Every part of checkout carries its own button and the script greys one out until its step is finished \u2014 except that \"finished\" was read off the controls inside that one step, and nothing else. **Place order** sits in the payment step, where a payment provider is preselected and the terms box is the only other thing to answer, so it was live from the moment the page painted: a shopper could press it with the address half typed and no delivery method picked, and all they got for it was the server refusing the submit and sending them back up the page. Readiness cascades now: a button is enabled only when its own step *and* every step drawn above it on the page are answered. So Place order stays greyed out while the addresses are unfinished or while a delivery step is sitting there with nothing chosen, and **Continue to payment** waits on the addresses the same way. A step's own `data-kc-step-ready` still means what it always did \u2014 this step's fields are done, which is what a theme styles a finished section with \u2014 and the button carries the cascaded answer, with the form itself marked `data-kc-checkout-ready` once everything on it is answered. Only the steps a page actually draws count, so an order with nothing to deliver is not waiting for a delivery step that was never there, and a delivery step that arrives after the fact joins the cascade as it lands. **Nothing changes with scripting off**: every button is live and the server gates the flow, exactly as before\n    * **A greyed-out checkout button now says what it is waiting for, and autofill counts as typing.** Each part of checkout carries its own button and the script greys one out until the step above it is answered \u2014 and it did that in silence. Fill in the whole billing address, watch **Continue to shipping** stay grey, and the page told you nothing: the email and the name in the Contact fieldset above are part of the same step, and there was no line anywhere saying so. There is one now, under the button: **Still needed: Email, Name** while a required field on that step is empty, and **Finish the address above first** or **Choose a delivery method** on a button further down the page, where what is in the way is a step above it. The field names come off the page's own labels \u2014 the `<label for=\u2026>` pointing at the control, the `<label>` wrapped round it, its `aria-label`, and only then the post name read as words \u2014 so a store that renamed a field, translated it or declared one of its own reads the name it chose rather than one the script guessed, and nothing in the file knows the name of a single checkout field. A list of choices with nothing picked is asked for by name rather than by heading: the delivery step and the payment fieldset carry `data-kc-choice-label`, which is why it reads \"Choose a delivery method\". A theme whose own override names nothing gets the heading over the list pointed at instead \u2014 \"Choose an option under Shipping method\" \u2014 rather than a legend dropped into a sentence it does not fit. The sentences are translated in PHP and handed over on the form as one JSON attribute, the way the buy button gets its quantity hints; a theme override that never carried the attribute across gets the line in English rather than no line at all, and a theme that draws its own `data-kc-step-missing` element gets the line exactly where it put it. **The other half is autofill.** A browser filling a whole address in at once fires no `input` and no `change`, so a form that was completely filled in could sit there with the button grey and the reason line saying fields were empty that plainly were not. Three things catch it now: a no-op `kahunacart-autofill` keyframes on `input:-webkit-autofill` in the stylesheet, with the form listening for that animation starting, which is how Chrome and Safari can be heard at all; `focusout` beside `blur`; and, for the first ten seconds after the page loads, a look at the form every half second \u2014 then it stops, because after a fill every change comes with an event of its own and a checkout page must not poll for as long as somebody has it open. **Nothing changes with scripting off**: there is no button to grey out, no line to print, and the server gates the flow exactly as it always has. See [Theming](theming.md#what-a-checkout-override-has-to-carry)\n    * **Unticking \u201cShip to the billing address\u201d now shows the delivery fields where you unticked it.** The box is a tick box, so unticking it ought to open the fields under it \u2014 and it did not. The delivery address was rendered only on a page that already knew the answer, so a shopper unticked the box, looked at the same form they were looking at a second ago, and had to press **Continue** and wait for a page load before there was anywhere to type. The fieldset is drawn on the page from the first paint now, carrying `hidden` while the box is ticked, and unticking it takes the attribute off. Everything else follows from being on the page: the delivery fields keep their `required` attributes either way, so the moment they are shown **Continue** greys out again and the line under it names them \u2014 **Still needed: Delivery city, Delivery postcode** \u2014 and the moment the box is ticked again they hide and stop holding the step. The state field follows the delivery country exactly as the billing one does, and the delivery options are repriced for the new destination a moment later rather than waiting for a submit. **And the preview now prices the address the parcel is going to.** It picked between the two addresses on the store's tax basis, which is the wrong question: a store basing tax on the billing address quoted a Denver shopper for Denver while the parcel was going to San Francisco. The rule is the submit's own now, letter for letter \u2014 box ticked means the billing address, unticked means the delivery fields, a delivery address with no country on it yet falls back to the billing one \u2014 and both addresses go down to the carrier drivers the way a submit writes them, so what the preview quotes and what the order is charged can no longer disagree. **With scripting off nothing changed at all**: untick, press Continue, and the page comes back with the fieldset open and everything else still filled in, which is what checkout has always done. **A theme that overrides the checkout template has to carry two attributes across** \u2014 `data-kc-ship-same` on the box and `data-kc-ship-to` on the fieldset, with the fieldset always drawn \u2014 or its customers get the round trip back; see [Theming](theming.md#what-a-checkout-override-has-to-carry)\n    * **Picking a delivery method now changes the total beside Place order.** The radios were read on the next submit and nowhere else, so clicking a faster service left the shopper looking at the cheaper one's price in the summary, with **Place order** live over a figure that was no longer true. The charge was always right \u2014 the submit stores the posted method before it reprices \u2014 but nobody should find that out from their statement. The click now posts the form with `checkout_step=shipping`, which is the very submit **Continue to payment** sends, so the address and the method are stored and the order repriced down the road they always went down; two regions of the answer come back onto the page, the delivery step and the order summary, and nothing else moves. No price is worked out in the browser. While the request is out both regions carry `aria-busy` and **Place order** is disabled, two quick clicks are sent one at a time rather than raced, and a request that fails leaves the page exactly as it was with **Continue to payment** storing the choice the way it always did. The collection points get the same treatment. A theme that overrides the checkout template needs `id=\"kahunacart-checkout-summary\"` on the element holding the totals and the button \u2014 see docs\/theming.md. The click is counted in the `fragment` rate-limit bucket rather than `checkout`, where a shopper comparing four services would have spent most of a checkout's allowance without leaving the page and met the refusal on **Place order** \u2014 it is the ordinary submit either way and still stores the method, and a form posted by a browser with no scripting is counted in `checkout` exactly as it always was; see [Hardening](hardening.md#rate-limits)\n    * **A variant's weight can now be typed in the admin.** `weight_grams` has been a column since the first migration, the API has always taken it, the CSV importer has always mapped `Weight (g)` onto it and core's own by-weight rate tables have always read it \u2014 and there was no box for it anywhere in the admin. A catalogue built entirely through the admin therefore had no weight in it at all, which left every by-weight method matching the bottom band and left a live carrier pricing every parcel on its size, the wrong number for anything dense. The variant row editor's **Size** block is now **Size and weight**: Weight in grams first, then Length, Width and Height in millimetres. It reads exactly as strictly as the three sizes beside it \u2014 blank means \"not recorded\" and is what almost every variant says, and a zero, a negative or a decimal is refused by name, because a zero is \"I do not know\" typed into a box that will not take a blank and 1.2 kg stored as 1 g is a parcel quoted at a thousandth of its weight. The same reading now applies to the products API, which used to write whatever arrived straight to the column. **Set for all variants** still offers price and stock only, as it did for the sizes\n    * **The zone method editor's Carrier select names the driver instead of its slug.** Binding a method to a live carrier offered \"Priced by your own rates\" and then `shippo`, lowercase, the registry key \u2014 beside a service select full of real names. Every driver has had a `labelKey()` all along and this was the one screen that never asked for it. `GET \/kahunacart\/shipping\/carriers` now answers a translated `label` per driver alongside the key, and the select prints it while still posting the slug. A driver whose key has nothing behind it in any language file is listed as its slug, which is what every driver read as before\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart status` lists the carrier drivers beside the payment providers.** A store whose live rates had gone quiet had nowhere on the command line to look, and the three usual causes all read the same from the storefront: the Shipping add-on is not installed, the driver registered under a code the shipping method does not name, or nothing was ever bound to it. There is now a **Carriers** block with a line per registered driver \u2014 its code, its label, whether it can list its own services (`N service(s) listed`, or `no catalogue` for a driver that does not offer one) and how many shipping methods are bound to it \u2014 and `none installed` on a store with no carrier add-on, which is the normal state since core ships no carrier of its own\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Changing the country on checkout no longer lets Continue through with the state still empty.** The state field is the one control the browser rebuilds by itself \u2014 a select of ISO 3166-2 codes for a country that publishes a list, a text box everywhere else \u2014 and the fresh control arrived carrying no `required`. So a shopper whose password manager filled the billing address in, country and all, got a step that read as finished, a Continue that came alive over \"Choose a state\", and a refusal from the server a click later saying State \/ Region is required. The control the swap puts in is now required exactly when a page rendered for that country would have been: the checkout publishes the countries it asks for a state in and the browser reads them off the field, rather than guessing. It keeps a state the browser had already written too, whichever of the two controls it landed in and whether it arrived as \"CO\" or \"Colorado\" \u2014 a browser fills an address field by field, and the state often lands a moment after the country did \u2014 and the steps are read again the instant the swap lands, so the line under the button names State \/ Region while it is still empty.\n    * **Place order works again on a checkout with \"Ship to the billing address\" ticked.** The delivery-address fieldset is drawn on every page now and hidden while the box is ticked, so that unticking it opens the fields on the spot \u2014 but its required fields kept their `required` attributes, and a browser asked to validate a required field it cannot show refuses the submit and has nowhere to put the cursor. The two Continue buttons carry `formnovalidate` and never met it; **Place order** does not, so pressing it did nothing at all, with only \"An invalid form control with name='shipping_address1' is not focusable\" in the console to say why. The hidden fieldset is `disabled` as well as `hidden` now, server-side and in the script, which is the honest description of a delivery address nobody is using: its controls are neither validated nor posted. Unticking the box takes both attributes off together, and the server reads `shipping_same` before it reads a delivery field either way\n    * **A live carrier's rate no longer takes the checkout page down with it, or prints its own working under the method name.** A driver hands core a rate with its own metadata on it \u2014 the carrier behind an aggregator, the reference for the quote, a collection cut-off \u2014 and core folded that whole map into the offer's metadata under one key, so the offer carried an array in among its strings. A theme prints an offer's metadata by looping it and printing each value, which is what the plugin's own partial and both Kahuna themes do, and Twig answers an array with `Array to string conversion`: a 500 on the checkout page, for every rate a driver returned any metadata with, which is every rate the [KahunaCart Shipping add-on](https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-shipping) produces. Flattening the map stopped the 500 and left the other half of the problem standing \u2014 the codes were now printable, so they were printed, and a customer choosing delivery read `demo cached driftwood_ground Driftwood Ground 1215 USD 5 domestic 6000 1 1 demo_rate_8e6c5d` under the method name. **An offer carries two maps now.** `metadata` is what a person reads, it is the driver's own notes and nothing else, and it is still flat and scalar all the way through \u2014 a nested map flattened onto `parent.child` keys, anything a theme could not print dropped. `carrier` is the machine half \u2014 `carrier_code`, `service_code`, `service_label`, `rate_ref`, `amount_minor`, `currency`, `estimated_days`, whether the quote came from the cache \u2014 and **no theme prints it**. A driver still cannot rewrite the quote by naming one of the rate's own fields in its metadata, and it does not get the number printed either. `CarrierRate` has no field for a rate reference and is not growing one, so core reserves the names `rate_ref`, `carrier_account`, `carrier_account_ref`, `attributes` and `estimated`: a driver hands one of those over in its metadata as it always has, and core lifts it onto `carrier` rather than printing it. Both maps go into the shipping adjustment's `source_json` for the method that won, so an add-on buying a label later still reads the reference the customer was quoted at. The one thing core says out of `carrier` is the transit estimate, as a sentence \u2014 \"Arrives tomorrow\", \"Arrives in about 5 days\" \u2014 because a number of days is not a sentence in any language. `shipping.metadata_enabled: false` was the only way round any of this and cost every theme the metadata it was written for\n    * **The PDF renderer stops writing into its own vendor directory.** dompdf parses a font's metrics once and caches the result as a `.ufm.json` beside the `.ufm` it read, so the next document skips a 200 KB parse \u2014 and where that cache lands is `fontCache`, which nothing here ever set. The default is dompdf's own font directory, which meant the first invoice, packing slip, credit note or report on any install wrote files into `vendor\/dompdf\/dompdf\/lib\/fonts\/` inside the plugin: files nobody put there turning up in the package, thrown away again by every GPM update since an update replaces the folder, and on a host where `vendor\/` is read-only re-parsed for every document forever. The cache is under `cache:\/\/kahunacart\/dompdf-fonts` now \u2014 the directory Grav already guarantees is writable and already clears on demand \u2014 and a cache directory that cannot be made or written falls back to dompdf's own default rather than refusing to render, because a cache miss is a slower document and never a failed one. Two of those stray metrics files had ridden into the repository back in August and are gone from it, with the pattern ignored so no more follow\n    * **A new customer group takes its code from its label again.** The Code box on the new-group form says a code is derived from the label if you leave it blank, and it was not: the form sends a box for every field it has, so an untouched Code arrived as an empty string rather than as nothing at all, and the fallback behind it only fired on a missing key. Creating a group without typing a code by hand failed with \"A customer group needs a code.\" \u2014 on the one screen where the code is the thing a merchant is least likely to have an opinion about. Two fixes, because one of them is the one you see: naming the group now fills the Code box in as you type, the way the slug fills in when you name a product attribute, and it stops the moment you type in the box yourself; and a blank code arriving over the API takes one from the label as the field always promised. **Editing an existing group never rewrites its code** \u2014 renaming Trade to Trade Partners leaves the code `trade`, and clearing the box on an edit keeps the code the group has rather than deriving a fresh one, because that code is what every price list rule and coupon condition names and re-slugging it would stop them matching anybody, silently. Renaming a code is still typing a new one\n    * **A carrier's own reason for refusing a service is what the customer reads now.** A driver that refuses says why in its own words \u2014 \"this service will not take a parcel over 900 mm on its longest side\" \u2014 and core has always kept that language key in the refusal's `reason_params.detail_key`. The shipped checkout partial printed core's generic `carrier_excluded` sentence instead, \"the carrier cannot send this order by this service\", so the useful half was sitting in the offer unread while the admin's Labels tab printed it. The partial prefers the driver's sentence when there is one and falls back to core's when there is not\n"},"1.2.8":{"date":"09\/13\/2026","content":"2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The Campaigns tab on the Coupons screen works again.** The campaigns half of the screen drew every control but listened to none of them, so the tab never opened, the campaign filter over the codes did nothing, and New campaign, Generate, Export CSV, Show its codes, the edit link, pause and delete were all dead to a click. It shipped that way with coupon campaigns on 30 August and went unnoticed because the first campaigns were set up through the API. Every one of those controls is wired now, and Show its codes switches to the Codes tab with the filter set to that campaign\n"},"1.2.7":{"date":"09\/12\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A declined card now tells the customer, not just you.** You have heard about a failed payment since the store-owner messages landed; the customer only ever heard about one they happened to be watching happen on the processing page. A payment that fails after they have gone \u2014 a redirect that comes back refused, a webhook that reports it ten minutes later, a bank transfer that bounces overnight \u2014 left them with an order that said nothing and no way back to it. There is now a **Payment failed** email on the Customers half of the Notifications screen, on by default and switchable off like every other: nothing was charged, the reason the provider gave if there is one a shopper can use \u2014 the same sentence the payment page prints, put through the same filter, so an identifier or a raw error object never reaches an inbox \u2014 and one button to **pay again**. A placed order carries a real pay link with its deadline in words; a payment that failed during checkout carries one with no deadline at all, because that order is still the customer's own basket and a deadline on a basket would lock them out of their own checkout once it passed. An order that has been paid by the time the message would go out is not sent it at all \u2014 they have their receipt, and \"nothing has been charged\" landing after it is the worst thing a store can say. An order that merely can no longer be paid, because you cancelled it or its deadline ran out, gets the shop link instead of a button that would only refuse them. Deduped per order over six hours, so somebody retrying a declining card three times in a minute gets one email and a failure that evening is news again. Never any card details. Queued as `order.payment_failed_email`, resendable from the Send email\u2026 menu, and previewable like the rest\n    * **A customer can ask for a refund.** The admin side of refunds has been complete for waves \u2014 through the provider, per line, credit notes, the email, the store-owner message \u2014 and there has never been any way for the customer to ask. Now there is one, and it is an ask rather than a refund: the money decision stays with you. On the receipt (the signed link, so guests get it too) and on every row of the account order history there is one spot that either offers **Request a refund** \u2014 a reason off a fixed list of six, an optional sentence, a button \u2014 or says that a request is already open, or says what came of one, or offers **Contact us** when the window has closed or the order is already refunded. `orders.refund_window_days` is 14 by default and counted from when the order was placed; `0` takes no requests through the website at all. `orders.contact_url` is where the Contact us link goes, and leaving it empty draws no such link at all. The customer is emailed \"we have your request\" with their own words quoted back and no date promised; you get an urgent `owner.refund_requested` message on whichever channels you have ticked, never deduped, because two requests are two customers. A `refund_requests` dashboard alert counts the ones nobody has answered, and a **Refund requested** filter above the Orders chips is the one filter on that screen the server answers rather than the browser, because five people waiting are easily on five different pages. The order screen grows a panel with what they asked and two buttons: **Approve and refund\u2026** opens the refund form you already know with the whole balance ready to go (approving less is a real answer, and the request is only marked agreed once the refund itself answers), and **Decline\u2026** asks for a sentence and sends it as the ordinary note-to-the-customer email, because a decision the customer never hears about is the thing this replaces. Routes are `GET \/kahunacart\/orders\/{id}\/refund-request`, `POST ...\/refund-request\/approve` and `POST ...\/refund-request\/decline`, with `get_refund_request`, `approve_refund_request` and `decline_refund_request` beside them, and `onKahunaCartRefundRequested` fires for add-ons. Migration 0075. Physical returns \u2014 labels, received-goods checks, RMA numbers \u2014 stay for the returns add-on, and the request row carries what the add-on needs, so it can hang off this rather than replace it\n    * **The receipt now tells your analytics that a sale happened.** Nothing on the confirmation page said a purchase had gone through, so anyone running GA4, Google Tag Manager, Plausible or Meta ads had to write Twig against the order row and work the money out themselves \u2014 which is how a store ends up reporting a hundred times its revenue, because the order row holds cents and every analytics tool wants decimals. The page now carries a JSON block describing the sale and fires `kahunacart:purchase` on `document` for your own snippet to pick up: order number, currency, total, subtotal, tax, shipping, discount, the coupon codes, the provider and the lines, with every amount a decimal string in major units and the tax on a tax-inclusive store the tax already inside the prices. A reload of a bookmarked receipt arrives with `repeat: true`, so one purchase is one conversion however many times somebody comes back to the page. It carries no email, no name, no address, no phone, no customer id and no payment reference \u2014 Google's terms forbid sending them any of it \u2014 and the plugin sends nothing anywhere on its own: which tool hears about the sale is the one snippet you add. `docs\/storefront.md` has four to copy, and `storefront.purchase_event: false` turns the whole thing off\n    * **Duplicate a product.** A store with twenty similar products used to build every one of them from a blank form. **Duplicate** is now in each row's \u22ef menu on the Products list and in the product editor's header, and it is `POST \/kahunacart\/products\/{id}\/duplicate` with a `duplicate_product` MCP tool beside it. The copy is titled \"Copy of \u2026\" with a slug derived from that, it is a draft whatever the original is, its publish and unpublish dates are cleared, and it lands at the end of the manual order. Everything that belongs to the product comes with it \u2014 variants and their option combinations, option axes and values, option prices, categories, tags, attributes, custom fields, customer-group restrictions, related products, prerequisites, kit items, price-list prices, quantity tiers, translations, and the media as files rather than as shared paths, because deleting a product deletes its folder. Copied variants come out with a **blank SKU**, so the store's own SKU template mints a fresh one, and with **zero stock**, because the copy has no units of its own. Downloadable files and releases stay behind with the product whose buyers they belong to, and so do reviews, sales figures, history, revisions, wishlist entries and download grants. The copy's first history entry says which product it came from\n"},"1.2.6":{"date":"09\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The store owner gets told about their own store.** The Notifications screen had twenty customer emails and one merchant one \u2014 the store copy of the confirmation \u2014 and no way at all to hear that a payment failed, that a webhook was being refused, or that a provider had stopped answering. It now has a **To** filter (All \/ Customers \/ Store owner, remembered per browser) and twelve store-owner rows: a sale, payment landed, a refund, a cancellation, the store copy, and seven problems \u2014 payment failed, webhook rejected, outbound webhook deliveries failing, a provider not answering, a dispute opened, background jobs stopped, and low stock. Every problem is deduped per cause, so a card declining six times in a minute is one message, and the two that can recover \u2014 a provider that starts answering, a queue that starts draining \u2014 clear their stamp so the next outage is news again. Three events are new and carry the facts: `onKahunaCartPaymentFailed`, `onKahunaCartWebhookRejected` and `onKahunaCartDisputeOpened`, the last fired by whichever driver receives the platform's dispute webhook. Add-ons register their own store-owner messages the same way, so kahunacart-polar's API-version fallback and kahunacart-subscriptions' past-due notice get a row by name\n    * **Channels: where the store owner is reached, with a Send test on every one.** A Channels page of its own, reached from the Notifications header, with **Email**, **Slack**, **Discord**, **Telegram**, **Pushover**, **ntfy** and a **Webhook** of your own. Channels are rows in `kahunacart_notification_channels` (migration 0074) with their settings encrypted at rest through the same cipher a payment provider's API key goes through, keyed on the store's own database-held secret; a secret is never sent back to the browser, and leaving its box alone on an edit keeps what is stored. **Send test** records a real `notification.test` event through the real queue and the real dispatcher, addressed to that one channel, and shows the service's own error when it fails \u2014 a 401 from Telegram is a wrong bot token and a 400 \"chat not found\" is a wrong chat id, and the screen says so. Email goes through the store's own mail pipeline, so it lands in the email history with the same From address and the same retries\n    * **Channels are ticked per message, and a message with none says so.** A store-owner row's Template column becomes **Channels**: a chip per channel, ticked or not, with a greyed chip per kind the store has not set up that opens the Channels page on that kind. A row with nothing ticked reads **Nowhere yet** rather than looking sent. At dispatch the rule is the union \u2014 a channel receives an event if the message's row ticks it, or if a channel in the config file lists the event by name \u2014 so a store with both arrangements gets both and neither is contacted twice\n    * **Push priority per message.** Each store-owner message declares `low`, `normal`, `high` or `emergency`, overridable per row from the \"\u2026\" menu, which Pushover and ntfy turn into how loudly a phone rings. A sale is quiet, a chargeback is emergency \u2014 on Pushover that repeats every minute for an hour until it is acknowledged, which is the one message with a deadline somebody else set. Slack, Discord, Telegram and email ignore it\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **A coupon refused for its per-customer limit says so.** The message was \"You have already used this coupon\", which reads as a code that works once; a customer who has used a five-per-customer code five times now reads that they have used it as many times as it allows\n    * **An add-on's notification can carry its own headline.** A `title` in the data recorded with an event is used as the Slack or Discord headline instead of the event's name, and is left out of the field list. Events without one read as before\n    * The five merchant events were renamed to match their new rows \u2014 `order.completed` is `owner.order_completed`, and so on for payment received, refunded, cancelled and low stock. **Every old name still works as a subscription**: a `notifications.channels` entry listing `order.completed` goes on receiving sales, and those channels are listed on the Channels page read-only, marked as coming from the config file. **If you run your own endpoint, read this one.** The subscription still reaches you, but the name inside the envelope is the new one, so a receiver that switches on `order.completed` would be sent every sale and quietly match none of them. The envelope and the headers now carry **both** names \u2014 `event` is the current one, `alias` the old one, with `X-KahunaCart-Event-Alias` beside `X-KahunaCart-Event` \u2014 so you can key off either until you have moved across. The alias table is in [Notifications](docs\/notifications.md)\n    * The store copy of the confirmation is a store-owner row with a switch and no channels. It is the customer's own confirmation email with the address from **Emails \u2192 Send order copies to** in the Bcc, so its row names where the copy goes instead of offering chips that could never fire, and `owner.store_copy` is not an event a channel can subscribe to\n    * **A channel says where it goes, and you can edit one.** The Channels page listed a name and a kind and nothing else, so checking which address a channel sends to meant removing it and typing it again. Every row now has a **Where it goes** column read off the fields the API answers in full \u2014 the addresses on an email channel, a Telegram chat id, an ntfy server and topic, and a Slack, Discord or webhook URL with its token-like path taken out, because that URL is itself the secret and `hooks.slack.com\/services\/\u2026` is as much of it as anybody needs to see. Pushover says so rather than showing a blank, since both of its boxes are secrets. Channels from the config file get the same summary, still read-only. The name in each row and **Edit** in its \"\u2026\" menu open the channel at `#\/notifications\/channels\/{id}` \u2014 an address of its own, so it can be linked to, reloaded and left with Back \u2014 with the label, the switch and every non-secret box filled in, every secret box empty, and a hint saying that leaving a secret blank keeps what is saved. Only what you changed is sent, and a form with something typed in it asks before you walk away from it\n    * **The channels live on their own page now, reached from the Notifications header.** They were a panel drawn under the table, so on a store with thirty messages pressing **Set up channels** opened something far below the fold and looked as if the button did nothing at all. **Set up channels** \u2014 **Channels (n)** once you have some \u2014 goes to `#\/notifications\/channels`, a screen with its own address and a back link, so it can be linked to, reloaded and left with the browser's Back button. A greyed **set up** chip on a row goes there too, with that kind already chosen, and coming back re-reads the registry so the chips show what you just added or removed\n    * `docs\/notifications.md` is rewritten around the screen and gains a step-by-step setup for each of the seven kinds of channel \u2014 where BotFather is, how to get a Telegram chat id and why a group's starts with a minus, where the Pushover user key and application token come from and what emergency priority actually does, why an ntfy topic name is a password and that an iPhone gets it through ntfy.sh's relay even from a self-hosted server. `docs\/provider-developer-guide.md` gains the three new events, the dispute recording contract for drivers, and how to register a store-owner message\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The discount-code banner no longer prints \"Hide this\" beside its close cross on pages outside the shop.** The label is meant for screen readers only, but the rule that hides it lived in the storefront stylesheet, which is not loaded on a blog post or a docs page. The banner's own stylesheet now carries it\n"}}},"kahunacart-stripe":{"name":"KahunaCart Stripe","version":"1.2.5","description":"Stripe payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted Checkout, subscriptions, webhooks, and refunds","description_html":"<p>Stripe payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted Checkout, subscriptions, webhooks, and refunds<\/p>","description_plain":"Stripe payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted Checkout, subscriptions, webhooks, and refunds","icon":"credit-card","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.3.0"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/stripe","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, stripe, checkout, subscriptions","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-stripe","license":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/premium\/license","tag_name":"1.2.5","date":"2026-09-25T16:07:41Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-stripe\/1.2.5","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-stripe"},"slug":"kahunacart-stripe","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-stripe","content_sha256":"417fdb042643f5eb6845a1de096607537cadfe7516e74a1002f053f4be50840e","changelog":{"1.2.5":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.2.4":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Agent payments: Google Pay through UCP.** `StripeProvider` implements KahunaCart's new `AgentPaymentInterface`, so an agent platform such as Google AI Mode or Gemini can complete a checkout with a card the buyer picked in Google Pay, without a redirect. The driver declares `agent_payments` when a publishable key is set and Managed Payments is off, advertises `com.google.pay` with Stripe's Google Pay gateway parameters (`stripe:version` `2018-10-31`), turns the Stripe token Google mints into a PaymentMethod with `card[token]`, and confirms a PaymentIntent with `error_on_requires_action`, so a card that wants a 3-D Secure challenge fails with a sentence the agent can relay rather than waiting for a browser. Manual capture holds the money as a two-step checkout does, both calls carry an idempotency key made from the order and the platform's own key so a retried completion never charges twice, and the intent carries the usual order and transaction metadata plus `kahunacart_source: agent`. The token goes to Stripe in one call and never into a log, a ledger row or a result, and the `payment_intent.succeeded` delivery that follows settles nothing a second time. **Needs the base plugin at 1.3.0**\n    * The test suite can load KahunaCart core from another checkout: set `KAHUNACART_TEST_CORE_ROOT` to its folder\n    * **3-D Secure on a Google Pay agent charge becomes a handoff to the buyer.** The PaymentIntent is confirmed with `return_url` (the store's payment return page) instead of `error_on_requires_action`, and a `requires_action` intent answers KahunaCart with Stripe's hosted authentication page and the intent id, which KahunaCart 1.2.15 turns into UCP's `requires_escalation` with the page behind the checkout's `continue_url`. A `use_stripe_sdk` action is followed only to a page on Stripe's own host; anything else still fails with a sentence the agent can repeat.\n    * **An agent's `payment_intent.succeeded` is reported by its transaction hash.** It settles a challenged payment and a charge whose answer the store never recorded; after an ordinary charge core's ledger gate makes it a no-op. An agent's `payment_intent.payment_failed` no longer looks for a Checkout Session that was never there.\n1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-stripe`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-commerce-stripe`\n"},"1.2.3":{"date":"09\/16\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The wallet button no longer disappears on a dark storefront.** It painted itself `#111111` on every theme but `light` and `outline`, so on the dark side of a theme like Kahuna the Apple Pay \/ Google Pay \/ Link button was a near-black rectangle on a near-black page, and `outline` was no better \u2014 a black edge and a black label with nothing behind them. The base plugin's `express.theme` has grown an `auto` value and made it the default, and this button now draws it the way the provider contract asks: the fill, the label and the edge come from `--kahunacart-express-auto-bg`, `--kahunacart-express-auto-fg` and `--kahunacart-express-auto-border` on the region wrapper, which the storefront stylesheet resolves from the page's own ink and ground. Dark button with a light label on a light page, light button with a dark label on a dark one, and it flips with the page rather than with a setting. `outline` follows the page as well now \u2014 the fill stays transparent and the border and the text take the page's own foreground, because picking an outlined button is a choice about the button's edge and never a choice to have it vanish. `dark` and `light` are unchanged and still absolute, so a merchant who picked one gets exactly that. **Needs the base plugin at 1.2.10**, which is where `auto` and the wrapper's colours come from\n"},"1.2.2":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **One shopper is now one Stripe Customer.** Every checkout used to go over with `customer_email` and `customer_creation: always`, which gives Stripe nothing to match a returning buyer on, so it opened a second Customer for every repeat order: two billing histories, two sets of saved cards, and a dashboard showing two strangers who each bought once. KahunaCart 1.2.3 keeps Stripe's own customer id per customer and hands it to the driver, which now sends `customer` with it instead, on one-time checkouts, authorisations and subscription checkouts alike. Never both parameters in one request, because Stripe refuses that pair outright. The id is reported back from the session Stripe answers with and from the `checkout.session.completed`, `payment_intent.succeeded` and `charge.captured` deliveries, so a store starts building the mapping from its very next order\n    * A held id that Stripe no longer knows, which is what a merchant who rotated keys onto another account or moved a store from sandbox to live is holding, opens a fresh Customer on the email path instead of failing the checkout. One line goes to the Grav log and the new id replaces the old one, so it corrects itself on the first order and nobody has to clean anything up\n    * Under Managed Payments the store still never asks Stripe to open a Customer, since Onelink is the seller there, but an id Stripe itself reported is sent back the same way\n"},"1.2.1":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Every request to Stripe now names the API version, so Managed Payments works on an account with an old default.** The driver used to inherit the account's default version, which is whatever version the account was opened on. On a 2020 default Stripe refused every Managed Payments checkout with \"not supported on API version 2020-03-02\", and the merchant's only way out was to upgrade the whole account. Each call now carries `Stripe-Version: 2025-09-30.clover`, the version the driver's references were checked against, which is what Stripe recommends for an integration anyway. Webhook payloads are unchanged: the endpoint is still registered without a version and the handlers read both layouts\n"},"1.2.0":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Stripe registers its own webhook endpoint. `StripeProvider` implements `ProviderWebhookSetupInterface`, so **Set up webhook** appears on the Stripe card under Payment methods: it finds or creates the endpoint for this store's URL, subscribes it to every event this driver acts on, and writes the signing secret Stripe hands back into the active mode's field. That replaces the step that breaks stores, a merchant copying a secret between two browser tabs and ticking eighteen event boxes, every mistake in which is silent, because checkout keeps working and paid orders simply never settle\n    * Pressing it twice adopts the endpoint already registered for the URL instead of creating a second one that would deliver everything twice. An adopted endpoint whose event list has fallen behind is corrected rather than merely reported, and one Stripe disabled after a run of failed deliveries, what a store that was down for a weekend comes back to, is switched back on in the same call\n    * Stripe puts `secret` in the answer to the create call and nowhere else, so an adopted endpoint has none to report. A store that already holds one is told its secret is left alone; a store that does not is told the two ways out, which are copying it from the dashboard or pressing **Reset webhook**. Reset deletes the endpoint at this exact URL and registers a fresh one, and deletes nothing else on the account: endpoints on this store's route at a previous address are reported so the merchant can remove them, never removed for them\n    * The endpoint is created with no `api_version`, so it follows the account default instead of being pinned for years to whatever version this driver was written against\n    * A restricted key that cannot manage webhook endpoints now fails with a sentence the merchant can act on, pointing at the Instructions tab. Stripe's own answer to that case is a developer's sentence with the key quoted inside it, and this result is rendered onto an admin screen and into whatever logs that screen, so neither half of it goes through. A key Stripe rejects outright gets its own sentence, since the fix is a different one\n    * `checkConnection()`, which Stripe did not have. It reads `GET \/v1\/account` with the saved key and reports the account that key actually belongs to, the country and default currency, whether Stripe is letting the account take payments, and what this store will do at checkout. A live key sitting in the test field is named outright, Stripe puts the mode in the key, and that is the one mistake here that charges real cards while a merchant believes they are rehearsing. A missing webhook signing secret fails the check rather than passing with a warning nobody reads, because a store in that state takes the customer's money and records none of it\n    * A `mode` setting is passed to the provider. Stripe serves both modes from one API host, so nothing about a request says which key pair it is carrying, and the driver has to be told: the signing secret it writes back after registering an endpoint belongs in that mode's field and writing it into the other one would leave a store rejecting every delivery\n    * An **Instructions** tab in the plugin settings, between General and API Keys. It walks a merchant through the whole sequence in order, the Stripe account or sandbox, the secret key for the mode, saving it, pressing Set up webhook, the manual fallback with the endpoint URL and both halves of the event list, the test cards and the Stripe CLI, and the three things to check afterwards. Written for somebody doing this for the first time rather than for somebody who already knows what a signing secret is\n    * The README describes the button, what adoption and reset each do, and points at the tab\n    * The two manual-capture events, `payment_intent.amount_capturable_updated` and `payment_intent.canceled`, are on the list the button subscribes and in the manual list in the README and the Instructions tab. The driver had translated them since 1.1.0 but no list told a merchant to tick them, so a hold placed or released in Stripe's own dashboard never settled the ledger on a store set up by hand\n    * A test reads the event types `mapEvent()` dispatches on straight out of the source and fails when the subscribed list differs, so the next event a release adds cannot go unheard\n    * A **Checkout Label** setting, like every other driver has: the customer-facing name of the payment option, empty for the default \"Credit \/ Debit Card (Stripe)\"\n    * A capture made in Stripe's own dashboard now reaches the store. `payment_intent.succeeded` and `charge.captured` are translated and are on the list the setup button subscribes, in the README and in the Instructions tab. Manual capture is a two-console arrangement and the dashboard is the obvious place to take the money for a merchant already there reviewing the payment, and without these the order sat at `authorized` for ever with the money taken, the admin went on drawing a Capture button, and pressing it answered \"this PaymentIntent has already been captured\" and wrote a failed capture row. A partial capture records the figure taken and the figure Stripe released\n    * The connection check resolves the Billing Portal configuration when one is set, and fails naming it when Stripe does not know it. Stripe keeps configurations per mode, so an id copied from a sandbox into a live store used to pass every check the store had and then fail for every subscriber who pressed the portal button, which meant the customer met the problem before the merchant did\n    * Every refusal from the webhook endpoint names itself. `verifySignature()` grew a `signatureProblem()` behind it that answers `no_secret_configured`, `missing_headers`, `stale_timestamp` or `bad_signature`, and the two other refusals answer `unparseable_body` and `not_an_object`. Nine distinct causes used to write one identical log row, and the log is where somebody debugging a webhook actually looks: clock skew, a secret pasted with a trailing newline and a proxy truncating bodies have completely different fixes. `no_secret_configured` earns its own code because it is the store's own setting rather than the sender's mistake, and it is the commonest reason a fresh store's paid orders never settle\n    * Every `ignored()` carries a reason and the platform's own id for the object, so the delivery log tells \"an event type we do not handle\" apart from \"a payment we refused to act on\"\n    * The store's own tax can be billed on a subscription. When the caller supplies the period's tax, the subscriber's price is minted at the taxed per-period total and the breakdown goes back on `SubscriptionResult::$taxLines`, so the add-on stores it on the row and every renewal order carries the same lines. A taxed price belongs to one jurisdiction and is deliberately not written back onto the plan\n    * A one-time checkout carries the caller's `description` to Stripe, so a shopper's Stripe receipt names what they bought instead of reading \"Order payment\", and it fills `client_reference_id` and the metadata with this store's own customer id. It also asks Stripe to create a Customer for the sale (`customer_creation: always`, and not under Managed Payments), so a merchant reconciling in Stripe's dashboard has something to click through. The README says what that costs on a store with a lot of guest sales\n\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A Managed Payments refund was scaled on the store's current setting rather than on the marker Stripe put on the session.** Both refund legs read the `managed_payments` config, so a merchant who trialled the mode and turned it back off refunded merchant-of-record orders light by the tax, and every refund made in Stripe's dashboard was written into the pre-tax ledger heavy by it. On a live order that is 100 net going back as 100 when the customer paid 106, and 106 landing in a ledger that counts in nets. The setting says how a store sells today; the session says how one order was sold, and they part company the moment the flag moves with live orders on the books. The session's marker now decides in both directions, so an ordinary store is unaffected beyond one extra read per refund\n    * **A refund larger than the whole charge was blamed on the store's settings.** Stripe refuses that with a different sentence from the one it uses for a refund larger than the remainder, and only the second was mapped. The first fell through to the handler that reads a named parameter as a misconfigured store, so the merchant was sent to check payment settings that were fine, with no reason code, which also meant the base plugin's refund balance notice never fired. Both refusals now say the same thing about the amount and carry the same reason code\n    * **A card Stripe kept was not written down here.** The completion delivery asked the store's `save_payment_methods` setting whether to read the method back, and that is the setting as it stands when the webhook arrives, not the one the checkout was created under. A merchant who turned saving off in that window left a card held at Stripe with no local row, which the shopper can neither see in their account nor remove. The checkout now stamps a marker on the session and the completion reads that, so the row is written on settlement whatever the setting says by then\n    * Two merchant sentences were in British English. The Billing Portal check and the unknown-payment refusal both said \"does not recognise\"; both are rewritten as \"has no record of\", which reads better than either spelling\n    * **A refund the store made settled in the ledger at zero under Managed Payments.** The driver reported `amount_minor: 0` on every refund event to mean \"do not open a row from this\", which was safe only while nothing read that key on the settlement path. Since the base plugin's `markSuccess()` gained a settled amount it is read, so a \u20ac100 refund confirmed by `refund.updated` rewrote its own ledger row to \u20ac0.00: the order never reached `refunded`, no credit note was raised, the reversal that revokes licences and download grants never ran, and the admin offered the same \u20ac100 as refundable again. The key is now omitted, which the base plugin reads as \"the driver did not say\"\n    * **A refund issued in Stripe's dashboard could not be matched to any order.** Two causes, and between them real refunds went back to customers with the ledger knowing nothing about it. Refund events named the charge (`ch_\u2026`) while the purchase row holds the PaymentIntent (`pi_\u2026`), so the lookup compared two id families and found nothing, every time, on every store; and on API version `2026-08-26.dahlia` the Charge in a `charge.refunded` event carries no refunds list at all, so the one event that had the order hash on it was ignored. Every refund event now carries `payment_ref` with the PaymentIntent id, which is the reference the purchase row holds, with `charge_id` beside it rather than instead of it. A refund delivery for a refund this store issued is logged against its order too\n    * **Managed Payments could not be turned on.** `http_build_query` writes a PHP `true` as `1`, and Stripe answers a boolean parameter carrying `1` with \"Invalid boolean: 1\", so the very first API call was refused and every checkout on a store with the flag on failed with a message that meant nothing to the merchant. Booleans are converted once, in the encoder, so the next one cannot be written the same way\n    * **A `customer.subscription.deleted` was recorded as a cancellation scheduled for the period end.** The add-on decides immediate-versus-scheduled from `ended` and `at_period_end`; Stripe's own field is `ended_at`, a timestamp, so both hints read null and the add-on scheduled it. A subscriber Stripe had already cut off kept their licences, downloads and entitlements for up to a full billing period nobody was paying for, and the storefront showed them a cancellation date Stripe had already passed. Both keys are sent now, and a scheduled cancellation says `ended: false` beside its `at_period_end`\n    * **A customer who pressed \"Renew subscription\" in the Billing Portal was never heard.** Stripe reports the undo as an ordinary update and the driver had a branch for a cancellation being scheduled and none for one being unscheduled, so the delivery was answered 200 `ignored`: Stripe agreed the customer was staying and went on charging them while KahunaCart cut off their access on the date it still had stored. Reported as `resumed` carrying `uncanceled: true`, on both the classic and the flexible-billing-mode portal payloads\n    * **A plan repricing was ignored at Stripe.** A stored price id was reused because it existed, so a merchant raising a plan from 6.00 to 7.00 saw the storefront, the order, the subscription row and the customer's own page all say 7.00 while Stripe went on charging 6.00 for the life of every new agreement. The stored price is read back and compared on amount, currency and interval before it is reused, and a fresh one is minted when they disagree. Nothing is archived: the old price is still billing live subscriptions\n    * **A guest's card was kept.** The driver asked \"is there a customer id\", and this store writes a customer row for every checkout keyed on the email, so the answer was yes for a shopper with no account anywhere. The card was stored against a record nobody can sign in to: never asked for, never visible, and impossible to withdraw. The base plugin answers the real question now and the driver asks it\n    * **A failed subscription read during activation was answered 200.** The read failed soft, so the activation arrived with no period and the add-on dated the agreement by arithmetic on the store's own clock, and a subscription that started on a trial was recorded as active because the trial flag is read off the subscription that could not be read. Stripe never redelivers a delivery it was told was handled, and the empty catch left not even a log line. That read now throws, the base plugin answers 500, and Stripe tries again; the payment half of the apply is idempotent, so the retry costs nothing\n    * Stripe's raw error text no longer reaches a shopper or a merchant. Every failure path goes through one sanitiser: a message carrying any part of an API key is replaced outright, a schema field name such as `line_items[0][price_data][tax_code]` becomes a sentence about the store's settings, and Stripe's object ids are taken out of what a merchant reads. There used to be two error formatters here and the one wired to the shopper was the one that passed everything through, so a merchant using a restricted key could have a partially redacted key shown to a customer at checkout and written onto a failed transaction row that outlives it\n    * A refused 3-D Secure challenge reads \"The bank did not authorize this card. Try again, or pay with a different card.\" Stripe's own sentence for it names a PaymentIntent and tells the shopper to try confirming again, which means nothing to somebody holding a card. An ordinary card decline still shows Stripe's own wording, which is written for that person and is better than anything this driver would invent\n    * The three refund refusals a merchant actually meets are said in words that name what to do next, and none of them quotes a Stripe id. A subscription's first period is refused before Stripe is called at all, with a sentence saying to refund it in Stripe against the invoice and record it here, rather than Stripe's \"No such charge\" with a 66-character session id in it. A refusal for exceeding the platform's balance carries `RefundResult::REASON_EXCEEDS_BALANCE`, so the base plugin can raise a notice naming both figures\n    * A refund reports what it sent. The settled amount is the net the store asked for, with the gross and the tax beside it on the row, so an accountant preparing a VAT return can see that a \u20ac100 refund cost the business \u20ac119 of which \u20ac19 is not coming back. The row also keeps Stripe's own refund status, the currency, and both ids for the money it came out of\n    * `refundRequest()` is implemented, so a Managed Payments refund that empties the order sends the charge's own remainder rather than a scaled proportion. Refunding \u20ac100 in three goes used to send \u20ac118 back against a \u20ac119 charge, leaving the customer a cent short and a euro at Stripe that nothing would ever claim\n    * A settlement always reports its currency, not only under Managed Payments. The base plugin has a complete currency-mismatch path and it compares against that key, so omitting it did not make the check lenient, it switched it off for every store not running Managed Payments\n    * The webhook signature timestamp window is enforced in both directions. A future-dated delivery used to be accepted with no upper bound, and the everyday value is diagnosis: a store whose clock is ahead of Stripe's now refuses with `stale_timestamp` instead of `bad_signature`\n    * A JSON list posted at the endpoint is refused with a 400 rather than answered 200 `ignored`. `is_array()` in PHP is true of `[1,2,3]`, so a scanner's junk used to produce log rows indistinguishable from ordinary unhandled traffic\n    * An empty credential is refused in the transport, before any network call, so every entry point behaves the same way. A store set to live mode before the live key was pasted in used to spend a round trip on every checkout to be told \"Invalid API key provided\", and the shopper read that sentence\n    * A webhook delivery's reads are bounded. A five-second connect timeout and a ten-second read, against Stripe's own thirty-second delivery window: both were thirty, and this driver can make two reads inside one delivery, so a slow minute at Stripe could cost sixty seconds and a run of those is how an endpoint ends up disabled\n    * A superseded Checkout Session is expired at Stripe before the replacement opens, and a session left payable by a decline is expired too. A shopper who hesitated, went back and started again left two Stripe pages payable for the same order, and paying both is not hypothetical when the old tab is still open. Wrapped, so a Stripe that will not close the old page cannot stop the new one\n    * The webhook setup button adds missing events and no longer removes surplus ones. An event a merchant subscribed that endpoint to themselves, `charge.dispute.created` for their own logging say, used to be stripped off the next time anybody pressed it. A reset on a store with no endpoint at this URL no longer claims to have deleted one\n    * `provider_customer_ref` rides on every recurring event, under the contract's name with Stripe's own spelling beside it. Three subscription branches carried no customer reference at all, and a subscription whose activation was lost and which is then cancelled through the portal reaches the store with none on the row, so there is no Billing Portal URL to build and the customer can neither undo the cancellation nor replace their card\n    * A scheduled cancellation is dated by Stripe rather than by local arithmetic. `SubscriptionResult::$effectiveAt` carries `cancel_at`, falling back to Stripe's own period end, so a customer's account page and their card statement cannot name different days\n    * In test mode the connection check no longer says Stripe is not letting the account take payments. `charges_enabled` is false on every sandbox until live onboarding is finished and test charges work regardless, so the sentence was untrue and it sat inside a green tick\n    * The README's claim that manual capture removes iDEAL, Bancontact and the SEPA debits is withdrawn. On a US account the same cart under both modes offered an identical set of methods with nothing dropped; the European methods were never on offer to that account either way, so the claim is untested rather than confirmed, and the file now says so\n    * A delivery whose `type` is a JSON list is ignored instead of being cast to a string, which was a 500 with debug on and the word \"Array\" without\n    * A subscription checkout reports the Checkout Session id, so the store has something to put on the purchase row it opened for the first period. That row's reference comes from the result and the only field there was is `providerRef`, which becomes the subscription row's `provider_ref` and is what every later webhook and the customer's own cancel and pause buttons are matched on, so the session id could not go there and the row was written empty: a merchant looking at a subscription checkout somebody walked away from had no id to quote at Stripe and nothing to search the dashboard for. It now goes back on the base plugin's new `SubscriptionResult::$transactionRef`, and `providerRef` stays free for the Subscription id that arrives on `checkout.session.completed`\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Two-step capture. `capture_mode: manual` creates the Checkout Session with `capture_method: manual` on the PaymentIntent, so checkout holds the card instead of charging it and the order stands at `authorized` until the merchant presses Capture or Void. `StripeProvider` implements `AuthorizeCaptureInterface` in full: `capture()` resolves a Checkout Session reference into its intent and posts `amount_to_capture`, `void()` cancels the intent, and `completeAuthorize()` reads the session Stripe stamped onto the return URL and reports what the intent's state actually says\n    * The README says plainly what the mode costs before a merchant switches it on: Stripe narrows a checkout to the payment methods it can hold, so iDEAL, Bancontact and the bank debits stop being offered to European customers while it is on. The mode is the merchant's decision and this driver will not make it for them \u2014 there is no store-wide capture setting in KahunaCart and there never will be, because a cart that hardcoded one is how another store lost those methods for months without noticing\n    * `payment_intent.amount_capturable_updated` and `payment_intent.canceled` are translated, so a hold and its release settle the ledger whether they were made here or in Stripe's own dashboard. The authorization carries `amount_capturable` \u2014 what Stripe actually held, which is the figure the merchant captures against, rather than the amount that was asked for\n    * Saved cards. `save_payment_methods` adds `setup_future_usage: off_session` to the Checkout Session, and `TokenizationInterface` describes and detaches what Stripe keeps. What is stored here is Stripe's reference, the brand, the last four digits and the expiry, and there is nowhere in KahunaCart for anything more. A removal detaches at Stripe first and the local row goes only once Stripe says it has let go; a method Stripe has already forgotten counts as removed, and a Stripe that cannot be reached fails loudly rather than claiming success\n    * Off-session charging through `OffSessionChargeInterface::charge()`, which hands the caller's idempotency key to Stripe's own `Idempotency-Key` header rather than inspecting it, so a retried renewal is one charge on a statement instead of two. The customer id is read off the method because Stripe refuses to charge an attached method without naming its customer, and a method that needs 3-D Secure fails with Stripe's reason rather than answering a redirect nobody is there to follow\n    * An express-pay wallet button, registered on `onKahunaCartRegisterExpressButtons` and drawn to the store's own style contract. It asks Stripe.js whether the browser has Apple Pay, Google Pay or Link and appears only when it does, so nobody is shown a wallet button that would do nothing. It posts nothing: pressing it hands the shopper to Stripe's hosted page, which is where this driver's payments have always happened\n    * Publishable-key settings for both modes, public by design and used only by that probe. Without one the express capability is not declared at all, which is better than declaring a button that cannot ask the browser anything\n    * `encryptedSettings()` names both secret keys and both webhook signing secrets, so KahunaCart stores them encrypted against a key that lives in the store's database and not in any file \u2014 a config file copied to staging or attached to a ticket no longer carries the Stripe key. Both modes are named rather than only the active one: a store that switched to live must not leave its test secret in plaintext beside it. The publishable keys are deliberately excluded, since they are printed into the page\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"08\/31\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * A completed Checkout Session now reports `amount_minor` \u2014 Stripe's `amount_total` for that session \u2014 so the base plugin settles the pending ledger row at what Stripe actually took rather than at the amount the row was opened with. A `mode=subscription` session that opened on a free trial arrives as `no_payment_required` with a total of `0` and is recorded as `0`, which is the difference between a trial and a full-price sale nobody paid for. A session naming no total at all omits the key rather than claiming a free sale, and the merchant-of-record split still reports its pre-tax subtotal where it applies\n    * Recurring billing: Stripe declares the `Subscriptions` capability and implements `RecurringBillingInterface`, so the KahunaCart Subscriptions add-on can sell plans through Stripe Billing. A plan checks out as a Checkout Session in `mode=subscription`; the plan's Stripe price is created on its first sale \u2014 product and all, in one idempotent call \u2014 and handed back so it is reused by every later subscriber, or supplied by the merchant and used untouched\n    * Recurring webhook translation on the same endpoint payments already use: `checkout.session.completed` in subscription mode is a paid order *and* an `activated` event carrying the period and the order hash; `invoice.paid` with `billing_reason: subscription_cycle` is a `renewed` event with the period Stripe billed, the amount, and the invoice id the add-on dedupes on; `invoice.payment_failed` is `payment_failed`, plus `past_due` when Stripe has moved the subscription there; `customer.subscription.updated` is `paused`\/`resumed`, `cancelled` (at period end), `payment_method_updated`, or `expired` when dunning leaves the subscription unpaid; `customer.subscription.deleted` is `cancelled` or `expired` depending on Stripe's cancellation reason; and Stripe's own `customer.subscription.paused`\/`.resumed` are translated too. Every decision is made from the delivery itself, so a redelivery produces identical events\n    * Both API-version layouts of the objects that moved in `2025-03-31.basil` are read: `current_period_start`\/`end` on the subscription or on its items, and `invoice.subscription` or `invoice.parent.subscription_details.subscription`. A webhook endpoint carries its own API version independently of the keys, so both still arrive\n    * A cancellation scheduled from the Billing Portal is caught in either billing mode \u2014 flexible mode, the default since `2025-09-30.clover`, records it on `cancel_at` and can leave `cancel_at_period_end` untouched\n    * Cancel at period end or immediately, pause with `pause_collection[behavior]=void` (nothing billed and nothing owed for the periods the customer did not use), resume by clearing it \u2014 which is also the uncancel, since the base contract has no `uncancelSubscription()`: the subscription is read first, and a scheduled cancellation is cleared with `cancel_at_period_end=false` in classic billing mode or `cancel_at=` in flexible mode, never both in one request, while a subscription with neither a pause nor a scheduled cancellation is refused rather than written to \u2014 and a Billing Portal session URL for card updates and invoices, with an optional `billing_portal_configuration` setting for stores keeping more than one portal configuration\n    * Trials: `trial_days` becomes `subscription_data[trial_period_days]`, and the `no_payment_required` checkout Stripe completes a trial with is treated as a paid order so the subscription actually activates\n    * Managed Payments sells subscriptions as well \u2014 Stripe stays merchant of record on every period, not just the first. The eligible digital-goods tax code and the explicit `tax_behavior: exclusive` move onto the plan's price and product, since subscription mode has no inline line item to carry them\n    * Recurring API writes carry an `Idempotency-Key`, so a retried checkout replays its session rather than opening a second one and two customers racing to be a plan's first subscriber cannot mint two prices. The key folds in the request it belongs to, so an attempt that genuinely differs \u2014 the base plugin opens a fresh ledger row each time payment starts \u2014 gets a key of its own instead of failing on Stripe's changed-parameters check\n    * Metadata the caller puts on the subscription context is carried through to Stripe's own, within Stripe's limits and never over the driver's four keys. That is how `transaction_hash` reaches the completion webhook, which is what settles the order against the ledger row the base plugin had already opened\n    * Managed Payments now declares the `DigitalGoodsOnly` capability, since Stripe's merchant-of-record programme accepts only products carrying an eligible digital-goods tax code. Checkout stops offering Stripe for carts holding a shippable line while the flag is on; ordinary Stripe is untouched and still sells anything\n    * Initial release: first-party Stripe provider for KahunaCart\n    * Hosted Checkout Session flow \u2014 `initiatePayment()` creates a Stripe Checkout Session and redirects, with order and transaction hashes attached to both session and payment-intent metadata\n    * Webhook handling with self-contained `Stripe-Signature` verification (HMAC-SHA256, 300s replay window, constant-time comparison, no SDK)\n    * Event mapping for `checkout.session.completed`, `checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded`, `checkout.session.async_payment_failed`, `payment_intent.payment_failed`, `refund.created`, `refund.updated`, `refund.failed`, and `charge.refunded`\n    * Full and partial refunds through `\/v1\/refunds`, by payment intent or charge reference\n    * Async refund lifecycle: a refund Stripe answers `pending` or `requires_action` is reported as pending and settled later by `refund.updated` \/ `refund.failed`, keyed on the same `re_\u2026` reference the refund call returned; `failed` and `canceled` release the held amount for a retry\n    * `charge.refunded` is now acted on only for a refund the charge already shows as settled, since it fires at refund creation and cannot confirm one Stripe is still working on\n    * Stripe Managed Payments support behind a `managed_payments` flag \u2014 Stripe becomes merchant of record, calculating and remitting sales tax, VAT, and GST itself and issuing the receipt and invoice, while KahunaCart's own tax engine stands down. Not a separate API: the same Checkout Session call with `managed_payments[enabled]`, an eligible product tax code (`managed_payments_tax_code`, default `txcd_10000000`), an explicit `tax_behavior: exclusive` so an account-level \"Include tax in prices\" setting cannot reinterpret a pre-tax total, and the statement descriptor dropped because Stripe rejects it in this mode\n    * Managed Payments declares `MerchantOfRecord`, which is what has the base plugin's `MerchantOfRecordTaxStage` price the order pre-tax; refunds convert that net figure to the gross Stripe's charge is measured in by reading the Checkout Session's `amount_subtotal` \/ `amount_total`, so refunding a whole order returns the tax with it. Refunds issued in the Stripe dashboard are not recorded locally under Managed Payments \u2014 Stripe puts no tax breakdown on a Refund or a Charge \u2014 and the gross is reported beside the row instead\n    * Test\/live mode toggle with separate key pairs; the provider registers only when the active mode's secret key is set\n    * Optional statement label applied to the payment intent\n    * Unit test suite covering signature verification, event mapping, Checkout Session parameter building, and the whole recurring path \u2014 every subscription webhook type, price reuse against price creation, cancel, pause, resume, portal requests, Managed Payments plans, and redelivery producing identical events"}}},"kahunacart-polar":{"name":"KahunaCart Polar","version":"1.1.9","description":"Polar payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, merchant of record tax, webhooks, refunds, and catalog sync","description_html":"<p>Polar payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, merchant of record tax, webhooks, refunds, and catalog sync<\/p>","description_plain":"Polar payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, merchant of record tax, webhooks, refunds, and catalog sync","icon":"globe","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.2.3"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/polar","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, polar, checkout, merchant-of-record","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-polar","license":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/premium\/license","tag_name":"1.1.9","date":"2026-09-29T23:43:34Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-polar\/1.1.9","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-polar"},"slug":"kahunacart-polar","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-polar","content_sha256":"a5f2dfa10a3d100e8f2fce0ba8acafa1acc73f439c6e631eeb849dbd9ad4eee3","changelog":{"1.1.9":{"date":"09\/29\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Other plugins hear about deliveries for orders this store never made.** Polar sends every order event on an organization to every endpoint on it, so a store sharing its organization hears about orders another app sold. Those were logged as unmatched and dropped. A verified `order.*`, `refund.*` or `subscription.*` delivery that carries none of this store's references now also fires the Grav event `onKahunaCartPolarWebhookUnmatched`, with the event type, the decoded payload, the Polar order id and the delivery id, so a plugin that imported the old app's licences can revoke one when its order is refunded. The answer to Polar stays 200, and a listener that fails is logged rather than retried\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **Polar's checkout names the store, not the plugin.** The private product every cart is charged against is the title a buyer reads above the amount on Polar's page, and it was created as \"KahunaCart Order\" on every store. A new one is now named after the store (\"Grav Premium Order\"; \"KahunaCart Order\" only for a store with no name). One that already exists is found by its metadata as before, so it keeps whatever name it has in Polar and can be renamed there, and the connection check now reports that name instead of the plugin's default\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The driver only touches its own webhook endpoint.** An endpoint counted as this store's when its URL merely ended in `\/webhook\/polar`, so on a Polar organization shared with another store or another app, the daily API version job would move that app's endpoint to this plugin's version and Reset would delete it. An endpoint is now this store's only when its URL is exactly one of the store's own webhook URLs, one for `system.custom_base_url` and one for each Site Addresses entry, with scheme and host compared without regard to case and a trailing slash ignored. Set up webhook, Reset, the daily job and the dashboard alert all use that one test. Other endpoints on the organization are left alone, not reported as stale, and counted under *Other endpoints* in the Set up webhook result. A store that names no address of its own has the daily job move nothing, with a log line saying what to set\n"},"1.1.8":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-polar`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-polar`\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"09\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The webhook endpoint follows the plugin's API version on its own.** A daily job moves every endpoint on this store's Polar webhook route onto the version the plugin reads, so an upgrade carries the endpoint with it without anybody pressing Set up webhook, and an endpoint left on a version Polar has since removed is rescued even after Polar has stopped delivering to it. It rides the KahunaCart worker, keeps exactly one run queued a day ahead, and logs what it moved. It also notes an endpoint still signed with Polar's original scheme, and Set up webhook shows the scheme beside the version; both schemes verify, and a secret generated fresh at Polar moves an endpoint to Standard Webhooks. A run that fails, over a token without the webhooks scope or a Polar outage, logs once and tries again tomorrow\n\n    * **You are told when the pin stops working.** A line at the top of the store admin says Polar no longer serves the pinned version and the store is selling on Polar's current one, or that Polar is not answering at all, or that an endpoint could not be moved; it rechecks every six hours and names what to do. The first time the fallback fires, one message goes to the store's notification channels, once per version. Both are behind a new *Warn me when the API version pin stops working* setting on the Polar screen, on by default; off, the Grav log still records it\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **A pin Polar has stopped serving no longer stops the store.** Polar removes an API version nine months after it opens, and from that day every pinned call answers a bare 404. The driver now reads the first one as the pin having expired: it logs that, retries the call with no version, which Polar answers in whichever version is current, and floats that way for the rest of the request. That is the behaviour the driver had before it pinned anything, so a store on an old plugin keeps selling while the log says to update it. A bare 404 with no version on it is still thrown, and the message names both things it can be, a wrong path or a removed version, rather than asserting one\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"09\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Every request to Polar is pinned to API version 2026-04.** Polar now versions its API by date: a new version opens each quarter, a request that names no version gets whichever one is Current that day, and 2026-10 becomes Current on October 1. Before this the driver named no version, so its contract with Polar would have changed on that day without a release. The version is one constant, sent as `Polar-Version` on every call; moving it is a deliberate release\n    * **A 404 from Polar's router is no longer read as a verdict on the resource.** Polar answers a missing product or order with `{\"error\":\"ResourceNotFound\"}`, and a path or API version it does not serve with a bare `{\"detail\":\"Not Found\"}`: the same status, told apart only by the `error` key. On the day a pinned version is removed the bare answer comes back for everything, and read as data it would make every product gone, every order unknown and every stored customer id stale. The driver now treats it the way it treats Polar being unreachable, with a message that names the version and says to update the plugin\n    * **The webhook endpoint asks for its payloads in the same version.** Set up webhook registers the endpoint with `api_version` 2026-04, and pressing it on an endpoint that carries another version, or none, moves it and says so. The endpoint's version is shown in the result beside its events. A delivery that arrives in a different version is still handled, and a warning in the Grav log names the version and says to press Set up webhook\n\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Webhook secrets generated before 8 September 2026 verify again.** Polar changed its signing scheme on that date: newer secrets sign with the decoded key, the way Standard Webhooks specifies and the way 1.1.4 reads them, but older secrets sign with the printable `whsec_` string itself. A store whose endpoint predates the change was refusing every delivery with `bad_signature` since 1.1.4. Both keys are tried now, the way Polar's own SDKs do\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"09\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Polar webhooks are verified against the right key, so paid orders settle.** A Polar signing secret is `whsec_` followed by the key in base64, and Polar signs with the decoded key. The driver hashed with the printable string instead, so every real delivery was refused with `bad_signature`, the order never moved off pending, and the return page span forever. The secret is decoded now, the way Standard Webhooks specifies. A secret pasted without the prefix still works as it did\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Polar is told which customer it is looking at. A checkout for somebody who has bought here before now carries Polar's own customer id alongside the store's `external_customer_id`, and the id Polar names on the checkout it creates and on the paid order goes back to the store so it is there for the next one. Before this, a hosted checkout built from an email address gave Polar nothing to match on, so a shopper who bought twice ended up as two customers in the Polar dashboard, with two billing histories and two sets of saved cards. An id Polar no longer recognises, after a move between organizations or from the sandbox to production, is dropped and the checkout goes again without it, so the shopper sees a working checkout and the new id replaces the stale one\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The README and the Instructions tab now say to set Polar's Default tax behavior to Exclusive.** The provider hands Polar the order's pre-tax total as one ad-hoc fixed price and records Polar's reported net as what was paid, so the organization setting under Payments has to treat that amount as the price before tax. Inclusive would carve the tax out of it and leave every order short; Location-based does the same for customers in some countries and not others. Nothing in the code changed\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * A subscription checkout reports the Polar checkout's own id, so the pending purchase row for the first period is no longer left with an empty reference. The only field a driver had was `providerRef`, which becomes the subscription row's `provider_ref` and is what every later webhook and the customer's own cancel and pause buttons are matched on, and there is no subscription to name yet because Polar opens one on the first `order.paid`. The id now goes back on the base plugin's new `SubscriptionResult::$transactionRef`, which is the same id a one-off payment has always written to its own ledger row, so a merchant looking at an abandoned subscription checkout has something to search Polar for\n    * A plan product Polar already holds is renamed in place when the plan's own title differs from it, so a store upgraded from a release that never sent the title stops selling as \"KahunaCart Plan N\" on the hosted page and the card statement; the carrier product's old merchant-facing description is rewritten for the buyer the first time a checkout finds it. A rename Polar refuses never stands between the customer and the checkout\n    * A refund clamped down to what Polar still holds now reports the figure it actually sent, and KahunaCart writes the row from that. The ledger row is opened at the amount the admin typed, and Polar's side can hold less \u2014 a discount the customer took on the hosted page, or a refund the merchant already issued in Polar's dashboard \u2014 so a refund that went out smaller used to leave the books saying money went back that never left: the order flipped to fully refunded, the restock and the download revocation ran, a credit note was issued for the full figure, and the customer was short the difference with nothing anywhere saying so. The refund's gross and tax ride along on the same keys a refund webhook writes, so a refund settled on submission and one settled by webhook now leave the same record behind \u2014 which in a VAT jurisdiction is the only note of what the customer actually got back\n    * Every recurring event carries the Polar customer id as `provider_customer_ref`, which is the key the Subscriptions add-on reads. It was written under `customer`, which nothing looks for, so the local subscription row never got a customer reference and the billing portal button was never drawn \u2014 and Polar's portal is the only place a card can be replaced, so a `past_due` customer had no way to recover and was revoked 21 days later. `customer` is kept beside the new key for the audit trail\n    * The connection check reads a configured checkout product back from Polar and reports the name Polar holds for it. It used to print a constant beside whatever id was configured, so a mistyped, stale or wrong-environment id produced a green light and an invented product name next to an id that does not exist \u2014 and the next real checkout failed with \"Product does not exist\". A missing or archived product now fails the check in a plain sentence, on the screen the merchant is already looking at\n    * A subscription checkout carries `transaction_hash`, as a one-time checkout always has. Without it the webhook has to attach the money to the order's most recent pending purchase row, which is the wrong row the moment a customer has two attempts open \u2014 and a customer whose first attempt was refused is exactly that customer\n    * `initiatePayment()` and `refund()` refuse an empty access token in plain words instead of making a real unauthenticated call and handing back Polar's \"Unauthorized\". The connection check and webhook setup already did; one guard on the request path makes the whole driver behave the same way\n    * A JSON list body is no longer read as an event. `[1,2,3]` decodes to a PHP array and walked straight past the \"is this an object\" gate, arriving as a 200 with nothing done; `array_is_list()` says what that gate meant\n    * Tidying up at Polar can no longer fail a checkout. Two housekeeping calls run while a checkout session is being created, rewriting the carrier product's old merchant-facing description and renaming a plan product whose title has changed, and both talked to Polar without catching a transport failure. A product endpoint that was briefly slow while the checkout endpoint was fine, which is the ordinary shape of a partial outage, meant the shopper waited out the connect timeout and was told the store could not reach Polar. No session was ever attempted, and the sale was lost again on the next try, for a cosmetic rewrite nobody asked for\n    * `revoke_benefits` is decided by KahunaCart, not hard-coded true and not worked out from the driver's view of one Polar order. A local order can be paid by more than one Polar charge, and the driver only ever sees the one it is refunding, so a refund that emptied one charge of two read as \"this empties the order\": Polar pulled the customer's licence keys, downloads and Discord access while KahunaCart still showed the line as delivered and the order as only partly refunded. The store said the customer still had the goods and Polar said they did not. The base plugin now hands the driver a `RefundRequest` carrying `exhaustsBalance`, which is whether this refund takes the last of the local order's refundable balance, and that alone decides the flag. A caller that cannot say revokes nothing\n    * A verified delivery whose `type` is a JSON array no longer answers 500. Casting an array to a string is a warning on a production error handler and an exception on a store with debug on, so the same delivery was read as the literal word \"Array\" on one store and answered 500 on the other, and a 500 is what tells a platform to send it again until it gives up. Anything that is not a string is not an event type, and it is refused as `ignored:malformed`\n    * A JSON list as an event's `data` is no longer announced as a completed payment. A list is an array in PHP, so it walked past the \"is this an object\" gate one level down from the one that already catches it on the envelope. Nothing settled, because there was no hash and no reference on it, but a garbage delivery reading as a payment is noise in the one table a merchant opens when they are already worried about money\n    * The connection check fails on a store with no webhook secret instead of passing with a warning. Checkout works perfectly without one and then no paid order ever settles: the customer pays, Polar takes the money, every delivery is rejected, and the order sits in the cart for ever. The sentence was already the clearest one in the driver and it is unchanged, but `ok` is what the admin badge is drawn from, and that badge was green on a store that could take money and could not record it. Everything the check did prove still shows beside the refusal\n    * A plan's Polar product carries its price and interval in the name on every mint, not only on the invented fallback. A repricing cannot reuse the old product, so it mints a second one, and the merchant was left with two rows in their own Polar catalogue both called \"Wave Log Spreadsheet\" with only a metadata key telling them apart, which Polar's dashboard does not show. Every later repricing added another. Names now read \"Wave Log Spreadsheet ($7.00 \/ month)\", and the lookup is unchanged: a product is still found by the `kahunacart_slug` this plugin stamps on it, whatever it ends up called\n    * A configured checkout product id that was never a Polar id is refused in this plugin's own words, without a round trip. Polar answers a malformed id with its own schema complaint about UUIDs and invalid characters, and that was repeated to the merchant on top of this plugin's sentence, telling them about Polar's validator instead of about the box they typed in\n    * Polar's UUID complaint no longer reaches the merchant on the refund path either. A refund against a reference Polar does not recognize answered \"Polar rejected the checkout details: Input should be a valid UUID, invalid character: found `v` at 10\", which names Polar's schema validator and a call nobody made. It now reads \"That does not look like a Polar id\", and every other refusal on that path names the order lookup it came from rather than a checkout\n    * A scheduled pause dates itself from Polar's period end rather than the store's. Polar leaves `pauses_at` null until the pause actually happens, so the driver read no date at all and the store fell back to its own `current_period_end`. On a store whose copy had drifted a month that meant telling the customer billing paused on 3 November while Polar was going to pause on 3 October. The scheduling flag plus the period end in the same PATCH response is Polar's own answer and is what is reported now, and a scheduled cancellation with no `ends_at` reads the same way\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * Polar's refusals are written for the person reading them. A rejected access token said `invalid_token` on the merchant's settings screen, and a checkout Polar would not accept handed the shopper its raw Pydantic validation list \u2014 the same complaint three times over, naming Polar's own schema fields. Now a token refusal says to check the token on the settings screen, an email address Polar will not take says exactly that, and any other validation list is cut to its first message. A refund that cannot read its Polar order names the order it could not read, instead of answering \"Refund failed: Not found\". Anything Polar already wrote in plain English is passed through untouched\n    * The carrier product's description is written for the buyer. Polar's hosted page prints it under the amount, so every shopper paying through the store was reading a note to the merchant \u2014 \"Carries checkout payments from this KahunaCart store. Each order overrides the price below.\" \u2014 that mentions a price they cannot see. It now names the store, taking the name from the invoice settings and falling back to the site title\n    * Catalog sync leaves two kinds of Polar product alone. This plugin's own private products \u2014 the carrier every cart is charged against, and the recurring product minted for each plan and again on each repricing \u2014 were pulled in as ordinary catalog rows, so a fresh store's first checkout dropped a draft \"KahunaCart Order\" priced at its $1 placeholder into the merchant's catalog. And a genuinely recurring Polar product pulled in as a one-time item, because the normalized product structure carries no interval: a $5\/month plan became a $5 thing a shopper buys once and is never billed for again\n    * A rejected webhook says why in the store's webhook log: `no_secret_configured`, `missing_headers`, `stale_timestamp`, `bad_signature`, `unparseable_body` or `not_an_object`, because a wrong secret and a clock that has drifted are different jobs and both used to read as \"rejected\". The reason is always a code this driver chose; nothing from the request is repeated into it\n    * A Polar outage fails fast. The connect timeout drops from 30 seconds to 5, inside an unchanged 30-second total, so a checkout submit making three sequential calls on a cold request no longer holds a customer's POST for a minute and a half against a `max_execution_time` that is commonly 30 or 60. A slow answer is still an answer; only the wait for a machine that is not there is cut\n    * An `order.paid` whose order says it is not paid is ignored rather than settling the order on the strength of the event's name, and the refusal names itself and names the order. Polar will not send one, but this driver is the reference the others are written against\n    * A refund this store submits carries the order's metadata, so the order and transaction hashes are on the refund and a `refund.updated` redelivered after a crash can find the row it settles. A refund issued in Polar's own dashboard still cannot be matched, because Polar copies nothing onto it \u2014 that stays documented as the limitation it is\n    * The base plugin dependency moves to 1.2.0, which is the release that gives `RefundResult` a settled amount and a payload and `WebhookResult::invalid()` a reason \u2014 the three things this release reports through\n    * The README covers the refund amount rule, benefit revocation, the customer reference, what the connection check now proves, what catalog sync skips and why, and what Polar's refusals say. It also records that Polar publishes no event at all for a declined card, so an order that was attempted and refused looks the same as a cart nobody tried to pay\n    * `no_secret_configured` is kept apart from `missing_headers`. An empty signing secret is the store's own setting and not something the sender did, it is the commonest reason a fresh store's paid orders never settle, and the merchant reading that log row needs to be sent to the settings screen rather than off looking at what Polar posted\n    * Refunds report the payment they are against as `payment_ref` as well as `order_id`, on both the create call and the refund events. `payment_ref` is the contract's own name for that value and the first key the base plugin tries when a refund arrives with no local row to match, and no shipped driver was sending it, so the name the contract tells a new driver to use was satisfied by nothing in the tree\n    * A second attempt at a cart no longer tries to close the session the first one opened, and the README and the provider contract now say why. Polar has no way to expire a checkout: `DELETE \/v1\/checkouts\/{id}` answers 405, `PATCH` takes the request and ignores a `status` key, and a session runs to its own `expires_at` about an hour out. The call this driver was making did nothing, silently, and passed its own unit test while doing it, because a returned 405 is not a thrown error. What catches a customer who finishes the old page is KahunaCart's superseded-row bookkeeping, which does work: the retired row still settles a late webhook so the money is recorded, the order history and a merchant notice name both references, and the refundable balance counts both charges so the second can be handed back\n    * A scheduled pause or cancellation reports Polar's own date for it. The store used to work the date out from its copy of the period end, which can drift, so a customer's account page could say billing pauses on the 14th beside a card statement showing a payment on the 15th. That disagreement is what turns into a chargeback rather than a support ticket. Polar does not fill in `pauses_at` until the pause actually happens, so a freshly scheduled pause is read from `pause_at_period_end` plus the `current_period_end` in the same response, which is the platform's own date and not the store's copy of it\n    * The driver declares the address fields Polar will not complete a checkout without: a state for the US and Canada, which is what its tax calculation needs. KahunaCart only asks for a full address when the cart holds something physical, so a shopper buying a single download sailed through the store's checkout, typed their card in, pressed Pay, and got a bare \"This field is required\" under an empty State box on a page the merchant does not control\n\n3. [](#new)\n    * An **Instructions** tab in the plugin settings, after General, that walks a merchant through setup in order: the account or sandbox, which credential to copy and from where, saving it, the webhook (button or manual steps with the endpoint URL and the events), test cards or sandbox accounts, and what to check afterwards\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * `captureMode()` is declared explicitly as `Automatic`, and there is deliberately no setting to change it. Polar's API is products, prices, checkouts, customers, subscriptions, orders, benefits, license keys, the customer portal, usage meters and webhooks \u2014 no authorisation resource, no capture call, no void call. Polar is the merchant of record and Stripe is *its* processor rather than the store's, so the only party who could authorise without capturing is Polar itself and it does not offer that to a seller; by the time an order exists to read back the money has moved and the tax has been computed and remitted against it. `Authorize`, `Capture` and `Void` are undeclared with it, so KahunaCart never draws a Capture or Void button on a Polar order\n    * `encryptedSettings()` names both access tokens and both webhook signing secrets, so KahunaCart stores all four encrypted against a key that lives in the store's database rather than in any file. A token can spend from the organization and a secret would let anyone holding it sign an event this plugin cannot tell from a real one. Both environments are named rather than only the active one, so a store that went live does not leave its sandbox credentials in plaintext beside the encrypted production ones. A value written in plaintext before this reads correctly and is rewritten encrypted on the next save, with nothing for a merchant to do. The organization ids and checkout product ids are left in the clear on purpose \u2014 neither is a secret, an organization id appears in Polar's own dashboard URLs, and both are what a merchant reads back when an order lands against the wrong product\n    * The README says plainly what this driver does not claim, and why `Subscriptions` being declared is not a contradiction. Polar does keep a card on file and charges it, but on a calendar it runs itself: there is no vault a payment method could be stored in from here, no method id to hand back, and no way to charge one on this store's own initiative, so `Tokenization` and `OffSessionCharge` stay undeclared. Its checkout takes wallets, but only on Polar's own hosted page after the redirect, so there is no SDK to mount an express button with either\n    * The base plugin dependency moves to 1.1.0, which is the release that introduced `captureMode()`, `encryptedSettings()` and the `CaptureMode` enum this driver now imports\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"08\/31\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Webhook setup now repairs an endpoint it adopts instead of only reporting on it. A store that pressed **Set up webhook** before the subscription events were added has an endpoint subscribed to a list that has since fallen behind, and nothing about that store looks broken \u2014 checkout works, orders settle, and only the newer events go unheard \u2014 so being told which ones are missing is no use when the button that could add them is the one that just declined to. An adopted endpoint whose events differ from the set this provider handles is now updated to exactly that set through Polar's `PATCH \/v1\/webhooks\/endpoints\/{id}`, which replaces the list wholesale, and the summary names what was added and anything dropped that this provider never handled. Only `events` is sent, so the endpoint keeps its URL, and the secret is untouched \u2014 Polar regenerates one only through a call of its own, `PATCH \/v1\/webhooks\/endpoints\/{id}\/secret` \u2014 which means deliveries already in flight stay verifiable and the stored secret is still the right one. An update Polar refuses fails the setup with Polar's own reason rather than reporting success because an endpoint exists; an endpoint listed without an id, which cannot be addressed, is still adopted as it stands with its gaps named\n    * A completed Polar order reports `amount_minor` only when Polar named an amount. It is still the order's `net_amount` \u2014 the store's own takings, Polar being merchant of record \u2014 and a zero-total `subscription_create` order, which is what a free trial produces, is now recorded as a `0` rather than settling the pending ledger row at the full plan price. An order naming neither a net nor a total leaves the key off entirely, so the base plugin falls back to the amount the row was opened with instead of reading silence as a free sale\n    * Recurring billing. Polar declares the `Subscriptions` capability and implements `RecurringBillingInterface`, so a KahunaCart plan priced through Polar is billed by Polar: it advances the period, charges the stored card, retries a failed renewal on its own schedule, and reports each step to the one webhook endpoint this provider already owns. Each plan gets a private recurring Polar product created on first checkout and recorded back on the plan \u2014 checked before it is reused, because a merchant who reprices a plan in KahunaCart would otherwise leave that id pointing at a product still charging the old figure, and a mismatch mints a fresh product rather than repricing the one every existing subscriber sits on. All four of KahunaCart's intervals are Polar intervals and Polar counts them, so nothing has to be refused for want of a counterpart; trials are sent per checkout, where they override whatever the Polar product carries, and a plan with no trial says so explicitly so a dashboard-configured trial cannot be given away. Cancel at period end and cancel now, real pause and resume \u2014 Polar has both now, and pausing is scheduled for period end because the customer has already paid for the period they are in \u2014 with resume doubling as the uncancel, since the base contract has no `uncancelSubscription()`: a scheduled cancellation is undone with Polar's own plain `cancel_at_period_end: false` update and no `revoke` in it, one intent per request because Polar's update schemas are mutually exclusive, and a subscription with nothing to undo is refused in plain words rather than in Polar's \u2014 plus a customer portal link minted per visit for replacing a card, which is the only way a past-due subscription recovers\n    * Renewals are recorded in the same net currency as every other Polar amount. Polar is merchant of record for a renewal exactly as it is for a one-time sale, so the base plugin's tax stage never runs for one and the ledger holds pre-tax figures throughout; a renewal is therefore reported as Polar's `net_amount`, with its tax beside it as `tax_minor` and the customer's charge as `gross_amount_minor`. A renewal is also read from `order.paid` and never from `order.created` or `subscription.cycled`, both of which fire before the money has arrived \u2014 and a renewal delivery never reports a payment against the original order, which matters because Polar copies a subscription's metadata onto every order it raises and that first order's hash rides along on all of them\n    * Failed renewals, without a failed-payment event to read them from. Polar publishes none; what it publishes is a subscription moving to `past_due` and an unpaid renewal order left open with `next_payment_attempt_at` set to the next retry, so that is what a decline is read from. When the retries run out Polar revokes the subscription and says nothing about why, so the cause is inferred: a subscription nobody cancelled that had entered `past_due` is reported as expired, and everything else \u2014 a scheduled cancellation reaching its date, a stated reason, a merchant's immediate revoke \u2014 as cancelled. A customer who chose to leave is never reported as having lapsed\n    * The webhook setup button now registers the recurring events alongside the payment ones \u2014 `order.updated` and the six `subscription.*` events the provider acts on \u2014 whether or not the store sells plans today, so a store that starts selling them later has nothing to come back and tick. `subscription.updated` and `subscription.cycled` are deliberately not among them: Polar announces most transitions twice, once on the catch-all and once on an event that says which change it was, and acting on both would report every transition twice\n    * The checkout now carries who the customer is, not just their email. `external_customer_id` is the KahunaCart customer id, so a Polar customer can be matched back to this store without guessing at an address; `customer_name` and `customer_billing_address` hand over what the customer already typed. The address matters beyond saving them typing it twice \u2014 Polar is merchant of record, so the country on it is where its tax calculation starts, and two records disagreeing about that is a tax position assembled from a guess. Polar normalises regions to ISO 3166-2 itself (`AZ` becomes `US-AZ`). US customers are still asked to confirm a full address whatever we send, which is Polar's own requirement\n    * Reset support and stale-endpoint reporting for webhook setup, plus the fix that made deletion work at all: a `204 No Content` \u2014 which is exactly what Polar answers a `DELETE` with \u2014 was read as a failed connection, so a deletion that had already succeeded was reported as an outage and the caller would have retried against something already gone. Empty bodies now only mean an outage when the status is 0\n    * Polar registers its own webhook endpoint. One button creates it with this store's URL and all six events the provider actually handles \u2014 `order.paid`, `order.refunded`, `refund.created`, `refund.updated`, `product.created`, `product.updated` \u2014 and keeps the signing secret Polar returns. Polar's `WebhookEndpointCreate` accepts no secret of ours, but the endpoint it answers with carries the one it generated, which removes the copy-paste rather than moving it. Pressing it again adopts the endpoint already registered for that URL instead of creating a second one that would deliver everything twice, and an adopted endpoint subscribed to the wrong events is brought up to date (above). Needs `webhooks:write` on the token\n    * **Check connection** in the KahunaCart admin's Payments section. It walks what a sale walks \u2014 resolve the organization, then find or create the carrier product \u2014 so it exercises the token, the `organizations:read` \/ `products:read` \/ `products:write` scopes, and the account the token is actually pointed at, then reports each of those back. A store with no webhook secret set passes with a warning rather than a clean bill: checkout works perfectly without one and then no paid order ever settles, which is exactly the failure worth catching before a customer finds it\n    * `organization_id` is no longer sent with an organization token (`polar_oat_\u2026`). Polar rejects the combination outright \u2014 \"Setting organization_id is disallowed when using an organization token\" \u2014 so the field is now omitted for those, including when a merchant has configured one, since a configured id cannot override what the token is already scoped to. Personal and OAuth tokens are unaffected and still send the discovered or configured organization. Found by the new connection check on its first real run\n    * Setup is now an access token and a webhook secret. The organization is discovered by asking Polar which organizations the token can act on, so `{mode}_organization_id` can stay empty \u2014 which, with the carrier product already found-or-created on first sale, leaves nothing else to copy out of a dashboard. A token reaching several organizations is the one case that still has to be answered by hand: KahunaCart refuses to guess and names the candidates, because picking wrong would sell through the wrong catalogue, the wrong payouts and the wrong tax registration. A token that cannot list organizations at all is unaffected \u2014 that is what an organization-scoped token looks like, and the product endpoints read the organization off the token. Wants the `organizations:read` scope\n    * `DigitalGoodsOnly` capability: Polar has no shipping, no delivery address and no physical fulfilment, so checkout no longer offers it for a cart containing anything that has to be posted\n    * Initial release: first-party Polar provider for KahunaCart, the reference hosted merchant-of-record + two-way sync implementation\n    * Hosted checkout flow \u2014 `initiatePayment()` creates a Polar checkout session priced with an ad-hoc fixed price equal to the order total, so any cart checks out without per-product mapping\n    * The carrier product is either configured per environment or resolved once from a private \"KahunaCart Order\" product the plugin finds by metadata and creates on first use\n    * `MerchantOfRecord` capability: Polar computes and remits tax at checkout, so KahunaCart's own tax estimate steps aside\n    * Webhook handling with self-contained Standard Webhooks verification (HMAC-SHA256 over `id.timestamp.body`, base64 signatures, 300s replay window in both directions, constant-time comparison, no SDK)\n    * Event mapping for `order.paid`, `refund.created`, `refund.updated`, `product.created`, and `product.updated`\n    * Full and partial refunds through `\/v1\/refunds\/`, with benefit revocation for one-time purchases. Polar's `amount` is net of tax \u2014 its ceiling, `Order.refundable_amount`, is documented as \"net, before taxes\" \u2014 and so is a merchant-of-record order's local total, so the amount passes through unconverted. The order is read first and the request clamped to what Polar says is still refundable, so a Polar-side discount code or a refund issued in Polar's dashboard cannot turn a full refund into a rejected one\n    * Refund and payment webhooks report the net amount to the ledger, with Polar's tax carried beside it as `refunded_tax_minor` \/ `tax_minor` and the customer-facing total as `gross_amount_minor`. Reporting net + tax would overstate a dashboard-issued refund by the tax on a ledger that never held it, and enough of those would push the refunded total past the captured total, mark the order fully refunded, and revoke the customer's downloads with money still owed back\n    * Async refund lifecycle: a refund Polar answers `pending` is reported as pending and settled later by `refund.updated`, keyed on the same refund uuid the refund call returned; `failed` and `canceled` release the held amount for a retry\n    * `order.refunded` is no longer acted on \u2014 it carries the order, so the only reference it can name is the Polar order id, which is the payment row's reference rather than any refund's\n    * Two-way catalog sync: `pushProduct()` creates or updates a Polar product idempotently by `kahunacart_slug` metadata, `pullProducts()` walks the paginated catalog into normalized product arrays\n    * Sandbox\/production toggle with separate access token, webhook secret, organization id, and checkout product per environment; the provider registers only when the active environment's token is set\n    * Unit test suite covering signature verification, event mapping, checkout parameter building, product push\/pull request and response mapping, and transport-failure degradation"}}},"kahunacart-paypal":{"name":"KahunaCart PayPal","version":"1.1.3","description":"PayPal payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, webhooks, and refunds","description_html":"<p>PayPal payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, webhooks, and refunds<\/p>","description_plain":"PayPal payments for KahunaCart \u2014 hosted checkout, webhooks, and refunds","icon":"paypal","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.1.0"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/paypal","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, payments, paypal, checkout","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-paypal","license":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/premium\/license","tag_name":"1.1.3","date":"2026-09-25T16:07:28Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-paypal\/1.1.3","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-paypal"},"slug":"kahunacart-paypal","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-paypal","content_sha256":"66fe6d2c0112f4820131c383af1ff396a35fe4072e3b3e337f411654665e225d","changelog":{"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-paypal`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-paypal`\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * A **Checkout Label** setting, like every other driver has: the customer-facing name of the payment option, empty for the default \"PayPal\"\n    * An **Instructions** tab in the plugin settings, after General, that walks a merchant through setup in order: the account or sandbox, which credential to copy and from where, saving it, the webhook (button or manual steps with the endpoint URL and the events), test cards or sandbox accounts, and what to check afterwards\n    * **Set up webhook** and **Reset webhook** on the PayPal card in the admin. `PayPalProvider` implements `ProviderWebhookSetupInterface`, so the button registers this store's endpoint on the REST app, subscribes it to the ten events the driver acts on, and writes the webhook ID back into the active mode's field. PayPal's equivalent of a signing secret is that ID, and unlike a Stripe signing secret it can be read back at any time, so the ID is stored whether the webhook was created or adopted \u2014 a store that lost its config gets it back by pressing the button\n    * The button asks PayPal which event types the app is offered before it registers anything. PayPal refuses the whole call over one name it does not recognise, and `event_types[6].name \/ Not a valid event name` gives a merchant no way to tell which of ten names it meant, so an unavailable event is left out and named in the result instead of failing the registration\n    * Pressing it twice adopts the webhook already registered for the URL rather than creating a second one that would deliver everything twice, and patches in any of the ten events it is missing. Events the merchant subscribed it to themselves are kept \u2014 the patch sends the union, not this driver's list\n    * **Reset webhook** deletes the webhook at this exact URL and registers a fresh one. Webhooks on this store's route at a *different* address, which is what a site that moved off a tunnel leaves behind, are reported and never deleted: one REST app is commonly shared between a staging store and a live one\n    * `WEBHOOK_URL_ALREADY_EXISTS` and `WEBHOOK_NUMBER_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` are translated into something a merchant can act on, rather than passed through as PayPal wrote them\n    * **Check connection** on the PayPal card. `PayPalProvider` implements `ProviderConnectionInterface`: it asks PayPal for a token with the saved credentials \u2014 the same call every other request makes first \u2014 reports the app id they belong to, and names the refund or webhook-management scope when an app is missing one. There is no second \"read my merchant account\" call because PayPal has none for a plain REST app\n    * The check reads the configured webhook ID back from PayPal, and fails on a store that has none or has one PayPal does not recognise. That is the state where checkout works, the customer's money moves and nothing settles, and a sandbox webhook ID left in the live fields is the easiest way into it. It also fails, naming them, when the webhook is not subscribed to every event the driver handles\n    * Two events joined the registered set that the docs only ever suggested: `PAYMENT.AUTHORIZATION.CREATED` and `PAYMENT.AUTHORIZATION.VOIDED` are now registered whether or not the store is on manual capture, so switching capture mode later needs nothing added at PayPal\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * The webhook ID is no longer declared a secret, and the docblock that claimed an attacker could forge with it is corrected. PayPal's verification call is authenticated with the merchant's own OAuth token, so the ID alone forges nothing and gets PayPal to sign nothing \u2014 it is the equivalent of a Stripe endpoint's `we_\u2026` id, which Stripe's driver prints, rather than of a signing secret, which it does not. The driver was calling it a secret and then rendering it on the connection check, in the webhook setup result and on the CLI, and of the two halves that is the one worth keeping: a merchant needs to read the ID back and compare it against their dashboard. `encryptedSettings()` now names the two client secrets and nothing else\n    * The PayPal order carries the cart's own lines and a description, so the approval page and the buyer's own PayPal record name what was bought rather than only an amount \u2014 the record they will look at in six weeks, and the one PayPal shows in a dispute. The lines are only sent when the arithmetic is honest: PayPal validates the breakdown against the charge and refuses the whole order when it does not add up, so a cart discount goes on as `discount` and a cart whose lines come to *less* than the charge \u2014 tax or shipping added on top, and nothing says which \u2014 sends the description alone rather than labelling somebody's VAT as a handling fee\n    * A refusal a shopper or a merchant reads is written for them. The refusals a real store meets \u2014 an unsupported currency, a declined funding instrument, a restricted account, a refund larger than PayPal has left \u2014 are translated into a sentence naming what to do next, and everything else falls back to PayPal's own sentence with the `debug_id` stripped. The debug id is kept on the connection check and the webhook buttons, which are the screens a merchant pastes into a support ticket\n    * Every `invalid()` and `ignored()` carries a reason, in the contract's own vocabulary. A merchant whose orders stop settling used to open the delivery log and find a column of bare `invalid` with nothing to tell \"our webhook ID is wrong\", which is on their own settings screen, from \"somebody found the endpoint and is posting junk\", which needs no action. `no_secret_configured` earns its place on this driver in particular, because the ID is typed by hand\n    * A JSON array at the top level is refused before the verification call rather than after it, so a stranger posting one cannot spend an outbound call to PayPal to find out it was ignored\n    * Payment and refund events carry the payment reference under `payment_ref` and `order_id`, the names the base plugin reads, so a delivery can be joined to its order in the log. Across the September 2026 sandbox run, 228 PayPal deliveries reached a store and three of them carried an order id\n    * An amount PayPal sent that this plugin will not parse is kept on the payload as `amount_unparsable` rather than being dropped. \"PayPal named no amount\" and \"PayPal named one I could not read\" used to be the same answer, after which the row settled at the figure it was opened with and nothing anywhere said so\n    * A settled refund reports what PayPal said it refunded, and keeps PayPal's `seller_payable_breakdown` \u2014 gross, fee, net, and the running total refunded against the capture. Those are the figures a VAT return and a reconciliation want and they cannot be recovered later; the same refund settled by the webhook has always kept them, so a refund recorded different things depending on which leg got there first\n    * The currency on a webhook payload comes back uppercased, which the contract asks for and PayPal happened to satisfy by accident\n    * A capture PayPal refuses names the issue in the message rather than repeating PayPal's field vocabulary, and a refusal that repeats this store's own credentials back at us \u2014 which PayPal does not do today, and is free to start doing \u2014 is replaced rather than rendered\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **HUF and TWD were charged a hundred times over.** The exponent table was doing two jobs at once: how many decimals PayPal accepts, and how many minor units KahunaCart counts in a major unit. Those are the same number for almost every currency and they are not for these two \u2014 ISO 4217 gives both an exponent of 2, which is what the ledger uses, and PayPal transacts both in whole units. So a store meaning 9,74 Ft asked PayPal for 974 forint, PayPal held 974, and the settlement parsed it back at the same wrong exponent and agreed with itself, so every mismatch check in the base passed and the only place the real figure existed was PayPal's dashboard. Refunds repeated it exactly. There are now two tables and a conversion between them. An amount PayPal cannot represent at all \u2014 1 000,50 Ft, which a percentage coupon on a HUF store produces \u2014 is **refused with a sentence** rather than silently rounded to 1 000 or 1 001\n    * **A refund event named the payment on `capture_id`, and the base plugin only reads `payment_ref`.** The value was always right \u2014 the driver digs the capture id out of the refund's `up` link \u2014 so every PayPal refund event was logged `refund_completed:unmatched`, a refund issued in PayPal's own dashboard never reached the order, and a refund whose response was lost stranded a pending row forever. `payment_ref` is now the name, with `capture_id` beside it because it is PayPal's own word for the same id\n    * **A capture PayPal refused vanished.** `CHECKOUT.ORDER.APPROVED` fires the capture call, and the result was thrown away and the delivery answered a bare `ignored`. An `INSTRUMENT_DECLINED` in the seconds between approval and capture is an ordinary PayPal outcome, and it left an order sitting in the cart table with no failed transaction, no notice, no history, no log line, and no second delivery coming \u2014 because 200 is the one answer that stops PayPal asking again. The three outcomes are now told apart: captured answers `ignored:captured` carrying the PayPal order id; a refusal is reported as the payment failure it is, so the pending row is marked failed and the order's timeline says what happened; and a call that never landed, or a PayPal 5xx, throws, so the base plugin answers 500 and PayPal redelivers\n    * **A capture PayPal refused was believed too readily, and the belief cost the sale.** The fix above read any 4xx as final, and that is not what a 4xx means. Twice in the September 2026 sandbox run PayPal answered one to a capture that the very next call completed normally, on properly approved orders \u2014 and a refusal fails the purchase row, tells the shopper their payment method was turned down, and is answered 200, so PayPal never asks again and a sale that was fine is lost while the store believes it handled everything correctly. Finality is now named rather than inferred: the driver refuses only on the issues PayPal's reference documents as refusals of the payment itself \u2014 the funding instrument, the payer, this store's ability to take the money, the amount or currency, and the order's own state \u2014 and reads anything else as worth another delivery. `PERMISSION_DENIED` is on the retry side on purpose: it means the REST app is missing a scope, which is a mistake in the configuration rather than anything about the payment, and a merchant who fixes it still has three days of PayPal redeliveries in which the money can move\n    * **A refused capture left nothing behind that said what PayPal had answered.** PayPal's status and issue went only into the sentence written for the shopper, which by design carries neither \u2014 so the two refusals above could not be identified afterwards, and the next occurrence would have been no more diagnosable than they were. Both outcomes that are not the money moving now put `HTTP 422, INSTRUMENT_DECLINED` and the debug id on the delivery-log row, which is the table a merchant already opens when an order did not settle. The shopper's sentence is unchanged and still carries no debug id\n    * **PayPal's raw validation text and a debug id reached a shopper.** With the store currency set to one PayPal does not take, a customer read \"If the currency supports decimals, only two decimal place precision is supported. (PayPal debug id: f34810357889a)\" \u2014 a sentence about a JSON field, shown to somebody holding a basket, that was not even right about the cause\n    * `REFUND_AMOUNT_EXCEEDED` and `CAPTURE_FULLY_REFUNDED` are classified as `RefundResult::REASON_EXCEEDS_BALANCE`, so the base plugin's drift line and its notice on the order both fire. Both mean PayPal holds less on the payment than this store thinks it does, which is what a store looks like after somebody refunded in PayPal's dashboard, and it was the only signal a merchant could get that the two ledgers had come apart\n    * The README and the Instructions tab said a refund issued in PayPal's dashboard could not be matched to an order, and that the Webhooks Simulator does not verify. Both were tested in the September 2026 sandbox run and both were wrong: the dashboard refund was the `capture_id` bug above, and the simulator verifies against the real webhook ID and reaches the event handlers \u2014 its limitation is the canned payload. The \"first six events are the minimum\" sentence was wrong too; the minimum for a store that sells and refunds is `CHECKOUT.ORDER.APPROVED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.COMPLETED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.REFUNDED` and `PAYMENT.REFUND.FAILED`, two of which were outside the six. The README also claimed KahunaCart >= 1.0.0 where the blueprint says 1.1.0, and the blueprint is right\n    * A refund now asks PayPal for the whole object back (`Prefer: return=representation`). PayPal answers a refund with an id and a status and no amount at all, and it silently **clamps** a refund larger than the capture has left rather than refusing it \u2014 so a $5.00 refund against $3.00 remaining came back `201` and the ledger recorded $5.00 as money the merchant never paid back. The row now says what PayPal actually returned, and the seller breakdown arrives in the same response\n    * Settlements in HUF and TWD record their amount again. The read path was parsing against the wire exponent \u2014 how many decimals PayPal accepts **from us**, which is none for those two \u2014 but PayPal is under no such rule and echoes them back with two, so `\"1000.00\"` was refused and a settled forint order recorded no amount at all. Reading is not writing: what counts is the precision the string actually carries, and precision beyond what the store holds is accepted only when it is all zeros, so `19.99` on a yen order is still refused rather than silently rounded\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Two-step capture. `capture_mode: manual` creates the PayPal order with `intent: AUTHORIZE`, so the customer's approval places a hold and the order stands at `authorized` until the merchant presses Capture or Void. `PayPalProvider` implements `AuthorizeCaptureInterface`: `capture()` posts to `\/v2\/payments\/authorizations\/{id}\/capture` with `final_capture` so a partial capture releases the rest rather than leaving it held for another three days, `void()` releases the authorization, and `completeAuthorize()` reads the order PayPal names on the return URL and reports what its authorization actually says\n    * An approval on a manual store calls `\/v2\/checkout\/orders\/{id}\/authorize` rather than `\/capture`, because an order created to be authorized cannot be captured directly and PayPal refuses the attempt. The call is idempotent the same two ways the capture already was \u2014 `PayPal-Request-Id` collapses concurrent retries, and a redelivery that arrives after the first one comes back `ORDER_ALREADY_AUTHORIZED`, which means the hold exists\n    * `PAYMENT.AUTHORIZATION.CREATED` and `PAYMENT.AUTHORIZATION.VOIDED` are translated, so a hold and its release settle the ledger whether they happened here, in PayPal's own dashboard, or by expiry. The authorization carries what PayPal actually held, which is the figure the merchant captures against\n    * A capture PayPal answers `PENDING` is reported as a failure rather than a sale, because a capture under review has moved no money and the completion event settles it when it does\n    * A reference that names the order rather than the authorization is resolved rather than refused, which covers a merchant who captures before the authorization webhook has landed\n    * `encryptedSettings()` names both client secrets and both webhook ids, so KahunaCart stores them encrypted against a key that lives in the store's database and not in any file. The client ids are deliberately excluded: they are not secrets, PayPal prints them into its own SDK tag, and a merchant needs to read one back to check it against their dashboard\n    * The README says plainly what this driver does not claim. PayPal has Vault v3 and it has Smart Payment Buttons, and neither `Tokenization` nor `ExpressPay` is declared because neither is implemented \u2014 KahunaCart draws its buttons from these flags, so a flag claiming something that is not there would put a control in the admin that could never work\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"08\/31\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Initial release: first-party PayPal provider for KahunaCart\n    * Hosted redirect flow on Orders v2 \u2014 `initiatePayment()` creates a PayPal order and redirects to the approval page, with the transaction hash on `custom_id` and the order hash on the purchase unit's `reference_id`\n    * Capture runs from the webhook: `CHECKOUT.ORDER.APPROVED` triggers `POST \/v2\/checkout\/orders\/{id}\/capture`, and the resulting `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.COMPLETED` event completes the order \u2014 no return-URL handler required\n    * Webhook verification through PayPal's `verify-webhook-signature` endpoint, with missing transmission headers, an unconfigured webhook ID, and cert URLs off PayPal's own domains rejected before any call goes out\n    * Event mapping for `CHECKOUT.ORDER.APPROVED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.COMPLETED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.DENIED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.DECLINED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.REVERSED`, `CHECKOUT.ORDER.DECLINED`, `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.REFUNDED`, `PAYMENT.REFUND.FAILED`, and `PAYMENT.REFUND.PENDING`\n    * Full and partial refunds through `\/v2\/payments\/captures\/{id}\/refund`\n    * Async refund lifecycle: a refund PayPal answers `PENDING` is reported as pending and settled later by `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.REFUNDED` or `PAYMENT.REFUND.FAILED`, keyed on the same refund id the refund call returned; `CANCELLED` and `FAILED` on submission are refused rather than counted as refunded\n    * `PAYMENT.CAPTURE.REVERSED` stays a payment failure rather than a refund failure \u2014 PayPal reversing a capture is money pulled back over the store's head, not the failure of a refund the store submitted\n    * OAuth2 client-credentials tokens, cached per request lifecycle and never persisted\n    * Idempotency keys on both order creation and capture, so a redelivered webhook cannot double-charge\n    * Sandbox\/live mode toggle with separate credential sets; the provider registers only when the active mode's client id and secret are both set\n    * Exact integer and string conversion between minor units and PayPal's decimal amounts at each currency's own precision \u2014 zero-decimal (JPY, KRW\u2026), two-decimal, and three-decimal (KWD, BHD\u2026) currencies all convert correctly, with HUF and TWD following PayPal's whole-unit handling rather than ISO 4217, and unrecognised formats rejected rather than rounded\n    * Unit test suite covering webhook verification, event mapping, capture-on-approval, order and refund body building, money conversion, and transport failure"}}},"kahunacart-dummy":{"name":"KahunaCart Dummy","version":"1.2.6","description":"Test payment provider for KahunaCart \u2014 no money moves; the reference implementation for provider developers","description_html":"<p>Test payment provider for KahunaCart \u2014 no money moves; the reference implementation for provider developers<\/p>","description_plain":"Test payment provider for KahunaCart \u2014 no money moves; the reference implementation for provider developers","icon":"flask","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.3.8"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/providers\/dummy","keywords":"grav, commerce, ecommerce, payments, testing, development, provider","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-dummy\/issues","license":"MIT","tag_name":"1.2.6","date":"2026-09-30T16:52:07Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-dummy\/1.2.6","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-dummy"},"slug":"kahunacart-dummy","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-dummy","content_sha256":"6d7c9aa220d8d3ce9fa68b7555abf4bb3d46355e5ed89d389354953117342cee","changelog":{"1.2.6":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The download no longer carries the plugin's tests.** `tests\/` and `phpunit.xml` stay in the repository and out of the release zip, 27 files fewer on every install. Nothing the plugin runs is affected\n"},"1.2.5":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The Test Provider's agreements table sorts by its column headings.** Press a heading to sort by it, and press it again to turn the order round. The whole list is on screen, so it sorts in the browser. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later, which draws the headings; on an older one the headings are plain as before\n"},"1.2.4":{"date":"09\/29\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Act as a merchant of record, for testing a store that will sell through one.** New setting **Act as Merchant of Record** (`merchant_of_record`, off by default). On, the provider declares the `MerchantOfRecord` capability, as Polar does, so a store rehearsing on it behaves the way it will in production: an order paid through it carries no local tax, and on a store where it's the only payment method on offer the cart and checkout say \"Any tax is added when you pay.\" instead of quoting a tax (that part needs the base plugin at 1.3.6). It still charges nobody. Off, nothing changes, because a store testing ordinary payments has to keep computing its own tax. A local site can turn it on from its `.env` without touching tracked config; see [Acting as a merchant of record](README.md#acting-as-a-merchant-of-record)\n"},"1.2.3":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The download no longer carries PHPUnit.** Every release until now committed a development `vendor\/`: PHPUnit and about thirty packages only the test suite uses, some 1,700 files in a plugin that needs no Composer package at run time. `vendor\/` is now a `--no-dev` install holding just the autoloader, and `psr\/http-message` is declared under `replace` because Grav core already supplies it. A test fails if a development install is ever committed again\n"},"1.2.2":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Composer's installed-package record carries the plugin's current package name, `kahunacart\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-dummy`, instead of the old `trilbymedia\/grav-plugin-kahunacart-dummy`\n"},"1.2.1":{"date":"09\/16\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Test express pay actually pays now.** The button drew itself to the store's express style and then linked to `\/checkout`, which on the cart page was a redundant link and on the checkout page was a button that did nothing at all \u2014 a placeholder where a wallet button goes, and a broken button is worse than no button. Pressing it now opens a fake wallet sheet drawn by this plugin's own script and stylesheet, over the page, looking like a wallet sheet rather than like part of the shop: the store's name, the total, a saved contact and address, the store's **real** delivery offers priced for that address with the first one chosen and a price on each, and one Pay button. Choosing a different method reprices the whole order \u2014 postage, tax and all \u2014 because the sheet asks the store rather than adding numbers up itself. **The purchase behind it is not faked.** Pay posts to this plugin's own endpoint, which places the cart through the base plugin's two new express entry points: the sheet's address, the chosen method priced by the store's own shipping engine with carrier rates in it where the shipping add-on is installed, the taxes, the plugin veto, this provider's test payment with whatever `outcome` is configured, and the store's own receipt at the end. An order bought this way is indistinguishable in the admin from one bought on the checkout page. **A store that cannot deliver to the wallet address gets a sentence and no Pay button**, for the same reason the placeholder had to go. The saved details are settings \u2014 **Plugins \u2192 KahunaCart Dummy \u2192 Express Pay** \u2014 defaulting to a US address any US shipping zone will match, so a store selling elsewhere can point them somewhere it covers. **Nothing about a payment passes through any of it**: no card field, no token field and no wallet payload in the button, the sheet or the endpoint, and the sheet prints `Test wallet \u00b7 no charge` where a real one would print a card. With scripting off the button is still a link to checkout. The whole flow is four short files, which is the point \u2014 this is the reference implementation, and a driver author should be able to read it start to finish. **Needs the base plugin at 1.2.10**, which is where `quoteExpressOrder()` and `placeExpressOrder()` come from\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The test express button no longer vanishes on a dark storefront.** It painted itself `#111111` on every theme but `light` and `outline`, so on the dark side of a theme like Kahuna it was a near-black rectangle on a near-black page \u2014 you could tab to it, and that was about the only way to find it. The base plugin's `express.theme` has grown an `auto` value and made it the default, and this button now draws that value the way the provider contract asks a driver with no SDK of its own to draw it: the fill, the label and the edge come from `--kahunacart-express-auto-bg`, `--kahunacart-express-auto-fg` and `--kahunacart-express-auto-border` on the region wrapper, which the storefront stylesheet resolves from the page's own ink and ground. Dark button with a light label on a light page, light button with a dark label on a dark one, and it flips with the page rather than with a setting. `dark` and `light` are unchanged and still absolute \u2014 a merchant who picked one gets exactly that \u2014 and `outline` now follows the page as well: the fill stays transparent, and its border and its label come from `--kahunacart-express-auto-bg`, the page's own foreground, because a black outline on a dark page is as gone as a black slab. This provider is also the reference implementation, so the whole change is three short match blocks anybody writing a real driver can copy. **Needs the base plugin at 1.2.10**, which is where `auto` and the wrapper's colours come from\n"},"1.2.0":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The Test Provider tab has a Settings screen, and it is the plugin's own form.** Every switch this provider has is a blueprint field, and until now reading one meant leaving the KahunaCart admin for the Grav admin's plugin list \u2014 the tab that drives the agreements could not tell you what `capture_mode` was set to, and the banner about unsigned webhooks sent you somewhere else to fix it. The tab now has two screens, **Agreements** and **Settings**, with Settings on a `settings` sub-route of its own so a reload and the Back button land where the address says, and KahunaCart draws the plugin's blueprint form on the second one: the manifest says `settings_plugin`, the screen renders the slot the base plugin forwards into, and the header's Save button saves it. Following **Plugins \u2192 KahunaCart Dummy** in the Grav admin lands here too, and the unsigned-webhooks banner now points at the tab beside it rather than at another page. Against an older base plugin the screen prints one line pointing at the Grav admin's page, so nothing is stranded\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart reset` now empties the fake platform's ledger.** Every row in `kahunacart_dummy_subscriptions` was made by somebody testing, which makes the whole table test data by definition \u2014 and until now a pre-launch reset left it behind, so the fake platform still held live agreements for orders and customers that were gone and the next worker tick renewed them. The `ref` is derived from the order rather than random, so a leftover would also collide with the first real subscription that reused the number. The table is declared on the base plugin's new `onKahunaCartRegisterResetTables` under both the orders and the customers scope, so either flag takes it, and it appears in the plan the command prints with its row count before it asks. Deliberately not behind this plugin's `enabled` switch: the provider ships switched off, and a guard would hide the fake agreements on exactly the store that has finished testing\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Cancel at period end is a switch.** The Test provider section drew it as a bare checkbox; it is the same track-and-knob switch the base admin uses for a standing on\/off setting\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The provider declares the features it understands.** Every demo store has this provider installed, and it declared nothing, so Order versioning and the Catalog index could not be switched on anywhere it was present: the base plugin takes an add-on's silence as \"not ready\". It now declares `order_versioning`, `order_status_registry` and `catalog_index`, which is honest \u2014 it reads no order line, no status string and no catalog table; it is handed a payment context and answers with a result\n\n1. [](#new)\n    * Two-step capture: `capture_mode: manual` makes checkout hold the money instead of taking it, and the order stands at the `authorized` payment status until the merchant presses Capture or Void. `DummyProvider` implements `AuthorizeCaptureInterface` in full \u2014 `authorize()`, `capture()`, `void()` and `completeAuthorize()` \u2014 so a store can walk the whole lifecycle without an account anywhere\n    * `authorize_outcome: captured` reports a capture the platform made despite being asked to hold. Not a simulated bug but a documented rule with a real cause: some payment methods cannot be authorized without being taken, the contract asks a driver to say so in the returned status rather than hide the method, and a store that has never met the branch will meet it the first time somebody pays with one of them\n    * `capture_outcome: fail` and `void_outcome: fail` refuse those operations with fixed messages, so the failure paths \u2014 an authorization that stays live, money that stays held \u2014 can be read before either button is trusted\n    * `payment_authorized` and `payment_voided` webhook actions, so a hold and its release can be driven from curl exactly as a payment and a refund already could. A `payment_completed` quoting a live authorization is read by the store as a capture the merchant made in the platform's own dashboard\n    * Saved payment methods: `TokenizationInterface` with `describePaymentMethod()` and `deletePaymentMethod()`, and all three ways a method reaches the store \u2014 attached to a payment result with `save_method`, carried on a webhook body's `payment_method` block, or asked for through `storePaymentMethod()`. What is kept is a reference and four display fields, and nothing that resembles card data can be stored at all\n    * `token_outcome: forgotten` answers that the platform no longer holds a method, so a stale row is retired rather than offered; `token_outcome: refuse_delete` refuses the removal, which is the loud failure that stops a customer being told a card is gone while the platform can still charge it\n    * Off-session charging: `OffSessionChargeInterface::charge()` folds the caller's idempotency key into the reference it answers with, so a retried renewal produces one reference and one charge. It is the rule a driver most often gets wrong and it costs somebody two charges on one statement\n    * An express-pay button, `dummy-express`, registered into both express zones on `onKahunaCartRegisterExpressButtons`. It draws to exactly the height, radius, theme and minimum width the store's `express.*` settings ask for, so a theme's express region can be laid out and checked against the style contract before a real wallet button exists. Nothing about a payment passes through it: no form, no input, no token field\n    * `captureMode()` answers from the plugin's own config, which is the exception the contract allows and not the rule it warns against \u2014 the warning is about a store-wide key that overrides what a provider says, and this platform genuinely holds money either way\n    * `encryptedSettings()` declared explicitly as nothing, because this provider has no credentials: no API key, no secret key, and deliberately no webhook signing secret. Its docblock carries the two lines a real driver writes instead\n    * Capabilities grew to match: `Authorize`, `Capture`, `Void`, `Tokenization`, `OffSessionCharge` and `ExpressPay` are declared alongside the refund and subscription flags, each because the fake platform genuinely does the thing the flag means. `HostedCheckout`, `MerchantOfRecord` and `DigitalGoodsOnly` are still not declared, for the same reason as before\n    * README sections on two-step capture, saved payment methods and the express button, each with the curl bodies that drive them\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"08\/31\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * Initial release: free test payment provider for KahunaCart, and the reference implementation for provider developers\n    * Config-selectable checkout behavior \u2014 `complete_paid` (instant capture), `complete_pending` (offline-style), `redirect` (bounces to the return URL and waits for a webhook), and `fail` (a fixed simulated decline)\n    * Unsigned webhook endpoint mapping `payment_completed`, `payment_failed`, and `refund_completed` bodies to the matching `WebhookResult`, so the whole payment lifecycle can be driven from curl\n    * Unsigned HTTP posts gated behind both the plugin's `enabled` and a separate `allow_webhooks` switch, each shipping off; a post that arrives while either is off is rejected with a 400\n    * Refunds and partial refunds always succeed, with references derived from the payment reference so an admin refund and a refund webhook agree on one ledger row\n    * Deterministic transaction references derived from the base plugin's transaction hash \u2014 stable across a retry, reproducible in a test, printable in a curl command\n    * Unit test suite covering the outcome matrix, webhook mapping, webhook gating, and refunds\n    * Async refund lifecycle demonstration: `refund_outcome: pending` makes refunds return pending so the ledger flow can be driven end to end, and the webhook endpoint accepts `refund_failed` to exercise the rejection branch\n    * Recurring billing: declares `ProviderCapability::Subscriptions` and implements `RecurringBillingInterface`, keeping its agreements in a table of its own (`kahunacart_dummy_subscriptions`, registered through KahunaCart's migrator) so a whole subscription lifecycle can be walked without a real provider\n    * A hosted checkout page of its own at `\/kahunacart-dummy\/subscribe\/{ref}` \u2014 deliberately outside the storefront and outside the theme, because that is what a hosted checkout is. Pressing Pay fires one webhook carrying the order's payment and the agreement's activation together, the way a first invoice really arrives; Cancel changes nothing. An `outcome: fail` store declines recurring checkout as it declines any other, writing no row and emitting no event\n    * Simulation controls for every event a real platform would eventually send \u2014 `renew`, `fail`, `past-due`, `cancel` (now or at period end), `expire`, `pause`, `resume`, `method-updated` \u2014 as `bin\/plugin kahunacart-dummy subscription <ref> <control>` and as buttons on a new Test Provider tab in the KahunaCart admin, gated on `kahunacart.settings`\n    * Every control delivers through this store's own `POST {route}\/webhook\/dummy` handler and into the same webhook log, so the real dispatch runs and nothing is a private path around the endpoint. In-process by default so it works from cron and CI, and so it needs no `allow_webhooks`; `--url` posts over HTTP for the times when the delivery itself is what is being tested, and meets the gate like any other post\n    * A read-only billing portal at `\/kahunacart-dummy\/portal\/{customerRef}`, so the account page's \"Update payment method\" button can be demonstrated on a store with no payment account anywhere\n    * Billing-calendar arithmetic that clamps rather than overflows: 31 January plus a month is 28 (or 29) February, and periods advance from where the last one closed rather than from the clock, so an event fired late still bills the period it was for\n    * Refuses simulated events a real platform could not have sent \u2014 renewing a cancelled agreement, resuming one with nothing to undo \u2014 so a test can only produce an event stream a store could really have received\n    * `resume` doubles as the uncancel, matching the real drivers and the base contract, which has no `uncancelSubscription()`. On an `active`, `trialing` or `past_due` agreement scheduled to stop at the end of the period it clears the schedule, leaves the status alone, and delivers the `resumed` event with `raw.uncanceled = true`; on a paused one it resumes billing as before; on one that is both, it undoes both. It is refused only when there is genuinely nothing to undo\n    * A `payment_completed` webhook now reports `amount_minor` \u2014 what this provider is pretending to have taken \u2014 and the store settles the pending ledger row at it rather than at the amount the row was opened with. The activation delivery a trial produces carries a `0`, so a free first period records no revenue; a body that names no amount leaves the key off and settles exactly as it always did\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * The hosted subscription checkout and every simulation control now work on a store with `allow_webhooks` off. Those deliveries never leave the process, so they are marked with a PSR-7 request attribute the provider trusts by construction: a marker no HTTP request can carry, because headers arrive over the wire and attributes do not. Pressing \"Pay and subscribe\" on a default store used to move the provider's own row to `active` and then have its activation thrown away at the gate, leaving the store's subscription `incomplete`, the order in the cart, and the shopper on \"Processing your payment\u2026\" forever. `allow_webhooks` still gates unsigned posts arriving over HTTP, `--url` deliveries included, exactly as before\n    * The hosted checkout page names the plan after the product rather than printing `Variant #21`, adding the variant's own title when it says something the product's does not. A variant that cannot be resolved still falls back to its id"}}},"kahunacart-licenses":{"name":"KahunaCart Licenses","version":"1.0.11","description":"License keys for KahunaCart \u2014 issuance, activation seats, updates entitlement, and a public validation API","description_html":"<p>License keys for KahunaCart \u2014 issuance, activation seats, updates entitlement, and a public validation API<\/p>","description_plain":"License keys for KahunaCart \u2014 issuance, activation seats, updates entitlement, and a public validation API","icon":"key","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.3.8"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/license-keys","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/selling\/licenses","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, licensing, license-keys, activation, updates","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-licenses","license":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/premium\/license","tag_name":"1.0.11","date":"2026-10-02T01:04:41Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-licenses\/1.0.11","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/license-keys","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-licenses","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-licenses","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-licenses"},"slug":"kahunacart-licenses","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-licenses","content_sha256":"2cb4c4e401cdf7650151bf396337ccd6cf89a5bd5e2aa8930cfad157467ed2ce","changelog":{"1.0.11":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Help text on the issue-a-key, key-details and product-rule forms now follows the admin's Help text setting.** It shows below the label or in a tooltip behind an info icon\n"},"1.0.10":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The download no longer carries the plugin's tests.** `tests\/` and `phpunit.xml` stay in the repository and out of the release zip, 104 files fewer on every install. Nothing the plugin runs is affected\n"},"1.0.9":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The tables in the Licenses screens sort by their column headings.** Press a heading on the keys list, on a key's activations and add-ons, or on the licensed products table to sort by it, and press it again to turn the order round. The keys list sorts on the server, so a heading orders every key that matches the search and not only the page on screen, and pressing one goes back to page one. `GET \/kahunacart\/licenses` takes `sort` (`key`, `project`, `product`, `status`, `seats`, `customer`, `order` or `expires`) and `dir`, a key with nothing in that column sorts last whichever way it runs, and any other `sort` is ignored, so the list is newest first as before. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later, which draws the headings\n"},"1.0.8":{"date":"09\/29\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A bundle's key validates for every package the bundle ships.** A key covered its included packages on the licence card, in licence files and in stamped zips, but `\/validate` with `product` only passed for the key's own product and the add-ons bought against it, so a bundle key asked about one of its packages came back 409 `product_mismatch`. `product` now passes for any slug the key covers: its own product, its add-ons, and the Included packages on either product's rule, including a package that is only sold inside bundles and has no catalog row at all. `\/activate` checks `product` the same way. A slug the key does not cover is refused exactly as before, and every existing field and status code is unchanged. See docs\/api.md\n    * **`\/validate` answers `covers`, the sorted list of every slug the key is good for,** so a package manager or an installer holding one key for a bundle learns in one call which packages it may install\n    * **A licence check without an HTTP call.** Code running on the same site as the store, such as a download proxy or a package repository, calls `KahunaCartLicenses::instance()->validator()->check($key, $product)` and gets exactly the status and `data` the `\/validate` route would answer, `covers` included, with no rate limit and no second PHP worker tied up calling itself. `validate()` beside it returns the payload and throws the refusal instead. The route answers through the same object, so the two cannot drift apart. See \"Checking a key from the same site\" in docs\/api.md\n    * **Your own servers can skip the per-address rate limit.** A server that checks keys on its customers' behalf sends every check from one address, so the per-address bucket capped all of its customers together at 120 a minute. List such servers, as IPs or CIDR ranges, under **Licenses \u2192 Settings \u2192 Rate Limits \u2192 Your Own Servers** (`api.rate_limit.trusted`). They skip the per-address bucket and keep a per-key one at a limit of their own, 600 a minute by default (`api.rate_limit.per_minute_trusted`). Trust is decided on the address the store resolved, never on a header the caller sent. New strings `API_RATE_LIMIT_TRUSTED`, `API_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE_TRUSTED` and their help text. See \"Validating from your own server\" in docs\/api.md\n    * **Keys from another system can be imported as they are.** `Service\\LicenseImport` (through `KahunaCartLicenses::instance()->licenseImport()`) writes a licence with a key the customer already holds, in any layout, with its product, customer, order, status, dates, seats, issuer, external id, meta and original issue date. It fires no events, sends no email and writes no order. It is idempotent on the issuer and external id, so it can be run again and brings rows in line with the source, and a key that is already here under another identity is reported as a conflict rather than overwritten. It is the piece an import command or a CSV importer builds on; there is no admin screen for it. See \"Importing keys from another system\" in docs\/admin.md\n    * **Introductions can pluralise.** `{one|many}` in the email or Licenses-tab introduction picks the first form above one key and the second above several, so `Your {license key is|license keys are} below. Keep {it|them} somewhere safe.` reads right however many keys an order carries. The default email introduction now uses it. See \"Placeholders\" in docs\/storefront.md\n    * **A product rule's key format can say \"no prefix\".** The rule editor's custom key format has a **Prefix** choice, and **No prefix** drops the store's prefix from that product's keys, so a product can mint new keys that look like the ones another system issued (four blocks of eight hex characters, for example) while the rest of the store keeps its own. Over the API it is `\"prefix\": \"\"` in `key_format`; absent still inherits. The example key in the rule editor now also shows the store's prefix when the rule has none of its own, which it left out before\n    * **The Licenses tab is a list, and every key has a page of its own.** The tab drew every key as a full card with its instructions, files and forms, so a customer with fifty keys got fifty sets of instructions on one page. It now lists one card per key, newest first: the product, the project name, the status, the key with a copy button and the day it was bought, and a **Details** button that opens the key (the product name does too). The key's page (`{base}\/account\/licenses\/{ref}`, inside the tab, with a way back to the list at its top and bottom) has the key, what it covers, its licence files, a link to the order it came from, its ready-to-run packages and its instructions, then a quieter **Manage this license** card with only what can mean something for that key: terms that limit something, renewals, the Project box when an add-on can attach to the key, and the sites list when the key has a seat limit or a site on it. `{ref}` is 16 hex characters worked out from the key's stored hash (an HMAC, never the key, its hash or its id), so the addresses do not count up; it stays the same for the key's life and changes when the key is regenerated. It is only ever matched against the signed-in customer's own keys, so a key that is somebody else's or does not exist gets the same \"not found\" and a 404. A signed-out visit comes back to the key's page after sign-in. Forms on the page post to it and bring the customer back to it. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.6, whose account tabs can have pages below them. New partials `license-detail.html.twig`, `license-manage.html.twig`, `license-status.html.twig`, `license-renewal.html.twig`, `license-project.html.twig`, `license-sites.html.twig` and `license-instructions.html.twig`; the receipt's key card includes the shared ones and renders as before. A build on the key's page shows the product file's `title` and `description` when core has those columns, and its name otherwise. See \"The Licenses tab\" in docs\/storefront.md\n    * **Download all.** When the store's licence files include one with a fixed name that writes a line per product (a Grav store's `licenses.yaml`), the Licenses tab offers it merged across every active key the customer owns: one line per package, the newer key where two keys cover the same package, sorted by slug. Served from the account area at `{base}\/account\/licenses?download={name}`, behind its login. See \"Download all\" in docs\/storefront.md\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **A receipt leaves out seats, expiry and updates for a key that has none of them.** A perpetual key with unlimited seats and every future release used to print \"0 in use, unlimited seats \u00b7 Does not expire \u00b7 Includes all future updates\" on the receipt; the line now only appears when one of them says something\n    * **An add-on's own Included packages now count for the key it is attached to**, on the licence card, in licence files, in stamped zips and in `covers`, the same way a licence rule's always have\n    * **A key's admin screen lists its Included packages under Also covers**, marked \"Included\", beside the add-ons bought onto it. A bundle key used to read \"Just the product it was sold for\" although it validates for every package the bundle ships\n    * **A key with no updates window reads \"Every future release\" under Updates until**, not \"Never\", which read as the opposite\n    * **The receipt prints the install instructions once, not once per key.** An order with three keys printed the store's instructions three times, one copy under each key. Each key is now a short card (`order-license.html.twig`): the product, its status, the key with its copy button, what it covers, its files (the builds are in the receipt's own Downloads list, so the card no longer repeats them), and for a buyer who can sign in a **Manage this license** link to the key's own page. The instructions follow once, under all the keys (`order-instructions.html.twig`), and a store whose instructions name the key still gets each key's own, headed with its product. The order email and its plain-text part do the same. The panel carries `instructions` (from `LicenseView::instructionsOnce()`) and each key `page_url` and `has_terms`. `license.html.twig`, the full card, is still there for any page that includes it.\n"},"1.0.7":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Installing through GPM no longer switches the add-on off.** The plugin loads its classes through Composer's `vendor\/autoload.php`, but `vendor\/` was left out of the repository and so out of the download GPM installs. Grav logged \"Plugin 'kahunacart-licenses' autoload failed: Failed opening required ...\/vendor\/autoload.php\" and turned the plugin off on every store that installed or updated it that way. The run-time half of `vendor\/` is part of the download now, and a test fails if it goes missing or if a development install is committed over it\n"},"1.0.6":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **An add-on rule can grant more than one product, so a bundle can be sold as a variant.** an add-on can now have a \"Photos + Video\" variant whose rule also grants Photos, and paying for it puts both on the customer's key. The rule editor has an **Also grants** list under an add-on rule, and the rule table prints *Also grants Photos* under it. Over the API it is `grants`, a list of product ids stored in the rule's `meta_json`, so there is no migration. A bad id is refused with a sentence: not a whole number, no such product, listed twice, the rule's own product, or the product it attaches to. Every extra product is granted on the same key with the same order and order item as the line itself, a rerun of the issuing job adds nothing, and the receipt, the order email and the order screen list each add-on the purchase granted. See docs\/add-ons.md\n    * **An add-on rule can require other products on the key first.** A rule's new **Requires** list names products the customer's key must already cover, so a cheaper \"add-on only\" variant is sold only to stores that can use it, while a bundle variant beside it grants both. It is met by the key the line lands on (its own product, its add-ons and its included packages) or by a line in the same basket for that product or granting it. It is checked when the line goes into the cart and again at checkout, so taking the required product back out of the basket is caught. A guest is asked to sign in or choose the bundle; a signed-in customer is told their license doesn't include it yet. The sentences name the products from the catalog and are four new language strings. Over the API it is `requires`, a list of product ids in `meta_json`, validated like `grants`, and a product cannot be both granted and required. See docs\/add-ons.md\n    * **The Licenses panel beside an order lists the add-ons that order put on a key.** An add-on mints no key, so an order that only bought add-ons showed nothing beside it. The panel now has an **Add-ons** list with the key each one landed on, and `GET \/kahunacart\/licenses\/issuance\/{orderId}` answers it as `addons`\n    * **\"Sign in\" is a link in the sentence a guest gets for an add-on that needs another product first.** \"Rentals only is for stores that already have Bookings. Sign in to the account that holds your KahunaCart license, or choose Bookings + Rentals.\" told the guest to sign in and gave them nowhere to click. Sign in now goes to the store's sign-in page and brings them back to the product page, or to the checkout when that is where they were told, so they can pick the license they already own. Every product name in these sentences goes in as typed. Needs KahunaCart 1.2.11, for its storefront message links\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A refund or a cancellation now takes back the add-ons the order put on a key.** Keys minted by the order were revoked, but an add-on mints no key, so a refunded Newsletters order left Newsletters on the customer's KahunaCart key for good. Every add-on the order granted is removed from the key now, the extras from a bundle variant included, while anything the key got from another order stays. The order's add-on records are marked reversed, so the receipt stops listing them and a payment webhook redelivered after the refund cannot grant them again\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **An order with a deposit paid and a balance owed says \"Keys are issued once the order is paid in full\".** It said \"once the order is paid\", which reads as though the deposit should already have done it\n    * **The Issue Keys On setting now says what happens on an order paid by deposit.** Such an order completes when the deposit is paid and only counts as paid once the balance is, so **Order Completed** issues the key on the deposit and **Payment Received** waits for the balance. The help text and the settings section of docs\/admin.md say so in one sentence each. Nothing about how keys are issued has changed.\n"},"1.0.5":{"date":"09\/15\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **An order now says which keys it issued, and each one opens its own licence record.** A licence has always named the order it came from; the order said nothing back, so a merchant answering \"where is my key\" had to copy the order number into the Licenses search to go the other way. There is a **Licenses** panel in the column beside an open order now, listing each key with its status, the product, the project name where the customer has given one, and the expiry where the key has one \u2014 and the key itself is a link straight to that key's record, where the seats, the activations and every action already live. The same panel draws beside a customer record, which is the one screen that answers \"what has this person bought a key for\" without anybody searching. A record with no keys behind it draws no panel at all, so an ordinary order does not grow an empty card \u2014 except for an order that is going to earn keys and has not yet, which is the one case where an empty column is a question rather than an answer. That order gets the panel with **No keys issued yet** and a line saying what it is waiting for: the money, if the store issues on payment, or the order being placed if it issues on completion, or the worker where the trigger has already fired. It costs one extra call, made only for an order that has no keys to list, to a new `GET \/kahunacart\/licenses\/issuance\/{orderId}` that answers what the order's lines are owed, what they have been given and which event mints them \u2014 the issuer's own plan, so a variant rule, a disabled rule and a renewal line (which extends a key rather than minting one, and is not counted) are all read the way the issuing job reads them. There is an `order_issuance` MCP tool beside it. It is mounted as a KahunaCart extension zone widget, `kahunacart-licenses.keys`, in `order.details.side` and `customer.details.side`, so a merchant can move it among the panels around it under **KahunaCart \u2192 Extensions \u2192 Zones**; both mounts carry the one widget id, so moving it moves it on both screens. New string `ADMIN_ZONE_KEYS` for the panel heading, and a new `admin-next\/widgets\/keys.js`. Needs a base plugin with extension zones; against an older one the mount is refused with a line in the log and nothing else changes. See docs\/admin.md\n    * **A customer can list the sites they use a key on, so \"Where this key is in use\" stops being a list nobody could ever fill.** Only `activate` ever took a seat, and nothing on a Grav install calls it, so the seat count sat at zero however many sites a key was on. It is filled by the customer now, not by counting downloads \u2014 a download is a poor witness to where software runs, since the same person pulls a copy for their laptop, one for staging, one for the live site and another when they rebuild it, and a row for each of those would be noise dressed up as a record. A box under the key adds a site by name; the name is what the list shows and, normalised, what stops the same site going on twice. Existing rows can be renamed, and freed seats can be named too. The seat limit still applies, because a count nobody can exceed is what makes it a count rather than a list. New `add_seat` and `rename_seat` actions on the account tab, with strings `SEAT_ADD_LABEL`, `SEAT_ADD_PLACEHOLDER`, `SEAT_ADD_SUBMIT`, `SEAT_ADDED`, `SEAT_EXISTS`, `SEAT_FULL`, `SEAT_NEEDS_NAME`, `SEAT_NAME_LABEL`, `SEAT_NAME_PLACEHOLDER`, `SEAT_NAME_SAVE`, `SEAT_RENAMED` and `SEAT_RENAME_CLEARED`, and a reworded `NO_ACTIVATIONS` that asks rather than reports. A seat no longer prints \"last seen\": a row the customer wrote is not an observation, and its timestamp never moved off the day they added it.\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **The order email offers a guest their account as well as their order.** It gave them one link, to the order page, on the reasoning that the page carries every key on the order. That holds on the receipt itself, where they are already looking at the keys \u2014 but months later the email is all they have, and the account is where the keys keep living. A second link goes to the licences tab, which sends somebody without an account to the sign-in page, and a line under it says an account can still be opened from the order page, so nobody lands on a login form wondering what to do. Somebody who already holds an account sees the manage link as before. New strings `EMAIL_SIGN_IN_LINK` and `EMAIL_NO_ACCOUNT_YET`.\n    * **Seats, expiry and updates read as a list in the order email, a size down from the prose around them.** They were three paragraphs in the same size and colour as the description above them, so the licence's standing and the copy explaining it ran together into one block.\n    * **The licence file in the order email now comes before the instructions, and is named as something to download.** The account card has always put the file above the instructions and the email put it below, so a merchant writing one set of instructions for both had no wording that was true in both places \u2014 \"the licence file above\" is what the card shows and what the email contradicted, and a customer following it looked upwards, saw no file and did the rest by hand. It was also printed as a bare filename on a line of its own, which reads as a footnote; it now says \"Download licenses.yaml\", the way the account card says it. Both the HTML and the plain-text panel.\n"},"1.0.4":{"date":"09\/14\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **An add-on's buy form no longer asks a first-time buyer \"which project\".** The field above Add to cart on an add-on such as Newsletters asked \"Which project is this for?\" and, for somebody with no licence yet, drew an empty key box under it. The first customer on launch morning read \"project\" and \"license\" and went looking through their old Grav admin for a project number that does not exist. The label now names what it is asking for, \"Which KahunaCart license is this for?\", and when there is no licence in the cart and none on the account the field stops being a question: it says there is none in the cart yet and offers the two ways to get one, a button to add the licensed product to the cart first and, for a signed-out visitor, a link to sign in. The key box is still there for an agency buying on a client's key, behind an \"I already have a license key\" button, and it posts nothing until revealed so the cart refuses in words rather than the browser refusing an empty box in silence. New language strings `ADDON_FIELD_LABEL` (now with the product name), `ADDON_FIELD_LABEL_ANY`, `ADDON_FIELD_NONE_YET`, `ADDON_FIELD_ADD_FIRST`, `ADDON_FIELD_SIGN_IN_LINK` and `ADDON_FIELD_HAVE_KEY`; the partial gains `attaches_product_url` and `account_url`, so a theme that copied it keeps working and can pick the new variables up when it wants them\n"},"1.0.3":{"date":"09\/13\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Tests cover the renewal link wording for keys that never expire.** The 1.0.2 change that made a perpetual key offer \"Extend updates and support\" instead of \"Renew\" now has its own tests, so the wording cannot drift back without a failing suite saying so. No behaviour changes\n"},"1.0.2":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A key that never expires offers \"Extend updates and support\", not \"Renew\".** The card said \"Does not expire\" on one line and \"Renew\" on the next, and a customer is right to wonder which one is true. Renewing a perpetual key buys another year of updates and support, so the link now says exactly that, and the terms line reads \"Updates and support until\" rather than \"Updates until\". A key with an expiry date keeps \"Renew\"\n    * **A licensing rule can now name the packages that come with the licence but are never sold on their own.** A key covers the product it was sold under and every add-on bought against it, and both of those are rows in a table somewhere. Bundled packages are not: on kahunacart.com the core licence also covers the Stripe, PayPal, Polar and dummy payment plugins, none of which has a product, a price or an entitlement, so the only way to say so was to type the four names into the stamp template by hand, type them again into the instructions, and then remember to keep both lists agreeing with the chips on the customer's licence card. There is now an **Included packages** list on the rule, under Delivery beside the licence files: a package slug and an optional title per row, up to a hundred of them. Whatever is listed there is added to what the key covers everywhere at once, so the chips on the card, the `{#products}` block in a stamped `licenses.yaml`, the licence files and the instructions all read from one place. The order is the order a customer would list them in: the product they bought, then each add-on as it was granted, then the included packages as they were written, and a slug the key already covers is printed once however many times it appears. The API takes the list as `includes` on `PUT \/kahunacart\/licenses\/products\/{productId}` and hands it back on every rule, stored in the rule's own metadata so there is no migration behind any of it. See docs\/admin.md and docs\/storefront.md\n    * **A stamped download can now keep an optional add-on out of the package a key does not pay for.** The ready-to-run zip on kahunacart.com is one file, built nightly for everybody, and it is about to carry the Licenses, Subscriptions and Newsletters plugins inside it. A customer who bought two of the three should unzip two of the three. A stamp now takes a **gates** list beside the files it injects: a path prefix inside the archive and the package slug that folder belongs to, up to fifty per stamp. Everything under the prefix is deleted from the copy on the way out unless the key covers that slug, and \"covers\" is the same list the licence card prints, so the product the key was sold under, every add-on bought against it, and everything under Included packages on the rule. Bundle an add-on by listing it there and its gate opens for everybody without the gate changing. A download handed over with **no key at all** gets nothing that is gated, because a key nobody holds pays for no package, and the customer still gets their file. Both ways out of the store honour it, the customer's own download link and the public `\/release` endpoint an update client calls. A stamp can gate folders without injecting anything, injected files still land whatever the gates say, and nothing stamped or gated is ever stored. The editor draws the list under each stamp, the API takes `gates` on `PUT \/kahunacart\/licenses\/products\/{productId}` and hands it back on every rule, and each gate that runs writes one line to the log at debug level saying how many entries it removed and which package was not covered. See docs\/admin.md and docs\/storefront.md\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The ready-to-run list on a licence card leaves out retired releases.** A nightly build whose version moved publishes a new release and the merchant unpublishes last night's, and the card still listed every zip the order had ever been granted, three of the same package on a receipt. A file on an unpublished release is dropped now, the way the base plugin's own downloads panel already drops it; evergreen files with no release stay\n"},"1.0.1":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The licence card reads like a purchase page now: the key, what it covers, the licence file, the packages to install, then the instructions.** It used to print the key, the terms, a wall of instructions, and a plain list of licence files right at the bottom, so the two things a customer had just paid for were the two hardest things to find. Under the key line there is now an **Included packages** row of chips, one per product the key covers: the product it was sold under first, then every add-on bought against it, printed as the slug, which is the name they type into GPM and the name a stamped `licenses.yaml` carries. Under that, each licence file is a button reading \"Download licenses.yaml\" instead of a link in a list nobody scrolled to. The card also picks up a **Ready-to-run packages** block, showing the builds the same order bought for the products the key covers, each with a **Download (44.7 MB)** button, read back from KahunaCart's own download grants so a revoked or expired one is never offered. The instructions stay where they were, last, and a store with no files attached to its products sees no packages block at all. The email panel gets the one line that fits a message (\"Included packages: kahunacart, kahunacart-newsletters\") and links to the order page for the rest. See docs\/storefront.md\n    * **Install instructions collapse into sections a customer opens.** Every `###` heading in the delivery instructions becomes a row with the heading as its label, so a product with three ways to install draws three lines instead of a screen and a half of scrolling between the key and the download. Anything written above the first heading stays open, which is where a sentence of context belongs, and instructions with no `###` in them render exactly as they always did. Order emails always get the flat version, because no mail client opens a collapsed section. There is default CSS for all of it, and every new class is in docs\/storefront.md for a theme that wants its own. See docs\/admin.md\n    * **An add-on bought alongside the key it lands on no longer gets a box of its own on the receipt.** The card for that key already lists the add-on among the products it covers, so the block underneath repeating it read as a second purchase the customer could not account for. An add-on bought for a key from an earlier order still gets its box, because nothing else on that receipt mentions it\n    * **A licence template can now write one line per product the key covers, with the new `{#products}...{\/products}` block.** One key covers the product it was sold under and every add-on bought against it, but a placeholder is a single value and there was no way for a template to say \"and repeat this for each of them\". So a stamped `grav\/user\/data\/licenses.yaml` named the base product only, and Grav's GPM turned round and asked the customer for a key they had already paid for the moment they installed the add-on. Write `licenses:` and then `{#products}  {slug}: {key}` with `{\/products}` under it, and the block repeats once per product: the one the key was sold under first, then each add-on in the order it was granted. `{slug}` and `{title}` inside the block are that product's, every other token still means what it means outside, and a customer with no add-ons gets the single line from the same template. It works everywhere a licence is rendered, so the account page, the admin's delivery preview, the public `\/file` endpoint, a stamped build from `\/release` and a stamped customer download all agree on what the key covers. A block naming a section that does not exist stays on the page exactly as typed, the same promise an unrecognised `{token}` gets. See docs\/storefront.md and docs\/admin.md\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **The add-on box on a receipt says what the merchant wrote, not `bin\/gpm install`.** The box a customer sees when an add-on lands on a key they already hold printed a Grav install command, which is the wrong sentence for a desktop app, a font or anything else this plugin licenses. It now prints the add-on's own rule's instructions, rendered with the covering key's values, and nothing when the rule has none. A new `{slug}` placeholder carries the product's slug into any template, and the add-on rules on a Grav store write their install line with it\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A second plugin's answer to `onKahunaCartReleaseEntitlement` is no longer overwritten.** The release listener assigned `entitled` outright, from the candidate rows, so a listener that had run before it at a higher priority and taken a release away had that release handed straight back \u2014 the customer's key said yes, so the answer was yes, and the other plugin's refusal never happened. It now intersects instead: this plugin's answer is kept only where the standing one agrees, so it can be one more reason a release is withheld and never a reason one is restored. Nothing changes on a store where this is the only listener, since intersecting a list with the whole candidate set is that list. A standing value that is not a list of ids is still what the base plugin reads as no answer at all, which means every candidate, so there is nothing to intersect with and this plugin's own answer stands. See docs\/events.md\n    * **Two machines activating the last seat of a licence at the same moment could both be let in.** On MariaDB, MySQL and PostgreSQL each one counted the seats before the other had taken theirs, so both saw a seat free and both got it, and the licence ended up carrying one more live seat than it allows \u2014 the kind of thing that shows up as a customer quietly running on three machines with a two-seat key. The activation now locks the licence row before it counts anything, so the two are handled one after the other: the second machine is told the licence is full and gets the seats list to pick from, and a machine that already holds a seat still finds its own rather than paying for a second. See docs\/api.md\n    * **Two renewals of the same key arriving at once could buy one term instead of two, or record the renewal twice.** A payment webhook delivered twice queues a second licensing job for the same order, and nothing stopped the two running side by side: each read the key's expiry before the other had written to it, each added a year to the same starting date, and the customer who paid for two years held one. The order's own record of what it had renewed had the same problem, since it is read at the top of the run and written at the bottom, so one of the two runs lost its stamp and a later refund had nothing to put back. Renewing a key now locks that key's row before it reads the date it is about to add to, and licensing work on one order \u2014 issuing, renewing, and putting a renewal back after a refund \u2014 locks the order row first, so two of them are handled one after the other. Both take the order row before any key, so a refund and a redelivered payment webhook queue behind each other rather than deadlock. Nothing changes for a store where the jobs never overlap, which is most of the time. See docs\/renewals.md\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **The storefront reads far fewer rows to draw the same pages.** The account tab used to ask for a key's live activations one key at a time and now reads them for every key at once, the buy form asks for the rules on the product it is drawing instead of every enabled rule in the store, the cart's add-on warnings reuse one read of the customer's licences rather than repeating it per line, and release filtering picks the customer's widest usable key once instead of weighing every release against every key they hold. Every screen answers exactly what it answered before.\n    * **The buy form's licence pickers read only the keys they can show.** A renewal or add-on field on a public product page asked for every key the customer owns and then walked the list in PHP, throwing away the revoked and suspended ones and stopping at fifty \u2014 so an account holding a thousand keys read a thousand rows to draw a dropdown of fifty. The exclusion, the product filter and the ceiling are all in the query now, and the list is exactly the list it was\n    * **The licensing settings are the plugin's own form now, drawn on Licenses \u2192 Settings.** The screen carried a second, hand-written copy of every field in `blueprints.yaml`, saving through a REST route of its own \u2014 so a merchant had to know which of the two places to open, every new setting had to be written twice, and the two could disagree about what they had saved. KahunaCart now draws the plugin's blueprint form inside the section (the manifest says `settings_plugin`, the screen renders the slot the base plugin forwards into it) and the header's Save button saves it, so **Plugins \u2192 KahunaCart Licenses** in the Grav admin and **Licenses \u2192 Settings** here are one screen rather than two. What is left above the form is the two things a settings field cannot answer: the example key the store is minting right now, and the signing secret's state with the **Rotate** button, since a secret is generated rather than typed and is shown once. The \"nowhere to write plugin configuration\" notice and the `user\/config\/plugins\/\u2026` path went with the old form. Against an older base plugin the screen prints one line pointing at the Grav admin's page, so nothing is stranded. See docs\/admin.md\n    * The license panel in the order email uses US spelling and shorter wording: \"Covered by your existing license\", \"Includes all future updates\", \"View this on your order page\"\n    * **The public validation endpoints are proxy-aware.** They read `REMOTE_ADDR` and deliberately ignored `X-Forwarded-For`, which is right on a store nothing sits in front of and wrong behind Cloudflare or a load balancer: there every request arrives from the proxy, so every customer in the world shared one rate-limit bucket and the first runaway installer locked out the rest. The header is now honoured when \u2014 and only when \u2014 the connection itself came from an address in the base plugin's `security.trusted_proxies`, which is empty by default and so changes nothing on an unconfigured store. That setting lives in the base plugin rather than here because there is one answer to \"who is this request from\" per store, and two settings would eventually disagree. The address also reaches the activation row's IP hash the same way, so \"which machines is this key on\" stops answering \"the CDN\" for every seat. On a base plugin older than trusted proxies the behaviour is exactly what it was, decided by looking at the method that is actually on the classpath rather than at a version number. The two counters also moved into named buckets of their own, `licenses_key` and `licenses_ip`, so they cannot spend a budget the shop is counting and the shop cannot spend theirs. See docs\/api.md\n    * `KAHUNACART_TEST_BASE_ROOT` points the test suite at a base-plugin checkout other than the sibling one, so this add-on can be run against a branch of the base before it merges. Unset, the run is exactly what it always was\n    * **An events chapter, `docs\/events.md`.** All five events this plugin fires \u2014 `onKahunaCartLicenseIssued`, `Revoked`, `Activated`, `Extended` and `Widened` \u2014 in the same `Event | Carries | For` table the base plugin's provider guide uses, with a section per event giving the exact payload keys, what each one holds, which code path fires it, and the cases where it deliberately fires nothing: a machine re-announcing a seat it already holds, an add-on re-applied by a queue retry, a regeneration of a key that was already dead. It also documents the two promises every one of them makes \u2014 they fire after the write and outside its transaction, and a listener that throws loses its notification and nothing else \u2014 and the one that costs a listener time, since the activation event runs inside the customer's own HTTP call. The chapter closes on the other side of the seam: this plugin's answer to the base plugin's `onKahunaCartReleaseEntitlement`, the three answers `ReleaseAccess` can give and why a keyless customer gets none, and the one thing a third plugin listening on the same event has to know \u2014 narrow what you find in `entitled` rather than assigning a list built from `releases`, which is what this plugin's listener does, so both answers hold whichever order the two were installed in\n    * **A `SECURITY.md` at the plugin root.** Report privately to security@kahunacart.com, never as a public issue, with the full policy \u2014 what to include, response times, scope, supported versions \u2014 left in the base plugin's own `SECURITY.md` rather than copied here where the two would drift apart\n    * The public API's `validate`, `activate`, `deactivate` and `updates` docblocks led with the route and its request-body signature, which a doc generator reads as the description \u2014 so four of the seven public endpoints documented themselves as `{key, instance, label?}` and said nothing about what the call does. Each now opens with a sentence naming what it does and what it answers with, followed by the signature and the refusal that method raises before the service sees it\n\n1. [](#new)\n    * **Every top-level screen is headed \"Licenses\", with a line under it that changes with the screen, and the tabs beneath.** The tab strip sat above the heading, the other way round from Subscriptions, and the Licensed products and Settings screens were headed with their own names. The three screens now start the same way Subscriptions and Newsletter do. A key's own screen, the issue form and a rule editor keep their own heading and a way back, and draw no tabs\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart reset` now takes the licence keys with it.** Verified on a live store: a reset that deleted every test order left every key those orders had minted live, activatable and answering yes to the client software that checks it \u2014 which never sees the store's order table at all. The add-on declares its tables on the base plugin's new `onKahunaCartRegisterResetTables` \u2014 the keys, their activations and their entitlements \u2014 under the orders scope, because the order that sold a key is what it belongs to. A customers-only reset keeps the orders as guest orders and keeps the keys with them, the same way core keeps the orders themselves. `kahunacart_license_products` is deliberately left out: which products mint keys, how many seats each allows and what renews what is configuration, and the reset promises the configuration survives\n    * Initial release: license keys for KahunaCart \u2014 issuance queued off the order lifecycle, activation seats, an updates entitlement that narrows which releases a key reaches, renewals that extend a key the customer already holds, add-ons that widen one, a public validation API for software running on a customer's own machine, and the storefront and admin screens for all of it\n    * **Subscriptions.** With the KahunaCart Subscriptions add-on installed, a plan renews a licence key instead of minting a new one every period. Write the same renewal rule you would write for a manual renewal and point the plan's renewal variant at it: the keys the first order minted are stamped with the subscription \u2014 whichever way round activation and the issuing job arrive \u2014 and every period's order carries them on its lines, so the unchanged `licenses.issue` path extends them with the same arithmetic, the same idempotency stamp and the same rollback on a refund. A subscription holding several keys gets one line per key rather than one line naming several. Each rule carries a **When a subscription ends** setting: `nothing` by default, so the customer keeps the term they already paid for, or `suspend` or `revoke` for software where the key is the access. It applies when the subscription is actually over, never when a customer schedules a cancellation with weeks left. The account tab reads *Renews automatically* or *Renewal due &lt;date&gt;* on those keys, links to the subscription's own page, and hides the manual Renew link while the subscription is live. Nothing changes on a store without the add-on. See docs\/renewals.md\n    * **A project name on every key, and add-ons that attach to one.** Somebody who runs three stores buys KahunaCart three times and ends up holding three keys that differ only in their random middle. They can now name each one \u2014 \"Surf shop\", \"Client: Acme\", \"Staging\" \u2014 from the **Project** box on their account's Licenses tab, and everywhere a key is printed for a person the name leads and the key follows: `Surf shop \u00b7 KC-XXXX-\u2026`. The admin edits the same name on the licence record, the list has a **Project** column, and the search box matches it beside the key, the buyer's email and the order number. It is a real `label` column added by `licenses_0005_project_labels`, nullable and staying that way, so every key already issued is untouched and prints exactly as it did. Renaming is the one place in this plugin where a key's *owner* matters: a key is a bearer credential everywhere else, but a name is a note on somebody's account, and the storefront route refuses a key that is not theirs with the same answer it gives for a key that does not exist. See docs\/add-ons.md\n    * **An add-on product asks which licence it is for.** A product carrying an `addon` rule now draws a **Which project is this for?** field inside the ordinary add-to-cart form, the way a renewal product asks for its key and through the same `line_data[license_key]` name. It offers the signed-in customer's own keys for the product the add-on attaches to \u2014 newest first, each reading *name \u00b7 key \u00b7 bought on* \u2014 plus a text box for a key that is not on their account, which is how an agency buys for a client. When the product it attaches to is already in the basket, the list starts with **\"The KahunaCart in this cart\"**: somebody buying both together has no key to name yet, so the line carries the sentinel `@cart` and the issuing job resolves it after the keys are minted. A visitor who is neither signed in nor holding one in their cart is told so in a sentence rather than left with an empty dropdown. Buying the same add-on for two projects is two lines with two choices, which the cart already keeps apart on its `line_data` hash. See docs\/add-ons.md\n    * **An add-on cannot be bought without a licence to put it on.** The cart-line gate grows an add-on check beside the renewal one, so a line is refused at add-to-cart \u2014 before any money \u2014 unless it names a key that exists, is for the product this add-on attaches to, and is neither revoked nor suspended, or names `@cart` while that product is actually in the cart. An expired key is accepted: a term that ran out does not stop an add-on attaching, and the customer may be renewing it in the same basket. The one case the gate cannot see at add time is the licence being removed from the cart *after* the add-on was added to it, since core never offers a line for veto when another line is removed \u2014 so a sentence goes beside the line on the cart and checkout pages, through `onKahunaCartCartLineNotes`, and the basket is checked again at the button (next bullet)\n    * **The order is refused if an add-on has nothing left to attach to, or a renewal's key stopped being renewable.** Core now fires `onKahunaCartCheckoutValidate` once per placement attempt \u2014 after its own checks, before any payment provider is touched, on the checkout form, the storefront JSON API and the express buttons \u2014 and this plugin re-runs both of its gates over the basket as it stands, in one pass. That closes the gaps the add-time check cannot see, because it looks at a line as it goes in and never again: a `@cart` line whose licence was taken back out of the basket, a key that was fine when the line was added and was revoked or suspended in the days the basket sat there, and a renewal or an add-on sold under a rule the merchant has since switched off \u2014 which mints nothing, extends nothing, attaches nothing and records nothing, so the customer would pay for a line that does absolutely nothing. That last one gets a sentence of its own, *This renewal is no longer on sale* or *This add-on is no longer on sale*; switching an ordinary licence rule off still refuses nothing, since a merchant who stops issuing keys for a product is still selling the product. Each message is pinned to the line that caused it, so the checkout page prints it beside that line rather than in a heap at the top. It refuses only where the purchase genuinely cannot be applied: a buyer who already holds a live licence for the attaching product is let through, looked up by their customer account and by the address on the order, which is exactly how the issuing job resolves the same line \u2014 and revoked and suspended keys count for nothing, since nothing can be added to a revoked key and an add-on on a suspended one still leaves the customer locked out. An expired key still renews and still takes an add-on. A store that has written neither an add-on rule nor a renewal rule pays one query for all of it, and a basket of one renewal and ten ordinary lines costs the same two reads of the rules table an add-on line does. The event is subscribed by name, so against a base plugin that has never heard of it the plugin loads exactly as before and the cart-page sentence is the whole of the second look. See docs\/add-ons.md and docs\/renewals.md\n    * **Issuance runs the minting lines before the add-on lines**, explicitly, through the new `IssuancePlan::ordered()`. It used to run the plan in whatever order the order's lines happened to be in, which was fine while every add-on attached to a key bought on an earlier order and is not fine now that one can attach to a key this same order is about to mint. Stable within each half, so two keys of one line still come out in `seq` order and an order with no add-ons is planned exactly as before\n    * **The admin says what an add-on rule is.** The Licensed products table printed \"License\" for an `addon` rule, because only `renewal` had wording of its own; it now reads **Add-on** with *Attaches to KahunaCart* under it. The rule form grows **Attaches to which product** for the add-on kind the way it has *Renews which product* for renewals \u2014 the column has existed since the entitlements migration and there was no way to set it from the admin at all \u2014 and the kind dropdown reads *License \/ Renewal \/ Add-on* rather than the three column values. The licence record grows an **Also covers** panel listing every add-on bought onto that key with its date and order, which is what answers \"what does the Surf shop license include\" in one place\n    * **MCP tools.** The plugin ships an `mcp.yaml` describing its JSON routes, which the Grav API plugin serves from `GET \/mcp\/tools` and an MCP server such as grav-mcp registers one tool per entry at startup. A model connected to the store can search and issue keys, suspend, reinstate, revoke and regenerate them, free a seat, write the per-product and per-variant licensing rules, read and save the store settings, and check a key, its updates entitlement or its newest release through the public endpoints. The admin tools carry `kahunacart.licenses` and are hidden from a caller who does not hold it; the three public ones carry no permission because the routes carry no authentication. The routes that answer with a file, the `OPTIONS` preflight handlers, and the two unauthenticated ones that spend a customer's seat are deliberately not tools, and a unit test holds that list against the routes themselves so the manifest cannot fall behind the API. See docs\/api.md\n    * **Stamped downloads.** A licence file is something a customer downloads beside the software; a stamp puts it inside the software, on the way out. The case is the ready-to-run package \u2014 a starter store, an installer, a project template somebody should be able to unzip and run without a licence screen first. That package has to carry their key and the file on disk cannot, because it is one file for everybody rebuilt nightly by a script that has never heard of a customer; the moment it carries a key there is a build farm where there was a cron job. So the key goes in at download time: the stored zip is copied to a temp file, the entries the rule names are written into the copy, the copy is streamed, and the copy is deleted. **Nothing stamped is ever stored** \u2014 no cache of per-customer builds, nothing to purge when a key is revoked, and nothing in a backup carrying somebody's credential. A rule carries a list of `{match, inject}` in the new nullable `stamps_json`, added by `licenses_0006_stamped_downloads` and edited from **Stamped downloads** on the rule form: `match` is a glob against the released file's own name, so a nightly build whose name carries its date matches one pattern forever, and each `inject` is a path inside the archive and a template written in the same `{key}` \/ `{product}` \/ `{expires}` \/ `{signature}` vocabulary a licence file body uses, substituted raw for the same reason. Both ways of getting a build stamp, governed by the same rule: the public `\/release` endpoint an update client calls, and the customer's own `{base}\/download\/{token}` link on the account page and in every order email \u2014 that one through `onKahunaCartDownloadDeliver`, a new base-plugin event fired after the grant is authorized and the path resolved. A path is relative with no `..` in it, refused with a sentence when the rule is saved and dropped if a stored column ever holds one, because a zip is opened on somebody else's machine. Only zip files are stamped; a stamp matching anything else delivers it exactly as it is and logs once. A stamped file is streamed by PHP whatever `downloads.method` says, since a temp copy is outside every root `X-Accel-Redirect` and `X-Sendfile` will serve from, and `Content-Length` then comes from the copy rather than the size recorded at upload \u2014 everything unstamped on the same store still goes to the web server. A customer holding no key gets the package unstamped rather than refused, which is the same call `ReleaseAccess` makes about a keyless customer and for the same reason; a revoked key is not stamped, an expired or suspended one is, and somebody holding several keys gets the one with the widest updates window. Requires the base plugin's `onKahunaCartDownloadDeliver`; against an older base the customer link delivers unstamped and `\/release` still stamps. See docs\/storefront.md"}}},"kahunacart-subscriptions":{"name":"KahunaCart Subscriptions","version":"1.1.4","description":"Recurring billing for KahunaCart \u2014 plans, subscriptions, renewal orders, provider and invoice modes","description_html":"<p>Recurring billing for KahunaCart \u2014 plans, subscriptions, renewal orders, provider and invoice modes<\/p>","description_plain":"Recurring billing for KahunaCart \u2014 plans, subscriptions, renewal orders, provider and invoice modes","icon":"refresh","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.3.8"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/subscriptions","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/selling\/subscriptions","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, subscriptions, recurring, billing, plans","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-subscriptions","license":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/premium\/license","tag_name":"1.1.4","date":"2026-10-02T01:04:53Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-subscriptions\/1.1.4","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/subscriptions","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-subscriptions","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-subscriptions","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-subscriptions"},"slug":"kahunacart-subscriptions","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-subscriptions","content_sha256":"bd38b06aa6d385bc3c2e6adf912175e3e1c8f0510aaf21830f22f57767188525","changelog":{"1.1.4":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Help text on the plan editor now follows the admin's Help text setting.** It shows below the label or in a tooltip behind an info icon\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The download no longer carries the plugin's tests.** `tests\/` and `phpunit.xml` stay in the repository and out of the release zip, 91 files fewer on every install. Nothing the plugin runs is affected\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A plan's customer group also fills the subscribe panel on paywalled articles.** KahunaCart 1.3.8 can hide the rest of an article from anyone who isn't a member of a customer group and show a subscribe panel instead. The panel now lists the plans that grant that group: one button for each plan on a product that is published (listed or unlisted), open to the visitor and not sold out, with its price the way the product page states it (the first payment, then the renewal when that is different, or the trial). A product with Monthly and Yearly billing options gets a button for each, in the order the shop lists products and then the order the product page offers the plans. Nothing to set up: a plan with a **Customer group while subscribed** is offered on every article that asks for that group. It costs one database query however many products the shop has. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later. See docs\/plans.md\n    * **The subscriptions list and the orders on a subscription sort by their column headings.** Press a heading to sort by it, and press it again to turn the order round. The list sorts on the server, so a heading orders every subscription the filters leave and not only the page on screen, and pressing one goes back to page one. `GET \/kahunacart\/subscriptions` takes `sort` (`customer`, `plan`, `status`, `billing`, `amount` or `next`) and `dir`, a subscription with nothing in that column sorts last whichever way it runs, and any other `sort` is ignored, so the list is newest first as before. The Plans table has no headings to press, because its rows are in the order the buy button offers them. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later, which draws the headings\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/28\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A plan can put its subscribers in a customer group while their subscription is live.** The plan editor has a new setting, **Customer group while subscribed**. Pick a group and everybody whose subscription on that plan is trialing, active or past due counts as a member of it, everywhere KahunaCart asks: price lists, coupon conditions, member-only products, and the `site.customer_group.<code>` permission a page's `access:` header can ask for. So a Downloads Pro page with `access: site.customer_group.members: true` opens for subscribers and closes the moment a subscription stops being live. Nothing is written to the group's member list: the store works it out from the subscription on each request, so a late webhook or a status set by hand can never leave somebody in a group they have stopped paying for. A cancellation at the end of the period keeps the group until that end, because the customer paid for it; a paused subscription grants nothing. Migration `subscriptions_0005_plan_customer_group` adds the column on SQLite, MySQL\/MariaDB and PostgreSQL. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.5 or later, the release that asks add-ons which groups a customer holds. See docs\/plans.md\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Billing options: several plans on one variant.** A membership sold monthly at $100, quarterly and yearly used to need three variants, and three variants kept three stock counts, so there was no single count of places left. One variant can now carry several plans, and all of them sell from its one stock count. Each plan can have its own price (leave it blank to use the variant's), a label for its choice on the buy button, and an order. The buy button shows the plans as a choice under **How often you pay**, as radio pills in the style of the variant options that work from the keyboard; picking one changes the price shown and the renewal terms, and the cart charges that plan's price through KahunaCart's line price event. A variant with one plan renders exactly what it did before. The Plans screen adds, reorders and edits a variant's options, and the variant picker no longer locks a variant that already has a plan. Migration `subscriptions_0004_billing_options` adds the columns and swaps the unique index on `variant_id` for a plain one on SQLite, MySQL\/MariaDB and PostgreSQL. See docs\/plans.md\n    * **When a subscription ends: its place stays used, or goes back to stock.** A per-plan setting for a store with a limited number of places. \"Its place stays used\", the default and the old behaviour, means twenty places is twenty subscriptions ever; \"Its place goes back to stock\" puts the unit back once the subscription has actually ended, so twenty places is twenty at a time. Never at a cancellation scheduled for the period end, never twice, and not when refunding the first order already put it back. The subscription's history records it\n    * A billing option's label follows the line's name to Stripe or Polar, so three options on one variant are three agreements that can be told apart in the platform's dashboard\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A renewal now charges what the customer signed up at.** Every period is an ordinary order built through the cart, and the cart priced the line from the variant, so a renewal charged whatever the variant cost on the morning it was built. The price frozen on the subscription was recorded and never read: a customer who subscribed with a coupon lost it on the first renewal, and raising a variant's price repriced everybody already paying, which is the one thing the Plans docs say the store will never do. In provider mode the platform kept billing the frozen amount, so the store's renewal orders and the money disagreed instead. The renewal line is now priced at the subscription's own amount, with a \"Price agreed at sign-up\" row under it on the receipt, and a sale running on the day is not taken off it again. A plan with a renewal variant keeps pricing renewals from that variant, as it always did. Needs KahunaCart 1.2.11 or later, for its line price event\n    * **Taking a plan off sale no longer stops the renewals of the people already on it.** The cart asked the same question of a renewal line as of a customer's basket, found the plan switched off, and refused the renewal as \"no longer available\". In provider mode that happened after the platform had taken the money, so the subscription stopped moving forward while the card kept being charged. A renewal the store builds for itself is now let through, and a plan that is off sale is still refused to anybody new\n"},"1.0.3":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Installing through GPM no longer switches the add-on off.** The plugin loads its classes through Composer's `vendor\/autoload.php`, but `vendor\/` was left out of the repository and so out of the download GPM installs. Grav logged \"Plugin 'kahunacart-subscriptions' autoload failed: Failed opening required ...\/vendor\/autoload.php\" and turned the plugin off on every store that installed or updated it that way. The run-time half of `vendor\/` is part of the download now, and a test fails if it goes missing or if a development install is committed over it\n"},"1.0.2":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The storefront stylesheet is no longer added twice on subscription pages.** The add-on puts KahunaCart's own stylesheet on the page itself, and looked for the file one folder too high to stamp it, so its link never matched the one KahunaCart adds and the browser downloaded the same sheet twice. It now finds the file through Grav, and the page links it once.\n"},"1.0.1":{"date":"09\/15\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * The package name and the repository links now point at the kahunacart GitHub org rather than trilbymedia: `composer.json`, the security policy, and the links into the base plugin's provider contract and developer guide from `docs\/provider-developer-guide.md`\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The mailing-list add-on is called Newsletters now, and the segment field follows it.** The event this plugin listens on is `onKahunaCartNewslettersRegisterSegmentFields` and the registry it checks for is `Grav\\Plugin\\KahunaCartNewsletters\\Segments\\Registry`. `subscription_status` still arrives in the segment editor the same way, and a store without that add-on still notices nothing. It needs Newsletters 1.0.0 or later; against the add-on under its old name the field is not contributed\n    * The subscription emails and the receipt panel read shorter and plainer, in US English with no dashes: the past-due, renewal and ended messages say what happened and what to do in one or two sentences, and the receipt panel's manage link is a real link with the address under it rather than a bare URL\n    * **The subscription page and the pay-link lookup read a customer's order history in a handful of queries.** Drawing a subscription used to cost a query for every renewal order behind it, so the customers who had been with the store longest waited the longest to see their own history, and the check for an unpaid renewal did the same walk again. Both read the orders together now\n\n1. [](#new)\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-subscriptions seed`, so an empty store is not what a merchant meets first.** Every screen this add-on adds opens on an empty table until somebody has been selling on a calendar for months, and the public demo's database is rebuilt every hour, so there was never anything on them to look at. The command writes what a small shop would have: two plans on the demo catalog, twenty subscribers across them, most running, two inside a trial, one whose invoice is late and inside its grace period, one paused, three who have gone, and a real order behind every period any of them was billed for \u2014 about sixty orders, each with the timeline entry that put it there. Idempotent on the subscription hash and the plan's variant, so the demo's reset runs it on a timer; `--purge` takes back exactly what it wrote, including the orders, and leaves a plan somebody is still subscribed to alone. Nothing is sent and nothing is left for the worker: orders are written without going near `OrderBuilder`, every live invoice-mode subscription renews further out than the longest rung of the reminder ladder, and the past-due one carries a complete invoice stamp, so a store running `bin\/plugin kahunacart work` finds no work in it. Dates are offsets from the moment it runs and the volume comes from a fixed seed, so two machines get the same people, the same billing dates and the same subscription URLs. See docs\/cli.md\n    * **A Settings tab, and the plugin's own form drawn on it.** \"Where do I change the reminder days\" used to be answered by leaving the KahunaCart admin for the Grav admin's plugin list, and nothing on the Subscriptions screen said so. The section now has a third tab beside Subscriptions and Plans, on the `settings` sub-route so a reload and the Back button land on it, and KahunaCart draws the plugin's own blueprint form inside it \u2014 the manifest says `settings_plugin`, the screen renders the slot the base plugin forwards into, and the header's Save button saves it. The invoice ladder, the grace period, the four emails and the admin switches are read and changed on the same screen as the subscriptions they govern, and following **Plugins \u2192 KahunaCart Subscriptions** in the Grav admin lands here too. Against an older base plugin the tab prints one line pointing at the Grav admin's page, so nothing is stranded. See docs\/admin.md\n    * **`subscription_status` in the Newsletters add-on's segment editor.** A store running both can now build a mailing-list segment out of what this add-on knows: everybody whose subscription lapsed last month, everybody active on a plan so the sales pitch leaves them out, everybody past due so the reminder goes to them and nobody else. Neither question is askable from the mailing list's own tables. The field is contributed on `onKahunaCartNewslettersRegisterSegmentFields` and is never a hard dependency \u2014 the newsletter is a separate add-on, the listener checks its registry class is there before touching anything, and a store running subscriptions without it notices nothing at all. The match is through the subscriber's linked customer record rather than by comparing addresses, so somebody who signed up to the newsletter with one address and bought with another correctly does not match: guessing that two addresses are one person is how a store emails somebody about somebody else's subscription\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart reset` now takes the subscriptions with it.** Verified on a live store: a reset that deleted every order and every customer left the subscriptions behind, still active and still due, so the next worker tick tried to bill people who were no longer there. The add-on declares its tables on the base plugin's new `onKahunaCartRegisterResetTables` \u2014 the subscriptions, their events, their renewal orders and the billing tick's cursor \u2014 under both the orders and the customers scope, because a subscription belongs to the customer who holds it and is the sale that created it. `kahunacart_subscription_plans` is deliberately left out: a plan is what the merchant configured, and the reset promises the configuration survives. The tables appear in the plan the command prints, with their row counts, before it asks\n    * Recurring billing core for KahunaCart: plans, subscriptions, renewal orders and the subscription event timeline, with a state machine that is the only writer of a subscription's status, UTC period arithmetic with end-of-month clamping, guest subscriptions by hash, and a refunded renewal that rolls the period back \u2014 unless the refunded period is an older one the customer has already billed past, which is recorded as a note and changes no dates\n    * Free trials that are actually free: a plan with `trial_days` starts its subscription in `trialing` and its origin order is completed and paid with a total of zero, carrying a translatable \"Free trial (14 days)\" discount adjustment, with the ledger row settled at zero to match. The first paid period begins where the trial ends \u2014 14 free days and then a whole month, not a month that quietly started a fortnight ago\n    * Checkout: a plan sells through the ordinary buy button and checks out alone \u2014 one plan line, quantity one, nothing else in the cart, and a second subscription added to a cart that already holds one is refused with a message beside the button whether it is the same plan or a different one. Providers that run their own recurring billing (Stripe, Polar, the test provider) take the payment over and KahunaCart mirrors every later event from their webhooks: activation, each renewal minted as an ordinary paid order, failed payments, past due, pause and resume, a cancellation scheduled for the period end and the one that ends it, and expiry. A provider that cannot run the agreement falls back to invoice mode rather than refusing the sale\n    * Invoice mode, for any provider: KahunaCart keeps the calendar itself, builds each period's renewal as an unpaid order with a pay link, sends the reminder ladder (`invoice.reminder_days`, default 14, 3 and 0 days before), marks the subscription past due when the period ends unpaid, and lets it lapse after the grace period (`invoice.grace_days`, default 7, per-plan override). A payment during grace brings it back; paying early adds time rather than replacing it. Emails for the reminder, the due date, past due and expiry, all translatable and switchable\n    * Customer side: a Subscriptions tab in the account, and a page for every subscription at `\/subscription\/{hash}` (guests included) with the timeline, the renewal orders, cancel at period end, resume, update payment method where the provider offers a portal, and renew now in invoice mode. Receipts and receipt emails say when an order started or renewed a subscription\n    * Admin: a Subscriptions section with Plans (product and variant, interval, trial, billing mode, renewal variant, grace, per-provider references) and Subscriptions (filters, search, detail with timeline and orders, cancel now or at period end, pause, resume, extend by N days with a note, resend pay link, mark renewal paid), a customer panel listing that customer's subscriptions, and active, past-due and monthly-recurring-revenue figures. Gated on `kahunacart.subscriptions.view` and `kahunacart.subscriptions.manage`\n    * MCP tool manifest: `mcp.yaml` describes all seventeen admin API routes as MCP tools, so a `grav-mcp` client picks them up at startup with no code per plugin. Every tool carries the permission its route enforces, so a caller holding only `kahunacart.subscriptions.view` is offered the six read tools and none of the eleven writes, and the descriptions carry the rules a caller has to know: what the two billing modes mean, when pause, resume, cancel and uncancel are refused, that a renewal is an ordinary order marked paid through the base plugin's own route, and that invoice mode needs the store's scheduler running\n    * Documentation for plans, the two billing modes, the customer account, the admin section, troubleshooting, and a provider developer guide for implementing recurring billing\n    * A complete event reference in the provider developer guide: all fifteen events this plugin fires, each with its exact payload keys, when it fires and what a listener may usefully do, plus the five keys every subscription event carries. `onKahunaCartSubscriptionRenewalOrderBuilding` is documented as the question it is \u2014 fired through `ask()`, with a listener writing `lines` back onto the event \u2014 and the two order events the plugin listens to are described as the handlers they are rather than as events anybody can fire\n    * `SECURITY.md`: report privately to security@kahunacart.com, never in a public issue, with the base plugin's own policy as the full text\n    * Scheduled pauses. Polar and Stripe both stop billing at the end of the period the customer has already paid for rather than the moment the button is pressed, and the store now records that as what it is: a `pause_at_period_end` flag and a `pauses_at` date (migration `subscriptions_0003`), the row still live and still billing, \"Billing pauses on 3 October\" on the customer's page and a **Pausing** flag in the admin, a **Do not pause after all** button on both sides, and `pause_scheduled` \/ `pause_unscheduled` on the timeline. The status moves to `paused` when the platform's own event says billing has actually stopped. A driver that reports the agreement as paused is still honoured immediately\n    * A contract test that runs each shipped recurring driver's own event mapping \u2014 Polar and Stripe, skipped where the checkout is not beside this one \u2014 through the real webhook listener, so a driver whose raw keys the listener cannot read fails a test instead of quietly leaving customers with no billing portal\n    * **The store tells a customer when a provider-billed renewal is declined.** The past-due email belonged to invoice mode alone, so a Stripe subscription whose card failed moved to `past_due` and the store sent nothing at all: the customer found out by happening to open their subscription page, or not at all, while the retries ran out and the agreement expired. The same message now goes out on the delivery that moves the row, without a pay link because there is nothing here to pay, and pointing at the subscription page where **Update payment method** opens the billing portal. That button is the only place a card can be replaced. `emails.provider_past_due` switches the provider-mode half off on its own, for a store whose platform already emails about declined renewals\n    * **The tax a payment platform agreed to bill is kept and put back on every renewal.** A driver that is not the merchant of record now reports the breakdown it priced the subscriber's recurring price with, as `taxLines` on the `SubscriptionResult`, and the add-on writes it onto the subscription row, copies it onto every renewal order that subscription mints, and rebuilds it as ordinary tax adjustments through the calculator, in the same type, label and `source_json` shape a tax zone writes, so receipts, credit notes, refunds and the tax report read a renewal exactly as they read a first purchase. Renewal orders used to carry no tax at all, because a renewal order has no address for a zone to be matched on, so the first period said \u00a37.20, the second said \u00a36.00 and the platform charged \u00a36.00 for both. The rate is the one that was agreed rather than today's: a merchant who edits a rate changes what new subscribers are quoted, and existing agreements keep the figure they were sold until the plan is repriced. The stage stands down on a store running a flat rate, which already taxes every order including renewals\n    * **The store hands a driver the tax it charged on the first period.** The other half of the tax fix below, and without it that half could not fire: nothing told a driver what the store's own calculator had already charged, so a platform billing the plan's untaxed price had nothing to bill differently. `SubscriptionContext::$metadata` now carries `tax_minor` and `tax_lines` on every subscription checkout, computed from the origin order's own tax adjustments, and a driver that is not the merchant of record mints the subscriber's recurring price at the taxed total and reports the same lines back. Both keys are always sent, `0` and an empty list on a store that charges no tax, so a driver reads two keys rather than working out whether they are there. Tax the store prices inclusively is deliberately left out, because it is already inside the price frozen on the subscription row and sending it would charge it twice a period for the life of the agreement. A second attempt at an abandoned trial checkout falls back to the breakdown already agreed on the row, where the order in front of it has been discounted to nothing and has no tax left to read\n    * The driver contract test now covers cancellation as well as activation: a shipped driver whose `deleted` or `revoked` payload does not say `ended` fails, rather than leaving a revoked subscriber with a cancellation dated a month into the future\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * A subscription checkout leaves an id on its purchase row. The pending purchase row for the first period took the agreement's reference, and on a hosted subscription checkout there is no agreement yet, because the platform mints one when the customer finishes paying, so the row was written with nothing on it and a merchant looking at a checkout somebody walked away from had no id to quote at the platform and nothing to search their dashboard for. A driver now reports the checkout's own id on `SubscriptionResult::$transactionRef` and the store writes that, leaving `provider_ref` free for the agreement, which is what every later webhook and the customer's own cancel and pause buttons are matched on. Stripe and Polar both report it; a driver that names no checkout goes on referencing the agreement exactly as before\n    * A refused transition reads as a sentence. The 422 was built from two raw status words, so a merchant who pressed Activate on a running subscription was told \"A active subscription cannot be active.\": wrong article, and a refusal denying the thing it is reporting is already true. Asking for the status a subscription is already in now says so (\"This subscription is already active.\"), every other pair gets the article its status word takes and the action it was actually refused (\"A cancelled subscription cannot be paused.\", \"An expired subscription cannot be renewed.\"), and `past_due` stops appearing as a column name in the middle of a sentence. The `from`, `to` and error code a caller branches on are untouched\n    * The billing portal link works: the return URL handed to the payment platform is now the page's absolute URL, where it used to be root-relative and Polar refused it with nothing in the log, so the \"Update payment method\" button sent the customer straight back with an error\n    * The billing portal is reachable again for every provider. `provider_customer_ref` on a subscription event is now read under the older `customer` spelling too \u2014 which is what every shipped driver was writing, so no Polar subscription ever had a portal \u2014 and a management call whose answer names the customer stamps it onto a row that has none, which is the only door open on a platform that has no customer until the first payment lands\n    * The portal button is drawn from the row rather than by minting a session on every render. Deciding whether to show it used to mean asking the payment platform for a one-shot portal URL on every single page view: the customer waited on that latency to read their own billing history, and a platform having a bad afternoon made the button quietly disappear. The session is now created when the button is pressed\n    * A subscription event the store could not apply is answered 500 so the platform redelivers it, instead of 200 and forgotten. A renewal whose order could not be minted used to leave the money taken, the period unadvanced and nothing but a log line; it now comes back until it lands, and the invoice reference keeps a redelivered renewal to exactly one order. A refusal the state machine will make again \u2014 an out-of-order `past_due` for a paused subscription \u2014 is still logged and answered, because retrying it could never help\n    * A subscription event for an agreement whose activation never arrived can find its row again. The order-hash fallback used to be for `activated` alone, so a store that missed the activation dropped every `past_due`, `cancelled` and `revoked` after it while the row sat at `incomplete` for ever. Any event carrying the hash may now open an `incomplete` row with no conflicting reference, and stamps the reference when it does\n    * A provider that reports a subscription the store has already ended as live again is written onto that subscription's timeline and into the Grav log, with the provider's own status, instead of being ignored in silence. Nothing is reactivated \u2014 a cancellation is final here \u2014 but a platform still billing a customer the store thinks has left is now something a merchant can find. Written once per agreement and event, so a redelivery does not fill the timeline\n    * A checkout the provider refused and the customer retried successfully is put back into provider mode, with the refusal note cleared and the platform that actually took it recorded. The row used to stay in invoice mode behind a live agreement, so the store's own tick would start emailing pay links for periods the platform was already charging the card for\n    * **Two changes to one subscription arriving at once no longer undo each other.** A merchant cancelling while the payment provider's renewal webhook landed could leave the cancelled subscription active again, with a new billing date the merchant had just taken off it. Both writers checked the status against their own copy of the row, and neither held anything while it wrote, so both were told the change was allowed. Every status change now locks the subscription, reads it back and checks the change is still allowed against what it finds before writing, so the second one is refused\n    * A subscription row opened by a refused or abandoned checkout picks up the corrected email address, and the customer id where a guest signed in between attempts. The account tab matches subscriptions to customers by email, so a stale address hid a subscription from the person paying for it\n    * An illegal pause is a 422 with `illegal_transition` whatever the billing mode. The transition is checked before the platform is asked anything, so a provider-mode subscription no longer answers 200 with the refusal buried in the response body\n    * The first period's link row is rewritten to the period the platform actually opened. It kept the period computed at checkout, so the customer's payments table printed dates their own receipt disagreed with \u2014 minutes usually, a whole day for anybody who opened a checkout at ten to midnight and paid after it\n    * A subscription delivery that named an agreement this store has no row for now says so in the delivery log. It used to be written as `subscription:past_due`, character for character the same row a delivery that updated a live subscription wrote, so a merchant whose add-on was switched off, or whose subscription rows were lost in a restore, read a log full of healthy-looking rows and had no way to tell that not one of them did anything. The listener reports the outcome back and the row reads `subscription:past_due:unmatched`\n    * A renewal for a subscription this store has already cancelled is written down instead of dropped in silence. The platform is saying it has taken money for a period, and the store both believes the agreement is over and used to keep no record that the message arrived: no timeline entry, no log line, and a delivery row reading `subscription:renewed` that looked exactly like one that worked. Nothing is reactivated, because a cancellation is final here, but the subscription's timeline and the Grav log now both say what the platform charged and that no renewal order was created, and the delivery log row reads `subscription:renewed:refused`. Written once per invoice, so a redelivery does not repeat it and a second billed period is said again\n    * The platform's id for the customer is taken off every event that carries one, not only the activation. A store that missed an activation delivery, or an agreement older than the release that started reading the key, had no billing portal for the life of the subscription, which is the only place a card can be replaced and the only way a past-due customer recovers. Polar sends the reference on its dunning notice, which is the delivery it matters most on. Only onto a row that has none: an event naming a different customer is a driver bug, not a correction to apply blind\n    * A scheduled pause is dated by the platform rather than by local arithmetic. The date the customer is shown came from this store's own `current_period_end`, which drifts from the platform's calendar, so the page could promise the 14th while the platform took one more payment on the 15th and stopped billing on the 16th. A driver that reports its own effective date now sets the date on the row, and the local period end is the fallback for one that does not\n    * Calling off a scheduled pause records the date that was cleared. The timeline entry carried an empty payload, so it could say a pause had been called off but not which day it was going to happen on, which is the one detail a customer asking about it will name\n    * **Each retry of a failed renewal counts.** Failures are deduplicated on the invoice reference so a redelivery cannot inflate the dunning counter, but a platform retrying the same invoice is not a redelivery: Stripe retried one invoice eight times over three weeks, delivered eight `invoice.payment_failed` events, and the row finished dunning reading `failed_attempts = 1` with one timeline entry for what was really three weeks of trying. The key now includes the try number where a driver names one (`attempt_count` in the event's raw payload, `attempt` accepted as a synonym); a driver that names none keeps exactly the old behavior\n    * **A store with more than two hundred subscriptions falling due at once invoices all of them.** A subscription that has been reminded is still due until it is paid or it lapses, so every sweep picked up the same first two hundred and the customers behind them were never reached at all. A sweep now carries on from where the last one stopped, and starts over from the earliest billing date once it reaches the end\n    * **A renewal invoice is matched to the order it belongs to.** A reference such as `invoice\/2026\/9` is kept inside the order's own stored data, and the way it was looked up could miss it, so a period the platform had already billed could be treated as a new one. The stored references are decoded before they are compared, which is exact\n    * **Two workers writing to one subscription at the same moment cannot lose one of the changes.** Different pieces of the same subscription's stored data, written at the same instant, could overwrite one another on PostgreSQL or fail outright on MariaDB. The update takes a lock on the row before it reads it"}}},"kahunacart-newsletters":{"name":"KahunaCart Newsletters","version":"1.1.8","description":"Email marketing for KahunaCart \u2014 subscribers with a real consent record, lists, branded campaigns, and open and click numbers of your own","description_html":"<p>Email marketing for KahunaCart \u2014 subscribers with a real consent record, lists, branded campaigns, and open and click numbers of your own<\/p>","description_plain":"Email marketing for KahunaCart \u2014 subscribers with a real consent record, lists, branded campaigns, and open and click numbers of your own","icon":"envelope","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.1.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.3.2"},{"name":"email","version":">=5.1.0"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/newsletters","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/newsletters\/overview","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, newsletter, email, marketing, campaigns, subscribers","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-newsletters","license":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/premium\/license","tag_name":"1.1.8","date":"2026-10-02T01:07:12Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-newsletters\/1.1.8","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/newsletters","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-newsletters","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-newsletters","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-newsletters"},"slug":"kahunacart-newsletters","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-newsletters","content_sha256":"447e3bc02cbaa34b98cc21c1bf3092b20c7a9ed4530ba6445ead10fb63f4a306","changelog":{"1.1.8":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.8.** The change below comes with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes\n    * **Help under a field follows the admin's Help text setting.** On the campaign, segment, automation, list and import screens the help under a field now shows below its label or in a tooltip behind an info icon, whichever the admin is set to\n"},"1.1.7":{"date":"09\/29\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.7.** The change below comes with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, route, permission and MCP tool name is the same\n    * **Newsletters name the store the way the store's own emails do.** Campaigns and the signup confirmation called the store by the site's title, so on a site where the store is one part of something bigger the footer read \"\u00a9 2026 Grav CMS\", the confirmation said \"start hearing from Grav CMS\" and `{{ store.name }}` gave \"Grav CMS\". They now follow KahunaCart's **Name the Store in Emails** (`emails.use_store_name`, KahunaCart 1.3.6): with it on, the header, the footer, the confirmation's subject and text, and `store.name` all use the store's name (`seller.name`, then `invoice.store_name`, then the site title), exactly as the order emails do. It's off by default, and on KahunaCart 1.3.5 and older, which have no such setting, nothing changes\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **One name on the Account page, not two.** The Account page had Profile's **Full name** (the account's) and, in Email preferences, **Your name** (the subscriber's), which saved to different places and drifted apart. For a signed-in customer the Email preferences panel no longer has a name box: the subscriber takes the account's name when the panel saves, and again whenever the profile is saved. An account with no name leaves the subscriber's name alone. The token preference center and signup forms keep their name box. `partials\/newsletter-account-tab.html.twig` no longer draws the field; a theme copy that still does is heard only when the account has no name\n"},"1.1.6":{"date":"09\/28\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.6.** The changes below come with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, permission and MCP tool name is the same; the one new admin route has no MCP tool\n    * **See the email a campaign sent, and any one customer's copy of it.** A campaign that has gone out has a Show the email button, which opens the email on the campaign's screen with a sample subscriber. Every recipient it went to has a View button in the recipient table, and so does every row of a subscriber's \"What they were sent\"; either opens that person's own copy, with their name, the subject line a subject test gave them and any coupon code minted for them. The layout and their name are as they are today. Viewing it is never an open or a click, and the unsubscribe and preferences links in it do nothing, so nobody can be unsubscribed from the admin by accident\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **View in browser shows the reader's own copy.** The \"having trouble reading this?\" page showed a sample coupon code and always the first subject line of a subject test. It now shows the code minted for that reader and the subject line they were sent\n    * **The confirmation page's tab title reads properly.** After the signup link confirmed someone, the browser tab showed the page title as the theme wrote it in HTML, so a theme that separates the page and site name with `&middot;` showed \"You are on the list &middot; HB Tack\". It now shows \"You are on the list \u00b7 HB Tack\"\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"09\/26\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.5.** The change below comes with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, route, permission and MCP tool name is the same\n    * **Every table sorts by its headings.** Press a column's heading to sort the table by it, and press it again to reverse the order. Dates, counts and rates start with the biggest, and words start from A; an empty value, like a campaign nobody has opened yet, always goes last. Subscribers, a list's people, Campaigns, a campaign's recipients, Suppressions and an automation's people sort on the server, so the order covers everybody and not just the page on screen, and it stays in the address so a reload or Back keeps it. Every other table sorts where it is. Each table opens in its usual order until somebody presses a heading\n    * **The MCP list tools take the same sort.** `list_subscribers`, `list_campaigns`, `list_campaign_recipients`, `list_suppressions` and `get_automation` accept an optional `sort` and `dir`\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"09\/26\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.4.** The changes below come with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, route, permission and MCP tool name is the same\n    * **A signup to more than one list says \"the lists\".** Checking two lists on the signup form, or naming two in the subscribe route's `lists` or the `newsletter` form action's `lists`, was answered \"...to be added to the list\" or \"You are on the list\". It now says \"the lists\"; one list checked is still \"the list\"\n    * **Rates keep their decimal.** Open, click, bounce and unsubscribe rates on the campaigns list, a campaign's own screen, the reports and the chart tooltips read \"8.0%\" beside \"0.2%\", rather than \"8%\", and the campaign performance report's tooltip has its percent sign\n    * **The Postmark card says what to do.** Before its server token is saved, Postmark has no Set up button, and its card had an empty gap where the card beside it says nothing is registered yet. It now says \"Add this address in Postmark by hand. The steps are below.\"\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/26\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.3.** The changes below come with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, route, permission and MCP tool name is the same\n    * **A signup's answer appears where the person is looking.** When the signup form takes an address, its fields and button give way to the answer in place, and the answer takes the focus so a screen reader reads it out; it used to be printed under the button, often out of view. A refusal scrolls into view and leaves the fields to correct. The partial's markup is unchanged; only its script moved, so a theme's copy of the partial keeps working as it did\n    * **Checkboxes are checked, not ticked.** The preference center and the account's Email preferences say \"Choose what you want to hear about.\", a signup refused for an unchecked consent box says \"Please check the box\", and the checkout opt-in note, the automations' per-run help and the no-events alert say checked, run and selected\n    * **One name for the preference center.** A subscriber's consent history and their Lists table now both say \"Preference center\" (the table said \"Preferences\")\n    * **Report charts write dates the way the rest of the admin does**, \"Jul 12\" on the campaigns report and on list growth (they read \"12 Jul\" and \"2026-07-12\"). The campaigns report's chart names its points by date (`Y-m-d`) for an MCP caller too\n    * **`#\/health` opens the Health tab**, the same as `#\/deliverability`\n    * **Under \"Sent to\", something the number does not already say**: the lists a campaign went to, or \"of 298\" while it is still sending, rather than \"298 total\". An automation's Left card reads \"Most often: marked it as spam\", its Moving card \"On a step the worker runs next\", and a webhook address wraps where its line is full rather than after the first hyphen\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **An automation names a tag by its name.** Its trigger read \"When the tag win-back is added\" and its tag steps \"Add the tag win-back\", the code the automation stores; they now use the tag's name, as the add-to-list step already names its list. The flows answers carry it as `trigger_name` and a tag step's `tag_name`\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Built on the newsletter kit 1.0.2.** The fixes below come with it. Nothing about a store's setup changes, and every address, route, permission and MCP tool name is the same\n    * **American spelling throughout.** Everything a merchant or a subscriber reads, in the admin, the public pages, the emails, the command help, the MCP descriptions and the docs, now says center, color, canceled, enroll, recognize and percent (the preference center, \"Enroll somebody\", a canceled campaign, \"When a subscription is canceled\"). Translation keys, config keys, stored values such as the `cancelled` status and the `subscription_cancelled` trigger, and URLs are unchanged, so nothing a store saved or linked to moves. The demo seed's copy follows (\"in two new colors\")\n    * **The MCP manifest names every provider.** `generate_provider_secret` and `set_up_provider_webhook` take all seven providers (`mailersend` was missing from the first, and the second said SMTP2GO was the only provider whose API could create its webhook, when SendGrid, Mailgun, Resend, MailerSend and Amazon SES can with the key on file and Postmark with its server token), and `create_list` and `update_list` take `public`, which the API always accepted. `bin\/generate-mcp.php` now fills the template with the kit's own filler, with every capability and a store's words, and writes the same manifest otherwise\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A signup form's own consent sentence is the one recorded.** A form given its own `consent_text` printed it, but the store's sentence was what got recorded against the person. The signup partial now carries the sentence it shows back to the route, signed with the site secret (a hidden `consent_form` field), and that sentence is recorded. A form with an empty `consent_text` shows the store's sentence while the store has one, rather than drawing no box, and the partial's script sends `consent` only when there is a box to tick. A theme's copy of the partial made before this keeps working and records the store's sentence; copy the new one to record a form's own\n    * **A new campaign starts from Track Opens and Track Clicks.** The campaign editor ticked both for every new campaign, whatever the settings said; it now starts from the settings\n    * **A campaign's cards and its chart count the same people.** Somebody who opened a message and then marked it as spam was counted by the chart and not by the Opened and Delivered cards. Both now count the delivered, opened and clicked stamps, and an open or a click after a complaint is counted like any other\n    * **A segment's editor says when the segments list shows another count.** The list shows the count last taken and the editor counts now, so the two could disagree with nothing on screen to say why; the editor now says so, and Recount brings the list up to date\n    * **The send-times heatmap gives the day names room**, so Wednesday no longer runs over the first hour, and a fresh report read in one millisecond says \"1 millisecond\"\n    * **An automation's steps read as the store would send them.** An email step shows its subject with the store's name filled in, and an add-to-list step names the list rather than its code\n    * **The Add people dialog's help describes both choices** rather than the one the list's setting picked, and what a bulk action or Add people did is drawn in the success colors when it worked\n    * **A draft campaign offers Delete, not \"Cancel it\".** Cancel is offered only to a campaign on its way (scheduled, sending, paused or stopped on failures)\n    * **The automations report names only the exit reasons there are.** Its definition listed \"no longer matching the automation's segment\", which never happens, and left out a suppressed address, a failed step and a deletion\n    * **The Welcome series recipe points at a trigger that exists.** Its note told a store without double opt-in to switch to \"When somebody joins a list\"; it now says \"When somebody signs up\"\n    * **The demo seed tells a whole story.** `bin\/plugin kahunacart-newsletters seed` now writes a consent history for every seeded person, the sends, opens and clicks of an automation's email steps, and dates nothing after the moment it ran; segments are counted after the automations have tagged people. Its rows differ from 1.1.1's for the same seed\n    * **Wording.** `export:csv --search` matches anywhere in an address or a name, and its help now says so (it said \"starting with\"); the From Name help says a campaign can use a different name in its editor, and a list through the API\n    * **Removed `unsubscribe.confirm_on_get`.** The setting was never read: the unsubscribe link has always opened a page with a button. It is gone from the Signup settings, `kahunacart-newsletters.yaml` and the docs, and a value left in a site's config does nothing, as before\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A subscriber's page shows every list and edits tags in place.** Each list is drawn with where the person stands on it (subscribed, waiting to confirm, left, or not on it) with Add to list or Remove beside it, and a list they left themselves says so and offers nothing. Tags are chips you can take off one by one, with a box that adds an existing tag or a new one\n    * **Bulk actions on subscribers.** The Subscribers table has a tick box per row, one for the page, \"Select all N matching these filters\" and a bar that adds people to a list or takes them off one, adds or removes a tag, unsubscribes them from everything, or deletes them with a counted confirm. A big selection is worked through 500 at a time with a running count. The same is `POST \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/subscribers\/bulk` (`kahunacart.newsletters.manage`) and the MCP tool `bulk_subscribers`\n    * **A list has its own page.** Clicking a list on the Audience tab opens it with its name, description and settings, an Edit button, Add people (paste addresses, optionally with names) and its people in the subscribers table, with chips for where they stand on the list and the same bulk bar\n    * **Adding somebody asks how they agreed.** Every add from the admin (Add somebody, Add to list on a person's page, a selection, pasted addresses) offers \"Send them an invite to confirm\" or \"Add as subscribed, they already agreed\" with a required note saying where. The default follows the list's own \"Ask people to confirm\". Suppressed, bounced and complained addresses are never added, and anybody who left the list, or every list, themselves is skipped with the reason\n    * **The Audience tab's counts open the table they count.** A list's count opens the Subscribers table filtered to the people subscribed to that list, a tag's count to the people carrying it, and the filter is drawn so it can be seen and cleared; the filters travel in the address, so Back and reload keep them. `GET \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/subscribers` takes `list_status` beside `list`, and so does the MCP tool `list_subscribers`\n    * **Every change to somebody's consent is kept.** Each signup, confirmation, admin add or invite, unsubscribe, return to a list, import row, bounce, complaint and automation \"add to list\" writes one row into a consent history (who, when, which list, how, the admin's note, and the address and browser a person's own change came from), never capped or rewritten. It is shown under Consent on the subscriber's page, answered as `consent_log`, and deleted with the person. Migration `newsletters_0013_consent_events`\n    * **A campaign can go to several lists, or to every list with a segment.** The campaign editor keeps its \"Send it to\" list and gains \"Also send to\" boxes for the others and an \"All lists, with a segment\" choice, with a live count of who it would reach. Each person gets one email however many of the lists they are on, and the unsubscribe link takes them off the campaign's lists they are on and leaves their others alone. The API and the MCP tools take `list_ids`, and `GET \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/campaigns\/audience` counts a choice before it is saved. A campaign to one list is stored and sent exactly as before. Migration `newsletters_0014_campaign_lists`\n    * **`site.name` and `site.url` merge tags.** They read the same two values as `store.name` and `store.url`, and a template may use either spelling. The editors keep offering `store.*`\n    * **Export one person's data.** A subscriber's page has \"Export their data\", which downloads one JSON file with everything the newsletter holds about them: profile, lists, tags, consent history, every send with its opens and clicks, what the mail provider reported, automation walks, suppressions and the imports that named them. The same is `GET \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/subscribers\/{id}\/export` (`kahunacart.newsletters.manage`, like the CSV export) and the MCP tool `export_subscriber_data`\n    * **One signup can join several lists.** The public subscribe route takes `lists` beside `list`, the Form plugin's `newsletter` action takes a `lists` parameter, and `kahunacart_newsletter_form()` a `lists` option, which draws the lists as boxes to tick (named by their list names, none ticked for them) and sends only the ones ticked; at least one has to be. A form with one list draws exactly the markup it always did. Each list keeps its own double opt-in, one confirmation email covers them all, a code that names no list is left out, and one signup counts once against the rate limit\n    * **The import preview says what the run would do.** Before you press Start, the admin's import screen shows how many people would be new, already here, skipped and suppressed against the list you picked, and why (duplicates in the file, people who left the list themselves, suppressed addresses and so on), the same count `import:csv --dry-run` makes\n    * **An import can say where its people agreed.** A new \"Where did these people agree?\" field on the import screen (`basis` on the API, `--basis` on `import:csv`) is kept in each imported person's consent history. Left empty, the file's name is kept there, as before\n    * **A file headed plain `email` imports without picking a column.** Headings that plainly name a field (`email`, `E-mail`, `Email address`, `Name`, `First name`, `Last name` and the like) are picked for you, on the import screen and on the command line\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **One-click unsubscribe now leaves only the campaign's lists.** The Unsubscribe button a mail client shows beside a campaign (Gmail, Apple Mail and others) now takes the person off the lists that campaign went to and leaves their other lists alone, the same as the unsubscribe link at the bottom of the email. It used to take them off every list. Confirmation emails and automation emails still take them off every list.\n    * **Requires KahunaCart 1.3.2 or later.** KahunaCart 1.3.2 brings the trusted site address (Site Addresses, `security.site_urls`), which the double opt-in confirmation now uses: its link is built only on an address the store vouches for, and with none set the email waits on the queue, without using up its attempts, until there is one, rather than going out with a link nobody can press or one on whatever host a signup claimed. KahunaCart fills the address in from an administrator's own on their first dashboard visit\n    * **The public newsletter pages answer 503 while migrations are pending.** On a MySQL or PostgreSQL store, which migrates by hand, the subscribe, confirm, unsubscribe, preference, tracking and webhook addresses answer 503 with `Retry-After` until `bin\/plugin kahunacart migrate` has run, rather than a 404 or new code running over the old tables. A person gets a page saying to try again in a few minutes, a mail provider's one-click POST and webhooks get a bare 503 they retry, and the open pixel still answers its image. One line goes to the log. A store that is up to date pays nothing for the check\n    * **API changes a store's own scripts may notice.** `POST \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/subscribers` without a `mode` now follows the list's double opt-in (an invite on a list that asks), `mode: subscribed` needs a `note`, and it refuses (422) somebody who unsubscribed themselves. `POST \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/subscribers\/{id}\/resubscribe` needs a `note` and no longer takes them off the lists it was not asked about. The bulk route's unsubscribe or delete over an empty filter needs `all: true`. The MCP tools `add_subscriber`, `update_subscriber` and `resubscribe_subscriber` describe the new rules\n    * **Rate limits.** A mail client's one-click unsubscribe with a valid link is never rate limited, so a busy send no longer leaves people who pressed the button still subscribed. A signup through the Form plugin's `newsletter` action now counts in the same per-visitor subscribe limit as the public signup route (10 an hour by default)\n    * **A confirmation an admin or an import asked for waits for the click.** The confirmation page presses its own button for somebody who signed up themselves; for somebody an admin invited or an import put on a list, it now waits for them to press it, so a link scanner that runs scripts cannot sign up a person who never filled in a form\n    * **Mail provider events no longer keep the address.** A bounce, complaint or delivery report is stored without the email address (the hashed address was always kept for matching), an erasure now deletes the person's provider events too, and migration `newsletters_0015_event_addresses` takes the address out of the events stored before\n    * **A retried or replayed provider event counts once.** Providers retry webhooks, and every copy used to be a new row, so one temporary failure sent three times counted as three soft bounces and suppressed the address. The same event again is now recorded as already received and changes nothing, and `webhook:replay` says so instead of \"would suppress\". Migration `newsletters_0016_event_keys`\n    * **A signup through the site's own form is recorded as `form`.** A signup through the signup partial, with JavaScript or without, or through a theme's own form on the site, was recorded as an `api` signup. JSON from anywhere else (a script on another server) is still `api`, and a caller may name it with a new optional `source` field, `form` or `api`; any other value is ignored\n    * **What a subscriber reads.** The confirmation page's button says \"Yes, sign me up\" rather than \"Yes, keep me on the list\", the unsubscribe page says \"Done, you are off the list\", and the campaign's plain-text footer reads \"Example Shop (https:\/\/example.com\/shop)\"\n    * **Refusing a suppressed address says so plainly.** Adding, putting back or confirming a suppressed address now answers \"This address is on the suppression list, so it is never mailed. Remove it from Suppressions first.\", a string a site can reword in its language file\n    * **The Health tab's percentages.** \"0.3%%\" on the Health tab and in `deliverability:check` is now \"0.3%\"\n    * **Built on version 1.0.1 of the newsletter kit**, pinned exactly\n    * **No em dashes in the admin's words.** The admin screens' sentences that carried one (the Health tab's findings, the report definitions, the import and segment hints and a few more) now read with plain punctuation\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Saving a subscriber who was waiting to confirm took them off their list.** The edit form's list boxes were ticked only for subscribed lists, so a pending one went up unticked and the save unsubscribed them from it. The form now saves only the name and the language; lists and tags have their own panels\n    * **Nobody who left by their own hand is put back by anybody else.** An admin's Add somebody, Add to list, the subscriber form's lists, a bulk add, an automation's \"add to list\" step and a CSV or customer import all skip a person who unsubscribed from that list, or from everything, themselves, and say why. An admin's Unsubscribe or a suppression pressed on somebody already gone no longer writes over how they left, which is what used to let the admin's own add put them straight back. \"Put them back on\" with a note saying they asked is the one way past it\n    * **The admin's CSV import did the opposite of its \"Ask everybody to confirm\" box.** Left off, as its hint says to for an export from another tool, it imported everybody waiting to confirm and emailed each of them a confirmation; ticked, it imported them as already agreed. It now does what the box says. The API and the command line were right all along\n    * **The campaign audience sample showed no names.** The people listed under a campaign's audience count now show their names\n    * **An import no longer rewrites how somebody joined a list they were already on.** Importing a person already on the list set that membership's source to `import`; it is now left as it was\n    * **The mail client's unsubscribe button works with a From address written with a name.** With the From email set as `Name <address>`, the `List-Unsubscribe` header carried the name inside its mailto, which no mail client can use. It now carries the address only; the one-click HTTPS unsubscribe was not affected\n    * **Somebody already subscribed who signed up for another list that asks people to confirm was never asked.** They were left waiting on that list with no email to answer, and anybody who typed their address into the form could put them there. They now get the confirmation email for that list and stay waiting until they press it; the page says the same as it does for anybody else\n    * **The customer import's preview counted people who had left the list themselves as already here.** The run skips them, so the preview now does too, against the list picked below it (it counts again when you pick another), with a \"Left the list themselves\" figure beside the others\n    * **The Subscribers table left out the lists somebody was waiting to confirm.** The Lists column now names them too, marked \"(pending)\", so somebody who has not confirmed yet is not shown as on no list\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The newsletter engine is a shared package, bundled inside the plugin.** The engine now comes from Trilby Media's newsletter kit, which the plugin ships in `vendor-prefixed\/` under the same class names as before and wired to the database classes KahunaCart bundles, so it still shares KahunaCart's connection, migrator and job queue. Nothing changes for a store: tables, migrations, routes, settings, links already mailed and class names stay exactly as they were\n    * **Requires KahunaCart 1.3.0 or later and Grav 2.1.0 or later.** KahunaCart 1.3.0 is the first release that bundles the database classes the engine is built on, and it needs Grav 2.1.0. With an older KahunaCart the plugin stays off and says so once in the Grav log\n    * **KahunaCart's segment fields, flow triggers, merge tags, coupons, customer import and store recipes are added by the plugin's KahunaCart side.** The nine customer and order segment fields, the seven order, cart and subscription triggers, the three `customer.*` merge tags, coupon minting, the customer import (its two admin routes, its job and `import:customers`), the customer card route and the post-purchase and win-back recipes now reach the newsletter engine through a small registry, so the engine no longer reads a KahunaCart table itself. Field codes, trigger codes, tags, labels, routes, job types, recipe keys, their order in the editors and the SQL a segment runs are all unchanged, so saved segments, flows and templates keep working as they are. `GET \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/labels` also answers `capabilities`, saying which of customers, products, coupons and subscriptions the site has (all four on a KahunaCart store). The segment recount's \"last queued\" mark moved from KahunaCart's settings table to the newsletter's own state table, so the first worker run after updating recounts segments once.\n    * **The newsletter emails ship with the engine.** `emails\/newsletter-campaign`, `emails\/newsletter-confirmation` and `emails\/newsletter-plain-layout.html.twig` moved from `templates\/emails\/` into the engine's own resources, which the plugin adds to Twig after its own `templates\/`, and the demo recipe and newsletter page stub moved with them. Template names are unchanged, a theme's copy of any of them still wins, and every email renders byte for byte as before\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The providers card shows the new state straight after \"Set up\".** The three settings fields shared one helper, and whichever loaded first built it for all three. When the transport or worker field loaded first, the providers card's refresh after setting up a webhook came back from the cache and kept showing the old state until the page was reloaded. All three fields now carry the same helper.\n    * **A signup through a Form plugin form records the same address as every other signup.** The newsletter form action stamped the consent record with Grav's client address, while the public signup route and the rate limiter used KahunaCart's, so on a store behind a proxy the two could disagree and the consent record could hold the proxy's address instead of the visitor's. The form action now uses KahunaCart's as well, which honours a forwarded address only from a proxy listed in `security.trusted_proxies`. A store behind a proxy or CDN that relied on Grav's `system.http_x_forwarded` setting (with a header such as `X-Forwarded-For` or `CF-Connecting-IP` switched on there, Grav took the address from that header on any request, whoever sent it) should list the proxy in KahunaCart's `security.trusted_proxies`, since consent records now use KahunaCart's trusted-proxy rule for both the storefront form and the Form-plugin action.\n"},"1.0.6":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The storefront stylesheet is no longer added twice on the newsletter pages.** The subscribe, confirm, unsubscribe and preference pages put KahunaCart's own stylesheet on the page themselves, and looked for the file one folder too high to stamp it, so the link never matched the one KahunaCart adds and the browser downloaded the same sheet twice. It now finds the file through Grav, and each page links it once.\n"},"1.0.5":{"date":"09\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A failed send can be tried again, one at a time or all together.** A send the mailer refused, usually because the transport could not be reached for a moment, stayed failed for good, and the only way to reach that person was a new campaign. A failed row on a campaign's Recipients table now has a **Retry** button, and a sent campaign with failures has **Retry failed (N)** beside its other buttons. Only failed rows go back in the queue (bounces and complaints are never retried). The campaign runs again as the same campaign with the same report, nobody already mailed gets it twice, consent and suppression are checked again at delivery, and the counters are recounted when it finishes. `POST \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/campaigns\/{id}\/retry-failed` with an optional `send_id`, and the `retry_failed_campaign_sends` MCP tool\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **A scheduled campaign's screen leads with Edit, not Send it now.** The campaign is already on its way, so the orange button is **Edit** and **Send it now** is a plain button beside it. A draft keeps **Send it now** as the primary\n    * **The Sent to card on a scheduled campaign says who is queued and when**, as \"50 queued for Sep 22, 2026, 2:00 AM\" (`ADMIN_CAMPAIGN_QUEUED_FOR`). It read \"No list chosen\" even with a list on the campaign and the audience already taken. A campaign with a list but no count yet now says the audience is worked out when it starts, and \"No list chosen\" is kept for a campaign that really has none\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A scheduled campaign now sends when its time comes.** Scheduling wrote the campaign as `scheduled` and queued its job for that moment, but nothing ever moved it on to `sending`, so the job woke up on time, found a campaign that was not `sending`, decided it was not its campaign and finished without mailing anybody. The job the campaign names now starts it when the moment has arrived, and waits if it wakes a little early. A job left behind by a reschedule still does nothing. Campaigns sent straight away were never affected\n    * **Editing a scheduled campaign shows when it is scheduled for.** The campaign editor always opened with an empty **Send at** box, so a campaign that had been scheduled looked like it had lost its time, and the button under the empty box read **Send it now**, one click from sending it hours early. A scheduled campaign now opens with its moment in the box in the browser's local time, and the button reads **Schedule it**. The schedule itself was always saved correctly\n    * **The Schedule it button lines up with the Send at box** instead of sitting halfway up its label\n    * **A campaign's Recipients chips name send statuses, not subscriber ones.** People queued for a campaign are `pending` sends, and the chip above them read \"Waiting to confirm\", the word for a subscriber who has not confirmed yet, even though everybody in the audience is confirmed. It now reads \"Waiting\", the same as the rows under it\n"},"1.0.4":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Customer groups are read from KahunaCart's membership table, so a customer in several groups is in all of them here too.** KahunaCart 1.2.11 lets a customer be in more than one group and keeps memberships in `kahunacart_customer_group_members` rather than a column on the customer. The **Customer group** segment condition and the customers import's group filter now match a customer in that group whatever else they are in, and the `customer.group` merge tag lists every group the customer is in, in the store's order. Requires KahunaCart 1.2.11\n"},"1.0.3":{"date":"09\/15\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A campaign that has gone out can be sent on to the people who joined afterwards, from a button on the campaign itself.** An audience is a snapshot taken the moment a campaign starts, which is what makes pause, resume and \"nobody is mailed twice\" work \u2014 and it also means the two people who signed up the evening of a launch never receive the announcement that made them sign up. The only answer until now was a segment saying \"was not sent campaign 2\" and a hand-copied duplicate of the campaign pointed at it, which leaves a store with two campaigns, two recipient tables and two sets of rates for one announcement. **Send to newcomers** sits beside the other buttons on a sent campaign, asks a question that names the number \u2014 \"3 people joined after this went out and never received it. Send it to them now?\" \u2014 and then re-runs the audience snapshot, which adds only the people who have joined the list and match the segment since, as pending rows, and leaves everybody already mailed exactly as they are. The campaign sends to them and finishes as sent again, with its counters recounted from the recipient rows, so it stays one campaign with one report. Nobody receives it twice, and nobody the campaign decided *not* to mail the first time is reconsidered \u2014 somebody suppressed has a row saying so, and a second pass does not offer to break that. A campaign that hands everybody their own coupon code mints a fresh one for each newcomer and changes nobody else's; a campaign whose subject test already picked a winner sends the winner rather than re-opening the test or making three people wait four hours for it; a campaign whose segment has since gone away is refused with the reason rather than quietly widening to the whole list. The button is drawn disabled with \"Everybody on the list has had this.\" when nobody has joined since, and the campaign's own screen keeps the record \u2014 \"Sent to newcomers 2 times, last on 14 Sep 2026\". Two routes, `GET` to count and `POST` to send, and two MCP tools beside them; migration `newsletters_0012` adds the count and the date. The **Was sent campaign** segment condition is unchanged and is still the way to build a different follow-up\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **The Send it panel is on a new campaign too, saying what it is waiting for.** A campaign that had not been saved yet drew a Save button and then a stray sentence about a test box that was not on the screen, so the Send at field \u2014 which only appears once a campaign exists \u2014 looked like something the add-on did not have, and a merchant writing their first campaign concluded it could not schedule anything. A new campaign now gets the **Send it** panel in the place the real one will be, reading \"Save the campaign first. Once it is saved you can send yourself a test copy, send it to the list straight away, or pick a date and time and schedule it.\" (`ADMIN_CAMPAIGN_SEND_AFTER_SAVE`). The same panel keeps its heading on a saved campaign whose status will not let it start \u2014 sending, paused, sent or cancelled \u2014 with one line naming that status and pointing at the campaign's own screen, instead of the panel quietly disappearing (`ADMIN_CAMPAIGN_SEND_BLOCKED`). Sending, scheduling, the API and the state machine are all unchanged\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Opening Campaigns straight after Subscribers no longer breaks the screen.** The Subscribers screen carries a short list of campaigns for its Sent \/ Not sent picker, trimmed to id and name, and the Campaigns tab drew that list as campaign rows for the instant before its own answer arrived, which threw on the missing counters and left the tab blank. Switching to a different screen now shows the skeleton instead of the previous screen's data; reloading the same screen still keeps its table on the page. The campaign editor's \"already fetched\" check was trusting the same trimmed list, so a new campaign opened from there could have no templates to pick from; it asks for the full index now\n"},"1.0.2":{"date":"09\/11\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Subscribers can be filtered by whether a campaign was sent to them.** Pick a campaign on the Subscribers screen and choose Sent or Not sent, and the list and its count follow. The same two parameters work on the subscribers API, so a follow-up to the people a campaign never reached is a filter, not a spreadsheet. The same question is a segment condition too, **Was sent campaign**, so a broadcast's audience can be everybody on one list who was not sent a particular campaign\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * Searching subscribers matches anywhere in the address or the name. It matched only the start, so `mac` did not find `rhuk@mac.com`\n"},"1.0.1":{"date":"09\/10\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Email preferences moved off the account tab bar and onto the Account page.** A customer's account had ten tabs across it and two of them wrapped onto a second line, which is how a row of links stops being a row of links. The preferences form is now a panel on the **Account** page, under the password form, with the same lists, the same checkboxes and the same save button. Nothing about the route changed: `{base}\/account\/email-preferences` is still where the form posts and still where the save is handled, behind the account area's own nonce and login check, and saving now returns the customer to the Account page they were standing on rather than to a page with nothing linking to it. The panel wants KahunaCart 1.2.1, which is where a section learned to skip the navigation, and the declared dependency stays at 1.2.0 because this degrades on its own: on an older base plugin the customer gets the tab exactly as before, so a site that updates one plugin this week and the other next week is never missing the screen\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Email preferences no longer offer a name field it cannot keep.** The Save button was drawn only under a list, so a store with no public list showed a name field and no button; and for a customer who is not yet a subscriber, saving a name with nothing ticked wrote nothing, because a subscriber row is only ever opened by ticking a list. The panel now shows the button whenever there is a form, and shows only \"There is nothing to subscribe to at the moment\" to a non-subscriber on a store with no public list\n    * **A test send or a preview of a campaign that gives everybody their own code no longer arrives with a blank where the code goes.** Those codes are minted per person as each message is built, so a test copy, which has no send row, rendered `coupon.code` as nothing and `coupon.url` as an empty link, which is the one thing a test send exists to catch. A test copy now mints one real code from the campaign's group, the same way the real send will, so the person reading it can click the link and watch the code land in a basket; it costs one code from the group, which the budget does not count until somebody redeems it. The preview pane shows the group's own shape with every generated character as an `X`, `EARLY-XXXX-XXXX`, and mints nothing, because it redraws on every keystroke\n    * **The logo in the header of a campaign no longer arrives broken.** The base plugin's email layout resolves the logo path and the footer link against the site root, and the campaign renderer was handing it the store URL instead, so a logo at `\/user\/images\/logo.png` was asked for at `\/shop\/user\/images\/logo.png`. The layout is given the site root now, and `store.url` in the message is unchanged\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"09\/08\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The add-on is called Newsletters now, plural, like Licenses and Subscriptions.** One name for the three add-ons, so the sidebar, the docs and the settings form all read the same way. It is a rename all the way down: the plugin is `kahunacart-newsletters`, its config file is `user\/config\/plugins\/kahunacart-newsletters.yaml`, its admin section is at `#\/section\/newsletters`, its API routes are under `\/kahunacart\/newsletters\/`, its permissions are `kahunacart.newsletters.view`, `.manage` and `.send`, and its tables are `kahunacart_newsletters_*`. An existing store needs `bin\/plugin kahunacart migrate`, which renames the tables in place and keeps every row, and then two things that cannot be done for you: the three permissions are new keys, so re-grant them to the accounts and groups that had the old ones, and a store that had written its own `kahunacart-newsletter.yaml` has to rename that file too, or its settings go back to the defaults. What people see on the storefront does not move \u2014 the signup page is still `\/newsletter`, the Twig function is still `kahunacart_newsletter_form()`, the Form plugin action is still `newsletter`, and the default list is still `newsletter` \u2014 because a store has one newsletter its customers join\n    * **The store's own customers, on the mailing list, from the Import screen.** A store that added the newsletter three years after it started selling has a customers table full of people who never saw the checkout opt-in box, and they are the best list it will ever have. **Newsletters \u2192 Subscribers \u2192 Import** now asks where the people come from before it asks anything else: a file from another tool, or your customers. Choose who \u2014 bought at least once, bought a given product or anything in a category, in a customer group, ordered in the last so many days or since a date, has an account or checked out as a guest, all optional and all combined with AND \u2014 and the preview says how many matched, how many would be added, how many are already subscribers, how many are suppressed or have bounced, and how many have no usable address, with twenty of them named. Then pick one of two things: **Invite them**, the default, which emails each customer a confirmation and subscribes only the ones who click; or **Add them as subscribed**, which writes them in straight away with one honest sentence beside it about needing their permission first and nothing else \u2014 no modal, no second question, because they are your customers and it is your call. Under the hood it is the same import run as a file, so a suppressed address is still never written, a bounce is still never revived, nobody already subscribed is sent back to pending, and the run lands in the same history with the same counters. Everybody written is tagged \u2014 `customer` unless you rename it \u2014 so the batch is a segment the moment it lands, and is joined to their customer record on the spot so a \"bought X\" segment answers for them the same day. Their source reads `customers` rather than `import`, because \"we bought this list\" and \"these people bought something from us\" are different answers to the same support question. Two routes and two tools \u2014 the preview is a `.view` and works on the read-only demo, since it writes nothing at all \u2014 plus `bin\/plugin kahunacart-newsletters import:customers`, which prints the preview and asks before it writes unless `--yes`. One thing quietly improved for file imports too: a repeat run no longer puts a second copy of the same confirmation email in somebody's inbox\n    * **A Reports tab that answers \"why\", where the Overview answers \"how is it going\".** Seven reports over whatever window of dates a merchant picks: campaign performance, one row per campaign sent in the range with its five rates and a totals row that divides the totals rather than averaging six percentages one of which was a test send to four people; list growth per day, week or month with a column for every way somebody can arrive; engagement, which is the report that says who to win back and who to stop mailing; the most pressed links against the share of that campaign's opens who pressed them; bounces and complaints by receiving domain and by transport; per automation, who went in, who is walking it now and why the rest came out; and send times as a CSS heatmap of when people actually read, in the store's own clock. Every figure carries the sentence that defines it, printed on the screen and answered in the payload, because the definition is the thing somebody argues with when they disagree with a number. Every rate is answered with the counts it is made of beside it and is a dash rather than 0% where there was nothing to divide by. The whole state lives in the address \u2014 `reports\/list-growth?from=2026-06-01&to=2026-09-05&bucket=week&list=2` \u2014 so a report somebody has built is a URL they can send to whoever asked for it, and every one of them exports to CSV with the same headings in the same order. Two `GET` routes and one MCP tool, all three `.view`: nothing on this screen writes anything, which is what lets a merchant hand it to somebody without also handing them the ability to send mail. Answers are cached for five minutes keyed on the whole question and say how old they are; Refresh throws away the one on screen and nothing else. No money on any of it \u2014 what a campaign earned is Analytics, which knows about refunds \u2014 and no comparison period, because two windows somebody chose by hand side by side is how a screen ends up lying politely. Migration `newsletter_0008` adds the two indexes the reports read, on sends by date and subscribers by date, so a year of history answers in the same time as a month\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-newsletters seed` fills an empty newsletter with a store somebody has been running for three months.** Every screen this add-on adds opens on an empty table until a merchant has built an audience and sent to it, which is a poor first hour for anybody evaluating it and an impossible one for a public demo whose database is rebuilt every hour. The command writes about four hundred people who joined on different days for different reasons, three lists, seven tags, three templates, seven campaigns \u2014 five sent with believable open, click, bounce and unsubscribe rates spread over their first forty-eight hours, one of them a subject test with a winner, one carrying a coupon and UTM tagging, one draft and one waiting for its moment \u2014 four segments with their counts filled in, the three automations this plugin ships as recipes plus a draft, and a suppression list with both kinds of row on it. Idempotent by code and name, so a demo can run it on a timer; `--purge` takes back exactly what it wrote and nothing of the merchant's; `--days` decides how much history. **Nothing is sent**: no mailer, no queue, no scheduler, a seeded campaign written straight to `sent` with its counters rebuilt from its own send rows, and every live automation enrolment due days from now. Deliveries, bounces and complaints go in through the same webhook handler a real provider's events go through, so a campaign's Accepted and Bounced figures come from the same place they do on a live store, and every address is under `example.com`, which RFC 2606 reserves so that no seeded row can reach a real inbox\n    * **Where a provider's answers come from is now that provider's own transport plugin.** Everything a mail provider knows about itself \u2014 how its delivery webhooks are verified and read, how a webhook is created from a pasted API key, what a sending domain's DNS has to say, what its transport does to custom headers on the way out \u2014 has moved behind a contract the Grav Email plugin owns, and each `grav-plugin-email-<provider>` implements it. This add-on consumes it through `Providers\\ProviderSource` and nothing more, which means adding a seventh provider is installing a plugin rather than editing the newsletter. It was backwards before: the add-on that happened to need a bounce parser first was the one that ended up carrying six of them\n    * **The header a send id travels in is named by the Email plugin, and it is `X-Grav-Send-Id`.** It used to be `X-KahunaCart-Send`, which was this add-on's name for it back when this add-on was the only thing that had ever stamped one \u2014 and which then had to be hard-coded into seven Team Grav transport plugins, where a Grav user with no KahunaCart on the site would read it and wonder. The name now lives in one place, the Email plugin's `Providers\\SendHeader`, so the end that writes the header, the end that registers it with a provider and the end that reads it back cannot disagree about it. A site that wants its own sets `providers.send_header` in the Email plugin's configuration. Postmark's twin follows it: `X-PM-Metadata-Grav-Send-Id`. Events arriving under the old name are still read, so mail already in flight when you upgrade still finds its send row \u2014 but **a store on SMTP2GO should press Set up again**, because SMTP2GO only echoes a header that is named on the webhook itself and the name on yours is the old one\n    * **A part-upgraded site keeps working and is told, once, what to update next.** A site is updated one plugin at a time and both halfway states are ordinary, so every provider is resolved on its own: the transport plugin's answers where it has been updated, this add-on's own parser where it has not, and the Providers card says which plugin to update in one sentence. An Email plugin that is a release behind is the same, for every provider at once, and the card says to update that instead. A transport that cannot report deliveries at all registers nothing, and the card says so outright rather than drawing a webhook address nobody will ever post to \u2014 which is a far more useful thing for a merchant to read\n    * **The verification keys belong to the transport plugin now, and the old fields still work for one release.** Mailgun's signing key, SendGrid's verification key, Postmark's basic auth pair and the rest are credentials for talking to that provider and belong beside the credentials that plugin already keeps to send with. The copies under `providers.<name>.*` here are read when the transport plugin has none, so an upgrade never silently stops a store's bounces arriving, and the card says which key is still being read from the wrong place. The secret in each webhook URL is the store's own and stays exactly where it is\n    * **The Settings screen's headers row is answered by the transport rather than guessed from a version number.** Whether `List-Unsubscribe` survives a transport was a version comparison against one plugin, which is precisely the thing this contract exists to stop doing; it is now that plugin's own `capabilities()`, asked separately about the unsubscribe pair and about custom headers generally, because an API transport can carry a named allowlist with the first on it and not the second. Where nothing answers, the row still reads \"nobody has checked this transport\", which is the honest answer and not a guess in either direction\n    * **Automations: a series of waits and emails that runs on its own, one person at a time.** Migration `newsletter_0005_automations` adds the flows, their steps and an enrolment per person, and the whole engine is one rule \u2014 a tick moves each person by exactly one step. Not by as many as are ready, because a runner that drained an automation in one pass would be one bad branch away from sending mail as fast as the transport would take it. So a wait of two days is over within a minute of two days, a welcome email arrives a minute after the signup rather than in the same second, and nothing anybody writes into an automation is worth to the second\n    * **Ten triggers, and the four rules about who is allowed in.** `subscribed`, `confirmed`, `tag_added`, `order_completed`, `first_order`, `product_bought` and `cart_recovered`, plus `subscription_started`, `subscription_renewed` and `subscription_cancelled` from the Subscriptions add-on, which are a no-op on a store that has not got it. Every enrolment is checked four ways and each of the four is a way for somebody to receive an email they should not have: they have to be subscribed and mailable \u2014 never pending, never bounced, never suppressed \u2014 on the automation's list where it names one, matching its segment where it names one, and new to it unless it allows re-entry. `first_order` counts orders the way the customer screen counts them, so \"their first order\" here and \"1 order\" there are the same fact\n    * **A flow's email is a campaign row, which is the trick the whole design rests on.** `kind = flow` on the campaign and `enrolment_id` on the send, and everything else comes free: the same renderer, the same open pixel, the same click redirect, the same UTM tagging, the same one-click unsubscribe headers, the same email log, the same counters. There is no second sending pipeline behind an automation and no second set of numbers to reconcile. What the word buys is that a flow's message is never listed with the campaigns, never scheduled, never started and never \"the last campaign\" on the Overview \u2014 because a merchant did not write it as one and would not recognise it in that list. Its sends are written one at a time as people reach the step\n    * **The same message reaches each person once.** Somebody who walks an automation a second time gets the steps and not a second copy of the same email, enforced by the same unique key that stops a campaign mailing anybody twice. A welcome series that sent its three emails again to somebody who signed up twice would be the most annoying thing a store does, and it is the kind of thing nobody notices until a customer writes in\n    * **A wait until a weekday lands at the right hour across a daylight-saving change.** \"Tuesday at nine\" means nine in the morning where the shop is \u2014 `reports.timezone`, falling back to Grav's `system.timezone` and then UTC \u2014 and it is computed in that zone with a calendar rather than by adding 86,400 seconds a day. The Monday after the clocks go forward is three days and twenty-two hours away rather than three days and twenty-three, and a store whose one week's email went out an hour early would have nothing on any screen to explain it. A wait reached at exactly the moment it names waits a week, because a wait that answered \"now\" would be a step that did nothing\n    * **Exit on purchase, and the exits nobody writes down.** A purchase takes somebody out of every automation that says so *before* it puts them into anything, so a store running a welcome series with \"leave when they buy\" and a post-purchase thank-you gets the purchase ending the first and starting the second in that order. An unsubscribe, a bounce, a complaint or a suppression takes them out of everything, by whichever route it arrived \u2014 the unsubscribe link, the one-click header, the preference centre, a provider's webhook, an admin adding a suppression \u2014 because the check runs before every step rather than at each of the four places in the add-on that write a status. Nothing is ever sent to them in the meantime, and the worst case is a screen that catches up a minute later\n    * **Pausing holds everybody exactly where they are, and resuming carries each of them on from the same step.** No step runs, no mail goes out, nobody new is put in and nobody is taken out; a wait that ran out while it was paused is simply over. `automations.enabled` set to false does the same thing for every automation at once, which is the switch to reach for while moving a store or changing a transport\n    * **Removing a step somebody is standing on is refused, and so is deleting an automation they are still walking.** Both refusals count the people rather than merely saying no, because \"38 people are waiting at step 3\" is something a merchant can act on. Deleting takes `force=1` for the merchant who meant it, and then the automation, its steps and its messages go, sends and all. Activating is refused for an automation with no steps and for one whose email step has no message written \u2014 both would enrol people and do nothing to them, which is a failure a merchant would learn about a week later from somebody asking why they never got the welcome email\n    * **The ladder is saved whole and a step keeps its people.** There is no add-a-step route and no reorder route: the editor posts the ladder it wants and the server works out what changed, which is one transaction rather than five and one place where \"step 3 goes to step 5 if they opened it\" can be checked against an automation that actually has a step 5. A step that comes back with its id keeps its row and everybody standing on it, so somebody three days into a welcome series does not start again because a typo was fixed in step four\n    * **Twelve routes and twelve tools, and two events anybody can listen for.** `GET`, `POST`, `PATCH` and `DELETE \/flows`, activate, pause, resume, enrol one person, take one person out, and the two recipe addresses reserved for what comes next. `onKahunaCartNewslettersSubscribed` and `onKahunaCartNewslettersConfirmed` are fired by the subscriber service and carry `subscriber_id`, `list_id` and `source` \u2014 the automations are one listener, and a theme or another add-on can be another. `newsletter.flow_email` appears on the store's Notifications screen with a switch of its own, separate from campaigns, because stopping the automations while a campaign goes out is a thing a merchant genuinely wants\n    * **`docs\/automations.md`, including what is deliberately not there.** No visual canvas, because the flows a store actually runs are a ladder with at most one branch in them and a list of steps is easier to read, easier to edit and impossible to draw wrong. No nested branches, no waiting on an event. And the thing worth knowing before writing the first one: on a store with double opt-in switched on, a welcome series belongs on `confirmed` rather than on `subscribed`\n    * **Confirm by hand.** For the person whose confirmation email went to spam or never arrived and who writes in weeks later asking why they are not hearing about a product: they have just asked in writing, and what they cannot do is press a button in somebody else's inbox. A pending subscriber's page carries the button, `POST \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/subscribers\/{id}\/confirm` and the `confirm_subscriber` tool do the same, and the memberships it writes are recorded as confirmed by the store rather than by the person, which is the audit trail. Somebody already on the list has nothing to confirm, somebody who left is a resubscribe with its own warning, and a bounced, complained or suppressed address is refused for the reasons resubscribe refuses it\n    * **A Deliverability tab that says why mail is not landing.** Nine checks over the domain the store sends from, the transport it sends through and the last thirty days of sending, each answering a status, a sentence saying what is true and a sentence saying what to do about it \u2014 grouped Domain, Transport and List, because those are three different people's problems, and within a group in the order a problem is worth looking for one, so the first red row down the page is the one to start with. A store can be entirely healthy from the inside and invisible from the outside: the campaign sends, every recipient is marked delivered, the job finishes clean, and the mail is in a spam folder because of four facts the store never sees. This is the screen that shows them\n    * **SPF read the way a receiver reads it, including the ten-lookup limit.** Not a string search for an `include:` \u2014 the check follows every `include:` and `redirect=` through the resolver and counts what a receiver would actually spend, because the store that breaks is never the one with four includes in its own record. It is the one whose Google Workspace include is six lookups on its own, who adds a help desk, and whose mail stops with nothing on screen having changed. Two `v=spf1` records is reported as its own failure rather than as a missing include, because a receiver finding two treats the domain as having none and the fix is always \"merge them\" rather than \"add one\". A store aligning through a custom return path \u2014 a name of its own pointing into the provider's zone \u2014 passes on that instead, so the recommended arrangement does not read as a red row\n    * **The DKIM selector is asked of the transport rather than of the merchant.** A selector cannot be discovered from DNS: there is no record listing them and no way to walk a zone, which is why every other deliverability tool asks you to type it in. On SMTP2GO this asks their API using the key already pasted into the email-smtp2go plugin \u2014 Andy's rule for every provider in KahunaCart, applied to one lookup \u2014 and the same call brings back the custom return path the SPF check needs. Anything else falls back to `deliverability.dkim_selector`, and a store with neither is told what to type and where to find it as a warning rather than a failure, because a domain whose selector nobody could name might be signing perfectly. A selector CNAMEd into a *different* provider's zone is its own failure with its own sentence: that is the store that changed transport and left the record behind\n    * **DMARC missing is a failure, `p=none` is a warning, and the difference is the point.** Gmail and Yahoo have both required a DMARC record of bulk senders since February 2024, so a domain without one is not being graded down \u2014 it is not arriving. A policy of `none` satisfies the letter of that and asks receivers to do nothing about mail forging the domain, which is the right place to start and the wrong place to stay; the fix says to read a month of reports and then move to quarantine. A `pct=` below a hundred is reported as the deliberate ramp it is rather than as a mistake\n    * **The from address, checked against what receivers actually reject.** A campaign sent as a Gmail or Yahoo address does not arrive at all any more, because their own DMARC policies tell receivers to reject anything claiming to be them that they did not send \u2014 and it is the mistake a small store makes most often, since the address in the from field is the address the owner reads their mail at. An address on a domain that has nothing to do with the store is a warning rather than a failure, because sending as a parent company is a real arrangement and only the merchant knows; setting `deliverability.sending_domain` settles it permanently\n    * **Bounces and complaints against the numbers receivers publish.** 0.3% complaints is Gmail's own line and 0.1% is what the same guidance asks senders to stay under, so between them is a warning; bounces are read against roughly where a mail provider suspends an account. Under thirty messages in the window the check says nothing at all, because one bounce out of four is a 25% bounce rate and drawing that as a red row on a store that has just sent its first test would be the screen inventing a crisis. The one that catches a bug rather than a list is bounces recorded with an empty suppression list: they are being counted and not acted on, which means the same dead addresses go out again next month\n    * **Every lookup is cached for ten minutes, and Check again is what empties it.** A tab that made a dozen DNS queries per redraw would get the store rate-limited by a public resolver on behalf of a merchant who is already frustrated, so answers live in `kahunacart_newsletters_state` under their own prefix and the second draw of the screen costs nothing. The button that matters is the one that throws them away, because the whole value of it is that somebody who has just added a TXT record can see whether it took\n    * **The screen draws rather than spins.** The whole checklist has a two-second budget: anything that has not started when it is spent answers \"still checking\" honestly instead of holding the page open, everything that did finish is drawn, and the lookups that were in flight land in the cache a moment later \u2014 so the tab asks once more on its own and fills itself in. A merchant on a slow or broken resolver gets a screen with most of the answers on it rather than thirty seconds of spinner and then a timeout\n    * **`GET \/kahunacart\/newsletters\/deliverability` and `POST \/deliverability\/recheck`, both with a tool.** The read is a `.view`; the recheck is a `.manage` and a POST, because it makes real DNS queries at somebody else's resolver and, where the transport can be asked, an API call carrying the store's own key \u2014 neither is something a read-only account should be able to make the server do on demand. The manifest describes when to run each in words a model can act on, so \"my mail is going to spam\" is a question an assistant can answer against the real store\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-newsletters deliverability:check`, with an exit code.** The same nine checks, the same three groups, the same sentences \u2014 one set of words resolved through Grav's translator for both readers, so a store running in French gets French in a terminal too. On a support call it is quicker to ask somebody to run one command and paste the output than to walk them around a screen, and it is the only way to get the answer out of a store whose admin nobody can reach. In a deployment it is a check to fail a build on: 1 when anything failed, `--strict` to fail on warnings as well, and a store that has simply not sent anything yet exits zero rather than failing over questions it cannot be asked\n    * **One dashboard alert, naming the first failing check.** Not a count \u2014 \"SPF\" is a thing a person can act on and \"3 deliverability checks failed\" is a thing a person dismisses. It exists because nobody clicks through to a screen they have no reason to suspect: a store's SPF record is right until the afternoon somebody at the hosting company migrates the zone, and the merchant's first sign of it is a campaign three weeks later with a fifteen percent open rate. Failures only, rechecked at most every fifteen minutes off the same cached lookups the tab reads, and dismissible for a day because the fix is usually a change somebody has to ask a registrar for\n    * **An add-on can register a check of its own.** `onKahunaCartNewslettersRegisterDeliverabilityChecks` hands over a registry with the built-in nine already in it, so nobody can redefine what `spf` means for the whole store; a second registration under a taken key is dropped and logged with the name of whoever tried. A listener that throws loses only its own checks, and a check that throws when it runs becomes a failing row naming itself rather than taking down the screen a merchant opened because their mail was already not landing \u2014 a question nobody could answer is never a yes\n    * **`docs\/deliverability.md`, with kahunacart.com's own DNS as the worked example.** Every check, what it reads, what to do about each answer, and the four records that do the whole job on the store this add-on was written for: the SPF include, the `em<id>` return-path CNAME, the `s<id>._domainkey` CNAME, the `link` tracking CNAME and the DMARC policy. Also what is deliberately not on the screen and why \u2014 a blacklist lookup that would report the provider's reputation rather than the store's, a spam score that means handing a third party the content of a campaign, and a verdict on a sending rate nobody can know\n    * **Segments: a saved question about the audience, not a saved list of people.** Migration `newsletter_0003_segments` stores the question as a condition document with three containers \u2014 `all`, `any` and `none` \u2014 and answers it against the live database every time it is asked, so a segment is never stale: somebody who buys Subscriptions this afternoon is in \"people who bought Subscriptions\" this afternoon and nobody has to rebuild anything. Three named boxes rather than a nested boolean tree, deliberately. A tree is more expressive and is what every rules engine grows into; it is also an editor nobody can use, because the merchant has to hold operator precedence in their head. \"All of these\", \"any of these\" and \"none of these\" cover what a mailing list is actually segmented by, read as English, and draw as three stacks of rows. A question that genuinely needs a tree has stopped being a segment and become a report\n    * **Eighteen things a segment can ask about, each declared exactly once.** The list, the tag, the status, when they joined, how they joined, their language, whether they are linked to a customer and which group that customer is in; how many orders they have, what they have spent, when they first and last bought, what products and categories they bought from and how long ago; which campaign they opened and clicked, and whether they have opened anything at all in the last N days. Each field carries its key, type, label, group, the operators it answers and the closure that turns a condition into SQL \u2014 one declaration, so the list the editor draws its pickers from and the list the engine compiles against cannot drift apart. A nineteenth, `subscription_status`, arrives from the Subscriptions add-on when that is installed\n    * **The same segment answers with the same people on SQLite, MySQL and PostgreSQL.** That is the property the whole engine exists for, and it is not free: MySQL compares strings case-insensitively by collation and PostgreSQL does not, so every text comparison goes through `LOWER()` on both sides; the three disagree about integer division, so \"days since their last order\" compares two moments rather than dividing a difference by 86,400; they disagree about where NULL sorts, so the recount fetches never-counted segments in a pass of their own; and SQLite applies a column's type affinity to a string-bound parameter but has none to apply to an expression, so a date or a count compared against a bound string matched nobody there and everybody elsewhere. Forty-six checks run the whole vocabulary against a store seeded identically on all three\n    * **Two fields whose obvious reading is the wrong one.** \"Has not opened anything in ninety days\" only matches people the store actually wrote to in those ninety days \u2014 somebody who joined last week has opened nothing in ninety days, and mailing them a \"we miss you\" makes the store look like it is not paying attention. A person the store has not written to in three months is not disengaged; the store is. And \"days since their last order\" is about people who have bought: somebody with no orders has no last one and matches neither side. \"Has never bought anything\" is `order_count` equal to zero\n    * **Orders mean what the customer's own screen means.** Completed and not refunded, dated by the moment the order completed, and read from the current version of an order's lines rather than counting a line that was edited twice. Six fields ask about orders and all six ask the same question, through the same constants the base plugin's own lifetime-total and order-count use \u2014 so \"spent over $100\" agrees with the number on the customer screen beside it\n    * **A segment never includes somebody who is suppressed, has left, or never confirmed.** Not a switch, because the alternative is a segment that looks right, counts right and mails an address the store was told never to mail again. The preview reports the three separately, in the order the reasons matter, so \"2,410 people \u2014 14 unsubscribed and 2 suppressed are left out\" means the same thing on the segment screen as on the campaign screen. Somebody who simply does not match the conditions is in none of those numbers: they were never part of the segment, which is different from being excluded from it\n    * **Refused twice, and the second time is the one that earns its keep.** A field the store has never heard of, or an operator a field does not accept, is refused with the field named when the segment is saved and again every time it runs. The second catches what saving cannot: a field an add-on contributed, saved while that add-on was installed, and asked for on a day it is not. That segment refuses, and a campaign pointed at it refuses to start, rather than quietly matching a smaller set of people \u2014 sending to the whole list because a condition could not be evaluated is the exact mistake a segment exists to prevent, and not one anybody can take back\n    * **A count is one query, however many conditions the definition holds.** Every condition compiles to an EXISTS or a scalar subquery over the subscriber row rather than a join, so twenty of them compose into one WHERE and somebody who bought a product on three orders is one person rather than three. A test counts the statements, because the failure it guards against works perfectly on a fixture and takes a minute on a real store\n    * **The counts refresh themselves, with nothing added to anybody's cron.** Answering a segment reads the whole subscriber table, so the number beside one is worked out by the worker rather than on every page view \u2014 twenty-five stale segments per run, at most every `segments.count_every_minutes` minutes, riding the one per-minute hook an add-on is given and rate-limited on a durable mark so a store queues one job per interval rather than 1,440 a day. Every screen prints how old the number is, a segment nobody has counted says so rather than saying nobody, and editing a segment's conditions throws its count away because it is the answer to a different question. One segment that will not count is logged and skipped rather than failing the job for the other twenty-four\n    * **A campaign can be sent to a list narrowed by a segment.** The audience snapshot keeps only the people the segment is about, one query per chunk of five hundred, and the summary gains `matched` beside its existing counts so the editor can say \"2,410 match this segment, 16 of them cannot be mailed, 2,394 will receive this\" rather than making a merchant work out from one number whether their conditions or their suppressions narrowed the audience. A segment tied to one list cannot be the audience for a campaign going to another, and the refusal names the list it belongs to. Deleting a segment is refused while a draft or scheduled campaign points at it, with the campaigns named; a campaign that has already sent does not block it, because the record of who received it lives in the sends table and does not change\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-newsletters segments:count` and `segments:preview`.** The recount is for the two moments that will not wait: straight after an import, when the store has ten thousand new subscribers and every number on screen is about the store as it was, and while somebody is still building a segment. The preview prints the count, the three excluded numbers and ten of the people \u2014 with the addresses masked as `a***@example.com`, because a diagnostic that prints a mailing list is one that ends up in a terminal recording, a pasted support ticket and a CI log. Somebody who wants the addresses has `export:csv`, which is a deliberate act rather than a side effect of checking a count\n    * **Any add-on can contribute a field.** `onKahunaCartNewslettersRegisterSegmentFields` hands over a registry, first registration wins so nobody can redefine `order_count` for the whole store, a listener that throws loses only its own fields and the failure is logged with its message in it, and the plugin's own eighteen are registered before the event is fired. `docs\/segments.md` carries the whole vocabulary table, the condition document, what is refused and when, and a worked listener \u2014 and a test compares that table against the registry in both directions, so a field added without a row fails and a row left behind for a field that has gone fails too\n    * **The audience, as its own thing.** Migration `newsletter_0001_core` brings seven tables: subscribers with a real consent record on every row (when they agreed, from what address, and a hash of the exact sentence they were shown), lists that a person can leave one at a time, per-list membership so a preference centre is possible at all, tags for the things the store noticed rather than the things the person chose, suppressions keyed by a SHA-256 of the address so they survive an erasure, and one row per CSV import so \"where did this person come from\" has an answer with a filename in it. A subscriber is deliberately not a flag on the customer row: `kahunacart_customers` has no consent column and should not grow one, `data_json` cannot be filtered in SQL across the three engines, and the two populations genuinely differ \u2014 somebody can subscribe from the homepage having never bought anything, and somebody can buy for years having never been asked\n    * **Campaigns and what came back from them.** Migration `newsletter_0002_campaigns` brings six more: templates as the starting points a merchant keeps, campaigns that snapshot a template once at creation and never read it again, one send row per recipient written before anything goes out, per-link click counts, every individual click, and the provider event log. The send row's UNIQUE on (`campaign_id`, `subscriber_id`) is what makes pause and resume safe \u2014 the work is enumerated up front, so resuming claims the rows that are still pending and nobody is mailed twice even when two workers race\n    * **A send's status only ever moves forward.** `SendRepository::advance()` walks a one-way ladder from `pending` through `sent`, `delivered`, `opened`, `clicked` to `bounced` and `complained`, and refuses anything at or below where a row already is. The reason is delivery reports: a provider's `delivered` webhook routinely arrives after the recipient has already opened the mail, because the pixel fires the moment they read it and the webhook queues behind whatever else the provider is doing. A naive write would turn an open back into a delivery and the campaign's open rate would fall as the reports landed. `stamp()` is the escape hatch that fills a missing date without moving the status, so \"opened at 09:04, clicked at 09:06\" survives\n    * **Every signup goes through one path.** `Service\\SubscriberService::subscribe()` takes a `SubscribeRequest` \u2014 email, name, list, tags, source, consent IP and the consent sentence \u2014 and answers a `SubscribeOutcome` that is one of `pending`, `subscribed`, `already` or `suppressed`. The store sees all four; the person sees two, and never which of them applies to an address that was already known. \"You are already subscribed\" is an oracle: anybody can type an address into a signup form, and a store that answers differently for a known address has just confirmed to whoever asked that a particular person shops there. A suppressed address gets whichever sentence the list would have produced anyway, so it is not marked out by getting a different answer from the address beside it\n    * **Double opt-in decided here and sent elsewhere.** When a list asks for a confirmation and the person has not given one, the outcome says so and a `ConfirmationDispatcher` is asked. The decision is a database question a unit test can ask with no container; the sending is a queued job that needs the store's mailer and its templates. A store running this build has double opt-in working right up to the email \u2014 subscribers arrive `pending`, memberships are written, and confirming them is a button rather than a link in an inbox\n    * **Imported contacts are never re-confirmed.** `importRow()` writes a subscriber as `subscribed` with `confirmed_at` and `consent_at` already set, `source = import` and the file name kept as evidence. Re-confirming a list that confirmed somewhere else is how twelve thousand subscribers become four hundred, and it is not something anybody does on purpose. What is not skipped is the suppression check: an address the store has been told never to mail stays that way whatever a CSV says about it\n    * **Suppressions survive erasure, and that is the whole design.** The table is keyed by a SHA-256 of the normalised address rather than by the address. When a customer is erased the subscriber row, their memberships, their tags, every send they were part of and every click they made are deleted outright \u2014 not anonymised, because an anonymised subscriber row is an address with the name taken off \u2014 and the suppression row keeps only the hash. So an address that hard bounced or complained is still never mailed again after the record of who it belonged to has gone. Erasure is never blocked: the listener on `onKahunaCartCollectErasureBlockers` is registered and contributes nothing, deliberately and permanently, with a test that says so\n    * **A token is the entire credential for every public URL.** `Security\\Tokens` signs an HMAC-SHA256 over the purpose, the subscriber, the send and the link, keyed by the store's own `SiteSecret` *and* a per-subscriber secret. Two consequences fall out and both matter: a token minted on staging does not work on production, and rotating one person's secret kills every link in every email they have ever been sent without touching anybody else's \u2014 which is the one thing a store can actually do when somebody says \"I am getting mail from a link I never signed up for\". Six purposes, and a token minted for one does not verify for another, so a confirmation link cannot open a preference centre. Confirm lasts seven days, unsubscribe and preferences ninety, and the pixel, the click redirect and view-in-browser never expire, because they live in a mail that will sit in an inbox for years and a link that quietly stopped working would read as a fall in engagement\n    * **Core's `SignedToken` was read first and does not fit, for one concrete reason.** Its token carries an expiry and a signature and nothing else \u2014 the subject has to be known before verification, which is right for a receipt at `{base}\/order\/{hash}` where the hash sits in the URL beside the token. Here the token is the whole URL, so the subscriber, the send and the link have to travel inside it. What is reused is the part that had no reason to be written twice: core's base64url encoder and decoder, so a newsletter token and a receipt token are the same kind of string in a URL\n    * **Starting a campaign enumerates its audience first.** `Service\\Audience::snapshot()` walks a list's memberships by id, five hundred at a time, each chunk in its own transaction, and writes one send row per person. Keyset rather than offset paging, because rows are being written to the sends table while the memberships table is being read and an offset-paged walk over a moving target skips people. Anybody suppressed, anybody who left this list and anybody who never confirmed gets a `skipped` row with the reason on it rather than being silently absent, so \"38,214 people, 412 excluded\" is a number a merchant can click through and act on. `total` counts only the mailable, so a finished campaign reads N of N rather than N of N-plus-the-excluded forever\n    * **The campaign state machine, as a table.** `Service\\CampaignService` is the only thing that moves a campaign, and `transitions()` publishes the table a test compares the documentation against. Editing is allowed from `draft` and `scheduled` and nowhere else \u2014 a campaign that is sending has already put its subject in somebody's inbox, and editing it would mean two halves of one audience receiving two different emails under one name. `sent` and `cancelled` are terminal; `failed` is not, because a campaign that stopped when the transport went down is exactly the one somebody wants to start again once it is back. An illegal move throws `IllegalTransition` carrying a sentence a person can read (\"A sent campaign cannot be paused.\") and a from\/to pair a caller can branch on\n    * **Pause reaches the job that is actually sending.** Pausing requests cancellation of the running job and then writes the status, so there is no window in which the campaign says `paused` while its job is still happily delivering. Resuming enqueues a *fresh* job rather than reviving the old one \u2014 a job that was still queued would go on delivering after somebody pressed Pause, which is the failure grav-licensing's campaign tool has today \u2014 and the send row's UNIQUE means the new job claims only what is still pending\n    * **Wired into KahunaCart through published extension points and nothing else.** Migrations, reset tables, feature compatibility, job handlers, the notification registry, the erasure pair, order completion, the admin section, the API route table, `onPagesInitialized` at priority -10, Twig template paths, the Form plugin action, and permissions. `newsletter.confirmation` and `newsletter.campaign` are registered on the Notifications screen from the first commit, so a merchant can see what the store will send before it sends anything\n    * **Three permissions, not two.** `kahunacart.newsletters.view`, `.manage` and `.send`. Sending is a permission of its own because a campaign is a one-way door: pausing does not get back the mail that already went, so a store can hand a copywriter everything they need to write and schedule a campaign without also handing them the ability to put it in forty thousand inboxes\n    * **The subscriber and the customer are linked when an order completes.** Somebody signs up on the homepage in March and buys something in June; two records, one person, and until they are joined the store cannot answer \"how many of my subscribers have bought anything\". The link is by address and one-directional: a subscriber already pointing at a customer is left alone, because a second customer record for one address is a merge somebody has to do by hand and repointing quietly would hide it. A customer who never subscribed stays that way \u2014 never having been asked is not consent\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart reset` takes the newsletter with it, except the one table it must not.** Every table is declared under the `customers` scope, because a subscriber is a person rather than a sale and wiping the orders on a store about to launch should not throw away the mailing list. `kahunacart_newsletters_suppressions` is deliberately not declared: those rows are the addresses the store has been told never to mail again, and a store wiped the day before launch would otherwise send its first real campaign to every hard bounce and every spam complaint it has ever collected. Removing one is a deliberate act on the Suppressions screen, one row at a time\n    * **The manifest test works from day one.** `mcp.yaml` carries an empty `tools:` list, `Api\\AdminRoutes` carries an empty route table, and `tests\/Unit\/McpManifestTest.php` compares the two in both directions through the real `ApiRouteCollector` over a real FastRoute collector. Both halves start empty together, so the first route added without a tool beside it is a failing test rather than a tool nobody notices is missing in a month\n    * **A test bootstrap that runs against a branch of the base plugin.** `KAHUNACART_TEST_BASE_ROOT` points at a worktree or a release candidate and wins over the sibling checkout, the same variable spelled the same way the licenses and subscriptions add-ons read. `tests\/Support\/NewsletterFixture` boots a migrated in-memory store with core's migrations plus this plugin's and exposes every repository and service over it, with a clock a test can move so \"this token expired\" is two lines rather than a sleep\n    * **A contact list from another tool, brought in properly.** `Import\\CsvImport` reads a CSV through a column mapping and does exactly one of five things to every row: writes a new subscriber, brings an existing one up to date, skips it, turns it into a suppression, or refuses it. The four counters on the import row account for every row in the file and add up to the total, which is what makes the report worth printing \u2014 a merchant whose twelve thousand addresses became eleven thousand four hundred reads across rather than guessing, and the arithmetic is asserted directly so a new branch through the importer cannot quietly lose rows\n    * **Rows the file says have unsubscribed are the ones that matter.** They become a suppression with reason `manual` and the note `import: {filename}`, and where the store already knows the address the subscriber comes off every list with `import` recorded as the reason they left. Importing only the subscribed rows and discarding the rest is how a store's first campaign from a new tool lands in the inboxes of people who opted out of the old one\n    * **An import will not revive a bounced or complaining address, and will not mail a suppressed one.** Both statuses are facts about mail this store sent \u2014 a message that came back undeliverable, somebody who pressed the spam button \u2014 and nothing in a file from another tool can overturn one. The row is counted under suppressed and nothing is written. The suppression check is by hash, so it goes on working after the person it belonged to has asked to be forgotten\n    * **A contact who never confirmed at the old tool is counted and skipped**, whatever the run's confirmation setting says. They have been asked once and said nothing, and mailing them a fresh confirmation is mailing somebody who already declined to answer. A merchant who wants to ask them again exports that slice and imports it deliberately, which is a decision worth making on purpose rather than by default\n    * **Confirmation is the one thing a merchant gets a say in.** Off \u2014 the default, and what a contact export means \u2014 imports everybody subscribed and confirmed with the file's own consent dates, and asks nobody anything. On imports them pending and sends each of them a confirmation. The help text is blunt about why the default is what it is: re-confirming an imported list is how twelve thousand subscribers become four hundred. Somebody the store already has as subscribed is left alone either way and is never asked to confirm what they have already confirmed\n    * **The EmailOctopus export maps itself.** `Import\\Preset\\EmailOctopus` recognises their file by its header row and fills the mapping in. Their export is column-configurable \u2014 the merchant picks \"visible fields\" or \"all fields\", and the visible set is whatever was last ticked on the list screen \u2014 so there is no one header row to match on and the preset matches on vocabulary instead, without regard to case, spacing or punctuation. `docs\/importing.md` lists every heading it knows and says the two things that go wrong first: the download is a ZIP, and their three statuses mean what this add-on's three statuses mean\n    * **A preview before anything is queued.** `Import\\Preview` answers the file's header row, the tool it was recognised as, the mapping being proposed, the number of rows and the first twenty of them as the importer would read them \u2014 including the ones it would refuse, each with its reason. It writes nothing and touches no table, so it can be re-run every time a merchant changes a dropdown, and the row count is a real pass over the file rather than a guess from its size, which would be wrong on any file with an address in it\n    * **The reader copes with what a real export actually contains.** A byte-order mark from Excel, semicolons instead of commas (which is most of Europe and most of the people migrating a store), quoted fields with commas and line breaks in them, and row numbers that count records the way a spreadsheet does rather than physical lines. Names arrive as one column or as two halves, tags arrive in one cell with any of three separators, a status is read in any capitalisation, and a date is read as ISO-8601, a plain date or a bare epoch\n    * **The upload is stored before the job is queued and removed as soon as it is done.** A queued import cannot read the browser's temporary file \u2014 the request that carried it is long over by the time the worker ticks \u2014 so the bytes move to `user\/data\/kahunacart-newsletters\/imports` under a generated name, never the merchant's, and the run remembers it. The directory is deny-all to the web; the file goes on success and on failure alike, because an uploaded contact list is every address a store has ever collected; and `import.keep_days` sweeps anything left by an upload whose job never ran\n    * **Progress moves while a long file is being read.** `newsletter.import` walks the file writing the counters to the import row every five hundred rows, so the admin's progress bar moves rather than jumping from nothing to done. Each row commits on its own rather than five hundred sharing a transaction: an import that dies two-thirds of the way through a forty-thousand-line file should leave two-thirds of those people on the list, not none of them. A run that was interrupted is re-run from the top, which is safe \u2014 running the same file twice does nothing the first run did not already do, and a test checksums every table to prove it\n    * **The subscriber list comes out again.** `Export\\SubscribersCsv` streams every subscriber matching the admin table's own filters as ten columns, a line at a time, over a keyset walk five hundred rows deep \u2014 three queries a page whether the page holds three people or five hundred, with a query-count test holding it there. A list somebody *left* appears as `code:unsubscribed` rather than vanishing, because that is exactly the fact that has to survive a round trip through another tool. The signing secret, the consent IP and the consent hash are deliberately not in the file\n    * **The `newsletter` form action, a drop-in replacement for `emailoctopus`.** It reads the form's address, optional name, list and tags, records the consent sentence from the field's own label so the hash is the wording the page actually printed, and stamps the visitor's address. A bad address sets the form error and stops the remaining process actions the way the old action did. Everything else succeeds and looks identical: new, already known and suppressed all produce one answer, because a form that said \"you are already subscribed\" would confirm to anybody who typed an address in that a particular person is on the list. A consent field the form declares as a checkbox has to be ticked; one that is a display field is read for its wording alone. `docs\/forms.md` carries the kahunacart.com homepage form rewritten verbatim, so the site swap is a copy and paste\n    * **Two CLI commands, under this add-on's own namespace.** `bin\/plugin kahunacart-newsletters import:csv` runs an import in the foreground and prints the counters and the reasons behind them, which is the right tool for a file already on the server and too big for a browser; `--dry-run` applies every rule and writes nothing at all, proved by checksumming every table before and after. `bin\/plugin kahunacart-newsletters export:csv` writes the list to a file or to standard output, so it pipes\n    * **Five admin routes, and three of them deliberately have no MCP tool.** `POST imports\/preview`, `POST imports`, `GET imports`, `GET imports\/{id}` and `GET subscribers\/export`. The two uploads are multipart, which version 1 of the manifest format does not describe, and the export answers with a document rather than a payload a model can read \u2014 all three are named in the manifest test's EXCLUDED list with those reasons, and a fourth test now holds that every name on that list is a route the plugin really registers, so an exclusion cannot outlive the route it excused\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 routes \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The seven public addresses now answer.** `{base}\/newsletter\/subscribe`, `confirm\/{token}`, `u\/{token}`, `p\/{token}`, `o\/{token}.gif`, `c\/{token}` and `v\/{token}`, claimed on `onPagesInitialized` at priority -10 the way the subscriptions add-on claims `{base}\/subscription\/{hash}`. Anything under `{base}\/newsletter\/` that is not one of the seven patterns is left alone entirely, so a store that already has a page there keeps it, and `webhook\/{provider}\/{secret}` stays recognised-but-unanswered until phase 3. None of them needs a session, a cookie or a login: a signed token is the whole credential, because the person holding one has usually never bought anything and may never log in to anything\n    * **A GET never writes, except where the request is the event.** Corporate mail scanners open every link in an inbox before the person does, which is the most common bug report every magic-link implementation eventually gets. So the confirmation page, the unsubscribe page and the preference centre each draw a page with one button and act on the POST \u2014 the same construction core uses for `{base}\/signin\/{token}` and for the same reason. The open pixel and the click redirect are the two deliberate exceptions, and they are exceptions because an image fetch from a mail client is the only signal that a mail was opened and there is no second request to move the write into\n    * **RFC 8058 one-click unsubscribe.** A POST whose body is `List-Unsubscribe=One-Click` \u2014 Gmail's own unsubscribe button, sent by the mail client without asking anybody anything \u2014 takes the person off every list and answers 200 with an empty body and no page. Somebody pressing unsubscribe in their mail client is not choosing between topics. The button on the page is the other path: it leaves the campaign's own list and leaves the others alone, and offers the preference centre to anybody who wanted to keep one of them. Neither is queued; there is no worker between a person and their opt-out\n    * **Leaving is counted against the campaign that sent the link.** Nothing else in phase 1 writes `campaigns.unsubscribed`, and it cannot be recounted afterwards \u2014 leaving is recorded on the membership rather than on the send \u2014 so it is written at the moment somebody leaves, by the route that resolved the campaign from the send the token names. The membership write and the counter are deliberately not in one transaction: a counter that failed leaves a campaign reading one short and somebody still unsubscribed, which is the right way round\n    * **The signup endpoint takes JSON, or a form with the storefront nonce, and nothing else.** `Content-Type: application\/json` is itself the CSRF story \u2014 a cross-site form cannot send it without a preflight the browser will not grant \u2014 which is the same rule the storefront JSON API uses. A form post carries `nonce_field('kahunacart', 'nonce')` instead, and one with neither is a 415. 200 for \"you are on the list\", 202 for \"check your email\", 422 for an address that is not one, and the byte-identical answer for a known address, an unknown one and a suppressed one\n    * **`{{ kahunacart_newsletter_form() }}`**, for a theme that wants a signup box in its footer without defining a form to get one. With JavaScript it posts JSON and prints the answer in place; without it, the same form posts normally and the store answers with a page saying the same sentence. It carries a honeypot that is a real labelled input moved off screen rather than a hidden one \u2014 a hidden input is the first thing a form filler skips and a labelled one is the first thing it completes \u2014 and a filled-in honeypot gets the ordinary thank-you and writes nothing\n    * **Three rate-limit buckets rather than one, and the numbers are the plugin's own.** Signing up is ten an hour per address, the token pages sixty, and the pixel and the click redirect six hundred \u2014 a company reading its mail through one gateway is a single address as far as the store is concerned, so a limit set for the signup form would lose a merchant most of a campaign's opens. Counted through core's own limiter, before the signature is verified so grinding tokens is not free. The defaults are not in core's `security.rate_limits` because core deliberately hands a bucket it has never heard of the strictest shipped default, three an hour, which is right for the shop and would be a disaster here; a store that would rather keep every limit in one file can still write them there and they are honoured\n    * **A spent bucket does not break a mail.** The token pages and the signup endpoint get an honest 429 with `Retry-After`, as JSON for a JSON caller and as a page for a browser. The pixel still answers its GIF and the click still redirects to the store, because a 429 in an `<img>` is a torn-page icon in the middle of somebody's mail and a 429 on a link somebody clicked is a dead end where a page should be\n    * **Three headers on every response, added centrally so no route can forget one.** `Cache-Control: private, no-store, max-age=0`, because every one of these URLs is a credential and a shared cache holding one would hand the next visitor somebody else's mailing-list membership. `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`, because without it the site a campaign links to receives the click token in a `Referer` header and now holds a working credential for one of the store's subscribers. And `X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow`\n    * **Every failed token looks the same.** Expired, forged, signed with another store's secret, signed with a secret that has since been rotated, or minted for somebody who has since been erased: the three pages answer 410 Gone with a sentence saying the link has expired and how to get a fresh one, the pixel answers its GIF, the click redirect sends the visitor to the store's front page, and a one-click POST gets its empty 200. A route that answered differently for \"expired\" than for \"forged\" would be telling whoever is probing it which half to work on\n    * **The click redirect checks where it is sending people.** The URL comes out of the store's own links table, written by the merchant in their own campaign, so this is not the usual open-redirect story \u2014 but \"the merchant typed it\" and \"it is safe to send a browser there\" are different claims, and a `javascript:` URL that reached a redirect would be a stored XSS with the store's own domain in front of it. http, https and site-relative go through; protocol-relative and everything else lands on the store root\n    * **The routes are Grav-free and tested as requests.** A request is a value object, an answer is a value object, and `Frontend\\StorefrontHooks` is the only file that touches a superglobal, a header or an exit. So every route is tested by building a request against a real migrated database with real subscribers, real lists and a real token signer, and reading the response back \u2014 including the rate limit tripping, the secret rotation killing one person's links and nobody else's, and the token minted for one purpose refusing to open another route's page\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end routes \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 sending \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A campaign is Markdown inside the store's own email layout.** `Render\\CampaignRenderer` composes six small steps in the order that matters: the subject, preheader and body go through a sandboxed Twig pass with this recipient's merge variables, the body becomes HTML through Grav's own Parsedown, links are rewritten for click tracking, the result is wrapped by `newsletter-campaign.html.twig` extending `kahunacart-email-layout.html.twig`, and the open pixel goes in last because before the layout there is no body tag to put it before. A campaign therefore looks like the order confirmation without anybody designing it, and a `plain` layout sits beside it for the message that has to read as a personal email rather than as a newsletter\n    * **The text part is rendered from the Markdown source, not from the finished HTML.** Stripping tags out of rendered HTML gives a text part full of the merchant's table markup with the tables taken out; the Markdown they typed is already the plain-text version of what they meant, so a bulleted list stays a bulleted list and a link keeps its address beside its label. It goes through core's `PlainTextBody`, which takes back out the HTML escaping Grav applies to every template it renders including a `.txt.twig`\n    * **Merge tags run in the same sandbox core's object templates already run in.** Fourteen variables, `if` and `set` only, a short filter and function allowlist, no `include` and no method calls \u2014 so a store that has learned what an editable email subject may do has learned what a campaign may do. A campaign reading anything else is refused at save time with the variable named. Two passes rather than one: the subject and preheader are not escaped, because the layout escapes them where it prints them and a subject reading \"Bikes &amp; Boards\" in an inbox is nobody's idea of correct; the body is escaped, because a merge value lands in HTML that the view-in-browser route serves to a real browser rather than only to mail clients that strip scripts\n    * **Every merge variable exists for everybody.** `customer.order_count` is `0` for somebody who has never bought anything rather than missing, because the sandbox runs with `strict_variables` on and a missing key would be a throw in the middle of a send, for one recipient, which is the worst possible place to find out. A merge tag that breaks at send time anyway falls back to the raw text and logs once per campaign rather than once per recipient \u2014 forty thousand identical log lines is how a store's disk fills up on the night of its first campaign\n    * **Every link in a campaign is counted per link, and three of them never are.** `Render\\LinkRewriter` points each outbound `href` at `{base}\/newsletter\/c\/{token}`, with the person, the send and the link all inside the token \u2014 so a forwarded email cannot count a click against somebody else's send. The same URL written twice in one campaign is one link row with one running count, which is what a merchant means when they ask how many people pressed the button. `mailto:`, `tel:`, anchors and this add-on's own routes are left exactly as written; the unsubscribe link in particular, because RFC 8058 asks the transport to trust the address in the header and bouncing the reader through a tracker first is the wrong answer to that\n    * **A campaign goes out as one job that walks a batch.** `Sending\\CampaignSender` claims up to `sending.batch` pending sends in id order, renders and sends each through core's `LoggedMailer`, and ends a run in one of four ways: finished, which marks the campaign sent and rebuilds its counters from the sends table; throttled; out of time; or aborted. One job rather than forty thousand, which is core's own `BulkOrderStatus` idiom \u2014 forty thousand queued jobs would drain at whatever rate the worker allows, interleaved with every customer's receipt, and the merchant who pressed Send would have nothing single to look at\n    * **The per-minute throttle is a rolling window read out of the sends table.** With sixty a minute and sixty already sent in the last fifty seconds the right answer is to wait ten, not to wait for the top of the minute and then send a hundred and twenty in the next one. Reading it from the rows rather than from a stored counter also means it survives a worker restart, two workers, and a campaign resumed an hour later, none of which a counter does. Both it and the forty-second time budget yield through core's `DeferJob`, which costs the job neither an attempt nor a backoff\n    * **The circuit breaker counts backwards from the newest attempt.** Twenty-five failures last Tuesday followed by four hundred successes is a campaign that is working, so `sending.abort_after_failures` looks at the run of failures ending at the most recent send and one success resets it. It is read out of the table rather than kept in a variable, which is what makes it trip on a store whose `sending.batch` is smaller than its threshold \u2014 exactly the store whose transport is struggling. When it trips the campaign pauses itself, and a dashboard alert says which campaign and what to do about it\n    * **Pause lands on the very next message rather than at the end of the batch.** The job asks core's `JobCancellation` before each recipient and re-reads the campaign after it, so a Pause pressed while the thirty-first message is going out means the thirty-second does not go. A resumed campaign gets a fresh job and the old one, waking up on another machine halfway through a batch, finds `job_id` pointing at somebody else and stops without sending \u2014 which is what makes resume safe, on top of the UNIQUE that already made double-sending impossible\n    * **Every message carries the headers that keep a store out of the spam folder.** `List-Unsubscribe` with both the `mailto:` and the HTTPS form, `List-Unsubscribe-Post: List-Unsubscribe=One-Click` for RFC 8058, `Precedence: bulk` so out-of-office replies do not come back forty thousand at a time, and `X-KC-Campaign`. The one-click header is what makes Gmail and Outlook draw their own unsubscribe button next to the sender name, and the thing people reach for when they cannot find one is the spam button. They go straight onto the Symfony message, because the Grav Email plugin has no parameter for a header it has not heard of\n    * **The `Message-ID` is the store's, and three records agree on it.** Generated before the message is built and stamped onto the send row first, so the same string is on the outgoing message, on the send row and in `kahunacart_email_log` \u2014 which is what lets a bounce report quoting a message id be traced back to a person and a campaign without guessing. Stamping it before the send also closes a small window: a worker that dies between a successful `send()` and `markSent()` leaves a row whose retry reuses the id, so the email log counts a second attempt against one row rather than growing a second row for one message\n    * **Opens and clicks are a timestamp and nothing else.** No IP address, no user agent, no location, and nowhere in the database for any of it to go \u2014 which is why the two switches on the Settings screen can be plain about what turning them on means. The first open moves the send row and counts the campaign; the fiftieth changes nothing, which matters because Apple Mail Privacy Protection fetches every image in every message whether or not anybody opened it. Every click is a row, because how often and when is what turns a flat rate into a picture of the first 48 hours, and one person is counted however many times they press\n    * **A click counts as an open.** Most clients that block images do not block links, so for a large share of any audience the click is the only evidence the message was read at all. Without the rule a store whose readers are mostly on such a client would show a 2% open rate beside a 6% click rate, which cannot happen and is the sort of number that makes people stop trusting a dashboard. An open arriving after a click still stamps its own date without moving the status back, so \"opened at 09:04, clicked at 09:06\" survives\n    * **`kahunacart_email_log.opened_at` is written, which is what the column is for.** Core documents it in three places as deliberately writable and deliberately never written by core itself \u2014 writing it there would mean putting a beacon in a customer's receipt. A newsletter is a different document with a different agreement behind it, so this add-on fills it in, against the same `message_id` the send row holds\n    * **The double opt-in confirmation is a queued job.** One sentence, one button, one line saying to ignore it, in the store's own layout, so the first mail somebody gets from a shop looks like the receipt they will get from it later. It is queued rather than sent inline because the person who just typed their address is waiting for a page to come back, and the dispatcher cannot throw \u2014 it is called after the consent record has been written, and an exception escaping would turn a signup that succeeded into a 500 and lose the address. Nothing is sent to somebody who was confirmed in the admin between the signup and the job running\n    * **A test send is the real message, and it never joins an audience.** Same layout, same Markdown, same merge tags, `[Test]` on the front of the subject, tracking off. The address gets a real subscriber row \u2014 the tokens are signed with a subscriber's own secret and a preview that faked those would be a preview of a different email \u2014 flagged in `data_json` and dropped out of every snapshot and every live count, so testing against your own address six times does not mail you six times when you press Send. An address that is already a real subscriber is used exactly as it stands, because flagging it would take a real person out of every future audience. Nothing about the campaign moves: no send row, no counter, no link row\n    * **The preview renders through the real layout and writes nothing.** It takes either a saved campaign or the fields as they stand in an unsaved editor, so the pane can update while somebody types \u2014 which is the difference between a preview people use and one they do not \u2014 and it validates the subject, the preheader and the body separately so a refusal says which of the three as well as what in it\n    * **Every top-level screen is headed \"Newsletters\", with a line under it that changes with the screen, and the tabs beneath.** The heading used to be the screen's own name, so the only thing saying you were in Newsletters was the highlighted sidebar row. Subscriptions already read this way; Licenses now does too, so the three add-on sections start the same. A campaign's, subscriber's, segment's or automation's own screen keeps its name as the heading, with a way back\n    * **Two tabs renamed: Deliverability is Health, and Lists, tags and segments is Audience.** The first was a long word for \"is my mail landing\", and the second was three nouns where every other tab has one. The routes, the API and the configuration keys keep their names, so a bookmark, an alert button or a script pointing at `deliverability` still lands\n    * **The preview pane fills in on opening, for readers too.** A campaign's or template's saved version is answered by a `GET` on the same preview route, which needs only the view permission, so an editor opens with the preview drawn before anything is typed, a reader without the manage permission sees it at all, and the read-only demo, which refuses every write, shows it as well. The `POST` that renders unsaved fields is unchanged\n    * **An alert's button opens the Settings screen on the right tab.** \"Set the From address\" lands on Sending and the provider alerts land on Providers, as `settings\/sending` and `settings\/providers` routes, rather than on whichever tab the form was last left on. Needs KahunaCart 1.2.0 and admin2 2.1.8; on anything older the same routes open Settings on the remembered tab, as before\n    * **Everything the Overview used to list under \"Needs looking at\" is on the admin's alert strip instead.** The Email plugin missing or switched off, no From address of the newsletter's own, a transport that drops the unsubscribe headers, and a segment that could not be counted were a panel of their own on the Overview, in the newsletter's own words and with no way to fold it away, while the store's other problems sat in the yellow strip at the top of every screen. They are registered there now (`newsletters_transport`, `newsletters_no_from`, `newsletters_headers_stripped`, `newsletters_segment_count`), so they collapse with the rest, can be hidden for a while, and open the Settings or Segments screen that fixes them. The transport one cannot be hidden, because nothing can go out until it is fixed; the From address one hides for a month, because it is advice rather than a failure. The overview API still answers its `alerts` list for anything reading it by hand\n    * **A campaign that stopped itself says so at the top of the admin.** `newsletters_campaign_paused` is registered on core's own alert strip, so it honours `alerts.enabled` and `alerts.min_recheck_seconds` with the other four and hiding it hides it for one person rather than for the store. The failure it reports is silent by nature: a store whose transport credentials expired on a Friday afternoon has a campaign that stopped and nothing at all on screen saying so\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end sending \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 admin \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The whole add-on has a screen, and every screen is a route.** `KahunaCart \u2192 Newsletters` is thirty-nine admin routes across seven controllers \u2014 overview, campaigns, subscribers, lists and tags, templates, suppressions and settings \u2014 every one of them extending core's `Api\\AdminController`, so the demo store's read-only gate and the store's permission checks are enforced in a constructor nobody can forget to call. Thirty-eight of them are MCP tools; `GET labels` is the exception, because a model has no use for an admin screen's button captions\n    * **Sending is checked as its own permission, after manage rather than instead of it.** `requireSend()` asks for `.manage` first and then `.send`, in that order, so somebody with neither is told the thing they are missing first. Start, resume and the test send are the three routes behind it. The screens read the permissions before they draw, so a reader gets every page with the buttons absent and a line saying why, rather than buttons that refuse when pressed\n    * **A refusal from the state machine arrives as a refusal, not as a crash.** `IllegalTransition` becomes a 422 carrying `X-KahunaCart-Error` and the sentence the service wrote \u2014 \"a campaign that is sending cannot be edited\" \u2014 so the screen shows the reason rather than inventing one. Every route that moves a campaign goes through the same three lines\n    * **One class stands between a database row and a payload.** `Admin\\Presenter` builds every object the section receives, which is how `store_id`, `consent_text_hash` and a subscriber's signing secret are absent from the API by construction rather than by remembering. The secret is the entire credential behind every unsubscribe link that person was ever sent, and an admin screen has no use for it. Rates are computed there too, `null` where the denominator is zero, so no screen divides by an empty audience\n    * **What a campaign may do next is read from the state machine, not written down twice.** `Presenter::allowed()` asks `CampaignService::transitions()`, so the buttons a screen offers and the transitions the service will accept cannot drift apart. Adding a status to the machine lights up the right buttons with no change here\n    * **The first forty-eight hours as a picture, bucketed in PHP.** `Admin\\Timeline` turns the send rows into four cumulative hourly series. Every engine spells date truncation differently \u2014 `strftime`, `FROM_UNIXTIME`, `date_trunc` \u2014 and doing it in SQL would mean three fragments and a dialect switch for what is one integer division over epoch seconds every engine already agrees about. Cumulative rather than per hour, because per hour makes the second hour look like a collapse when it is only the tail of the first. A campaign an hour old draws one point rather than forty-seven hours of flat line into the future, and an open on day three is counted in the total and left off the chart, which is what \"the first forty-eight hours\" means\n    * **The one question the configuration cannot answer.** `Admin\\Transport` reports whether the Email plugin is installed and enabled, which engine it is on, and whether `List-Unsubscribe` and `List-Unsubscribe-Post` survive that engine \u2014 the headers that put an unsubscribe button at the top of a Gmail message and that Gmail and Yahoo's bulk-sender rules require. An API transport builds a JSON body rather than an SMTP envelope and can drop a header it was never taught about; SMTP2GO's did until 1.0.1, so the check reads that plugin's own `blueprints.yaml` and names the version it found. A transport nobody has checked is reported as unchecked rather than as broken\n    * **The import screen is a mapping grid, because that is the screen merchants have already used.** After the upload it shows the file's header row, the first twenty rows, and a dropdown above each column listing the eight fields the importer knows plus \"Ignore this column\" \u2014 pre-selected from the mapping the preview endpoint proposes, so an EmailOctopus export arrives already aligned and the only work left is correcting what is wrong. Changing any dropdown re-reads the file under the new mapping and the rows update in place, refusals included, each with its reason. Start stays disabled until a column is pointed at the email address and no two columns are pointed at the same field. No new route: the preview endpoint already accepted a mapping override\n    * **Adding somebody by hand no longer asks them to confirm.** A merchant typing an address into their own admin is saying this person asked them, and mailing that person a confirmation email they have to click is both surprising and a way to lose them. `SubscribeRequest` grew `alreadyConfirmed`, which `subscribe()` honours \u2014 deliberately not a way to switch double opt-in off, but a way for a caller to say the question has already been answered. No public route passes it and the storefront's own never does\n    * **Putting somebody back on a list is refused for a bounce, a complaint or a suppression.** Those are facts about mail the store already sent, and no button in an admin overturns one. The suppression has to be lifted on its own screen, as its own decision, which is the point of it being a second decision\n    * **Deleting a subscriber leaves the suppression table alone.** `Privacy\\Erasure::forget()` removes the person, their memberships, their tags, every send they were part of and every click they made \u2014 and not the suppression, because an address that hard bounced last month is still an address this store must not mail. That is what makes it different from an erasure, which nulls the address and keeps the hash\n    * **Three things the admin deliberately will not do, each for one reason.** A subscriber's email address cannot be edited, because it is the key every consent record, suppression, send row and signed link hangs off, and changing it would be creating a second person and calling it an edit. A campaign that has sent anything cannot be deleted, because its recipient list is the only record of who received it and that is the question a complaint always asks. A list cannot be deleted at all, because the memberships hanging off it are consent records and neither answer to \"what happens to them\" is obvious enough to pick by default\n    * **The section is one file with no imports and no build step, and it still speaks the store's language.** `Admin\\Labels` is a dictionary of four hundred and one keys with their English fallbacks; `GET labels` resolves the same keys through Grav's translator and the component asks for `t(key, fallback)`, so a store with a translation gets it and a store without gets English rather than a key. `languages\/en.yaml` is generated from the same map, so the two cannot disagree\n    * **A campaign's own CSS cannot repaint the admin.** The preview pane is a sandboxed iframe fed through `srcdoc`, which is the only way to render a merchant's arbitrary `<style>` block honestly \u2014 an inlined preview would show them a campaign styled by the admin and hide one that is broken\n    * **`ctx` has no way to upload a file, so the section borrowed the host's own.** The multipart post for the import goes through `ctx.base` and the same `X-API-Token` the host's `apiUpload` uses, unwrapping `json.data` the same way. It is flagged in the code as the obvious `ctx.apiUpload` addition to the section contract, and it should be made one rather than copied a third time\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end admin \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 segments-admin \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **Segments have a screen, and it is the one they belong on.** \"Lists and tags\" is now **Lists, tags and segments**, with segments as a third block beside them rather than an eighth tab: a list is a topic somebody chose and a tag is something the store noticed, and a segment is a question asked of both. Each row shows what it last counted, when that count was taken as a sentence rather than a timestamp \u2014 \"Counted 12 minutes ago\", because the question anybody actually has about a number is whether it can still be trusted \u2014 and how many campaigns still point at it. Recount takes one now, Duplicate makes a copy to change rather than a segment to build again, and Delete asks once, says how many campaigns are in the way before it asks, and keeps the server's refusal on screen as a banner rather than as a toast that slides away while somebody is still reading the campaign names in it\n    * **The condition builder.** Three groups \u2014 All of these, Any of these, None of these \u2014 and a condition is a field, a question and an answer. The field select is grouped the way the vocabulary groups it and carries every field the store has, including anything an add-on contributed. The operator select carries only the questions that field declares, so \"total spent is before\" is not something anybody can build. The value control is chosen by the type and the operator together rather than by the type alone, which is what a date needs: `before` and `after` want a date, `within_days` and `older_than_days` want a number of days, and they are the same field. A list or a tag is a picker, money carries the store's currency symbol and is typed in whole units, a product is a search box against the catalog showing the title and the SKU, and \"has a customer record: yes\" has no answer box at all. Changing the field resets the question and the answer, because an operator left over from the field before it is one the server would refuse\n    * **A field that has gone away is named rather than dropped.** A segment built on a field an add-on contributed, opened after that add-on has been turned off, keeps the field's name in the select with a note beside it. Without that the browser would select whatever happened to be first in the list and the condition would quietly have become a different question \u2014 on a segment a campaign is pointed at\n    * **The count is beside the form while the question is still being asked.** A debounced preview answers how many people match, one quiet sentence for who is left out and why \u2014 \"14 unsubscribed and 2 suppressed are left out\" \u2014 and ten of them by name, drawn with the same row presenter the subscribers table uses so the sample and the table cannot read as two different things, with the verbs off and each row opening that person. That is what makes a segment checkable before a campaign is sent to it rather than after. A condition somebody has not finished filling in is left out of the count rather than sent half-built, and the panel says so, because a panel that spends its life red while somebody types is a panel nobody reads. A condition the store refuses is marked on the row it belongs to with the reason, worked out from the field the server named \u2014 one message at the top of a nine-condition form leaves somebody counting rows\n    * **A campaign can be narrowed to a segment.** Beside the list picker, a segment picker offering the segments that apply to that list \u2014 the ones limited to it and the ones limited to nothing, never one limited to a different list, which would be an audience of nobody. The count under it becomes the matched number rather than the list's size, with the same excluded line under it, and that number is the one in the confirmation before it goes. The campaign's detail screen reads \"List \u00b7 Segment\" in one row and links to the segment, since the next thing anybody wants after reading its name is to see what it asks\n    * **A segment that could not be counted is an alert on the admin's strip.** A count that failed is recorded on the segment's own row, and the one thing that makes a campaign's audience figure a guess deserves saying at the top of the admin. The alert names the segment and opens it; several name the first and count the rest\n    * **The admin section is now rendered before it is released, not just parsed.** It is one file with no build step, so the only thing between a broken screen and a merchant is somebody opening it. `tests\/js\/section-render-check.mjs` mounts the real file against a stub of the host's `ctx.ui`, seeds the screens with the payloads the contract says they are given, and reads the markup back \u2014 so a method that does not exist, a template hole nothing fills or a number put through the formatter twice is a failing test rather than a blank panel. The PHP suite runs it and skips where node is not installed. It found both bugfixes below on its first run\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end segments-admin \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 audience \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The preference centre can now put somebody on a list, not only take them off one.** `{base}\/newsletter\/p\/{token}` draws every list the person holds a membership on, plus every list the merchant has marked **public**, each with its own description, alongside the name field and \u2014 on a site with more than one language \u2014 a language select. Public is off for every list until it is switched on, including on a store upgrading from phase 1, so nothing appears in front of forty thousand people because a plugin updated; a private list is still joined through a form, an import or the admin, it is simply not offered as a checkbox to everybody the store has ever mailed. Joining from here asks for no second confirmation when the person is already confirmed, because they are holding a link that was mailed to their own address and that is the question double opt-in exists to ask; somebody who has *not* confirmed joins as pending, stays pending and gets no second email, since the confirmation they already hold confirms every pending list at once. Unticking is an unsubscribe on that list with `preferences` recorded as the source, and an id the page did not draw still does nothing at all\n    * **\"Email preferences\" in the customer account.** The signed-in twin of the same screen, at `{base}\/account\/email-preferences`, registered through `onKahunaCartAccountSections` with a POST handler so the account area's own nonce and login check run before this add-on sees anything. Same form, same rules, same class underneath, so the two cannot drift apart \u2014 the only difference is how the store knows who is asking. A customer who has never subscribed is not shown an empty tab: they get the public lists with every box clear, and ticking one creates their subscriber on save with `source = account`, confirmed straight away, because an account address is one the store has already verified and a confirmation email would be asking a question that has been answered. Ticking nothing writes nothing, and a suppressed address is still never written down\n    * **A checkout opt-in that only counts once the money has.** An unticked checkbox in the contact section, declared through `onKahunaCartRegisterCheckoutFields` so a merchant can relabel it, move it, require it in one country or switch it off from the Checkout screen without touching a template \u2014 and so that it can never refuse an order. It acts on `onKahunaCartOrderPaid`, and also on `onKahunaCartOrderCompleted` for an order that completed already paid, which is every instant-capture provider and which never fires the paid event at all; the two together are exactly \"every order that has been paid for, once\", and a second order from the same address writes nothing. Consent is stamped with the moment the order was placed rather than the moment the webhook arrived, because on a redirect provider that is minutes and on a bank transfer days. Which list they join is `checkout.list`, falling back to the store's *first* list rather than its default \u2014 a store whose default list is `Order updates` should not quietly start sending marketing to it \u2014 and a store with no list at all gets one log line naming the order and no subscriber\n    * **The subscriber-to-customer link, everywhere.** `Listeners\\OrderListener` now links on `onKahunaCartOrderPaid` and `onKahunaCartCartRecovered` as well as on completion, and a background sweep catches everything the events miss: an order placed while the add-on was switched off, a customer typed in by hand, every row an import wrote, and the eleven thousand customers a store already had when it installed this. `newsletter.link_customers` is queued from the per-minute job-handler hook on a durable claim, so it runs at most every fifteen minutes rather than 1,440 times a day, and it walks two directions from high-water marks in the new `kahunacart_newsletters_state` table \u2014 new subscribers joined to customers, new customers joined to subscribers. Because it starts from the marks rather than the whole table, a store where nothing has changed costs the same handful of indexed queries whether it has four hundred subscribers or four hundred thousand. It never repoints a subscriber that already names a customer, and it never creates a subscriber from a customer \u2014 which is what keeps an erasure erased, since all that survives one is a suppression carrying a hash and no address\n    * **A newsletter card beside a customer in the admin.** Mounted into `customer.details.side` as the widget `kahunacart-newsletters.customer`, so a merchant can move it or reorder it against the other cards from the Extensions screen. It draws which lists they are on, the last campaign they were actually sent with its date, whether they opened it and whether it bounced, and a link straight to their subscriber screen. A recipient a campaign has not reached yet is not counted as \"last campaign\", because that would put a date on a message nobody received \u2014 and a customer with no subscriber gets no card at all rather than a card reading \"not subscribed\" on every customer the store owns\n    * **A list is public or private, and the Lists screen says which.** A column in the table, a checkbox on the creation form and a toggle in the edit dialog, with the help text saying what the difference actually is: a public list is offered on the preference centre and in the customer account so somebody can join it themselves, and a private one is joined through a form on the site, an import or the admin. `public` also travels through `create_list` and `update_list` in the MCP manifest\n    * **Migration `newsletter_0004_audience`.** `public` on lists, defaulting to 0 so an upgrading store's preference centre is unchanged until it ticks a box; guarded re-checks of the `description` and `language` columns `newsletter_0001_core` already carries; and `kahunacart_newsletters_state`, a name\/value table with a UNIQUE on (`store_id`, `name`) where periodic work claims its interval \u2014 a claim rather than a read-then-write, so two workers whose cron runs overlap sweep once between them rather than once each\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end audience \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 campaigns \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A campaign can test two subject lines against each other.** Tick \"Test two subjects\" under the subject box and a slice of the list \u2014 between ten and fifty percent, on a slider \u2014 gets one of the two, and everybody else gets whichever did better once the window is up. The winner is the subject more people opened, or the one more people clicked on a campaign that is not counting opens, and a tie goes to the subject you wrote first because a test that learned nothing should not quietly change anything. Only the subject differs between the two copies: the body, the links, the layout and the headers are identical, since a test that changed two things at once could not tell you which of them made the difference. Migration `newsletter_0007_variants` adds `subject_b`, `ab_sample_percent`, `ab_window_minutes`, `ab_winner` and `ab_decided_at` to campaigns, and `variant` to sends\n    * **The wait is a queue row with a date on it, not something held in memory.** The send job letters the sample, sends it at the store's usual throttle, and then defers itself to the end of the window through the same `DeferJob` the per-minute throttle already uses \u2014 attempt returned, nothing logged, nothing to go wrong. A worker restart, a deploy or a machine reboot in the middle of a four-hour wait costs nothing. Pause and cancel work at every point of it, including in the middle of the wait, for the reason they already worked: pausing cancels whichever job is holding the campaign, and resuming queues a fresh one that walks back in and finds the test exactly where it was left\n    * **Which people are in the sample is decided by a hash of their send row, not by taking the first N.** The first N rows in id order are the people who joined the list first, who are a store's oldest and most engaged subscribers and are not a sample of anything. The same hash splits the sample down the middle, so it is an exact half each rather than the roughly-even split a hash's low bit would give, and the odd person goes to A. Because the ordering is a function of the send id alone, a campaign interrupted halfway through assigning and resumed produces the identical sample \u2014 and once a letter is on a row, nothing rewrites it\n    * **The test's numbers stay the test's numbers.** Everybody who receives the winner after the decision is recorded under a third letter rather than under the winner's own, so the winner's rate on the campaign screen is still two hundred people's and not the whole campaign's. The alternative puts two numbers side by side that cannot be compared, on the one screen whose entire job is comparing two numbers. The rates themselves are counted from the send rows rather than stored, so the panel recomputes exactly what the send job compared, on any day, from the same rows\n    * **The campaign screen shows the test in both of its states.** While it runs: both subjects, how many people are in each half, what each half has opened and clicked so far, and the time the winner goes out. Afterwards: the winner marked and the loser struck through, because what did not work is half of what a test told you. The recipients table grows a Subject column while a test exists and not otherwise, since a column of dashes on every other campaign teaches nobody anything\n    * **The editor says what will happen in numbers rather than in percentages.** \"482 people get one of the two subjects now; the other 1,928 get the winner at 4:15 PM\" is the sentence under the slider, and it moves as the slider does. \"20% over 4 hours\" is a setting; that is what you are agreeing to, and it is the difference between a merchant who ticks a box and one who knows that their campaign now takes four hours to leave\n    * **Campaign links back to your own store are tagged for your analytics.** `utm_source`, `utm_medium`, `utm_campaign` and `utm_content` go on every link whose host is the store's own, whether or not the campaign counts clicks, which is how Google Analytics, Plausible, Fathom, Matomo and Umami all tell a store which of its visitors arrived from a campaign. Store defaults are `utm.source`, `utm.medium`, `utm.campaign` and `utm.content` on the settings screen; every campaign can override any of them from a group in its editor, which draws the store's own value as each box's placeholder and shows a live example of the link a reader will actually get\n    * **Links to anywhere else are never tagged, and that is the point of it.** Tagging a supplier's site or a news article with this store's campaign name tells the other site nothing useful, puts a store's internal naming in somebody else's logs, and changes a URL the store does not own \u2014 which on a signed or order-sensitive link is a link that has stopped working. What the author tagged themselves is left alone one parameter at a time, so a link written as `?utm_source=twitter` keeps that source and still gets the other three. And nothing else in the query is touched: the obvious implementation parses it and rebuilds it, which turns a dot in a parameter name into an underscore and re-encodes every value somebody typed by hand, so this appends to the query string that is already there and leaves it byte for byte\n    * **`utm_campaign` defaults to the campaign's own name, slugged.** A campaign called \"September news\" tags its links `utm_campaign=september-news`, which is the name a merchant will recognise in a report a month later. One fixed value on the settings screen would file every campaign a store ever sends under a single heading, so the store-wide default for that one is empty and empty means \"use the campaign's name\"\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end campaigns \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 flows-admin \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **An Automations tab, and the three screens under it.** A campaign is something you send; an automation is something that sends itself, one person at a time, starting the moment the store notices something about them. The list says what starts each of them in words rather than in a stored code \u2014 \"When somebody confirms\", \"When the tag vip is added\", \"When somebody buys Subscriptions\" \u2014 with how many steps it has, how many people are in it right now and how many have been all the way through, and filter pills for Active, Paused and Draft whose counts are for the whole store rather than for the rows on screen. A store with none of them gets one sentence saying what an automation is and one button to write the first, rather than an empty table with six column headings over it\n    * **The editor is an ordered list of cards rather than a canvas.** A canvas is what everybody expects from this feature and it is the wrong tool for what these automations actually are: a line of at most a dozen steps with one branch in it. A list has an order that reads out loud, works on a phone, gives the branch selects something to point at, and can be moved with two buttons instead of with a mouse gesture nobody discovers. Six kinds of step, each with only its own fields on the card: an email with the same merge tags, layout and preview a campaign has, a wait written either as a length of time or as a weekday and an hour, the three tag and list steps as pickers over the store's own tags and lists, and a branch on whether somebody opened or clicked the email above it\n    * **A step somebody is standing on cannot be removed, and the button says so before the save rather than after it.** The API refuses that with a 422 and a sentence naming the step; the editor refuses it in place, because the count is on the step and a disabled button with a reason beside it is a better answer than a refusal three fields later on a form somebody has since changed twice\n    * **The two switches every store gets wrong once, each with a sentence under it.** *Let somebody go through this more than once* is off by default, which is what a welcome series wants and what a thank-you-for-buying does not. *Take somebody out as soon as they buy something* stops the rest of the emails the moment an order completes, which is worth having on anything trying to talk somebody into buying so that nobody is nudged towards what they already own. Neither is a checkbox with a two-word label somebody has to guess at\n    * **A wait until a weekday names the store's timezone rather than leaving it to be assumed.** \"Tuesday at 9:00 AM\" that turns out to mean nine in the morning in UTC is the kind of thing a merchant finds out from a subscriber. The line under the two selects names the timezone the store is actually in, and a store whose configuration has not said is told where to set it\n    * **The detail screen shows where every person actually is.** Four figures across the top \u2014 moving, waiting, finished and left, with the commonest reason people left under the last of them \u2014 then the automation drawn as the numbered list it is, each email step carrying what it did with its open and click rates and a button into the campaign behind it. Underneath, everybody who has ever been in it: \"Waiting until Tue 9:00 AM at step 3\", filterable by status, paged, with the reason under anybody who left\n    * **Enrol somebody by hand, and take somebody out.** The search is over subscribers by name or address and it offers everybody it finds, because a manual enrolment goes through exactly the same rules as an automatic one and the server's refusal names the reason \u2014 hiding people from a search would leave nobody to explain the absence. Taking one person out asks first and says what it means: they stop where they are, and what has already been sent to them stays sent\n    * **Deleting one with people still in it is refused, and the second question offers the way past it.** The API answers 422 with a sentence saying how many are still moving through it; that sentence is what the confirmation asks with, rather than a guess at the number, and agreeing takes them out with a reason of \"the automation was deleted\" rather than leaving them in a flow that no longer exists\n    * **One line on the Overview when anything is running.** \"1 automation is running, with 41 people in them\", with a link to them. A store with nothing running draws nothing at all, because a line reading \"0 automations are running\" is a line about a feature that store does not use\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end flows-admin \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 recipes \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **Three automations that come ready-made, written out in full.** A welcome series, an after-somebody-buys thank you and a win-back \u2014 steps, waits, subjects, preheaders and Markdown bodies, all of it \u2014 behind a Start from a recipe panel on the Automations screen. An empty automation editor is a hard thing to start from: a merchant knows they want a welcome series and has no idea whether the first email goes out straight away or the next morning, whether the second is two days later or five, or what it should say. Creating one writes a **draft**, so nothing is sent, nobody is enrolled and no discount code is minted until the merchant reads it through and switches it on themselves. Each recipe also says what to change first, in its own words, rather than leaving somebody to work out which of the three emails is a placeholder\n    * **A recipe is a file rather than code.** `recipes\/<key>.yaml` holds the whole thing, so a fourth one is a fourth file and the copy can be read and argued about without opening a class. A test parses every shipped file and puts every message through the same merge-tag sandbox the campaign editor uses, because a tag misspelled in a body would otherwise throw for everybody who reaches that step, weeks from now, unattended\n    * **`GET \/flows\/recipes` and `POST \/flows\/recipes\/{key}`, with `list_automation_recipes` and `use_automation_recipe`.** The listing carries what each recipe does, its ladder in a sentence a step, what to edit before switching it on, and whether this particular store can use it with the reason when it cannot. Creating one goes through the same save path the editor posts through, so a recipe's ladder is validated, numbered and given its campaigns by the one piece of code that does that rather than by a second way of writing the same rows\n    * **The post-purchase recipe leaves its review email out on a store that already asks for reviews.** KahunaCart can email customers for a review of its own on `reviews.request_after_days`, and two review requests a week apart is one too many \u2014 so where that is switched on the recipe writes the thank you and stops, and its description on the panel says so before anybody creates it. Decided once, when the draft is written, rather than checked on every send: a branch about a setting rather than about the person walking the automation is not what a branch is for\n    * **An email step can hand out a discount code that belongs to one person.** `config_json.coupon_group` names a coupon group by the words on the Coupons screen, and the step draws one code for each person as their message is built \u2014 through core's own `CouponGenerator`, so the code is stamped from the same group template, carries the same limits and appears in the same group listing as one generated by hand. The message reads it as `{{ coupon.code }}`. A win-back email carrying one code for everybody is a code on a voucher site by Thursday\n    * **One code per person, and never two.** The code is drawn after the send row exists and inside the same transaction, so a redelivered tick finds the row already written and mints nothing, and a step that fails rolls the code back with the send. A retry cannot cost a store a second discount. A group that does not exist, one somebody switched off, or one whose pattern has no room left all end the same way: that one person leaves with `failed` against their name and a line in the log naming the group, and nothing is mailed \u2014 an email carrying a blank where a code should be is worse than an email that did not go\n    * **The win-back recipe makes its own tag and will not make its own coupon group.** A `tag_added` automation whose tag does not exist never starts anybody and never says why, and a tag is a label \u2014 so it is created. A coupon group is money, and an add-on inventing a discount it was never told the size of would be the wrong kind of helpful: the recipe is listed as unavailable until the group exists, with a sentence naming what to make and where\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end recipes \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 overview-chart \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **\"Last campaigns\" on the Overview: the last few sends, side by side, as the five rates that say how each of them did.** One group of bars per campaign, oldest on the left, and five bars in each group for the open, click, unsubscribe, bounce and complaint rate \u2014 all of them a share of what that campaign actually reached, so a send to four hundred people can be held against a send to four thousand. It exists because a figure on its own says nothing: a 24% open rate is a good month or a bad one entirely depending on the five before it, and that is a comparison a merchant was previously making by opening five campaigns in five tabs. Bounces and complaints are on it for the same reason \u2014 neither moves enough in one campaign to be noticed, and both are what eventually stop a store being able to send at all. It sits beside \"New subscribers\" where the window is wide enough for two charts and stacks where it is not, the picker at its head chooses the last 5, 10 or 20 and remembers what you chose, and only a campaign that has actually gone out is on it: a draft with five bars on the floor would read as a campaign that did badly rather than as one that has not happened\n    * **`campaigns.performance` on the overview payload, and three more rates on every campaign.** `unsubscribe_rate`, `bounce_rate` and `complaint_rate` join `open_rate` and `click_rate` wherever a campaign is answered, with `reached` beside them naming the number they are all a share of \u2014 one denominator, worked out in one place, so the chart and the table under it can never be dividing by different numbers. The chart's own read is separate from \"Recent campaigns\" because that list is the five newest rows whatever their status, drafts included; this is the last twenty that went out, oldest first, as a slim projection rather than twenty campaign bodies. `get_overview` answers it too, so \"how do the last few sends compare\" is a question an assistant can answer against the real store\n    * **A `barChart(spec)` helper beside `lineChart`, in the same frame.** Grouped bars in core's own `.chart` frame with the numbers on the gridlines, the tooltip that opens on a tap as well as on a hover, an axis that tops out at a round percentage above the highest bar rather than fixed at 100 \u2014 because an axis at 100 draws an 0.4% complaint rate as nothing at all \u2014 and no colour written down anywhere but the host's own custom properties. A line answers \"how did this change\" and bars answer \"how do these compare\", which is why the five rates of one campaign are not drawn as five lines across five sends that have nothing to do with each other\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end overview-chart \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 unique-codes \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A campaign can give every person on the list a code of their own.** A campaign has always carried one `coupon_code`, a single string rendered into forty thousand messages \u2014 which is one code on a voucher site by Thursday, and no way of telling how many people it actually brought back. Only an automation's email step could mint per person, and an automation is the wrong shape for the announcement a store sends once. The campaign editor now has a second box beside the first: **Give everyone a code of their own** names a campaign on the Coupons screen, and every recipient has a code minted from it as their message is built, through the same `CouponGenerator` the Coupons screen's own Generate button calls. Same template, same limits, same pattern, same listing \u2014 so the Coupons screen's **Codes**, **Used** and **Budget** columns count them and a redemption records the order it was redeemed on. That is the whole point of it over a shared code: how many of the codes you sent came back, rather than how many times one string was typed in. Write it into the message the same way either way, as `{{ coupon.code }}` or `{{ coupon.url }}`; naming a group wins over the shared code. **Minted once however many times the row is attempted.** A campaign is one job for the whole campaign and that job is re-entered by design \u2014 the throttle defers it, the time budget defers it, a pause and resume replace it, a dying worker has it redelivered \u2014 so the code cannot be minted per attempt. It goes on the send row, and only an empty column mints, exactly as the message id already worked. Two workers reaching one row produce one winner, and the loser abandons its code unredeemed rather than mailing somebody a code the store has no record of sending. **A group that cannot answer fails the row rather than sending without a code**, because \"here is your code:\" above a gap is worse than a message that did not go; enough in a row trips the campaign's circuit breaker and pauses it, which is the right end for a group switched off or out of pattern. Naming a group that does not exist is refused when you save, so the usual typo costs a sentence rather than an audience. Migration `newsletters_0010_coupon_groups` \u2014 `coupon_group` on campaigns, `coupon_code` on sends. See docs\/campaigns.md#one-code-or-a-code-each\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end unique-codes \u2500\u2500 -->\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **A campaign says what address it will go out as, before it goes.** Three settings can supply the From and the most specific wins \u2014 the campaign's own, this add-on's, then the Email plugin's \u2014 which means the two that lose are invisible. A store that set the newsletter's From while trying one provider carries on sending as that provider's domain after moving to another, and finds out when the new provider refuses the message for an unverified sender: an error naming a field, on a different screen, that nobody remembers touching. The campaign's Details panel now names the address and which of the three settings supplied it\n    * **A provider card redraws itself after Set up, and says whether the webhook has ever been heard from.** The settings payload behind the cards is fetched once and shared by the two fields that read it, and setting a webhook up is precisely the moment that answer stops being true \u2014 so the card sat there offering first-time setup over a webhook it had just created, and only a page reload told the truth. The read can now be told its answer is stale, and Set up tells it. Beside the address there is a mark for what is known about it: a tick where events have actually arrived, and an ellipsis where the webhook is registered and nothing has ever been heard from it. Two marks rather than one mark in two colours, because this gets read in screenshots and by people who do not see grey against green \u2014 and the second state is the one worth drawing, since a store in it looks finished and is not. Every campaign screen can show delivered, opened and clicked from the store's own pixel and click redirect while the provider has reported nothing at all\n    * **A campaign screen can watch itself.** It is the one screen in this add-on whose numbers move while somebody is looking at them \u2014 deliveries land over seconds, opens and clicks over the following minutes \u2014 and the only way to see any of it was to reload the page, which threw away your place in the recipient table. There is a Refresh button now, and a \"Keep it up to date\" switch beside it that fetches the campaign again every thirty seconds while the screen is open. Thirty because that is the pace of the thing being watched; anything faster is asking four times for the same numbers. The refresh draws over what is already there rather than blanking the screen and showing a loading state, keeps the recipient page and status filter you had chosen, and says nothing when one timed request fails, because a screen somebody left open is not the place to raise an alert that the next tick will repeat if it is real. The switch is remembered between visits, and the timer stops when you leave\n    * **A draft says who it is going to, before it goes.** The one screen carrying a Send button could not answer the one question worth asking before pressing it. It printed the size of the campaign's list, which is a different number \u2014 a list counts people who have unsubscribed, never confirmed, or landed on the suppression list, and on a store that has been running a while the two are far apart. A draft now shows the figure the send itself would produce, worked out by the same walk that writes the send rows, with the first twenty people named underneath it and a line saying who else is on the list and will not be mailed and why. Twenty is enough to recognise an audience \u2014 to notice that every address is on one domain, or that this is the list imported by mistake last week \u2014 and few enough that nobody reads it as the audience itself. In the campaigns table, a draft's \"Sent to\" column showed a dash, which made the one row still worth acting on the only row with nothing in it; it now carries the size of its list as \"1 on list\", with a tooltip saying what that counts, because the exact figure costs a walk of the list and belongs on the campaign rather than repeated down a page of them\n    * **The recipient list says how many links each person pressed, and their record says which ones.** A campaign's `clicked` counts people rather than presses \u2014 one row per recipient who pressed anything, which is what stops one keen reader working through a whole message and taking the click rate past a hundred percent \u2014 and that left nowhere at all showing that they had pressed more than one. Recipients now carries a **Clicks** column, counted for the whole page in one grouped query rather than one per row, and a subscriber's **What they were sent** lists the actual URLs under each result with the time of each press, fetched only for the messages that were clicked. Both read rows the store was already writing and nothing new is recorded\n    * **A link is shown without the campaign tagging this store put on it.** `?utm_source=newsletter&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=\u2026` is the store repeating its own working back at itself, and on a long campaign name it is most of the line. The `utm_` parameters are dropped for display and everything else is kept \u2014 `\/shop?category=hats` is the merchant's own URL and every part of it is something they wrote \u2014 with the whole address still on the link and on its tooltip, because that is what the person actually pressed\n    * **The Email plugin writes the campaign headers now, where it is new enough to.** `List-Unsubscribe`, `List-Unsubscribe-Post`, `Precedence`, `X-KC-Campaign` and `Message-ID` used to go onto the Symfony message from here, because the Grav Email plugin had no parameter for a header it had not heard of. It has one as of 5.0.9 \u2014 a `headers` parameter on `buildMessage()`, and a public `applyHeaders()` for a caller like this one that builds its message with `message()` \u2014 so on a store with that version the plugin does the writing: it knows which names Symfony insists on as something other than loose text, it replaces rather than repeats, and it logs anything it could not write. Which of the two happens is decided by asking the installed plugin rather than comparing version numbers, so a backport counts, and the old path is kept rather than dropped \u2014 the headers in question are the ones that give a recipient a way off the list, and a store that has not updated the Email plugin yet must not lose them\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 webhooks \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **Provider webhooks, so the numbers on a campaign screen stop being guesses.** `{base}\/newsletter\/webhook\/{provider}\/{secret}` receives what a mail transport knows and this add-on cannot: whether a message reached the receiving server, whether it came straight back, and whether the person who got it pressed the spam button. Six parsers \u2014 SMTP2GO, Amazon SES over SNS, Postmark, Mailgun, SendGrid and Resend \u2014 each normalise a completely different payload into the same five words, so everything downstream is written once and a seventh provider is a parser and a fixture rather than a branch in the handler. Every parser was written against that provider's current documentation and is tested against the documented sample payloads, which are kept in `tests\/fixtures\/webhooks\/`: each of these providers has renamed a field at some point, and a parser written from memory is one that reads null forever without failing anything\n    * **A hard bounce ends an address and a soft one does not.** Hard stamps the send, writes a suppression with the provider's own words and the provider's name on it, and sets the subscriber to `bounced` on every list they were on. Soft stamps the send and nothing else \u2014 a mailbox that was full this morning is not a customer worth losing \u2014 but **three soft bounces for one address in thirty days are treated as a hard one**, because at that point it is not a full mailbox. A complaint suppresses and unsubscribes from every list with `complaint` recorded as the reason, since somebody who pressed the spam button is not choosing between the store's topics. An event that cannot be tied to a campaign but names an address the store holds still suppresses it: the suppression list protects a person and the send row keeps a campaign's numbers, and the first does not depend on the second\n    * **Nothing is ever counted twice.** Every one of these providers retries a webhook it did not get a 200 for and several send the same delivery twice on their own, so no counter is incremented per event: the send rows are written idempotently \u2014 a send's status only ever moves forward and every stamp is guarded by an `IS NULL` \u2014 and a campaign's counters are rebuilt from those rows once after each batch of events, the same rebuild `markSent` uses. A provider's open for a send our own pixel already recorded changes the numbers not at all\n    * **Correlation needs nothing configured on five of the six.** This add-on already mints the `Message-ID` before a campaign message leaves and stores it on the send row, and five of the six providers echo it back, so the join is direct. The sixth is Postmark, whose `MessageID` is a UUID of its own invention and which returns no headers on any webhook ever \u2014 so every campaign message now carries `X-KahunaCart-Send` naming its send row, and `X-PM-Metadata-KahunaCart-Send` beside it, which is the same number under the name Postmark wants it in. Both go on every message rather than on the messages of a store that happens to be on Postmark, because nothing in the render path knows which transport the mail will leave through and the alternative is asking a merchant to configure something. `docs\/providers.md` says per provider which path it uses and what to tick for it to work\n    * **The address gives a stranger nothing.** A provider slug nobody has heard of is a 404 before the body is read; a wrong path secret is the same 404, compared in constant time, so a scanner cannot tell \"no such provider\" from \"wrong secret\" and learns nothing about what a store uses. Four of the six sign their requests and a forged signature is a 401 \u2014 that one *is* distinguishable, and has to be, because a merchant who pasted the wrong key needs their provider's dashboard to show a failing webhook rather than a green tick beside events nobody is recording. Refusals are logged once a minute at most, so a wrong key retried five hundred times is five hundred retries and one log line\n    * **Amazon's certificate is only ever fetched from Amazon.** SNS signs every message with a certificate whose address travels inside the message, so a receiver that fetched whatever URL it was handed would be checking signatures against a certificate the attacker supplied. The scheme has to be `https` and the host has to match `sns.<region>.amazonaws.com`, the same rule is applied to the `SubscribeURL` before a subscription is ever confirmed, an `UnsubscribeConfirmation` is never followed, and the certificate is cached under `user\/data\/kahunacart\/newsletters\/` named by a hash of its URL so a campaign's worth of events does not fetch one forty thousand times\n    * **A payload nobody can read is still a 200.** Logged with its first 500 bytes and answered normally, because every one of these providers treats a 4xx as a reason to retry for days and Postmark treats most of them as a reason to drop the event outright \u2014 so a 400 turns one bad payload into a week of noise or a permanently lost bounce. A GET is a 405 with `Allow: POST` rather than a 404, so a merchant who pastes the URL into a browser is told what it wants; a HEAD is a 200 with no body, because several providers probe an address before they will save it. Every answer is JSON, small, and says nothing a stranger could use\n    * **The Providers block on the Settings screen.** One card per provider with its webhook URL copyable, the secret in it, whether the key that provider signs with is on file, and \"Last event received 3 minutes ago\" or \"Nothing received yet\" \u2014 the one line that tells a merchant their setup works and the one nothing else on any screen would say. **Generate** writes a fresh thirty-six-byte secret into the plugin's configuration, because a merchant asked to invent one invents `newsletter`. For SMTP2GO, **Set up in SMTP2GO** creates the webhook through their API for the five events this store acts on, with the output format set to JSON and the send header registered \u2014 the whole setup, one button. Every card carries a short paragraph naming that provider's own screens, because \"configure a webhook\" is not instructions and every one of these dashboards calls it something else\n    * **Three things on the admin's alert strip.** The store's bounce rate over thirty days above `alerts.bounce_rate_percent`, five per cent by default, which is the number the big mailbox providers watch. A campaign that has gone out with a webhook configured and nothing heard from it in thirty days, which is the failure this whole area exists to make visible \u2014 every number on the campaign screen looks fine and every one is missing its second half. And something posting at a provider's address with no secret configured, which is almost always the merchant halfway through setting one up with the wrong URL, in which case those events are being thrown away and nothing else would say so. All three link straight at the Providers block\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-newsletters webhook:replay <provider> <file>`.** Runs a saved payload through the real parser and the real handler and prints what it would do \u2014 which send row it found, whether it read the bounce as hard or soft, what it took the address to be, and exactly what it would write. Every one of those is a place the answer could be \"nothing\", and knowing which one is the whole diagnosis. `--apply` actually does it; without the flag it writes nothing at all, and the dry run walks the same code rather than a second implementation's guess at it\n    * **Migration `newsletter_0006_providers`.** `subtype` and `send_id` on the events log, so a bounce's hardness is stored rather than merely acted on and the log can be joined back to the send it was about; `provider` on sends, written by the first event that arrives for a row and left alone afterwards, so a store that changed transports mid-campaign can tell the two halves apart. Two indexes: the soft-bounce count over an address and a window, and \"when did we last hear from this provider\", which the Providers block and one of the alerts both ask on every admin screen that draws them\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end webhooks \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The Providers block draws a card only for the transport plugins that are installed.** Six webhook addresses on a store that sends through one provider were five addresses nobody would paste anywhere, and the one that mattered was harder to find among them. A provider's card appears when its `email-<provider>` plugin is on the site, whether or not its settings have been saved; with none installed the block says which plugins to install\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 webhooks \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **\"Delivered\" and \"Accepted\" are two different words now, and the card says whichever is true.** The charts leaf found the disagreement and left it: a campaign's `delivered` counter counts every send at or past `delivered` on the status ladder, and this add-on's own open pixel moves a send past it \u2014 so a store whose transport reports nothing showed \"Delivered 1\" beside a chart that correctly drew no delivered line at all. With no delivery event ever received, the card now reads **Accepted** and shows what the transport took off our hands, with a hint saying the transport has not been asked to report deliveries and a link to the Providers block. Once any delivery event has arrived it reads **Delivered** and counts `delivered_at` stamps, which is the same column the chart draws, so the two cannot disagree by construction. A campaign sent before the webhook was set up honestly shows zero\n    * **A message the provider refused is no longer a subscriber lost.** Every provider has a word for a message it would not send at all, and two very different things arrive under it: the provider refusing the *address*, because it holds it on its own suppression list or it bounced, complained or unsubscribed there \u2014 and the provider refusing the *message*, because the store reached its daily quota, a virus scanner read an attachment or the provider did not like a header. This add-on treated both as a hard bounce, which was defensible when SendGrid's `dropped` was the only one it ever saw and is not now that five providers report one. A refused address still suppresses and still sets the subscriber to `bounced`, exactly as before. A refused message marks the send bounced, so the campaign's bounce figure is honest \u2014 the message really did not arrive \u2014 and leaves the subscriber, their lists and the suppression list untouched. It is written to the events log under its own subtype, `refused`, so three quota refusals in a month do not add up to a suppression under the three-strikes rule. Which of the two a given event is comes from the transport plugin: SendGrid's `Unsubscribed Address`, `Bounced Address`, `Spam Reporting Address` and `Invalid` are the address and its `Spam Content`, `Invalid SMTPAPI header` and `Recipient List over Package Quota` are the message; Mailgun's `suppress-*` and Resend's `email.suppressed` are always the address; Amazon's `Reject` is always the message\n    * **The webhook has a rate-limit bucket of its own, and it is the highest.** It counted in the signup bucket before \u2014 ten an hour \u2014 which would have meant a campaign to forty thousand people recording ten delivery reports, answering 429 to the rest, and then answering 429 to the retries as well. `rate_limits.webhook` defaults to six thousand an hour, because nothing at that address is a person and everything at it arrives in bursts the size of a campaign from a handful of the provider's own addresses. What actually protects that address is the path secret and the signature; the limit is the ceiling that keeps a flood from becoming database writes\n    * **The parsers are gone, and every provider is its own transport plugin's.** Six webhook parsers, an SMTP2GO API client, an SMTP2GO domain lookup, Amazon's certificate store, a table of SPF hosts and DKIM zones and a closed list of six provider names have all been deleted. `Providers\\ProviderSource` answers out of the Grav Email plugin's contract and nowhere else, so a provider arrives by installing a plugin and the Providers block draws a card for each one that did. The webhook route answers 404 for a provider nobody registered, which is the same 404 a wrong secret gets\n    * **What moved where.** The parsers, their sample payloads and their tests are in `grav-plugin-email-smtp2go`, `-amazon`, `-sendgrid`, `-postmark`, `-mailgun`, `-mailersend` and `-resend`, each in that plugin's own `classes\/Provider\/` and `tests\/`. The SMTP2GO API client and its `domain\/view` lookup are in `email-smtp2go`; Amazon's certificate store is in `email-amazon`; the DNS facts each provider needs are each provider's own `DomainFacts`. The SMTP2GO-specific half of `docs\/providers.md` is now that plugin's README\n    * **The settings fields for the verification keys are gone.** `providers.smtp2go.api_key`, `providers.postmark.basic_user`, `providers.postmark.basic_password`, `providers.mailgun.signing_key`, `providers.sendgrid.public_key` and `providers.resend.signing_secret` have been removed from the blueprint and are no longer read. They live in that provider's own transport plugin, beside the credentials it already sends with: `email-mailgun`'s signing key, `email-sendgrid`'s verification key, `email-postmark`'s basic auth pair, `email-resend`'s signing secret, `email-smtp2go`'s API key. **A store that never moved one across should move it now** \u2014 the previous release read both places and said so on the card; this one reads only the plugin. `providers.<name>.secret` stays where it is, and so does `providers.smtp2go.auth_header`, which is a header a merchant invented rather than a credential SMTP2GO issued\n    * **A store whose plugins are not all current is told which one, and nothing else breaks.** No card and one sentence where the Grav Email plugin is older than 5.1.0; a card naming the plugin to update where a transport plugin is a release behind; \"this transport cannot report deliveries\" where it has no API to report with. Sending, campaigns and automations do not go through any of this and are unaffected\n    * **The Settings screen no longer keeps a version number for somebody else's plugin.** Whether custom headers survive a transport is the transport plugin's own answer, and where nothing answers the screen says the transport has not been asked rather than comparing `email-smtp2go` against `1.0.1`. The engine row takes its name from the provider's own label for the same reason\n    * **The send id header goes out as `X-Grav-Send-Id` even where the Email plugin is too old to name it.** It used to fall back to `X-KahunaCart-Send` on a site with no contract \u2014 but a site with no contract has no provider at all and nothing there could read either name, so mail sent today is stamped with the name the site will use the moment it updates. Events arriving under the old name are still read, for the release after which nothing will be sending under it\n    * **`docs\/providers.md` and `docs\/deliverability.md` are rewritten around what your transport plugin does.** Where each credential lives, what each of the part-updated states looks like on screen, and which questions the checks can only answer when a transport plugin answers them\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end webhooks \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The four public pages look designed now.** Confirm, unsubscribe, the preference centre and the one-sentence message page are drawn as one centred card on the page ground, with a mark above the heading that says at a glance whether it is good news, a question or a dead link, and the store's own button. The stylesheet reads the base plugin's `--kc-*` tokens, so a theme that has coloured KahunaCart has coloured these too, and it is not enqueued at all when `builtin_css` is off. Every rule is scoped and wrapped in `:where()`, so a theme's own selector wins on its own weight; the Kahuna theme's page container for bare storefront sections is honoured by putting the card inside the section rather than on it\n    * **Confirming a signup is two clicks now, not three.** The page the email's button opens presses its own button the moment it loads, posts the confirmation in the background and swaps in the answer, so a person lands on \"you are on the list\" straight from the email with no reload and never sees a second button. Spam filters that follow links without running scripts still only see the page, so the record that a person acted is kept; browsers driven by automation are left to the button. `confirm.auto_submit`, on by default, turns it off for a store that would rather have the click\n    * The Settings screen is four panels in the store's card grid (Sending, Tracking, Who campaigns come from, Can this store send mail?) with the worker note underneath, rather than two loose columns of headings. The transport panel's second row is labelled \"Status\" instead of repeating the panel's own title, and the from fallback reads \"Not set, so the Email plugin's own is used\"\n    * Cards stacked on one screen, the lists over the tags and the settings blocks over the worker note, now sit apart the way the dashboard's cards do\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 polish \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A test send no longer puts a stranger on the mailing list.** Sending yourself a copy of a campaign writes a real subscriber row \u2014 it has to, because the links in that copy are signed with a subscriber's own secret and the unsubscribe link has to point at somebody. The audience already excluded those rows; the table, the status counts, the overview tiles, the thirty-day growth line and the CSV export did not, so a merchant who tested one campaign found their own address in the subscribers table as \"Waiting to confirm\" and a chip above it reading \"Waiting to confirm 1\" that led to an empty table. The flag lives in `data_json`, which cannot be filtered in SQL across the three engines, so the same fact is now written a second time in two ordinary columns \u2014 `source = admin` with `source_ref = test-send`, written by the one code path that writes the flag \u2014 and the repository excludes on those. A `test` filter takes `only` or `all`, and the Subscribers screen grows a **Test addresses** chip that is only drawn on a store that has actually sent a test\n    * **The test subscriber has a name, so a merge tag has something to print.** It was created nameless, so `{{ subscriber.first_name }}` rendered as nothing and the first test copy of the first campaign arrived with the subject \", KahunaCart early access is open\" \u2014 which is the one moment somebody is looking hardest. The row is now called \"Test\". The other half of that problem is the merchant's to solve and the answer is core's `default` filter, which the merge sandbox already allowed and which is now written down: `{{ subscriber.first_name|default('there') }}` is in `docs\/campaigns.md`, in the editor's \"Merge tags you can use\" panel, and held there by a test\n    * **The `Message-ID` follows the store's canonical URL rather than the host somebody reached the admin on.** A local store reached through a tunnel minted ids ending `@kahunacart.rhuk.net` while the store's own URL was something else, because the site root a job carries is the root of the *request that queued it* \u2014 which is exactly right for a link a customer has to click, and exactly wrong for a name that a bounce report quotes back weeks later and that `sends.message_id` and `kahunacart_email_log.message_id` join on. `system.custom_base_url` now wins for the id where it merely comes second for a link, and the stamped root is the fallback. Neither branch reads a request header\n    * **The Settings screen says which SMTP2GO transport a store is on.** Their plugin registers one engine and two transports \u2014 an HTTP API and plain SMTP \u2014 which are two different answers to every other question on that screen, including whether the one-click unsubscribe headers survive. The engine row now reads \"SMTP2GO (API)\" or \"SMTP2GO (SMTP)\", so it and the unsubscribe-headers row underneath are visibly talking about the same thing. The row that reports those headers is also now labelled \"Unsubscribe headers\" rather than \"Send a test\", which is what it said\n    * **Counted nouns are pluralised.** \"1 people will receive this\" was the sentence directly above the button that mails everybody, and \"Send this to 1 people?\" was the confirmation on the button itself. Every sentence in the section that names a number and then a noun now goes through a two-form `plural()` helper against a `_ONE` sibling key, and every `_ONE` key in the map has to be reachable from the section or a test fails\n    * **Screens about one thing are no longer headed with the name of the list they came from.** The campaign editor's first fieldset said \"Campaigns\", so did the heading over the facts on a campaign's own screen, and the campaign's created-at and created-by were labelled \"Joined\" and \"Add somebody\" \u2014 the subscribers screen's words, on a campaign. Campaigns now have \"Details\", \"Created\" and \"Created by\" of their own, and the tab title and both placeholder headings say \"Campaign\" rather than \"Campaigns\". A test now holds every `SCREAMING_SNAKE` literal in the section against `Labels::MAP`, so a key that exists nowhere on the server \u2014 the way a string ends up in English forever in every language \u2014 is a failing test\n    * **The button that saves a new campaign says \"Save\".** It said \"Write a campaign\", which is the empty state's call to action and the heading above the form, and reading it as the button underneath the form suggested it would open something rather than save what was in it. Same for a new template\n    * **\"Send it\" is its own box, under \"Send a test\".** The schedule field and the button that starts the campaign sat inside the panel headed \"Send a test\", so the control that mails forty thousand people read as part of testing. They now have their own group with the schedule field and the button together and the line about the worker beside them\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end polish \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 charts \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **Both charts are drawn against an axis now, by one helper rather than two.** The Overview's growth line was bars in an SVG with no axis and a campaign's first forty-eight hours was four lines in an SVG with no axis, so a store with one recipient got a solid grey rectangle filling the panel and a store where everybody joined on one day got a single bar leaning against nothing. Both go through the same `lineChart` now, and its frame is the frame core's own revenue chart uses: a plot that is a fixed height at every width instead of a viewBox that goes short and wide on a big monitor, gridlines that carry the numbers a point is read against, ticks along the bottom, and a tooltip that is a real element appearing on the first hover and on a tap rather than an SVG title the browser shows after a second and never shows on touch at all\n    * **A count axis tops out at a round number whose quarters are whole.** Nobody counts people in quarters, so an axis is four times a round quarter rather than the tallest point: one recipient is a point a quarter of the way up an axis labelled 4, 3, 2, 1, 0, and thirty-one signups sit against 40, 30, 20, 10, 0. The curves are monotone cubic, which means a step \u2014 thirty days of nobody and then thirty-one people in an afternoon \u2014 bends into place without the dip below zero a plain smoothing curve draws on the way in, and a growth chart that dips under its own axis is a chart claiming people unsubscribed\n    * **The growth panel shows the month before the month it is drawing.** Thirty-one people in a month is a good month or a bad one entirely depending on the previous one, so the thirty days before are drawn dashed underneath with both ranges and both totals in the legend. The overview answers them from the same single query it already made, over sixty days instead of thirty, so nothing new is asked of the database\n    * **The last campaign is on the front screen.** The morning after a send, the Overview is the screen somebody opens and the last campaign was three clicks further in \u2014 Campaigns, find the row, open it. It now sits under the four figures: the campaign's name and status, when it went out, its own figures including the unsubscribes, its first-forty-eight-hours chart through the same helper as the detail screen, and a button to open it. A store that has never sent anything shows no panel rather than an empty one\n    * **A campaign's detail screen is laid out like a dashboard.** The figures stay the first row; the chart is a full-width panel with its title on the left and when sending started on the right; the campaign's details sit beside the per-link clicks in the shared two-column grid; and the recipient table is its own panel underneath rather than a fourth thing in a stack\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end charts \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 settings-home \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The newsletter's Settings screen is the newsletter's settings.** It used to print the values it could not change beside an **Edit these settings** button that threw you out of KahunaCart into the Grav admin's plugin list, which is two places to stand for one job. It is now the plugin's own configuration form \u2014 the same fields, the same help text, the same validation, saved by the same **Save** button in the admin's header \u2014 hosted by KahunaCart inside its own admin. A store on a KahunaCart older than 1.2.0 gets one line saying where the settings still are, and a link to them\n    * **The three things a form cannot answer are on the form, among the settings they are about.** No mix of boxes on one screen and read-only summaries on another: **Can this store send mail?** sits at the top of the Sending tab, above every number about how fast, and says who campaigns go out as, whether the Email plugin is sending and whether the unsubscribe headers survive the transport; one line about the queue worker sits under those numbers, because none of them means anything if nothing is running the queue; and the **Providers** tab is the provider cards. All three are web components this add-on ships in `admin-next\/fields\/`, drawn by admin-next as blueprint field types, and every string in them comes from the same label dictionary the admin section reads, so nothing is written in English twice\n    * **The blueprint has no provider secret fields.** Seven text boxes for values nobody should type: the path secret is thirty-six random bytes the **Generate a secret** button mints and the card then shows in the URL beside it, and a person asked to invent the one thing standing between a stranger who guessed the address and this store's suppression list invents `newsletter`. The keys are unchanged and are still editable by hand; `docs\/configuration.md` documents them as config-only, along with the four records the two buttons write. The bounce-rate alert moved to the Deliverability tab and the webhook rate limit to Routes, beside the other three\n    * **Set up writes down what it did, and the card says it.** *Registered in SMTP2GO on 5 September 2026, listening for delivered, bounce, spam complaint, open, click and reject*, in place of the instructions for doing the same thing by hand \u2014 which fold away behind **Do it by hand instead**, because somebody who has pressed the button does not need to be told how to press it. Generating a new secret says outright that the webhook registered there is now posting at an address that answers 404, and keeps saying so until Set up is pressed again. The dashboard's \"nothing has arrived\" alert leaves a webhook registered in the last day alone: the store has to send something first, and an alert about a webhook thirty seconds old is one that teaches people to ignore the strip\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end settings-home \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The campaign screens count the statuses in one question, and a link repeated through an email is rewritten once.** Opening Campaigns used to ask the database for a page of campaigns per status just to read the total off it, bodies and all, and a tracked address that appears in ten places in one email was minted ten times over. Both screens and every render do the work once and reuse the answer.\n\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 markdown-editor \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The message box has core's markdown toolbar over it.** Bold, italic, code, heading, quote, lists and a link, with cmd\/ctrl+B, I and K, on the campaign body and on every automation's email step. It is core's own control rather than a copy \u2014 `ctx.ui.markdownField()` \u2014 so this add-on's message box and the product description are the same thing, and a change to one is a change to both. The textarea underneath is unchanged, so what is posted, validated and previewed is what it always was, and the live preview panel beside it still renders the real layout server-side. On a KahunaCart too old to have the helper the field falls back to the plain box it used to be\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end markdown-editor \u2500\u2500 -->\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **The Set up button reads the store's own record rather than the provider's receipt.** It asked whether the provider had handed back a webhook id, and Mailgun has none to hand back \u2014 it registers one webhook per event type \u2014 so a card with six live webhooks on it still offered first-time setup, which is the one line on the screen that would have told a merchant it had worked. It now asks whether this store has set the provider up, which is the same fact the sentence underneath was already using\n    * **The card no longer judges the webhook verification key.** All it could check was whether the field was non-empty, and that was wrong in both directions on real stores: one with an account API key pasted into the signing key field read \"Ready\" while every event it received was being refused, and one a single press away from having Set up fetch the key for it read \"Needs a key\" over a red line saying every event would be refused. Neither was told anything true. The card still says whether a provider signs and still names the key, for a merchant doing it by hand \u2014 but whether events verify is now answered by whether events have arrived, which is the card watching what happens rather than guessing from a filled-in box\n    * **A failed send reads as the provider's own sentence, without a class name bolted on the front.** A refusal already explains itself \u2014 \"all recipient addresses must share the same domain as the 'From' address\" is Postmark telling a merchant exactly what to do next \u2014 and `RuntimeException:` in front of it is noise on a screen a merchant is reading because something went wrong. A PHP `Error` is the opposite case, a bug in this code rather than an answer from out there, and keeps its class name, because that is half of what makes it findable in a support thread\n    * **A verification key a provider offers but does not require is no longer reported as missing.** Whether a provider signs its webhooks was inferred from whether it asks for a verification key, and for Postmark that is wrong in the way that matters: it signs nothing, its own guidance is that the secret in the webhook address is protection enough, and the Basic auth pair it offers is checked when set and skipped when not. So a store that had taken Postmark's advice and left the pair empty got a card reading **Needs a key** over a red line saying every event would be refused \u2014 while the provider's own `verify()` was answering `unsigned` and accepting every one of them. Postmark now says outright that it does not sign, through the flag that already existed for the opposite case, and a card only counts a key as missing where the provider actually signs\n    * **An event is logged at the moment it arrived, not at whatever time the provider claims it happened.** `events.received_at` was being written from the provider's own timestamp, and every query on that column is about arrival: the retention sweep, the \"this provider has gone quiet\" alert, and the thirty-day window the three-strikes soft-bounce rule counts in. So a provider with a stale clock was not a cosmetic problem \u2014 its bounces landed outside the window that suppresses an address, and its events were swept early. Found by pressing SMTP2GO's own **Test this webhook** button, which posts a canned payload dated July 2019: all six events were accepted correctly and the Providers card then read \"last event received 7 years ago\". The send row still carries the provider's time in `delivered_at`, `opened_at` and `clicked_at`, because when a message was delivered is a fact about the message, and the provider's own timestamp is kept in the stored payload either way\n    * **Delivery reports are matched by the id the provider itself gave the message, so they stop being filed against nobody.** There were two ways to join an event back to a send and neither holds for most providers. The send header needs the provider to echo a custom header, and of the seven this store reads events from only SMTP2GO does. `Message-ID` needs the provider to keep the one the message left with \u2014 and a provider running on Amazon SES, which is what Resend is, mints its own on the way out and reports that one instead. So a campaign sent as `nl-4-1-73da0c85@example.com` came back as `010001a0\u2026@email.amazonses.com`, matched nothing, and every delivery, bounce and complaint was written to the log and attached to no send row: the campaign read \"sent 1, delivered 0\" while the provider's own dashboard said it had been delivered, and no screen anywhere could say otherwise. Found by sending one real campaign through a real account, because it looks correct at every step until the event arrives. There was a third id all along and nothing kept it \u2014 every API transport hands back the provider's own handle for the message when it accepts it, and the parsers already read the same string off the webhooks into `Event::providerId`, where it was kept for the event log and never matched on. Migration `newsletters_0011` adds `sends.provider_message_id` and its index, {@see OneSend} stamps it after the provider answers, and it is now the first thing a webhook is matched by, ahead of `Message-ID` and the send header. Needs the Email plugin at 5.2.0, which is where that id is first answered; an older one sends exactly as before and stores nothing. This is not a Resend fix \u2014 five of the seven providers never echo the send header, so all five were relying on a join that only ever worked where the provider happened to leave our `Message-ID` alone\n    * **A campaign that has not been sent says who it is going to, rather than nothing four times over.** A draft has no send rows and no total, and every place that number appeared printed a zero \u2014 \"Sent to 0\", \"0 total\", a Recipients panel reading \"Nobody yet\", and a 0 in the campaigns table. All four are literally true and all four read as \"this is going to nobody\", on the same screen as the Send button. The detail route now hands the screen the chosen list's member count, which five other routes already asked for and this one did not, so a draft says **Going to Newsletter \u2014 1 person on this list will receive it when you send**, and the card underneath the heading says what is waiting to go instead of a total that does not exist yet. In the campaigns table an unsent campaign shows a dash rather than a zero, because that column is the number that went out and there is not one\n    * **The deliverability advice stops naming SMTP2GO at people who do not use it.** Four strings every provider shares \u2014 the SPF and DKIM fixes, and the help under the DKIM selector and Custom Return Path boxes \u2014 ended with a sentence about what SMTP2GO does, left over from when SMTP2GO was the only provider this add-on could read events from. A store on Resend reading \"or on SMTP2GO just fill in the DKIM selector\" under a failing SPF check has been handed a step it cannot take and a reason to doubt the rest of the sentence. They say what to do in terms of the provider you actually send through; the behaviour they were describing is real and belongs in that provider's own instructions, which is where the card now points\n    * **The provider card catches up on its own after Set up.** Setting a webhook up also writes the provider's signing secret into its transport plugin's settings, and the state chip is read from the keys that are still missing \u2014 which the setup route does not answer and cannot, because that is the transport plugin's config. So the card patched the four fields it did get and went on showing \"Needs a key\" over a webhook that had just been created, until a reload showed it green and working all along. It refetches now, and clears the busy flag before doing so rather than after \u2014 clearing it after left every button on the card drawn disabled, because the refetch renders and the flag was still set when it did, so a merchant whose webhook had just been registered correctly could not press anything until they reloaded. The button on a card that is already registered reads **Set up again in** rather than **Set up in**, because the old wording over a finished webhook is how somebody ends up pressing it a second time to find out whether the first press worked. **And the cards stop opening with a page of somebody else's console.** Two things were printed flat: what a provider does with headers and which tag its events echo, and the step-by-step for registering the webhook by hand. The directions were behind a disclosure only where Set up had already been pressed \u2014 so the cards that had them open were exactly the cards nobody had touched yet, which on a fresh install is all seven, and the Providers tab opened as a wall of paragraphs about seven different dashboards. Both are behind disclosures on every card in every state now, worded for what that card can do: **How to set this up** where the store cannot register the webhook itself, **Do it by hand instead** where it can. What is left above the fold is the same six things for every provider \u2014 name, state, webhook address, buttons, whether events are signed, and whether any have arrived \u2014 with the disclosures on the floor of the card so a row of them lines up. Signed reads Yes or No rather than a sentence, with the sentence kept on hover\n    * **A store sending from a subdomain is no longer told it has no DMARC record.** DMARC's fallback is that a subdomain with no record of its own is covered by the one on the domain above it, and the check only ever looked at the exact name \u2014 so a store sending as `send.example.com` with its record on `example.com` was told it had no policy at all, and the obvious fix for that, a record at the subdomain, would have overridden the policy it was already inheriting. It now reads the organisational domain when the sending domain has none of its own, applies `sp=` where the parent has one and `p=` where it does not, and says which domain the policy came from. Deliberately not a walk up the tree: a receiver reads the organisational domain and nothing in between, so a policy on `b.example.com` is never reported as covering `a.b.example.com`. Found on a real Resend setup, whose own default is to verify a subdomain\n    * **A deadlock between two workers no longer reads as \"that row is already there\".** Signing up, tagging somebody, suppressing an address, enrolling somebody in an automation and queueing an automation's email all insert a row they expect might already exist, and every one of them treated any database error on that insert as the row already being there. On MySQL and PostgreSQL two workers meeting on the same person deadlock, which is the database asking to be tried again, and it was read instead as \"already done\": a signup dropped, a tag left off, somebody quietly left out of a flow, or a welcome email skipped for good with the enrolment walked past it. They now take that path only for a genuine duplicate, as the connection judges it, and hand everything else back to the transaction that knows how to retry it.\n    * The signup endpoint answers through core's finish helper instead of a bare exit, so Grav's shutdown runs after the answer and the confirmation email goes out the moment the signup is answered rather than on the next worker tick\n    * **\"SMTP2GO has said nothing in 30 days\" no longer counts mail sent before the webhook existed.** Generate a secret records when the address was minted (`providers.<name>.secret_at`), and the alert looks only at campaigns sent after that moment or after Set up registered the webhook, whichever is later. A store that mailed last week and set its webhook up this morning is not a provider that has gone quiet\n    * **Generate a secret and Set up in SMTP2GO answered \"unexpected error\".** Both handlers took the provider's name as a second argument, and the API router hands a handler the request alone, so every press was a fatal error before anything ran. The name is read off the route now, and the route table test checks that no handler needs more than the request\n    * **The dashboard alerts' buttons go where they say.** \"Open the Providers block\", \"Open the campaign\" and the deliverability alert's button were written as `?view=` routes with a fragment, which the admin shell never hands to a section, so they landed on the newsletter's front page. They are section paths now, and the Settings screen opened at `settings\/providers` scrolls to the Providers block with the secret and the Set up button\n    * **A tab left on an automation that has since been deleted goes back to the list.** Reloading the editor or detail screen of a flow that no longer exists (deleted, or wiped by a store reset) drew an empty \"Write an automation\" form with a red \"Flow not found\" banner over it, which read as a broken new-automation screen. The section now returns to the Automations list and says the automation is gone; any other failure still stays on the screen it happened on\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 segments-admin \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The send confirmation no longer asks \"Send this to NaN people?\".** The audience count was formatted twice \u2014 once at the call site and once inside the plural helper \u2014 and the second pass read \"38,214\" back as a number, which it is not. Every store with more than nine hundred and ninety-nine people on a list saw it, at the one moment in the whole add-on where the number matters most. The count is a number until the last step now, and a campaign whose audience genuinely cannot be counted yet asks the question without a number in it rather than with one that is not one\n    * **The admin section's stylesheet is no longer cut in half by a comment.** A backtick inside a CSS comment in the section's own style block closed the template literal it lives in and turned the rest of the file's styles into a tagged template, so the section threw as it loaded. It parses cleanly, which is why `node --check` had nothing to say about it; a headless render of the screens is what found it\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end segments-admin \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 audience \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A subscriber's screen said \"Not linked to a customer\" for every subscriber, including the linked ones.** The lookup behind the \"Customer record\" line selected `first_name` and `last_name` from `kahunacart_customers`, which are columns that table has never had \u2014 the base plugin keeps one `name`. On a real store the statement threw, the surrounding catch turned it into null, and the screen reported the absence of a link that was there in the row. The catch is the reason nobody saw it, so there is now a test that runs the query against the base plugin's real migrated schema and fails if it names a column that is not there\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end audience \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * The from address is no longer wrapped twice when the Email plugin's `from` field already holds a display name, such as `KahunaCart <notifications@example.com>`. The sender now takes the address out of that form and adds the name once, so a test send no longer fails with Symfony's \"does not comply with addr-spec of RFC 2822\" error. The Email plugin's field still wants the bare address with the name in `from_name`, since core's own order emails compose it the same way.\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 settings-home \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A generated secret went into the wrong file, and the settings form showed an empty field.** The Generate button resolved the plugin's yaml through Grav's `config:\/\/` stream, which answers whichever environment Grav booted under \u2014 so on a site with a `user\/env\/<host>\/` directory the secret landed in that host's overlay while the settings form read and wrote the base file. Which file a config write lands in is the admin's environment picker's answer, not this plugin's, so the button now resolves it exactly as the API plugin's config save does: the picker on **Default** writes `user\/config\/plugins\/kahunacart-newsletters.yaml`, and only a named environment that exists on disk sends the write into `user\/env\/<name>\/config\/`\n    * **The first press of Generate drew a card with no URL on it.** The write was fine; the read that followed it came out of Grav's compiled config cache, which the write had not cleared \u2014 so the card redrew from a configuration with no secret in it and the second press \"worked\". The write clears that cache the same way a settings save does, and the card is redrawn from what the button answered rather than from a second read at all\n    * **A From address with a display name in it was reported as not being an email address.** `KahunaCart <notifications@kahunacart.com>` is what the Grav Email plugin accepts and what Symfony parses, and the deliverability check called it invalid while the store's mail was going out perfectly well \u2014 and the SPF, DKIM and DMARC checks beside it were looking up `kahunacart.com>`. There is one parser now, in `Support\\Address`, and the check, the checks that take the domain, the Settings screen's From line and the sender in `MailSender` all read the address through it\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end settings-home \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 polish \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A campaign saved for the first time no longer forgets which list it is going to.** Straight after creating one the \"Send it to\" select came back reading \"Choose a list\u2026\" although the list had been saved, and a reload showed it correctly. The save was discarding the campaigns index the editor draws its list and template pickers from, so the select was redrawn with no options in it and the value had nothing to select. The form is now rebuilt from what the server sent back and the index is left alone; the same fix applies to the template editor, which was losing its merge-tag list the same way\n    * **The \"Joined\" column in the subscribers table is no longer clipped to \"Sep 4, 20\".** The cells were already `nowrap`, but the email and lists columns take everything auto table layout will give them, so the date column was squeezed under its own content and the overflow was cut with no ellipsis to say so. It now asks for exactly the width its content needs and no less\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end polish \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **Somebody who unsubscribed while a campaign was waiting no longer gets it anyway.** The audience is written when a campaign starts, and a message could sit in the queue for hours after that. Every send now checks the subscriber, the suppression list and their membership of the campaign's list in the moment before the message is handed to the mailer, and skips it with the reason where consent has changed. A skip is not a delivery failure: it does not count against the campaign or trip the circuit breaker.\n    * **A paused automation no longer holds up an active one.** Paused and draft automations were picked up with everybody else when the worker asked who was due, so two hundred people held in a paused flow filled the batch and the flow that was running sent nothing. Only active automations are due now, and pausing still holds everybody exactly where they are.\n    * **On PostgreSQL, rebuilding a campaign's audience stopped at the first person already in it.** One duplicate recipient aborted the transaction, so everybody after that person was dropped and a resumed or rebuilt audience quietly went out short. Recipients are written in batches with the people already there taken out first, and a row that arrives from somewhere else mid-write is tolerated rather than fatal.\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 unique-codes \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **Saving an automation in the editor stripped the coupon group off its email steps.** A step that minted a code per person carries `coupon_group` in its config, and the editor had no field for it \u2014 so `stepPayload()` built an empty config, the API writes a step's config whole rather than merging it, and the group was gone. The failure was quiet and it landed on the one workflow anybody would actually use: create a flow from the win-back recipe, open it to reword the email in your own words, press Save, and every message after that went out with an empty `{{ coupon.code }}` in it. Nothing warned, and the editor still looked right because it had never drawn the field. The box is there now \u2014 the same **Give everyone a code of their own** control a campaign uses \u2014 and what is in it survives a save\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end unique-codes \u2500\u2500 -->"}}},"kahunacart-shipping":{"name":"KahunaCart Shipping","version":"1.1.6","description":"Live carrier rates, boxes and packing, labels and tracking for KahunaCart","description_html":"<p>Live carrier rates, boxes and packing, labels and tracking for KahunaCart<\/p>","description_plain":"Live carrier rates, boxes and packing, labels and tracking for KahunaCart","icon":"truck","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.3.8"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/shipping","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/shipping\/overview","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, shipping, carriers, rates, labels, tracking, shippo, easypost, shipengine","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-shipping","license":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/premium\/license","tag_name":"1.1.6","date":"2026-10-02T01:05:00Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-shipping\/1.1.6","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/shipping","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-shipping","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-shipping","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-shipping"},"slug":"kahunacart-shipping","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-shipping","content_sha256":"d2fafd46eaa571fd55e35cf4c4f9489188c562067e1838ab1590c0225eaeb214","changelog":{"1.1.6":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Help text on the Shipping carriers screens now follows the admin's Help text setting, showing below the label or in a tooltip behind an info icon\n"},"1.1.5":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The download no longer carries the plugin's tests.** `tests\/` and `phpunit.xml` stay in the repository and out of the release zip, 211 files fewer on every install. Nothing the plugin runs is affected\n"},"1.1.4":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The tables on the Shipping screens sort by their column headings.** Press a heading on the Labels, Boxes or ship-from tables to sort by it, and press it again to turn the order round. The Labels list sorts on the server, so a heading orders every label and not only the page on screen, and pressing one goes back to page one. `GET \/kahunacart\/shipping\/labels` takes `sort` (`order`, `carrier`, `service`, `tracking`, `paid`, `status` or `bought`) and `dir`, a label with nothing in that column sorts last whichever way it runs, and any other `sort` is ignored, so the list is newest first as before. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later, which draws the headings\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/25\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Installing through GPM no longer switches the add-on off.** The plugin loads its classes through Composer's `vendor\/autoload.php`, but `vendor\/` was left out of the repository and so out of the download GPM installs. Grav logged \"Plugin 'kahunacart-shipping' autoload failed: Failed opening required ...\/vendor\/autoload.php\" and turned the plugin off on every store that installed or updated it that way. The run-time half of `vendor\/` is part of the download now, and a test fails if it goes missing or if a development install is committed over it\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Ready for KahunaCart 1.3, which bundles brick\/money under its own namespace.** A carrier's price is turned into cents by asking core's `Money::exponent()` for the currency's decimal places, instead of calling brick\/money's `Currency` class directly and falling back to a table of its own. From KahunaCart 1.3 the unprefixed `Brick\\Money\\Currency` does not exist, so the old code would quietly have used the table for every store. `Money::exponent()` has been in core since 1.0.0, so nothing changes on the KahunaCart this add-on already requires, and the plugin's own currency table is gone\n    * **The test suite runs against KahunaCart 1.3.** When it borrows the base plugin's dependencies it now also loads core's `vendor-prefixed\/autoload.php`, where brick\/money and YetiSearch live from 1.3. Against an older KahunaCart without that file nothing changes\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **Auto-buy waits until the order is paid.** It was built to buy the label the moment an order was placed, paid or not (it never actually did, see below), so a bank-transfer order sitting at pending, a card that was only authorised, or an order taken with a deposit at **Part paid** would all have got a label before the money was in. It now buys only once the order counts as paid: straight away for a card payment, and otherwise when the rest of the money arrives, whichever way it arrives \u2014 a pay link, a webhook, a capture, or the merchant marking it paid. An order paid at checkout gets one label, a payment delivered twice still buys one label, and a cancelled or refunded order voids a label bought later the same way it voids one bought at checkout. Buying a label by hand from the order screen works at any time\n2. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Auto-buy never actually bought anything.** The label planner hands the chosen service back beside the purchase options rather than inside them, and auto-buy passed the options on without it, so every automatic purchase was refused as incomplete before the carrier was asked and only a log line said so. The Buy label sheet and the demo seeder were never affected. There is now a test that runs auto-buy through core's real hook runner and the demo carrier and checks a label comes out the other end\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/16\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **The tracking webhook is two buttons now, on the Shipping carriers screen.** All three drivers could register their own webhook from the first release and nothing exposed any of it, so the only way to set inbound tracking up was four steps done by hand \u2014 one of which, on EasyPost, was running a `php -r` line to work out the value EasyPost has to sign with. There is a card under the driver's capability matrix now: **Generate secret** makes the random part of the address and saves it encrypted, **Register webhook** hands the address to the aggregator, and the card shows the address with the secret masked to its last four characters, a copy button carrying the real one, the last result and the last few deliveries. Registering adopts an existing webhook pointing at this store rather than adding a second, reports any older address still registered, and on EasyPost sets the derived signing key itself. A store nothing outside the building can reach \u2014 a `.test` hostname, a private address, plain `http` \u2014 is told so where the button is, rather than after registering something no carrier could ever deliver to. `bin\/plugin kahunacart-shipping webhook:secret` and `webhook:register [--reset]` do the same two things for a store whose setup is scripted, and there are three MCP tools beside them\n    * **A generated secret is encrypted the moment it is written, not the next time somebody presses Save.** It goes through core's own settings path rather than a second one of this plugin's, so the whole file is swept as it is written: the webhook secret the button generates and the API key typed into the tab beside it both end up encrypted at rest, and an already-encrypted value is left exactly as it is\n    * **EasyPost, as a second driver in the same plugin.** Pick it on the General tab, paste your keys into the EasyPost tab, and everything below the driver is exactly what it was: the same packer, the same boxes, the same labels table, the same tracking, the same screens. One account, one key, and rates from USPS, UPS, FedEx, DHL and the hundred-odd others behind it. It implements every interface the Shippo driver does \u2014 rates, carrier accounts, labels, voids, address validation, tracking, the inbound webhook and the connection check \u2014 and one thing Shippo cannot do at all. See docs\/carriers.md\n    * **EasyPost signs its webhook deliveries, and this checks every one of them.** `signed_webhooks` was in the capability matrix from the first release with Shippo answering **no**, because Shippo's only self-serve authentication on an inbound delivery is the secret in the address and a published list of source addresses \u2014 HMAC exists there but needs an account manager and ten business days. EasyPost signs by default: an `X-Hmac-Signature` header carrying an HMAC-SHA256 over the raw body, keyed on a secret set on the webhook at creation. Registration hands EasyPost that secret, and every delivery is checked before a byte of it is read \u2014 a signature that does not match is a **401** and a refused row in the delivery log, and so is a delivery with no signature at all. The card now prints a yes on that row for the first time, which is the whole reason the row is in the matrix\n    * **The signing key is derived from your webhook secret rather than being it.** That secret is also the token in the inbound address, and a URL is the part of a request that ends up in an access log, a referrer and a bug report somebody pastes into a ticket. So the key EasyPost actually signs with is worked out from the secret, deterministically, by both halves \u2014 the registration that hands it over and the check that runs on every delivery. One string for a merchant to manage and rotate, two different strings on the wire, and an address that leaks costs nothing but the address. Changing the secret means pressing **Register webhook** again, which is said in the field's own help text \u2014 and a store registering by hand instead has the one line that prints the derived value in docs\/tracking.md\n    * **Inches and ounces, converted once, at the driver's own boundary and nowhere else.** The store keeps millimetres and grams, because that is what a variant records and what Shippo takes directly. EasyPost is hardwired to inches and ounces to one decimal point. That conversion was the real test of whether the aggregator contract had been built around one API, and it passes: it is two functions inside the EasyPost driver, called by the parcel payload and the customs line and by nothing else in the plugin. They round half-up, and a measurement that is really there but rounds to nothing comes back as a tenth rather than a zero \u2014 a zero dimension is refused by every carrier, and \"no dimension\" is not what the merchant typed. There is a test for the edges\n    * **The service codes are the same on both sides, so changing aggregator does not mean rebinding a shipping method.** Shippo hands over `usps_priority` as one token; EasyPost hands over `USPS` and `GroundAdvantage` as two, and the driver joins them into `usps_ground_advantage` by breaking the token at its word boundaries. `usps_priority`, `ups_ground`, `ups_next_day_air`, `fedex_ground` \u2014 identical either way, with a test pinning each spelling. Paste the new keys, change one setting, press **Check connection** and **Refresh carriers**, and every method that was bound still resolves\n    * **EasyPost will only list your carrier accounts to a production key, and the add-on says so instead of failing quietly.** Its carrier account endpoints are production-only and refuse a test key outright, which is the one thing about setting EasyPost up that catches people. So **Refresh carriers** is asked with the production key whichever mode the store is in \u2014 which is also the right answer, because your carrier accounts are your carrier accounts and the same ids rate in test mode \u2014 and nothing else touches it: quoting, buying, refunding, tracking and address checks all use the mode's own key, and nothing spends money until Mode is Live. A test-mode store that has not pasted a production key yet gets a connection check that **passes** and tells it exactly that, rather than a red card about an endpoint it was never going to need until step three\n    * **A rate reference that carries two ids, and nothing above the driver knows.** EasyPost buys a label with a rate id *and* the shipment it belongs to, where Shippo needs only the rate. The contract has one opaque string for this, so the EasyPost driver's is `<shipment id>:<rate id>` and the composition stays inside the driver. A reference with no shipment on it \u2014 one recorded by an older version, or a rate that has gone stale \u2014 falls through to re-quoting the shipment and picking the service the merchant chose, which is exactly what the label request has always carried a service code as well as a rate reference for\n    * **One contract, three drivers, and a shared test that proves it.** `AggregatorContractTestCase` is the rules every driver keeps \u2014 a code the registry does not rewrite, a capability matrix that answers every case as a boolean, **every declared capability having the interface actually behind it**, a webhook parser that makes no outbound call, a signature verdict that matches what the driver declared, amounts in whole minor units, a rate's metadata carrying only sentences and the names core reserves, a connection check that never throws. Each driver extends it with five hooks. A second driver is the only thing that can prove an interface was written around a contract rather than around whichever API happened to be first, and a third is what proves the second was not a coincidence \u2014 so this is that proof rather than an argument for it\n    * **Three places where the first driver's name had been baked in, now reading under whichever driver is configured.** The API key was read from `shippo.test_token` whatever the aggregator was, the inbound webhook's secret from `shippo.webhook_secret`, and its optional address allowlist from `shippo.webhook_ips`. All three now read under the configured driver's own key, which is the rule the settings were always meant to follow \u2014 **a driver's settings live under its own key** \u2014 and a store that has pasted keys for both aggregators keeps both. The Carriers screen's \"paste a carrier key to begin\" now names the aggregator you actually chose, and a connection check on a store with nothing pasted answers in that driver's own vocabulary: EasyPost has an API *key* beginning `EZTK`, and telling an EasyPost merchant to look for a token beginning `shippo_test_` would send them hunting for a field that is not there\n    * **Every driver's secrets are declared for encryption, not just the configured one's.** A merchant who has pasted keys for both aggregators has both in `user\/config\/plugins\/kahunacart-shipping.yaml`, and a list that shrank to the driver of the day would leave the other one in plaintext the next time anything wrote that file\n    * **Carrier codes for EasyPost's spellings.** Shippo separates a carrier's words with underscores and EasyPost runs them together, because its tokens come from a class name: `DhlExpressAccount` becomes `dhlexpress`, and no amount of substituting hyphens gets that to core's `dhl-express`. The run-together spellings are now in the carrier map beside the underscored ones, so a parcel sent by DHL Express, Canada Post, Australia Post, Royal Mail or An Post through either aggregator gets a customer-facing tracking link to the right carrier's own website rather than falling through to **Other**\n    * **Written against EasyPost's published API with recorded fixtures, and honest about what that does and does not prove.** There was no EasyPost key on the machine that built this, so every branch is proven against a fixture written from EasyPost's own documented examples and none of it against the API itself. That is said out loud in the docs rather than glossed, and docs\/testing.md now carries a fifteen-step real-key campaign for the person who has a key \u2014 including the two steps a fixture can never stand in for: reading back the parcel EasyPost actually received to prove the unit conversion in the direction that matters, and `curl`ing the webhook endpoint with a wrong signature and with none to prove the 401 is real\n    * **ShipEngine joins Shippo and EasyPost in the same plugin.** Sandbox carrier accounts and services, live rates, PDF\/PNG\/ZPL labels, recovery after a lost purchase response, synchronous voids, tracking, address validation and customs, with mode checks before any API call. Webhooks require a derived shared-secret header and are honestly listed as unsigned by this driver.\n    * **ShipEngine setup and test coverage.** Three encrypted settings, an eight-step Instructions section, shared service codes, fixtures based on the published v1 API and a documented sandbox campaign. Sandbox tracking and genuine webhook delivery remain separate from fixture validation.\n    * **A bought label is drawn as a label.** The parcels panel beside an order used to print four lines of text with a small grey button reading Open the label under them, which was the quietest thing on a screen whose whole point is that file. It is now a card with the carrier's name on a colour band across the top and, under it, the service in big type, who the parcel is going to, the tracking number over a barcode rule with a copy button beside it that puts the number on the clipboard and opens nothing, and chips for the price and the format \u2014 and the whole card is the button that opens the file. The card, the line saying what you paid and the three verbs all start on the same left edge, and the ring around a focused card belongs to the keyboard \u2014 Tab to the card and you get one, click it and you do not. Reprint, Track and Void stay underneath as small text actions doing exactly what they did before, through exactly the same routes. A refunded label is stamped Voided and goes grey, a refused one gets a dashed card and the carrier's own words, a delivered one a tick. Carrier names are set in type and there are no carrier logos anywhere: a colour band with the name on it reads as USPS from across the room without borrowing USPS's trademark, and the demo carriers get invented colours so a demo store screenshots like a real one\n    * **And it shows the label itself beside those details where one can be had for nothing.** The picture sits at 4x6 proportions down the left of the card with the service, the address, the number and the chips next to it, so a merchant gets both and never one instead of the other; on a panel too narrow for two columns it moves above them. EasyPost renders every label as a PNG whether or not anybody asked, so a store printing 4x6 PDFs gets the picture as well for one download and no extra postage; the demo carrier draws its own with GD, banner and all. Shippo prints one file per purchase and has no way to re-render it, so its cards carry the details alone rather than buying a second label for a thumbnail. The picture is served by the same authenticated route as the label \u2014 it is the same document with the same customer's address on it \u2014 and labels bought before this have none, which is exactly the case the card was built to stand on its own for. See docs\/labels.md\n    * **The demo carriers now read like couriers a real shop would use.** Driftwood Post and Lagoon Express are **Kahuna Post** \u2014 Kahuna Ground Basic and Kahuna Air Mail \u2014 and **Kahuna Express** \u2014 Kahuna Express Saver, Kahuna Priority Overnight and Kahuna Express Worldwide. Still two carriers, because one cheap and slow against one dear and fast is the whole point of them, and still invented: they carry the store's own name rather than a real courier's, and the label they print still says NOT VALID FOR POSTAGE across the top. Every price, band, refusal, limit and tracking timeline is exactly what it was \u2014 only the names and the codes moved. A store seeded before this keeps its old parcels, because the driver answers to the five old service codes for this one release: an existing label still names its service and its carrier instead of reading Other, and a label bought against an old code comes back stamped with the new one. The one thing an alias cannot fix is a shipping method still bound to an old code \u2014 core matches a method to a rate by that code and no rate carries an old one now \u2014 so repoint those methods on the zone, or run the seeder's purge and seed again, which rewrites them. See docs\/demo-carrier.md\n    * **The demo store has two months of parcels behind it.** The seeder used to write eleven orders, all of them placed in the same second the demo reset ran, which read like a shop that had opened an hour ago however good the parcels on it looked. It now writes thirty-three more spread over the past sixty days: most of them delivered with a full history, five still in transit or out for delivery right now, one missed at the door and delivered on the second attempt, one gone back to the sender, two abroad with a customs declaration, two paid and waiting for somebody to buy a label, one refused at the counter, and a couple posted minutes ago so the compressed clock still has something moving while a visitor watches. A handful belong to the demo customer whose storefront password the demo prints, so signing in shows the customer's half of the add-on rather than an empty account. Every order, its parcels and its whole timeline are dated to the day it happened. See docs\/demo-carrier.md\n    * **A parcel bought before today took the days its service quotes, rather than half an hour.** The demo's clock compresses a whole journey into `demo_carrier.transit_minutes` so that something visibly moves on the screen, which is right for a parcel bought this morning and absurd for one bought six weeks ago. A parcel younger than a day still runs compressed; an older one ran at carrier speed \u2014 one day for Kahuna Priority Overnight, three for the Express Saver, five for Ground Basic, ten or fourteen for Air Mail, the same numbers on the rate sheet. Nothing is stored: the clock is still a pure function of when the label was bought, so a history written by the seeder and a history recomputed by a poll an hour later agree to the second\n    * **A parcel that is missed at the door and delivered on the second attempt.** The demo had one parcel that went wrong and it went wrong permanently \u2014 postcode `00000`, failed, returned to sender. `00001` is now the commonest thing that goes slightly wrong instead: out for delivery, \"Nobody home \u2014 a card was left\", out again the next day, signed for. One postcode per journey, which is the rule the address validator already used\n    * **A label the base plugin's own seeder left without one now gets a parcel.** `bin\/plugin kahunacart seed --account` writes a fulfilled order with a jumper on it and no label behind it, because core knows nothing about parcels \u2014 and on a store running this add-on that reads as a shop that posts things without printing labels. Found by what the order is rather than by a number, so whatever that seeder writes next is covered too, and **only for orders carrying core's own seed stamp**: a merchant's fulfilled March is never written onto. `--purge` takes those labels back off with their shipments and their history lines\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **The admin section is called Shipping carriers now, not Carriers.** The add-on is called Shipping everywhere else, so the sidebar entry under Add-ons and the heading on the screen say so too. The word *carriers* stays on the end because core already owns *Shipping* and *Shipping profiles* in the Setup band, and a third plain *Shipping* would be the worst thing on the screen. Nothing else moved: the route is still `#\/section\/carriers`, the permissions are the same, and the tab inside the screen is still **Carriers**, because that one really is the list of carrier accounts\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Every label the seeder bought was stored with no file behind it, so Open the label opened nothing.** A demo label is a PDF and a PDF is a Twig template: core's renderer asks Twig for one, Twig asks the resource locator for `theme:\/\/`, and nothing registers that scheme on the command line until the theme has been initialised. It threw, the driver caught it the way it catches any renderer failing, and every seeded label came out bought and correct with an empty file path. A seeded demo now prints its labels\n    * **`--purge` left rows and files behind.** The shipment written for each seeded order stayed in the database pointing at an order id that no longer existed, the order's history lines stayed with it, and the PNG thumbnail beside each label stayed on disk under a name of its own. All three go now, so a purge really does leave the store it found\n    * **The seeder queued two dozen emails to invented customers.** Writing the back-dated parcels puts every one of them through the same tracking service a webhook and a poll write through, and reaching *delivered* is what queues the customer's delivered message \u2014 so a seed left twenty-odd of them sitting in the queue, where a demo box's jobs screen is meant to show the three it seeded on purpose, and where any store with a mailer wired up would have sent them. It also paged the merchant about parcels nobody posted. The seeder now writes through the same object with both of those taken off, so the history is still written by the code that writes a real parcel's and nothing goes out\n    * **The seeder fell over on a store that sells anything in pairs.** It wrote its order lines through the cart service, which enforces the store's own quantity rules, so a product with a minimum of two refused the single the seeder asked for and took the whole seed down with it under `set -e`. Quantities are now clamped the way the storefront clamps them before the line is added\n    * **ShipEngine sandbox findings corrected.** Ordinary boxes exclude carrier flat-rate packaging; domestic purchases save their recovery ID on a fresh matching quote; sandbox purchases omit the unrecoverable sample flag. Recorded responses cover recovery and exact-format downloads, and supplied PNG links feed the new label cards without another purchase.\n    * **An international EasyPost label could not be bought at all.** A customs declaration goes onto an EasyPost shipment when the shipment is created, and a shipment cannot be changed afterwards \u2014 so the shipment behind a rate reference, which a rate call made and a rate call has no declaration to send, had `customs_info: null` on it for ever. Every international purchase was a buy against that, and EasyPost refused every one with \"Customs info error: missing required field: ContentsType\". An international purchase now re-creates the shipment with its declaration, its label format and its paper size, and buys the rate the merchant chose off the fresh quote. Found on the first run against a real key; a domestic purchase is untouched and still buys the rate it was quoted\n    * **Every EasyPost label came back as a PNG, whatever format you asked for, and was written to disk under the wrong extension.** The 4x6 PDF setting was sent on the shipment the *label* code builds, and the shipment actually bought is the one the *rate* call built \u2014 which names no paper size, so EasyPost defaulted to PNG, `label_pdf_url` came back null, and the driver fell back to the PNG and saved it as `label-47.pdf`. A file that says PDF and is a PNG opens in nothing. The driver now asks EasyPost to render the format that was asked for whenever the bought shipment does not already carry it, which is EasyPost's own answer to this and buys no extra postage. ZPL works the same way, and a shipment that already has the right format costs no extra call\n    * **A purchase the carrier refused could never be tried again.** The key that stops a double-click buying two labels is built from the order, the service and what is in the boxes \u2014 and a refusal is almost never about any of those. An address USPS would not verify, a ship-from with a typo in it, an account with no balance: all fixed somewhere the key cannot see, so the second press produced the same key, the unique index refused it, and the merchant read **\"Already bought \u2014 a label already exists for this parcel\"** about a label that had never been bought. That parcel was locked out of that service permanently. A refused row is now taken back and tried again, with a conditional write so two merchants racing still produce one purchase; a pending, bought or voided row is still a duplicate, which is the whole double-click defence and has not moved\n    * **A test key could not quote anything.** EasyPost's carrier account endpoints are production-only, so a store in test mode with only a test key pasted can never have a carrier account row \u2014 and the bridge treated an empty account list as \"this carrier has nothing to ask\", so checkout quoted nothing at all. Meanwhile EasyPost's rate endpoint answers a test key perfectly well and needs no account filter. A driver can now say that an empty list proves nothing about it, and EasyPost says so exactly while no production key is pasted. Paste one and the ordinary rule applies again\n    * **Refresh carriers explained itself in EasyPost's words rather than in useful ones.** A test key answered `MODE.UNAUTHORIZED`, \"This resource requires a production API Key to access.\", which a merchant reads seconds after a connection check that passed. It now says which key to paste, that it is only ever read with and never bought with while the store is in test mode, and that the store quotes and prints test labels without it \u2014 with EasyPost's own sentence kept on the end. The Carriers screen was also dropping everything after the first full stop of any EasyPost refusal, because it only knew how to unwrap a Shippo one\n    * **Service codes did not match Shippo's for a carrier quoted through EasyPost's own shared accounts.** A rate bought through one of those names the shared account rather than the carrier \u2014 `FedExDefault`, not `FedEx` \u2014 which minted `fedexdefault_fedex_ground` where Shippo, and the same EasyPost account the day its own FedEx contract goes on, both mint `fedex_ground`. Three things broke at once: the codes stopped matching Shippo's, so a store moving between aggregators lost every method binding; a refusal could not be attributed to the carrier that made it; and the codes changed under a merchant who added a contract. The carrier token is now read the same way everywhere\n    * **The same address problem was printed twice.** EasyPost runs two checks over one address and both report it: a postcode that does not exist fails `zip4` and `delivery` with one \"Address not found\" each, and the merchant read it once beside the address and once under the street field. One fact, said once\n    * **A refused address offered a \"correction\" identical to what was typed.** A verification EasyPost cannot make answers with the submitted address copied back out, field for field, so the buy sheet put a button in front of a merchant that would change not one character. A correction now has to differ somewhere postal before it is offered as one \u2014 a name in capitals or a phone number with its punctuation taken out is not a correction\n    * **A tracking scan with no time on it landed at the first of January 1970.** EasyPost writes one of these for a parcel it knows nothing about yet, with an empty message and `datetime: null`, and there is nothing to invent a time from \u2014 the event's time is half of the index that makes a redelivered webhook a no-op. The scan is dropped and the tracker's own status reaches the merchant dated from its `updated_at` instead, which is the same fact told honestly\n    * **Every carrier account came back with no services, so a store with its production key pasted still had no service list.** EasyPost's carrier metadata filter is all or nothing: one name that endpoint does not know answers **422 over the whole request**, and `dhlecs` is one of those names. So all six carrier accounts on a real key came back with nothing on them, core read the catalogue as empty, the service box in the zone method editor stayed a free-text field, and the Carriers screen told the merchant to press **Refresh carriers** \u2014 which would fail in precisely the same way every time. A 422 now drops the filter and asks again for everything EasyPost publishes, keeping only the carriers this store holds. It is one extra call on a button pressed by hand, and only on the one status this can be: a refusal that is not about the request is left where it was, because asking twice would answer the same thing and cost another wait to hear it\n    * **DHL eCommerce is two names at EasyPost and only one of them can be asked for.** A carrier account says `DhlEcsAccount` and a rate bought through it says `DhlEcs`, so this store's token is `dhlecs` \u2014 and the published carrier metadata calls the same carrier `dhlecommercesolutions`. No amount of expanding or hyphenating gets from one to the other, so it is a map. The carriers are asked for under the name metadata knows them by and every answer is filed straight back under the token the account produced, because a service code minted from EasyPost's other spelling is one no rate from that account would ever match\n    * **A connection check with both keys pasted did not say which key had read what.** A test-mode store with a production key has two of them used in one check: the test key against the shipments endpoint, the production key against the carrier accounts, because that is the only key EasyPost will list those to. The summary said **\"Connected to EasyPost.\"** and left a merchant reading six carriers and a test-mode badge to work it out. It now names the mode in the sentence people actually read, says the count came from the production key, and says the thing anybody wants to hear about a production key on a test store: nothing is bought with it until **Mode** is Live\n\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"09\/15\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Live carrier rates, from a key you paste once.** KahunaCart 1.2.0 published a carrier rate contract and shipped no carrier, deliberately \u2014 a first-party driver in core would commit core to maintaining somebody else's API version. This is the carrier. Paste a [Shippo](https:\/\/goshippo.com) key, press **Check connection**, pull your carrier list, bind a shipping method to a real service, and a customer's postage comes from USPS, UPS, FedEx, DHL or any of forty-odd others behind that one account. Shippo is an aggregator, which is the whole point of doing it this way round: a merchant who wants UPS and USPS gets both from one integration. EasyPost is the second driver and goes in this same plugin. To core the whole aggregator is one carrier with the code `shippo` and its services are `<carrier>_<token>` tokens \u2014 `usps_priority`, `ups_ground` \u2014 so the customer's tracking link still points at USPS and not at Shippo. See docs\/getting-started.md\n    * **A box catalogue and a packer, so a rate is for the parcel the order actually goes in.** Core packs an order into one package per shipping profile and says, in its own docblock, that working out which box things fit in is a carrier's job with a carrier's data. This is where that data arrives. A box is a code, a name, its outside measurements in whole millimetres and a maximum weight in whole grams; the packer expands each line into units, sorts them largest first, and puts each one into the first open parcel that will take it or into the smallest box it fits in \u2014 first-fit-decreasing, which is the standard answer and within a few per cent of optimal for the parcel counts a shop actually has. Nothing goes into a box that would take it over its maximum weight, the empty box counts towards its own limit because it goes on the scale too, and an item that fits no box at all travels on its own at its own dimensions rather than being refused, because \"this goes on its own\" is right far more often than \"this cannot be sent\". Each parcel's billable weight is the greater of its actual weight and its dimensional weight \u2014 length times width times height in centimetres over a divisor, 5000 by default, with per-carrier overrides beside it, rounded up the way every carrier rounds it. See docs\/boxes-and-packing.md\n    * **Packing is deterministic, and there is a test that proves it.** The same order packs the same way every single time: every sort breaks its ties on facts about the order \u2014 the line's id, the unit's place in its line, the box's code \u2014 and never on anything about the request. The suite packs one order a hundred times, reshuffling the lines each time, and compares the bytes. A merchant who sees two parcels on one page load and three on the next will never trust the number again, and nor should they\n    * **A store with no boxes still quotes.** One built-in box, every parcel flagged as an estimate, and a plain sentence saying the prices are approximate until real boxes are filled in. Refusing to price an order because nobody had typed a box in would be a worse answer than an approximate one. A line whose variant has no recorded size joins the pile of unknowns, lands in a box anyway, and the parcel says which lines it had to guess about \u2014 so the fix is visible rather than mysterious\n    * **Ship-from addresses.** A carrier will not quote, and certainly will not print a label, without knowing where the parcel starts, and core's only store address is `invoice.store_address`, a free-text block meant for the bottom of an invoice that no carrier API will take. The add-on keeps its own, structured, with the same column names `kahunacart_pickup_locations` uses so the two read alike. The first one added becomes the default, because a store with one address and no default cannot be quoted for and nothing on screen would say why\n    * **A connection check that presses the real key against the real account.** Not a non-empty-string test: a real call, reporting which account it reached and how many carrier accounts that account holds, which is the fact that actually predicts whether rates will appear. A key that works against an account with no carrier switched on is reported as a failure rather than as a pass with a warning nobody reads, because a store in that state quotes nothing. Two refusals happen before anything is sent \u2014 an empty token, and a token of the wrong kind. A live key pasted into the test field is caught by its prefix, which is a mistake somebody makes once and pays for when a test order buys a real label\n    * **An aggregator driver contract, with five optional interfaces declared beside it.** `AggregatorInterface` is the required half \u2014 the code, the name, the capabilities, the carrier list and the rates. `LabelPurchaseInterface`, `LabelVoidInterface`, `TrackingInterface`, `AddressValidationInterface` and `AggregatorWebhookSetupInterface` sit beside it, each small and each optional, and all five are implemented for Shippo. Every one of them is answered honestly in `capabilities()`, because a capability flag that lies turns a missing feature into a broken one \u2014 the admin card's matrix is built from what a driver says it can do, in a fixed order, rather than from a list somebody keeps in a template. `signed_webhooks` is in that matrix and Shippo's answer is no, because Shippo's only self-serve authentication on an inbound delivery is a secret in the URL and a published IP allowlist, and a merchant is entitled to know which they have. See docs\/development.md\n    * **Two permissions, not one.** `kahunacart.shipping.manage` covers carriers, boxes and ship-from addresses; `kahunacart.shipping.labels` covers buying and voiding, because that spends money \u2014 the same reason core separates `kahunacart.orders.refund` from `kahunacart.orders.manage`. A packer who prints labels all day should not also be able to repoint the store at a different carrier account. Reading a label rides on `kahunacart.orders.view`, which is the permission somebody answering \"where is my parcel\" already holds\n    * **Fourteen REST routes and an MCP tool for each**, under `\/kahunacart\/shipping`: the aggregator and its connection check, the carrier accounts and their refresh, the box catalogue, the ship-from addresses, and a packing preview that answers what an order \u2014 or a made-up basket \u2014 would pack into. The preview is the route behind **Test the packer**, and packing is the one part of this a merchant otherwise cannot see and therefore cannot trust. Every write honours `demo.readonly` the way core's do, and the manifest is checked against the routes in both directions, so a route added without a tool is a failing test rather than a 404 somebody finds in a month\n    * **Migration `shipping_0001_core`** adds `kahunacart_shipping_accounts`, `kahunacart_shipping_locations` and `kahunacart_shipping_boxes` through the base plugin's own migration runner. All three carry `store_id` the way core's tables do, and none of them is declared to `bin\/plugin kahunacart reset` \u2014 they are configuration, and a reset promises to keep the configuration\n    * **Buy a real postage label from an order, and print it.** One button on an order that has something left to ship: pick the parcels the packer worked out, pick a service, see the price, buy. What comes back is a label file, a tracking number and a shipment written onto the order through KahunaCart's own fulfilment service \u2014 so the fulfilment status moves, the shipped email goes out, and **the tracking link on it works**, which is the one thing a store cannot have without this. The purchase is seven steps in a fixed order and the order is the whole design: lock the order, work out a key, write the row **before any money is spent**, buy, settle the row, record the parcel, link the two. The step that writes first is what turns \"we never found out whether that worked\" into something a job can finish by itself. See docs\/labels.md\n    * **A double-click cannot buy two labels, and it is the database that says so.** The key is made from the store, the order, the service and what is actually in the parcels \u2014 never from anything about the request, never from the price \u2014 and a unique index on `(store_id, idempotency_key)` refuses the second write. A check-then-insert is a race; an index is not. Shippo has no idempotency mechanism of its own at all, so this is not a nice-to-have: the same transaction sent twice buys two labels and charges twice. There is a test that forks a second PHP process and races it against the first\n    * **A purchase whose answer never arrived is chased until it is settled.** A timeout leaves a `pending` row, not a failure, and `shipping.reconcile_label` asks Shippo every minute for six hours \u2014 by the plugin's own key, and through `GET \/transactions?rate=` which is the only way to ask \"did this rate ever become a label\". A purchase that did go through settles by itself and records the parcel; one that did not is marked failed. If six hours is not enough the merchant gets a **Label outcome unknown** notification, raised once per label for ever, and a banner on the order with **Record the parcel** on it. The one state this deliberately does not roll back is a bought label whose shipment write failed: throwing away paid-for postage to tidy up a database error is the wrong trade, so both facts go on the banner and the merchant picks\n    * **Void a label and get the postage back.** The refund is asked for, and then it is waited for honestly: a carrier takes up to fourteen business days to settle one, so `void_requested` is a normal state with a sentence rather than a spinner, and the reconcile job keeps asking. A request more than ninety days after the label was bought is refused here before anything is sent, naming the date, because nothing recovers it. A label the carrier has already scanned comes back `void_refused` and goes back to being what it is \u2014 a bought, used label\n    * **Tracking, two ways, because a store needs both.** An inbound webhook is prompt and a six-hourly poll is reliable, and **switching every webhook off and letting only the poll run still takes a parcel to delivered**. Every carrier's vocabulary is written down as one of seven statuses, a status nobody recognises is kept as `unknown` with the carrier's own words beside it rather than dropped, and the unique index on `(label_id, status, occurred_at)` is what makes a redelivered webhook and an overlapping poll write one event between them. The delivered email, the history line and the parcel-came-back notification all hang off that **write** rather than off the status, which is the whole of \"it goes out once, and once only\". See docs\/tracking.md\n    * **An inbound webhook route that brings its own body cap, delivery log and authentication.** Core's webhook door only opens for a payment provider, so this registers on the API plugin's public-route mechanism the way the Licenses add-on does. It refuses a body over 256 KB with a 413 **before** anything else happens, compares a per-store secret in the path with `hash_equals`, optionally checks the caller against Shippo's nine published addresses, and writes a row per delivery \u2014 kept thirty days \u2014 because \"the webhook never arrives\" and \"the webhook arrives and is refused\" are two completely different problems that look identical without one. A wrong secret is a **404**, so a scanner learns nothing; a body nobody can read is a **200**, because every aggregator treats a 4xx as a reason to retry for a week and the payload will not have become readable by then. **The endpoint writes no tracking at all** \u2014 it queues a job that re-reads the parcel with the store's own key \u2014 so a forged ping costs one API call and writes nothing, which is what makes an unsigned webhook acceptable here. It also keeps inside the three seconds Shippo allows a receiver, and there is a test asserting it makes no outbound call\n    * **The driver can set its own webhook up**, and for this release nothing calls it. `setupWebhook()` adopts an address it registered before rather than adding a second, and refuses anything that is not `https` or is over Shippo's 200-character limit before sending it \u2014 but no route exposes it, so there is no card and no button, and the four manual steps on the Instructions tab are what a merchant has. Written up as a finding in docs\/development.md rather than worked around\n    * **Customs declarations, built only when the parcel actually crosses a border.** Per line: a description, a quantity, a total weight, a total value and a country of origin \u2014 totals, not per-item, which is the thing everybody gets wrong once. The facts come from the product's own customs row, then the order line, then a store-wide default, most specific first and **field by field**, so a store that recorded a code on the product and an origin on one variant gets both. **Missing facts are named before the button, not by a carrier's error array after it**: \"three items need a country of origin\", with the products linked. Four rules are enforced here because the carrier's own wording for them is poor, including the one where Shippo accepts a harmonised code under two field names and silently prefers one. See docs\/customs.md\n    * **Buy a label automatically when an order is paid.** Off by default and worth leaving off until the packing looks right. It hangs off KahunaCart's compensating hooks rather than an event listener, and that buys two things a listener cannot have: an order that completes twice buys exactly one label, and a cancelled or refunded order **voids the label it bought** \u2014 only that one, because a label bought by hand was a decision and undoing somebody's deliberate action is not what this is for\n    * **The customer's tracking link points at the carrier, not at the aggregator.** A label bought through Shippo for USPS records the carrier as USPS on the shipment, so the link on the receipt goes to USPS's own page. A carrier KahunaCart has no code for is recorded as **Other** with the aggregator's own tracking URL kept, so the link still works, and a store can name it properly by adding it to core's own `shipping.carriers` with no change here\n    * **The label file is kept on this server.** Every aggregator's hosted label link is pre-signed and expires, so a merchant reprinting a label from March would get a 404 at the moment they need it most. The bytes are downloaded as the label is bought, sharded two levels deep so a shop printing forty a day does not end the year with fifteen thousand files in one directory, and kept for ever by default because a postage record is a financial record. If the file has gone, **Open the label** falls back to the aggregator's link and says so; if that has expired too, it says that too, which is the only honest answer left\n    * **A delivered email, in the order's own language**, carrying the carrier, the number, a working link and the date \u2014 and nothing about the label, the postage cost or the box. Four owner notifications beside it, each deduped to the thing that actually went wrong rather than to how many times it was retried: a failed purchase is once per order per day, an unresolved purchase is once per label for ever, an unreachable carrier is once per aggregator per day, and a parcel coming back is once per parcel\n    * **Migration `shipping_0002_labels`** adds `kahunacart_shipping_labels`, `kahunacart_shipping_tracking`, `kahunacart_shipping_item_customs` and `kahunacart_shipping_deliveries`. The first, second and fourth go with `bin\/plugin kahunacart reset --orders`, because they belong to the orders they describe; the customs facts stay, because they are catalogue configuration and a reset promises to keep it. Two unique indexes carry real design load rather than being belt and braces: `(store_id, idempotency_key)` on labels and `(label_id, status, occurred_at)` on tracking events\n    * **Checked against a real Shippo test account, not only against fixtures.** A live run bought a label, downloaded and stored it, tracked it to delivered through the inbound webhook, sent the delivered email and asked for the refund \u2014 and found three things a green suite could not: the driver was being built without the plain HTTP client, so no label file was ever kept; nothing at all enqueued the reconcile job, so a purchase whose answer never arrived would have sat pending for ever; and once it did, the job booked a second copy of itself on every run. All three are seams between pieces that were each correct on their own, all three are fixed, and each has a test that would have caught it\n    * **Ten more REST routes and an MCP tool for each** \u2014 quote, buy, void, record a parcel, list and read labels, serve a label file, read and refresh a parcel's tracking, read and write a product's customs facts, and check an address. Buying, voiding and recording sit behind `kahunacart.shipping.labels` because they spend the store's money; reading rides on `kahunacart.orders.view`, which the person answering \"where is my parcel\" already holds. The manifest is still checked against the routes in both directions\n    * **A Carriers screen, and it is called Carriers on purpose.** Core already owns two sidebar entries in the Setup band called *Shipping* and *Shipping profiles*, and a third one would be the worst thing on the screen \u2014 Carriers also reads as the sibling of *Payments*, which is exactly what it is: the place the accounts that move things live. Four tabs under one heading. **Carriers** holds the aggregator's card \u2014 the brand name bold, the buttons underneath, then a fixed check-and-cross matrix of the ten things a driver may or may not do, drawn from what the driver declares rather than from a list somebody keeps in a template \u2014 with the ship-from addresses and a card per carrier account under it, each with the switch that says whether this store offers it and its service codes as chips you can copy. **Boxes** is the catalogue and the packing rules. **Labels** is every label the store has. **Settings** is the plugin's own form, drawn inside the section so there is one place to paste a key rather than two. See docs\/getting-started.md\n    * **Test the packer, and see what a merchant otherwise cannot.** Pack a real order by its id, or a basket that does not exist yet, and read back the parcels the packer would make: the box each one landed in, what it weighs on the scale, what it weighs dimensionally, which of the two the carrier would bill and why, and every line whose size nobody has recorded. Packing is the one part of this that is invisible until a customer is charged for three parcels where two would have done, and a merchant who can look at the answer can trust it \u2014 or go and fix the box catalogue. The route behind it is a **GET** and writes nothing at all, which is what lets a read-only store show it\n    * **One status strip above the carrier cards, drawn only when something is wrong.** Live carrier rates switched off in KahunaCart, no fallback method nominated, a shipping method naming a service no enabled carrier offers \u2014 each one a sentence and a button that goes to the screen that fixes it. **It never fixes anything itself.** Core's configuration is core's to write, and a button on somebody else's screen that silently rewrites `kahunacart.yaml` is how a merchant loses an afternoon\n    * **The parcels of an order, beside the order.** A panel in the admin's own `order.details.side` zone: the carrier, the service, the tracking number as a link, what the postage cost, the buttons a bought label has, and the tracking events underneath with a place and a time on each. An order that has bought nothing yet draws **what the packer proposes instead**, so a merchant can see what the parcel will be before spending anything. An order with nothing physical on it draws nothing at all \u2014 a panel reading \"no parcels\" on every digital order is noise on every digital order\n    * **Buy label, in the order's action row, and a sheet that names the price on the button that spends it.** \"Buy this label for $9.41\", not \"Confirm\" \u2014 the same reason core's refund confirm says the amount. The sheet asks for a **fresh** quote rather than reusing checkout's, because that number can be fifteen minutes old and this one is what gets charged; it draws each parcel with its box, its billable weight and its contents, every service the carrier quoted with its price and its transit estimate, the ship-from address, and the delivery address exactly as it will be printed with the carrier's own validation messages under it. While it works the button is disabled and says so, because a double-click here buys two labels. **When it fails the sheet stays open** with the carrier's own words on it and the button live again \u2014 a sheet that closes on failure has thrown away everything the merchant chose. The service picked on the sheet is the service bought, even when the order's own method names none \u2014 a method set to take the first rate the carrier returns is the common case, and it must not be the one case a merchant cannot buy postage for. The name the carrier gave the service is kept with the label, so every screen afterwards says *Priority Mail Express* rather than `usps_priority_express`\n    * **Void asks a question that names the window and the wait.** \"This label was bought 3 days ago. A refund can be asked for up to 90 days after purchase and takes up to fourteen business days to settle, and it is refused outright if the parcel has already moved.\" What comes back is reported in the carrier's own words, and a refund the carrier has taken and not settled is drawn as exactly that rather than as a spinner. Fourteen days is not a loading state\n    * **Where the parcel is, on the receipt and in the account.** Two storefront panels and one partial, with an email twin beside it, both stamped so the drift report can see a theme copy fall behind. They print the carrier's name, the tracking number, its link, the dates and where the parcel has got to \u2014 and **nothing else, ever**: no cost, no label file, no box, no internal note. That is not a rule somebody has to remember, it is an allowlist with a test on it that fails if a key is added to one and not the other. A tracking status goes through a language key rather than the carrier's own English, so a store running in French reads French\n    * **The API token and the webhook secret are encrypted on save.** Core opened `onKahunaCartCollectEncryptedSettings` for any plugin to declare its own secrets \u2014 the gap this add-on wrote up in its first leaf \u2014 and this subscribes to it, handing over the driver's own declared list rather than a second copy of it. A `type: password` field added to the blueprint and forgotten in that list is a failing test rather than a key sitting in a config file in plaintext\n    * **A demo carrier, so the add-on can be seen working without a carrier account.** A driver that quotes, prints, tracks and refunds and never opens a socket: two invented carriers, five services, three distance bands and a whole parcel journey compressed into thirty-six minutes. It is off by default and it takes two switches to turn on \u2014 `aggregator: demo` names it and `demo_carrier.enabled` allows it, and with either off it registers nothing at all, exactly as a real driver with no key pasted does. It is deliberately **not** keyed off KahunaCart's `demo.readonly`, because those two switches mean different things: a staging box wants a read-only admin and still wants its real carrier, and a merchant who turned read-only on for an afternoon must not have had a simulation printing labels under a real carrier's methods for that afternoon. It is not a mode either \u2014 there is no flag anywhere that changes how the real driver behaves, every fake lives behind the aggregator interface, and nothing above it learns that a demo exists. See docs\/demo-carrier.md\n    * **The label it prints is a real PDF, and it says so across the top.** Rendered through KahunaCart's own PDF renderer \u2014 the one that already prints invoices and packing slips, with remote assets off \u2014 at 4x6 inches whatever the store's invoice paper is set to, carrying **SIMULATION \u2014 NOT VALID FOR POSTAGE**, the addresses as they would be printed, the parcel's billable weight and the tracking number. The barcode on it is decorative on purpose: a demo that printed a scannable code of an invented tracking number would be a demo that could produce a label somebody tried to post. The bytes go through the same store, the same authenticated route and the same retention sweep a real label's do, with no special case anywhere\n    * **A parcel that moves while you watch it, and one that goes wrong.** Six events from label to doorstep across the configured transit time, so a visitor who stays five minutes sees the parcel move and a demo store that rebuilds itself on the hour fits a whole journey inside one reset. A parcel going to postcode `00000` reaches *on its way*, then writes a failure with **action required** on it, then comes back to the sender \u2014 which is the same postcode the address check refuses outright, so there is one rule to remember and two places it shows up. It covers the two tracking statuses a happy path never reaches, and it is what gives the **Parcel came back** notification something to fire on\n    * **The clock runs on KahunaCart's own worker tick.** Core fires one after its queue drains on every pass, so a store that already runs the worker needs no cron line of its own and the demo moves on the store's clock rather than on one this add-on keeps. `bin\/plugin kahunacart-shipping demo:tick` is the same pass by hand, for a person watching a screen and for a read-only demo, where `demo.readonly` refuses every write through the admin and explicitly leaves the CLI open. Both write through the add-on's own tracking service \u2014 the same object the webhook job and the poll job write through \u2014 so what a demo parcel does is what a real one does. The tracking poll is deliberately not what does this: its one-hour floor exists to keep a real store inside a carrier's rate limits, and a setting invented to make a demo look good is how a real store ends up throttled\n    * **A tracking page the add-on serves itself.** A real label's link points at the carrier's own website; there is no carrier, so there is a bare page at `{api}\/kahunacart\/shipping\/public\/demo-track\/{number}` with its own markup, no theme, no assets and `noindex` on it \u2014 registered only when the demo carrier is the configured aggregator. It is public and unauthenticated, which is exactly what a tracking page is, and the price of that is an allowlist with a test on it: **a status, a time and a place, and never a name, an address, an order number or a price**\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-shipping seed` fills a store with parcels.** A ship-from address, four boxes, both carrier accounts written exactly as **Refresh carriers** would have written them, sizes onto the seeded catalogue \u2014 which carries a weight and no dimensions, so without them every parcel is an estimate \u2014 one oversized product that exists to be refused, shipping methods bound to the demo's services in the zones core's own seeder makes, and eleven orders carrying one part of the story each: delivered, out for delivery, in transit, one bought two minutes ago, two parcels on one order, an international parcel with a customs declaration, one that failed and came back, one refunded, one the carrier refused in its own words, one with no label yet so the packer's proposal is on screen, and one with nothing physical on it at all. Every label is bought through the add-on's own label service and every shipment written through KahunaCart's own fulfilment service, so a bug in the purchase path is a bug in the demo rather than something the demo papers over. Run it twice and the store is the same store; `--purge` asks first, defaults to no, and takes back exactly what it wrote and nothing else\n    * **One service exists to say no.** Driftwood Ground refuses a parcel over 900 mm on its longest side, one over 20 kg billable, and any destination abroad \u2014 because a store cannot demonstrate its refusal table with a carrier that says yes to everything, and \"the service you picked will not take a parcel this long\" is the most common real refusal there is. The seeder puts a 1,150 mm wall rack in the catalogue precisely so a visitor can add it to a cart and watch a method disappear from the checkout with a sentence naming why, and the other services stay priced. The carrier refuses at the counter what it refused at the quote, too, so a stale rate or a mis-picked service is a `failed` label carrying the carrier's own sentence rather than an exception nobody sees\n    * **The demo driver is also the worked example for anybody writing a second one.** It is the aggregator contract with all the API noise taken out of it: one class, every method, every honest refusal, and not a line of cURL in the way. It declares its capabilities honestly including the noes \u2014 no tracking webhook and no signed webhooks, because there is nothing on the other end to post one \u2014 and it takes no HTTP client at all, which is asserted by reading its constructor rather than promised in a comment. Its suites run the real label service, the real tracking service and the real ticker with it underneath, so a divergence between the demo and the product is a failing test rather than a demo that behaves differently from the thing being sold. See docs\/development.md\n    * **Documentation, end to end.** Twelve pages in `docs\/` for somebody reading on GitHub \u2014 getting started, carriers, boxes and packing, labels, tracking, customs, the demo carrier, configuration, a reference of every route, tool, permission, CLI command, job, table and event, troubleshooting, development and testing \u2014 and a whole Shipping chapter on kahunacart.com written for a merchant reading a website. `docs\/testing.md` carries the four layers the suite is built in, the real-key campaign in the order it has to be run, and the demo walk that needs no key at all\n    * **A `kc-shipping` test store**, served locally, that comes up with the demo carrier on and the seed applied, so every screen has something on it before anybody pastes a key, and puts itself back to a clean store in one command while keeping the catalogue, the zones, the boxes and any key that has been pasted\n\n1. [](#improved)\n    * **A store that installs this and pastes nothing is byte-identical to one that has not installed it.** No driver is registered until a token is configured, so no method is carrier-bound, nothing in the checkout path changes, and no call is ever made. The same is true of switching the plugin off: every bound method goes unavailable with a reason and the merchant's fallback carries checkout, which is core's own documented behaviour rather than anything this plugin invents\n    * **A carrier never blocks checkout**, and the suite asserts it rather than assuming it. A driver that throws, times out, is switched off or was never configured leaves every bound method shown unavailable with a sentence a customer can act on, and the storefront still renders\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A label whose stored file has gone opens the carrier's own copy instead of a broken tab.** The file route has always answered the hosted link as JSON with a 200 on it rather than redirecting to a link that may itself have expired \u2014 and the parcels panel beside an order opened every answer as bytes, so what a merchant actually got was a tab of unreadable JSON. The worst possible way to be told a file is missing. The panel reads the content type now: the carrier's link opens in the new tab, and the card says the stored copy has gone and that those links expire, so the fix is to void the label and buy another. The thumbnail beside the details falls back to the drawn card as it always did\n    * **A delivery option no longer prints a line of codes under its own name.** A driver's rate carries two kinds of thing \u2014 Shippo's \"Delivery in 1 to 3 business days\", and the service code, the band and the reference a label is bought against \u2014 and all of it went onto the offer's one metadata map. A theme prints that map by looping it, so the checkout picker read `demo cached driftwood_ground Driftwood Ground 1215 USD 5 domestic 6000 1 1 demo_rate_8e6c5d` under the method name. KahunaCart 1.2.9 splits an offer's metadata in two \u2014 the half a person reads and the half a machine does \u2014 and both drivers now put only sentences in the first. Shippo sends its `duration_terms` and its `arrives_by`, and no longer sends the service zone or a One Rate variant's `terms`, which were codes and are already said by the service code. The demo driver sends nothing at all: its band, its billable weight, its parcel count and its `simulated` flag were its own working, and a customer choosing between two delivery options learned nothing from `domestic 6000 1`. The underlying carrier is not sent either \u2014 `usps_priority` already names it. What a label needs later still rides across: `rate_ref` and `carrier_account` travel under the names core reserves, land on the offer's `carrier` map, reach the order's audit trail, and are never printed\n    * **A customer no longer reads `%service% will not carry a parcel this long.` at checkout.** A driver refuses a service with its own language key and its own placeholders, core carries both halves \u2014 `detail_key` and `detail_params` \u2014 and the storefront theme prints the key with no arguments at all. Every parameterised refusal therefore reached a customer with the per cent signs still in it, the two the Shippo driver produces included: `%carrier%: %message%` when a carrier refused a service, and `The carrier quoted in %currency%` when a rate came back in the wrong money. All six now read correctly with nothing substituted into them, because the fact each would have named is already beside it \u2014 the method's own label is the service's name \u2014 and the carrier's own words still reach the merchant verbatim on the Labels tab. Found on the first live checkout with the oversized product in the cart. The other half is a theme that substitutes named parameters, and it is written up in docs\/development.md\n    * **The Carriers tab no longer tells a demo store to paste a carrier key.** `GET \/kahunacart\/shipping\/aggregators` answered `configured` from the Shippo token, whatever driver the store was actually running, and the whole tab is gated on that one boolean \u2014 so a store on the demo carrier, which has no token and never will, drew the empty state over a registered driver with two carrier accounts that was pricing a live checkout at that moment. It asks the registry now, which is the same question of Shippo and the right one of every other driver\n    * **The docs no longer describe two buttons that do not exist.** `docs\/tracking.md` and `docs\/configuration.md` both said the webhook secret is made by a **Generate** button on the Carriers screen and handed to Shippo by a **Register** one. The driver implements the whole webhook-setup contract and the plugin can build the address, but no route exposes any of it, so there is no card and there are no buttons. Both pages now carry the four manual steps \u2014 make a secret, save it, build the address, paste it at Shippo \u2014 and the Instructions tab carries them too, which is where the finding said they should be until the routes land\n    * **A refusal reaches the merchant in the carrier's own words rather than as \"HTTP 422\".** Buying, voiding and recording a parcel all answer an HTTP error when the carrier says no, and every fetch helper in the admin throws on a bad status and prints the error's message \u2014 so a body carrying `outcome` and `reasons` under `data` gave it nothing to print but the status number, and the branch meant to draw the carrier's sentence could never run. The refusal now answers a problem document with a title and the carrier's own sentence in it, which is what a thrown error carries. Found on a live store: a refund the demo carrier refused because the parcel had already been collected arrived as \"HTTP 422\"\n    * **A 403 on the buy sheet no longer blames the carrier.** A store with `demo.readonly` on, or an account without the labels permission, is the store refusing, not the carrier, and sending somebody to look at their carrier account over it wastes an afternoon. The sheet says **The store refused it** for a 403 and keeps core's own sentence underneath\n    * **The demo label's footer no longer falls off the right-hand edge of the page.** dompdf adds padding to a declared width rather than taking it out of it, so `width: 100%` plus side padding made the label's content box twenty points wider than the 4x6 page and the end of every long line was cut off. A block fills its parent on its own\n    * **`item_excluded` is produced for the first time.** Core reserved the reason code for an add-on and has never itself produced one. A comma-separated list of shipping classes this carrier will not take \u2014 empty by default, so a store that configures none behaves exactly as it did \u2014 takes every method bound to the carrier off the list and names the product, instead of leaving the customer looking at a shorter list with no explanation. The refusal is made in `supports()`, which core's runner asks before it spends any of the timeout, so it costs no network call; the exclusion event, which fires after the carrier has already been priced, is what rewords the reason so it says what actually happened"}}},"kahunacart-bookings":{"name":"KahunaCart Bookings","version":"1.1.4","description":"Appointments, classes and anything else with a time on it, for KahunaCart","description_html":"<p>Appointments, classes and anything else with a time on it, for KahunaCart<\/p>","description_plain":"Appointments, classes and anything else with a time on it, for KahunaCart","icon":"calendar-check","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.3.8"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/bookings","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/bookings\/overview","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, bookings, appointments, scheduling, calendar, classes","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-bookings","license":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/premium\/license","tag_name":"1.1.4","date":"2026-10-02T01:05:12Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-bookings\/1.1.4","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/bookings","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-bookings","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-bookings","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-bookings"},"slug":"kahunacart-bookings","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-bookings","content_sha256":"5423c1f19929e357366e090f7a1d04cb9d25ed32de113963adbcc6071dd0219f","changelog":{"1.1.4":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Help text in the Bookings section's forms now follows the admin's Help text setting, showing below the label or in a tooltip behind an info icon\n"},"1.1.3":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The download no longer carries the plugin's tests.** `tests\/`, `phpunit.xml`, the desk app's source (`desk-src\/`; the built app in `assets\/desk` still ships) and `bin\/` stay in the repository and out of the release zip, 133 files fewer on every install. Nothing the plugin runs is affected\n"},"1.1.2":{"date":"09\/30\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Every table in the Bookings section sorts by its column headings.** Press a heading to sort by it, and press it again to turn the order round. The Bookings list sorts on the server, so a heading orders every booking the filters leave and not only the page on screen, and pressing one goes back to page one. `GET \/kahunacart\/bookings` takes `sort` and `dir` with `service`, `with`, `seats` and `order` added to the four it already had (`start_at`, `created_at`, `status` and `reference`), so a heading and the parameter are one control, and Export CSV follows the order on screen. Sessions, a class's dates and who is coming to each, Services, People, Hours, Places and the Troubleshoot answer sort in the browser. A date's waiting list stays in the order a place would be offered, and a service's options stay under their service. Needs KahunaCart 1.3.8 or later, which draws the headings\n"},"1.1.1":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The test suite runs against KahunaCart 1.3, which bundles brick\/money and YetiSearch under its own namespace.** When it borrows the base plugin's dependencies it now also loads core's `vendor-prefixed\/autoload.php`. Against an older KahunaCart without that file nothing changes. Nothing in Bookings itself names brick\/money or YetiSearch, so the add-on needs no other change\n"},"1.1.0":{"date":"09\/22\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **New booking at the front desk picks a day off a month.** After the service, the sheet shows a month calendar with a bar under each day saying how much room it has, so a receptionist on the phone can say \"Thursday's nearly full, Friday's wide open\" without trying days one at a time. Closed, full and past days are greyed out, and it opens on the first day with room. Tapping a day lists its open times in one column per person or place, which is how the question is actually asked: \"who's free at two?\" On a tablet the month and the columns sit side by side\n    * **An appointment can be made longer or shorter at the desk.** A \u221215 \/ +15 control under **How long?**, with a \"usually 45 min\" link back to the service's own length, for the customer who always needs a bit more time. Every time offered is one that length fits, and the conflict guard checks the length that is booked. `desk\/slots` and `POST desk\/bookings` take `minutes`; only an appointment can be stretched, because a class runs as long as the class runs\n    * **Move and Cancel are one tap away on the booking sheet.** They sit under the main button instead of behind More. Move uses the same month and columns as New booking, opens on the booking's own day with its own person first, and can move it to somebody else or change its length on the way; the booking being moved no longer stands in its own way, so a half-hour nudge is offered. Cancel asks first, takes an optional reason for the trail, and keeps the **Let the customer know** tick, because a tablet on a counter gets tapped by accident\n    * **A double-booked booking can be put right at the desk.** Money that arrives after its time has gone makes a booking that holds no time, and until now nothing could clear that: the desk would not move it, and moving it anywhere else left it flagged and waiting. Moving it onto a free time now claims that time through the same guard, clears the flag, confirms it the way a paid booking of its service would be, and writes **resolved** in its trail. The person on the phone to the customer does that from the booking sheet; handing money back stays the owner's. The sheet says what to do instead of \"the owner has to sort this one out\", and its chip just says **Double-booked**\n    * **A customer sheet at the desk.** \"Patel rang, when was she last in?\" Find opens the customer rather than jumping to their next booking, and so does the name on any booking. The sheet has tap-to-call and tap-to-email, how often they come and how often they do not, their next and last bookings (each one opens), and **Book for them**, which starts New booking with them already filled in. A stylist sees only the bookings in her own column, the same rule the booking sheet follows. Still no address, no orders and no spend; those are the owner's, in the admin\n    * **The desk can put a customer's details right without ringing the owner.** **Edit details** on the customer sheet changes the name and the telephone number, and on an account that works the whole diary the email too \u2014 refused, and saying who, when another customer already has that address, because that is the same person twice and joining them up is the owner's. The sheet says the email is also how they sign in to the shop. Orders are filed by customer number, not by address, so nothing is split by changing it. Two new desk routes, `GET` and `POST desk\/customers\/{id}`, which the route test now lists\n    * **`desk\/slots` counts a range of days.** Asked about `from` \u2026 `to` it returns how many open times each day has in the store's timezone, for the month calendar, rather than a month of times; asked about one `date` it lists the times as before. `except` names a booking being moved. Still nine desk routes\n\n2. [](#improved)\n    * **The New booking sheet folds each answered question down to its answer.** Services and options are tiles, a picked one shows alone with **Change** beside it, and **Who with?** is a row of pills, so the calendar is on screen without scrolling past every service in the shop. The **Book it** button names what it books \u2014 day, time and who with \u2014 because the time picked is usually scrolled away by the time the customer is found\n    * **Check in is offered from the day a booking starts.** A booking for Thursday no longer has Check in as its big button on a Tuesday. A cancelled or turned-down booking no longer says \"Pay today\", and the sheet draws no buttons it has nothing behind\n\n3. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Saying yes to a double-booked booking confirmed an appointment nothing was keeping free.** The desk offered **Yes, book it** on one, and `approve` would have marked it confirmed while it claimed no time at all. The button is gone and the server refuses it: move it to a free time first, or cancel it\n    * **The desk offered a class at every half hour its teacher was free.** `desk\/slots` sent a class through the appointment scheduler, so a colour workshop that runs one Thursday evening was offered at eleven on a Saturday morning. It now answers with the dates the class actually runs, leaving out any that are full or past their registration deadline, the same way the storefront does\n    * **New booking hung on \"Loading\u2026\" in a store with a rule per variant.** `desk\/services` listed a product once for every rule it had, so a groom with a rule on Small and on Large came back twice and the sheet stopped with `each_key_duplicate`. Each product now comes back once, with its variants inside it as before\n"},"1.0.1":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The docs and the diary seed say where the deposits switch is.** KahunaCart 1.2.13 moves **Deposits and part payment** from **Settings \u2192 Security** to **Settings \u2192 Commerce**. The massage clinic walkthrough named a tab that never existed (**Settings \u2192 Checkout**) and now names **KahunaCart \u2192 Settings \u2192 Commerce**, [Payments and deposits](payments-and-deposits.md#deposits) says where the switch is, and `seed diary` says the same when it skips the part-paid booking on a store with deposits off\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **Bookings, the add-on.** Appointments, and everything else that takes somebody's or something's time. Core keeps the product, the price, the cart, the order, the money and the customer; this keeps what a product *means* as a booking \u2014 how long it takes, who or what it needs, and when it can be had. This first release is the engine and the tables underneath all of it, with no screens on top yet: what is here is installable, migrates clean, and answers \"when can a full groom be booked next Thursday, and if not, why not\"\n    * **One kind of record for anything that can be booked.** A stylist, a grooming tub, a range lane and a treatment room are all **bookables**, with a number of identical units on each \u2014 so ten lanes is one row with ten units, and the same engine books a person, a room and a machine without knowing which it is holding. That is the one modelling change over Schedule Pro, and everything else here follows from it\n    * **A service says what it needs, and a time is only offered when all of it is free.** A full groom needs one groomer from {Ann, Bo} with the customer choosing, and the tub with no choice. A range lane needs one unit from Lanes. A massage needs a therapist and a room. Candidate start times come from the first need; every other need has to have somebody or something free for the whole span, buffers included, before the time goes on the page. For each need the customer did not pick, the first free one by position takes it \u2014 and which one that is rides on the slot, so what a customer was shown and what gets claimed when they pay are the same answer rather than two computations hoping to agree\n    * **A blocked time says why, in words.** Too soon, too far ahead, full, booked, held by somebody else, time off, closed, busy on another calendar \u2014 and when a need is what failed, the reason names it: **\"Tub: booked\"**. That is one code path with the availability query, not a second one beside it, so the explanation cannot drift from what a customer is actually offered. \"Why can nobody book Thursday\" is the commonest question a booking system gets, and this is the answer to it\n    * **A service is a KahunaCart product of type Booking.** Registered as a product type with `ships: false` and `holdsStock: false`, and declared to core as a **service** rather than as a download \u2014 which is what keeps EU digital-services VAT off a haircut in Denver and keeps a digital-goods-only payment platform from being offered for one. Duration options are variants, so \"Small dog, 60 minutes, $55\" and \"Large dog, 90 minutes, $80\" are priced with nothing new built, and a rule written for a variant replaces the product's for that variant only\n    * **Working hours in three rungs, and time off where the person is.** A bookable's own schedule, else its location's, else the service's fallback \u2014 which is how a merchant thinks about it: a part-timer with no hours of her own is available when the shop is open. A weekly plan carries per-date overrides on the same record, so a bank holiday closes a day without needing a second kind of thing. Time off is whole days in the bookable's own timezone, because \"away on the 4th\" means the 4th where they are\n    * **The timezone and DST work came over from Schedule Pro untouched, matrix and all.** Spring forward, fall back, Kolkata's half hour and Auckland straddling the date line are the same tests they were, passing against the same code, with only the namespace line edited. Two bookables in two zones serving one service meet in the middle, and there is a test that says where\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-bookings slots`** prints the times for a product on a day, and `--explain` prints the blocked ones with the reason beside each. It is how this release is demonstrated with no screens at all, and it stays afterwards as the thing to run when a merchant says Thursday is empty. `seed` writes a whole business to point it at\n    * **Twelve tables, all created at once.** The seven this release writes to, and the five the next ones will \u2014 the bookings, the holds, the claims, the audit trail and the record of what has already been emailed. All of them now, so the leaves that follow add columns and never argue over who creates what. `reset --orders` takes the diary and leaves the salon standing, which is also what an hourly demo reset wants\n    * **Availability reads one table and nothing else.** A hold and a booking are both rows in `claims`, so they block in exactly the same way and turning one into the other changes no arithmetic anywhere. The reader behind it is an interface with one method on it, which is what lets the whole engine be driven from an array in a test \u2014 no database, no container, nothing on disk\n    * **Two customers cannot be sold the same chair.** Every path that takes a time \u2014 a visitor holding a slot, an order completing, a merchant booking somebody in over the phone, a customer moving Thursday to Friday \u2014 goes through one guard: a write lock on every bookable involved in ascending id order, a sweep of lapsed holds, a fresh look at what is free with this service's own buffers on it, and a unique key on the claim underneath all of it as the last word. No `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` anywhere, because KahunaCart does not take row locks that way and this does not start. There is a test with six real forked processes racing one SQLite file for the same two o'clock: one wins, five are told the time has gone, and against a bookable with two units, two win\n    * **A picked time is held while the customer finishes.** A hold is a claim like any other, so it blocks everybody else exactly as a booking does and becomes one without a single sum changing. Fifteen minutes in the cart, an hour once the payment page opens, and the longer of the two always wins \u2014 a payment page that outlived its own hold would be a customer paying for a time somebody else has taken. A hold is only ever read, resized or released by the browser that made it, so a guessed id frees nobody's Thursday. One hold per service and as many services as you like, which is a haircut and a massage in one basket\n    * **The appointment rides in the cart line.** The hold, the start, the timezone, the person and the words the customer saw, on core's own `line_data` \u2014 so two appointments for one service are two lines rather than one line of quantity two, the cart page prints \"Thu 25 Sep, 2:00pm with Ann\" under the product name, and the receipt says the same thing months later whatever anybody does to Ann's name in the meantime. A booking added with no time on it is refused with a sentence the customer can act on, and so is a hold that lapsed while they were deciding\n    * **The order and the diary are wired through core's compensating hooks.** An order completing makes the bookings, money landing confirms them, and cancelling or refunding gives the times back \u2014 each one idempotent per order and each one declaring how to undo itself, so a redelivered webhook makes no second appointment and a failed step leaves nothing half-done. The release deliberately has no undo: putting a cancelled booking back would claim a time the shop has already offered to somebody else\n    * **Money arriving for a time that has gone is somebody's job, not a swallowed error.** If a hold lapses on the payment page and the slot goes while the card is being typed, the booking is still made \u2014 pending, marked as conflicted, claiming nothing, with the whole story in its trail \u2014 and the shop gets the loudest notification this add-on sends. A person rings the customer and moves them or hands the money back. The alternative is selling one chair twice and finding out when both parties arrive\n    * **Some bookings have nothing to pay.** A store or a single service can take payment on the day, and a free consultation costs nothing wherever it is set; those go straight into the diary with no cart, no order and no checkout. Everything with a price on it goes through the basket, and a storefront that asks to book one directly is told to add it to the cart instead rather than shown an error\n    * **Deposits work, and what actually confirms a booking is money moving rather than an order being fully paid.** A part-paid order is correctly not paid as far as every report and provider check is concerned \u2014 but a shop that has taken a deposit for Thursday morning is busy on Thursday morning, and the diary says so. Building this found that core announces `payment_captured` only on the authorise-then-capture path, so bank transfers and marked-paid orders come in by a different door and are listened for there too. Without that, a booking paid by transfer would have sat waiting for ever with nothing saying why\n    * **Cancelling never moves money on its own.** It frees the time, writes the trail, tells the shop and opens a refund request for a person to look at, because what a customer gets back is a merchant's policy and a merchant's money. A full refund cancels the bookings on the order; a part refund is a note in the trail and nothing more, because a goodwill gesture is not a cancellation\n    * **A calendar link per person, and outside calendars that block their time.** A stylist opens one link on her phone and every booking arrives on its own: moves follow the appointment rather than duplicating it, cancellations disappear, and there is nothing to install and no admin login to give out. What goes in it is a choice \u2014 everything, initials only, or the word \"Busy\" and the time \u2014 because a calendar link ends up in places nobody planned, and one person can answer differently from the rest of the shop. Pointed the other way, the evenings somebody has blocked in their own Google, iCloud or Outlook calendar stop being offered to customers, with no OAuth and nothing to keep signed in\n    * **A second calendar link, for Airbnb, VRBO and Booking.com.** A **channel link** per property, at a privacy level below all three of the others: the summary is \"Not available\", there is no guest, no service, no notes, no telephone number and no way back into the shop, and there is no setting anywhere that opens it up \u2014 because that link is pasted into somebody else's website and read by their machines. It is a separate token on a separate button, so leaving a channel does not take everybody's phone down with it. It carries the weeks somebody is **paying for right now** as well as the weeks already sold, since the gap between two polls is exactly when the same week gets sold twice; a basket left open is not in it, because baskets are abandoned all day long\n    * **Whole dates, counted where the property is.** A booking of a mode that books by the day goes out as `DTSTART;VALUE=DATE` and an exclusive `DTEND`, which is all a channel reads \u2014 and the dates come off the **property's own clock** rather than Greenwich, which is the difference between a right answer and one day out for anywhere west of UTC. The channel form leaves the day a booking ends on free, because somebody arrives that afternoon; a guest's own invitation still draws the banner across the days they are away, which is a different question with a different answer\n    * **An all-day event from a channel blocks the days it covers and not one more.** Read literally, \"the 9th to the 16th\" is midnight to midnight and takes the night before the arrival off the market as well \u2014 a genuinely sellable night refused, every time, at every property. A mode that counts days now says what its days run between, and the same event becomes check-out on the first date to check-in on the last: exactly the days sold, with both changeover days still bookable. An appointment book still reads an all-day entry as \"away all day\", because that is what it means there. A whole date with no end on it is one whole date, the way the standard says, instead of an hour nobody would notice\n    * **A cached calendar is only good for the zone and the dates it was fetched for.** Two things fell out of reading whole dates that were survivable while everything was hours and are not now. A `VALUE=DATE` value carries no timezone of its own, so the instants a parse produces belong to wherever the fetch was made for \u2014 two properties in two zones sharing one Airbnb address, or one property read once through the **Test** button and once through the calendar, were a whole day apart, with the night before an arrival refused and the last night the channel had sold quietly back on the market. And a copy is clipped to the dates it was asked about, so a month's answer was being served for every other month and pressing Test wrote thirty days over a year. The zone is in the cache key now, a copy carries the range it covers, and nothing narrower ever replaces something wider\n    * **`DURATION:P7D` on a whole-date reservation is seven days and not 604,800 seconds.** The week the clocks go back is 169 hours long; adding a fixed number of seconds lands an hour the wrong side of midnight, the exclusive end reads as the day before, and the last night goes back on the market. Real exporters write a reservation both ways and the two now agree\n    * **A channel that echoes our own bookings back at us is ignored.** Give Airbnb our link, then add Airbnb's link here, and every stay would otherwise arrive back as somebody else's reservation and block itself \u2014 and the worse half is the cancellation, where a stay we let go of would be held for ever by its own shadow at the other end. Our own ids are recognised on the way in and dropped before anything is cached, holds included\n    * **A calendar that will not load never closes the diary.** The last copy we got is used for up to a day, then the feed is skipped and the shop carries on selling \u2014 because one bad address emptying a whole salon is a worse mistake than one double booking against somebody's dentist. What is new against Schedule Pro is that the merchant is *told*: a dashboard warning names the calendar and the day it stopped, and it goes away on its own when the feed comes back. A blocked time names the calendar too, so a stylist with three feeds knows which one took the evening\n    * **A merchant typing a URL into a box is how a server-side request forgery starts, so that box is guarded.** `https` only, `webcal:\/\/` rewritten to it, the host resolved and every address it answers to checked against private, loopback, link-local and reserved ranges, at most two redirects with each hop checked again, and the address that passed pinned onto the connection so a name cannot resolve to something else a millisecond later. Two megabytes, no cookies, no sign-in. All of it refuses with a sentence a merchant can act on, and all of it is tested with a fake resolver rather than by firing requests at real private addresses\n    * **Our own bookings never come back at us.** Subscribe to a KahunaCart calendar link in Google, then point the add-on at that same Google calendar as a busy source, and nothing breaks: our own events are recognised and ignored. Without that every appointment would block itself, the diary would look full, and moving one would be refused by its own shadow\n    * **Repeating events in somebody else's calendar block every week they repeat.** Schedule Pro read `RRULE` as an unknown property and blocked the first occurrence only, so \"Tuesdays, six to eight, every week\" cost one Tuesday. Deleted occurrences are skipped, an event marked *free* in the owner's own calendar does not block anything, and the expansion is capped so a daily rule with no end cannot run away\n    * **Seventy-one endpoints, and the eleven public ones are public on purpose.** The storefront asks what is bookable, what times are on offer, takes a hold, books, and lets a customer see, move or cancel their own appointment with the token they were emailed \u2014 no login, no session, an origin check and a honeypot on every write, and a token pattern strict enough that a booking id in the URL is a 404 rather than somebody else's afternoon. Fifty of the fifty-one admin ones are MCP tools \u2014 the one exception is the CSV export, which answers a file rather than JSON \u2014 and neither the eleven public ones nor the front desk's nine is a tool; the reason for each exclusion is written down where a test can fail if a new one turns up without one\n    * **A booking picker inside the store's own buy form, on whatever theme the shop already uses.** A month calendar showing only the days with something free, the times on the day a customer picks, an option selector when the service has variants and a \"who with\" selector when it lets them choose \u2014 all of it arriving through core's own buy-button extension point, so a shop that installs this and does nothing else has a working booking storefront. Picking a time holds it, and from there one of two things happens, asked of the store rather than guessed: with money to pay, no details are asked for, the five `line_data` fields are filled in and the store's own quantity box and Add to cart button appear, so the customer goes through core's cart, core's checkout and core's provider and gives their details once; with nothing to pay, a short form and straight into the diary. **Add to cart stays hidden until a time is held**, and that is done from the script rather than from a stylesheet on purpose \u2014 a store that has turned `builtin_css` off does not load this add-on's CSS either, and a plain Add to cart button on a booking product is a cart line the store refuses by name\n    * **The same picker anywhere else on the site.** `[kahunacart-booking product=\"full-groom\" \/]` and `{{ kahunacart_booking('full-groom') }}`, on a landing page, in a sidebar, on a blog post. Off a shop route the shortcode brings its own script and stylesheet with it, once per request, and every colour in that stylesheet falls back to something \u2014 the store's `--kc-*` tokens are only defined on KahunaCart's own routes, and a calendar in no colours at all is what happens without it\n    * **The appointment on the receipt, in the order email, and in the account.** A panel on the receipt page with Move it and Cancel it on it, a matching block in the order email, a Bookings tab listing what is coming up and what already happened, and a card on the account overview showing the next appointment and nothing else. The email panel **holds the receipt back** until the booking exists rather than sending one that says nothing about the appointment the customer just paid for \u2014 except after a conflict, where there is nothing left to wait for and a person has to ring them. Both writes go through the store's own account form with core's nonce and login check on them, and a booking that is not this customer's is refused in exactly the same words as one that does not exist\n    * **A page a guest changes their own appointment on.** `[kahunacart-booking-manage \/]` on a page of the shop's own making, named in the settings \u2014 and until it is named, no message carries a link at all, because a link to a page nobody has made is worse than no link. The token is never rendered into the page: the script reads it off the query string, since a page Grav has cached would otherwise hand out whoever's booking was drawn first. It is checked against its pattern before anything is looked up, so a guessed token never reaches a query\n    * **Eight emails to the customer, and a paid booking gets one of them.** Request received, you're booked in, the calendar invitation, cancelled, moved, declined, the reminder and the thank-you \u2014 every one a switch a merchant can turn off and a template pair a theme can replace. The rule underneath is that a customer who paid gets **one** receipt: core's own already names the appointment in its panel, so a booking that rode in on an order never gets the long confirmation. It gets two sentences and the `.ics` file, and turning that off leaves exactly one email. A request waiting for a person to approve it beats both, because \"have they said yes\" is the one thing the customer is actually wondering\n    * **A calendar invitation that replaces the entry instead of adding a second one.** `REQUEST` to create and update, `CANCEL` to remove, and a sequence number off the booking that rises on every move \u2014 which is what makes Apple, Google and Outlook move the appointment rather than quietly keep the old one. The port from Schedule Pro changed exactly one thing: the line folding walks back to a character boundary, so a service name with an accent in it no longer hands somebody's calendar a file it cannot read\n    * **Reminders that go once, whatever the store is running.** A day before and an hour before out of the box, as many or as few as a shop wants. The row saying a reminder went is claimed **before** the message is sent, not after \u2014 two workers that pick up the same booking race on one insert, one wins and sends and the other does nothing. The other way round has both of them send. An appointment that moves earns its reminders again, because the row is about the booking and its lead time and not about the time it is at\n    * **A thank-you afterwards, and nothing in it.** Two hours after the appointment ends, on by default, with a lower bound of a week so that switching it on does not mail everybody who has ever been to the shop. No discount code and no review request: those are a merchant's decision and belong in a campaign\n    * **Five messages to the shop, deduped by cause.** Created, waiting for a yes, cancelled, a class selling out, and the conflict \u2014 the last two of those loud, because a request nobody has answered and money arrived for a time the shop no longer has are the two where doing nothing costs something. Created, needs-approval and cancelled are one message per booking however many times a webhook is delivered; the conflict is one per **order**, because three late lines is one mess for one person and three high-priority messages about it is two too many. **A payload carries a reference, a service, a time and a status, and never a name, an email address or a telephone number** \u2014 these go to Slack and to somebody's phone, and there is a test asserting it on the key names\n    * **A customer with an appointment next week cannot be forgotten yet.** Core's erasure asks every add-on whether it holds anything, and this refuses until the appointment is over or called off: erasure rewrites a name in place, and doing it to somebody who is coming in on Thursday leaves the shop holding an hour for a person it can no longer ring. The refusal names the reference and the date and **never the service**, because a blocker's label goes on an admin screen and into a log. Once it is allowed, past bookings lose their notes and their form data and keep their times, because an hour the shop was busy is a fact about the shop\n    * **A front desk for the people at the counter, at `\/desk` on your own site.** A tablet app, signed in with the Login plugin, on its own screens: Today with what is next and who has arrived, a Diary you press and hold to drag an appointment to another time, and Find for when the telephone rings. One big button for whatever comes next \u2014 Check in, then Done \u2014 and the other six actions behind More, because a thumb finds one big button and hunts for six. The app is Svelte and the build output is committed, so an installed add-on needs no Node and no build step\n    * **The desk cannot leak a number it was never handed.** It is not the admin with the figures hidden: it is nine routes of its own that have never carried an order id, an order line, a total, a payment provider, a refund or a coupon, and a test walks every payload they can return looking for those keys. What a stylist may know is decided once, on the server, by which routes exist. Money on the tablet is one line and nothing to press \u2014 **Paid online**, **Deposit paid \u2014 $25.00 to pay today**, **Pay today: $80.00**, **Free** \u2014 and cancelling something that was paid for opens a refund request for a person to look at rather than moving anybody's money from a counter\n    * **Two permissions, and `kahunacart.bookings.desk.own` means one column.** A stylist linked to her bookable by the account field sees her own day and nothing else: the diary hands her one column whatever she asks for, an appointment that is not hers answers `404` with the same sentence an id that does not exist gets \u2014 so the desk never confirms somebody else's afternoon is there \u2014 and booking into another column is refused before anything is written rather than after. A desk account with nothing else ticked reaches no admin screen and no other API route, and there is a test that says so\n    * **Writes carry a desk nonce, because a session cookie on its own is not a defence.** The desk is authenticated by a cookie, and a cookie rides along with a cross-site request; `desk\/me` mints a nonce from the session and the username, every write sends it back in `X-Desk-Nonce`, and one without it is refused with a sentence telling the person to reload. Only a request that arrived with a session cookie is asked for one, so an API key or a token client needs nothing new. Admin Next was checked first and is not a precedent: it signs with a JWT and never rides the session\n    * **The sign-in page is the desk's own, not the shop's.** The Login plugin does the authenticating either way, but a tablet screwed to a counter should not get the shop header, the menu, the cookie banner and the footer on the way in. The whole app extends no theme template and loads no theme asset, so a shop that buys a new theme gets the same desk on Monday morning as it had on Friday night. It carries a web manifest and an icon, so it adds to a home screen and opens without browser furniture, and there is a Sign out within reach because the tablet is shared\n    * **Booking somebody in at the counter is allowed inside the shop's own notice period.** A customer ringing at ten to ask for eleven is exactly what a shop wants to say yes to, so the desk ignores minimum notice and the booking window \u2014 and nothing else. The conflict guard, the working hours, the time off and every need are checked exactly as they always are, and there is no flag anywhere in this add-on that turns those off\n    * **Arrivals are recorded.** A new `arrived_at` column, stamped by Check in, and only ever the first time \u2014 pressing it twice does not rewrite when somebody actually walked in. It is what lets Today say who is here and who is five minutes late without asking anybody to keep a list\n    * **An appointment no longer stands in its own way when you move it.** Pushing somebody back half an hour \u2014 the commonest thing a front desk does all day \u2014 was refused, because the availability check in front of the move counted the appointment's own claim as somebody else's. The store underneath had always excluded it when it wrote the new time; the check in front of it had never been told. Now it is, so a nudge shorter than the appointment's own length works, from the diary, from the sheet and from the admin\n    * **Classes and workshops, as a service in session mode.** A Tuesday evening bootcamp that repeats for eight weeks, a Saturday safety course with twelve seats, a colour workshop on one afternoon. It is an ordinary KahunaCart product with a recurrence rule on its booking rule, so its price, tax, picture, category, coupons and translations are the product's like anything else the shop sells \u2014 no second kind of record, no separate price, no separate catalogue. A date is identified by its own UTC start and there is no occurrence table: editing the rule changes next month's dates and leaves last month's bookings exactly where they were\n    * **Six o'clock stays six o'clock.** A weekly class is expanded from a **local** first date in the host's own timezone, so a Tuesday evening bootcamp is at six in the evening in March and at six in the evening in November and the clocks changing in between moves its UTC start by an hour instead of moving the class. Dates can be skipped or **moved to a different time**, both written against the day as a person says it, and both survive the clocks changing for the same reason\n    * **Sell each date, or sell the whole course.** One purchase buys a place on one date, or a place on every date that is left under one course number. The places left on a course is the fewest left on any of its dates, because a place on the course is a place on every date of it \u2014 and a course is held whole while somebody pays for it, so nobody is gazumped on week four while they are still typing their card number\n    * **The host cannot be in two places at once, on a day nobody has registered yet.** A class takes its trainer and its room for the hour the moment the merchant says it runs, which Schedule Pro never guaranteed \u2014 an event there blocked its staff member only through the bookings on it, so an empty class was an empty diary and the first person to find out was whoever turned up. There is no row behind any of it: the occurrences are expanded on demand and handed to the availability walk and the claim guard as the claims they both already read. Troubleshoot says **\"Trainer: teaching a class\"**, and a second class on the same person at the same time is refused when it is saved, naming the one it clashes with and the date it first happens on\n    * **One seat guard, not two.** Places on a class are counted off the same claims, under the same row lock, inside the same transaction as everything else this add-on writes \u2014 the guard from the release before this one, doing what it already did for a group service. A second guard on a different row would be two locks protecting one number, and the day they disagreed somebody would be standing in a room with no chair for them. There is a test with six real forked processes going for the last two places on a twelve-seat class: two win, four are told it is full, and the class sells exactly twelve\n    * **A full class takes names, and a place that comes free is offered rather than given.** A waitlisted booking has no order, no money and no seat, and joining is free even on a class that is otherwise paid for in the basket, because nothing is charged for a place in a queue. When a place opens up on a **free** class the first person waiting simply gets it. On a **paid** one they are offered it: a place is held in their name for twelve hours, they get a link that puts it in their basket, and paying turns the booking they already have into the confirmed one \u2014 same reference, one booking, not two. Schedule Pro confirmed these outright, which on a paid class hands somebody a seat they have not paid for. An offer that runs out goes to the next person, and the one who let it lapse keeps their place in the queue for the next. Three emails go with it \u2014 on the list, a place is being held for you, and the place is yours \u2014 and only the last carries a calendar invitation, because a place in a queue is not an appointment and a place held while somebody pays for it is not theirs yet\n    * **Registration deadlines, checked three times.** How many hours before a class the doors shut, honoured when somebody picks a date, when they add it to the basket and again at the checkout button \u2014 because a basket can sit open for an hour and a class two hours away with a two-hour deadline closes while it does. It reads **\"Registration for that date has closed. Pick another one.\"**, which is deliberately not the same sentence as a hold lapsing\n    * **The register, and calling a date off.** Who is coming on one date and who is waiting behind them, with Offer and Promote on each; the whole room marked as having come or not having come in one go, because thirty names should not be thirty clicks; and **Cancel this date**, which cancels everybody on it, lets the queue go and hands back the list of orders that were paid so a person can refund each one. It refunds nothing by itself, which is the same rule the rest of this add-on follows\n    * **`[kahunacart-sessions \/]` and `kahunacart_sessions()`** put the upcoming classes on any page or in any template, drawn on the server so the dates are in the HTML a search engine reads and still there with JavaScript switched off. Every date reads as one of three things and there is no fourth: places left with a Book button, full with a Join the waiting list button, or full or closed with no button at all \u2014 because offering one would be offering something the server is going to refuse\n    * **The owner's section, inside the KahunaCart admin.** Nine screens under a Bookings tab \u2014 no second app, no second login, and the same stylesheet, pills, row menus and empty states as every other screen in the admin. Core puts every add-on's section in its own band below its own screens, so Bookings does not sit between Orders and Customers; it is **first among the add-ons**, which is as near to Orders as core lets a section get. It is one plain JavaScript custom element with no imports and no build step, the way an add-on section is meant to be\n    * **An overview calendar, read-only on purpose.** A week draws a row per person, room and machine with their opening hours shaded, time off hatched and every booking sitting at the time it really occupies, in that person's own colour; a month draws a cell a day with a count, a bar of how full it is and a flag where something is waiting or clashing. Nothing on it can be dragged. Moving an appointment is a decision with a customer on the other end of it, and it is made on the booking's own screen where the thing being moved is named\n    * **Bookings in conflict have a row of their own.** A booking paid for after its time had gone holds nothing at all, so it belongs in nobody's column \u2014 it used to be on the calendar's numbers and on none of its squares. There is a **Holding nothing** row above everything else now, and it appears only when something is in it\n    * **Money is a link on every screen in this section, and never a figure.** The strip above the calendar counts bookings, how full the span is, who is waiting, the no-shows and the cancellations, and where takings would go there is a way through to core's own Reports. A booking's Payment panel says how it was paid for and links to the order. What was taken, what is owed and what a refund would do are the order's questions and the order's screen answers them; a copy here would be a second number to keep in step with the first\n    * **A booking in conflict is not offered Approve.** Approving one would confirm an appointment that holds no time, which fixes nothing. Moving it to a time that is free is the primary instead, and turning it down and handing the money back through the order is the other way out\n    * **Troubleshoot answers \"why can nobody book Thursday\" on a screen.** Pick a service, a day and optionally one person, and every start time the engine considered is listed with what stopped the ones it did not offer. It asks the engine the same question the storefront asks, so what it says is what the customer was told. Where a whole day was refused for one reason it is said once and the times fold away behind it, rather than printing fourteen identical cards\n    * **Four setup screens, and a service that is not set up says so.** Services lists every booking product with its rule and its options; an option with no rule of its own follows the product's, the way a price list does. People and places is who and what a booking occupies, with their hours, their timezone, the account they sign in with and the days they are away. Schedules is the named weekly hours with per-date overrides. Locations is where it all happens\n    * **The plugin's settings are drawn on its own Settings tab** rather than on a page of its own in the Grav admin, through core's settings home. On a core that does not host them the tab says so and links straight to the page that does\n    * **Two panels elsewhere in the admin, and both draw nothing when there is nothing.** An order that has a booking grows an Appointment panel beside its details; a customer who has ever booked grows a Bookings panel with their next appointment and how many they have had. An order without one and a customer who never booked grow nothing at all\n    * **Two dashboard alerts that land on the answer.** Bookings waiting for a yes, and bookings in conflict \u2014 each a link into the list already filtered to exactly the ones it is about, so \"three are waiting\" is one press from the three\n    * **Export CSV** gives whatever the filters leave on screen, in the same order, with the cells hardened so a reference that starts with an equals sign is not a formula when somebody opens it in a spreadsheet\n    * **Five new admin endpoints behind those screens** \u2014 the overview, troubleshoot, the CSV, the booking products and the site's accounts. The accounts one answers a username and a name and nothing else, because it exists so the admin can offer a picker rather than so anything can enumerate a site's users\n    * **The library it needs travels with it.** `rlanvin\/php-rrule` is what reads a repeat rule, and `vendor\/` was in `.gitignore` \u2014 so an add-on installed the way every customer installs one, as a folder of files, had no RRULE library at all. It is committed now, the runtime half of it only, and a test reads what is actually in the repository and fails if a dependency is added without its vendor folder or a development install is committed over the top\n    * **A missing part of this add-on is loud.** Reading a repeat without the library used to answer \"no dates\", which is exactly what a course that has finished looks like \u2014 a shop could lose its Tuesday evening class and have nothing anywhere to go on. Both places that expand a repeat now stop by name, with a sentence saying which library is missing and how to put it back, and the dashboard carries a red alert saying the same thing for somebody who does not read logs\n    * **The guest manage page serves itself, at `\/booking`.** It used to be a page the merchant had to make, and until they made one no message carried a link at all \u2014 so a guest, the one customer with no account to fall back on, got an email pointing at the account area. The shop answers that route itself now, out of the box, and every message carries the absolute `https:\/\/your-shop\/booking?token=\u2026` link for a guest and an account holder alike. It wears the shop's own theme, unlike the front desk, because it is a page a customer opens from an email. A page of the merchant's own at that route still wins and the `[kahunacart-booking-manage \/]` shortcode still works on it, and clearing the setting switches the whole thing off and leaves the old behaviour standing\n    * **The two-bookable race runs on MariaDB and PostgreSQL too, and so does a new one.** The lock ordering \u2014 ascending bookable id, which is what stops two bookings that both want Ann and the tub from each taking one and waiting for ever for the other \u2014 is the only part of the guard that can fail on one engine and not another, and it was only ever raced on SQLite, which serialises writers anyway and cannot show the bug. Both servers now run the two-bookable race, and a third arrangement where all six racers want the same two bookables and half of them name the two the other way round. Checked by breaking the guard on purpose: PostgreSQL killed a racer with a deadlock it could not retry its way out of, and the fixed guard is clean on three consecutive runs of all three engines\n    * **One place decides what goes in a calendar file.** The feed used to scrub control characters out of a customer's note itself, as a belt to the builder's braces, from back when a lone carriage return could still get past the escaping. The builder was fixed and the belt has come off with it, so a note is escaped in exactly one place and the feed is tested against that rather than against a second copy of the rule\n\n    * **A Sessions tab, because a class is not an appointment and should not read like one.** Every class the shop runs with the dates coming up and the places left on the next one; one class with every date it runs on, when registration shuts on each and which are taking names; and one date's **register** \u2014 who is coming, who is waiting and in what order, Offer the place, Give them the place, Take them off the queue, and marking the whole room as having come or not, because thirty names is not thirty clicks. **Calling a date off refunds nothing**, cancels everybody on it, lets the queue go, and then names every order that had money on it with a link to each, so a person refunds them where refunds live\n    * **The Services editor knows what a class is.** It had no mode control anywhere in it, so a class read as an appointment and the six columns that say *when it runs* could not be seen or set from any screen: the first date, how it repeats, the dates that are skipped or moved, the places on each, when registration shuts, the waiting list, and whether one purchase buys a date or the whole course. The first date is written and shown in the shop's own time and never converted, which is the whole reason a Tuesday evening class is still a Tuesday evening class in November. Turning an appointment into a class, or back, while people are still booked on it is refused with a sentence and a count rather than done quietly\n    * **Somebody waiting for a place had no buttons at all.** None of the states a booking's record knew about was a waitlisted one, so the screen drew the status correctly and then offered nothing \u2014 not even a way off the queue. It offers the place, gives the place, takes them off, and links to the whole register for their date\n    * **The calendar link and the outside calendars are on the person's own screen now.** Make a link, copy it, make a new one with a confirm that says in plain words that the old one stops working, take it away, and choose what it says about each booking for this one person. Underneath it, the calendars that block their time: each one with its host and the last six characters of its address \u2014 never the address again, because it is a password that happens to look like a link \u2014 when it last answered, a Test that fetches it there and then and prints what came back, and a box to paste a replacement into\n    * **The Troubleshoot screen names what is in the way.** A class says *which* class (\"Teaching Tuesday bootcamp\") and an outside calendar says *which* calendar (\"busy on Ann's Google calendar\"), because a stylist with three feeds and two classes needs to know which one took the evening, not that something did\n    * **The front desk button comes from the store's settings.** It used to guess the address and ask the server whether anything was there, which was wrong twice over: a shop that had moved the desk got a button to a page that is not there, and a shop that had switched the desk off still got the button\n\n    * **Five businesses, each one command.** `seed salon`, `groomer`, `trainer`, `massage`, `range` \u2014 and `all`, which writes the five and then a fortnight of diary across them. Each writes its own products, people, hours, rules and needs and shares nothing with the others, everything in the **store's** timezone, and every one of them is idempotent: running it twice changes nothing, because everything is found by slug before it is written. Between them they cover what the model is actually for \u2014 a colour that waits for a yes, a groom that needs a person and a tub, a course sold whole, a treatment that asks for a deposit, and ten range lanes as one record with ten units on it\n    * **`seed diary` puts a fortnight on the board.** A week behind and a fortnight ahead, in every status a shop actually sees: one waiting for a yes, one that arrived after its time had gone and holds nothing, one part paid with a balance owed, a class full with two people waiting, and somebody away next week. It is what makes a screenshot, a demo and an afternoon of testing possible without booking forty things by hand, and the times come out of the same availability walk the storefront uses, so nothing in it is a time the shop would not have offered\n    * **A whole documentation set, written for the person who cuts hair rather than the person who wrote the plugin.** Twenty-two pages under `docs\/`: getting started in ten minutes, the same ten minutes written five times over for a salon, a groomer, a trainer, a clinic and a range \u2014 each one matching its seed recipe step for step \u2014 every setup screen, the front desk, a single page to print and stick by the tablet, payments, the messages, the storefront, calendar sync, twenty-four things that can go wrong with the sentence you are looking at, and the two reference pages for anybody building on it\n    * **One way of writing a time, and one setting to change it.** A time of day is written `6:00pm` \u2014 minutes always, because `6 pm` reads well in a sentence and badly in a column \u2014 and a shop that thinks in 24-hour time says so once under **How times are written** and gets `18:00` everywhere instead. Everywhere is the whole of it: the storefront picker, the basket line, the receipt, the account, the eight emails, the calendar message, the admin diary and its hour gutter, Troubleshoot's reasons, the panels beside an order and a customer, the front desk, the `slots` command and the export. There is one formatter on the server and one in each front end, each fed the setting by the server rather than guessing at the browser's locale, so the owner reading the diary from an airport sees the same clock the customer got in their email\n    * **The picker is something a page of your own can be written against.** A salon's own site with a Book button that opens a dialog used to have nothing to hold: no event said a booking had been made, so the dialog could not close itself; closing it could not give the held time back; and a picker put in the page after load could only be started by re-appending the script tag. Now there are five bubbling `CustomEvent`s on the mount point \u2014 `kahunacart-booking:ready`, `:held` with the expiry, `:booked` with the reference and whether it is still waiting for a yes, `:released` and `:error` \u2014 and one namespace, `window.KahunaCartBookings`, with `mount(element)` to boot a picker whenever it arrived and `release(element)` to hand a held time back the moment a dialog closes. The customer's manage token is never in any of it. The two-column layout now follows the **picker's** own width rather than the window's, so a picker in a 480px dialog on a wide screen stacks instead of overflowing, `{{ kahunacart_booking('cut', { boot: false }) }}` renders one as a template a page can copy rather than as a picker that draws itself into hidden markup, and `Seed\\Catalog::product()` takes a `visibility`, so a showcase can seed its services `unlisted` in one write. `docs\/storefront.md` has the whole contract with an example\n    * **A third kind of booking can come from another plugin.** Appointments and classes are Bookings' own and are unchanged in every way; beside them there is now one narrow seam another plugin registers a **mode** through \u2014 a way of booking with its own idea of what a unit of time is, its own rules table, its own availability payload and its own words for a booking. A mode answers one method with an offer, and that offer goes into exactly the same claim every appointment goes through: the same write lock in ascending order, the same sweep, the same recheck, the same unique key. Nothing a mode returns can bypass the double-booking guard, and there is no way to ask it to. It touches the service editor, the picker's availability, the hold, booking straight away, booking at the front desk, moving a booking, Troubleshoot, the reason codes, the cart line, the receipt, the emails, the calendar invitation, the manage page, the account tab, the admin record and the desk \u2014 and every one of those is exactly what it was for a service that is not one. A calendar invitation can now be dates rather than instants, which is what a week in a cottage is. `nightly` stops being a word refused by name and becomes the first registered mode, in the KahunaCart Rentals add-on; a service set to a mode nothing installed provides is still listed and still editable, and is refused at the picker and at the cart door with a sentence naming the add-on that would bring it, the way core treats a product of a type nothing declares. A word that is neither built in nor registered is now refused when it is saved rather than quietly turned into an appointment. `docs\/development.md` has the whole contract with a mode in about two hundred lines beside it\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A class could not be bought from its own page.** Availability answered a class the way it answers a haircut \u2014 every half hour its trainer was free \u2014 and every one of those times was then refused at the hold with \"The class does not run on that date\", so the Book button on the classes list led to a page that could not take the booking. It answers the dates the class runs on now, one time on each, with the places left on it, and a date that is full or whose registration has shut is not offered at all\n    * **The times a shop offers are its own, whatever moment it is asked from.** Candidate starts used to be counted out from the instant the availability walk was handed, so a question asked at twenty past nine was answered with twenty past nine, ten to ten, twenty past ten. The seeded diary hit it twice, and so \u2014 every time, unnoticed \u2014 did any customer whose own midnight falls in the middle of the shop's working day: a customer in Mumbai and a customer in Denver were offered the same salon's times half an hour out of step with each other. The grid belongs to the shop's opening hours now and the window only filters, which also closes a way the front desk could book a minute the shop never offers\n    * **A customer offered a place on a paid class can take it.** The route that hands the held place over had been there since the classes leaf and nothing in the front end called it, so the email told people to open a link and put the place in their basket, and the page it opened drew nothing at all. It draws it now: what is being held, until when in their own timezone, what the whole thing costs, and one button that hands the hold to their browser and drops the line in the shop's own basket. Paying turns the booking they already have into the confirmed one \u2014 same reference, one booking, not two. A place with no offer on it says where they stand in the queue and lets them leave it, and leaving gives back a place that was being held for them instead of letting it sit there for twelve hours. A place that has already been offered and lapsed says so. The Bookings tab in the account says all of the same things, and a place on a waiting list is listed under what is coming up rather than under what has already happened\n    * **A whole course reads as a whole course.** Eight Tuesdays bought in one purchase is one order line and eight bookings, and the receipt drew the one the line pointed at \u2014 so the customer who had just bought eight evenings was shown the first Tuesday and nothing else. It is one card saying *8 Tuesdays, 6:00pm, the first on 22 September*, on the receipt and in the order email, with a link to where every date is listed and each one moved or called off on its own. The weekday is only named when every date falls on one; a course that jumps around says how many dates instead. A guest's own booking page now lists the whole course too, each date with its own status and its own link, which is where that link lands when there is no account behind it\n    * **A class's own page reads like a class.** It used to draw an appointment's furniture \u2014 \"60 minutes \u00b7 $160\" over a month calendar with four of its thirty squares live and \"Pick a day to see the times\" underneath \u2014 for an eight-week bootcamp. It lists the dates the class runs on now, the next two months of them, each row saying when it is, how many places are left and a button to take one; the line under the name says what one purchase actually buys, \"for the whole course, whichever date you start on\" or \"for one date\"; and there is a **How many places** box above the dates. That number is now the number the shop is asked about, which it never was \u2014 a date with two places left used to say \"Book it\" to a party of three and then refuse the hold. A full date says **Full** and offers **Join the waiting list** where the class keeps one, and is left off the page where it does not, because a button that cannot do anything is worse than no button\n    * **A booking that is for more than one person can say so.** A range lane takes four, a studio takes six, a tasting room takes eight \u2014 and there was nowhere to write that down, so the range's own documentation told merchants to put it in the product description and hope. Services now have **Party size up to**, the picker asks **How many people** when it is more than one, and the number rides on the booking, the basket line (*Thu 25 Sep, 2:00pm \u00b7 3 people*), the receipt, the emails, the admin record and the front desk (*Lane hire \u00b7 3 people*). It changes nothing the engine counts: four friends hiring a lane hire **one lane**, and a range with ten of them still sells ten at two o'clock. That is the difference between a party and a class, and confusing the two is what made the first draft of the range recipe cap the whole range at four hires an hour. Per-person pricing is deliberately not part of it: a lane costs what a lane costs whoever is standing on it\n    * **The places a customer asked for never reached the question.** The picker's own box asked how many, and then asked the shop what was free for **one** \u2014 so a class with two places left drew a Book button for a party of three and refused the hold a moment later. The number is part of the availability question now, and the page draws its button off the answer\n    * **A service whose lengths are an option had no names on it.** Build \"30 min \/ 60 min\" as a product option, press Generate, and core writes the variants with no title of their own \u2014 the name lives on the option values and is worked out when it is printed. This add-on read the title straight off the row, so the Bookings \u2192 Services list showed two blank rows to write a rule against, the storefront picker offered two blank buttons at two different prices, the front desk's service list had two blank options on it, and every email about the appointment named the service and nothing else. Only the seeded shops looked right, because a seed writes the titles out by hand. There is one answer to \"what is this variant called\" now, it is core's own rule, and every screen asks it\n    * **A class on a pool everything else uses took a lane per player.** A class writes one block claiming the units its needs declared \u2014 four of the venue's eight lanes \u2014 and every registration then writes a claim of its own carrying the places it took, so seven people on league night read as eleven lanes gone out of eight and Tuesday between 7:00pm and 9:00pm had nothing left to hire at all. The number that decided it was how many had signed up, which is not a number the merchant set anywhere. A class takes exactly what it declared now, for its whole span, whether twelve people are coming or nobody is; the other four lanes are there to be hired, a hire of five is refused and what it is told names the class \u2014 **\"Lanes: teaching Ironbark League\"** \u2014 rather than the unhelpful \"booked\". Nothing about the class's own places changed: they are still counted one per person against its capacity. It only ever bit a class sharing a pool, which is the ordinary case for a venue and no case at all for a salon\n    * **A page of your own can dress the month grid and say things in its own words.** Round days were written into the stylesheet rather than taken from a token, so setting a radius to zero still got circles, and a free day's ten per cent tint of the accent is nothing at all on a dark panel \u2014 the first host page built against this overrode nine rules to get a legible calendar. The month grid reads `--kc-day-radius`, `--kc-day-free-bg`, `--kc-day-free-border`, `--kc-day-off-opacity` and the rest now, its own rules are one class each so a host's two-class rule wins whatever order the stylesheets loaded in, a shut day clears 3:1 on a dark ground and on white, and `--kc-card`, `--kc-input` and `--kc-surface` fall back to a faint tint of the text colour rather than to `transparent`. `kahunacart_booking()` and the shortcode also take `places_first`, which puts **How many people** above the option box for a business whose first question is the party, and `i18n`, which hands one picker its own words for as many strings as the page names \u2014 so a venue whose two options are already called *An hour* and *Ninety minutes* can empty `minutes` and have the picker leave the digits out rather than say the same thing twice. And \"We will email you as soon as the shop says yes\" is now \"as soon as it is confirmed\", which is true of a venue, a clinic and a studio as well as a shop\n    * **The picker asks how many people first, and names the option it is pricing.** Three decisions about what every shop gets out of the box rather than what a page of somebody's own can ask for. **How many of you** is now above the length box, because how many are coming is the thing somebody already knows when they open the page and the length is what they work out from it \u2014 a page that really does sell a length first writes `places_first: false` and gets the old order. The line under the service's name says which option is being priced, so a groomer reads *Large dog \u00b7 90 minutes \u00b7 $80.00* rather than *90 minutes \u00b7 $80.00* with nothing saying which dog; a service with one option is left exactly as it was, because one option is not a choice anybody made and its name is very often the product's own. And a service can say **the option names already say how long**, which takes the digits out for a venue whose two options are called *An hour* and *Ninety minutes* \u2014 a tick on the rule rather than a guess at English, off out of the box so nothing ever loses its length by accident, and it works on the shipped product page where there is no template of yours to put an override in. The approval sentence and its neighbours lost the word shop while we were in there: a status now reads *Waiting to be confirmed*, a cutoff says *Give us a ring*, and a clinic or a studio reading any of them is not being told it is a shop\n    * **A booking mode names its own price, and the store still owns the money.** A mode quoting a week in a beach house answers with a figure and the rows behind it, Bookings hands both to core through its line-price contract, and core makes the line: the unit price is the quote, the breakdown prints under the product name, the quantity box is gone and a coupon skips the rows that say they are not to be discounted. **Nothing here computes or stores what was paid.** An appointment and a class are untouched by every word of this \u2014 they are still priced by the product and its option the way they always were, and there are tests asserting the same line data, the same events and the same totals as before\n    * **The price a guest was shown is the price they pay.** The quote is frozen onto the hold the moment they take it, and the cart reads back the frozen figure rather than asking again \u2014 so an owner raising the rate while somebody is at the checkout does not reprice the basket under them. The hold is the credential for it as well: a line pointing at a hold that has lapsed, that belongs to another browser, or that was taken for a different product is refused by name, and so is a hold carrying no price the store will take \u2014 because a line nobody prices is a line charged at the catalogue price, and the catalogue price of a holiday cottage is one night. A request that names no visitor at all now only gets a hold that names none either, so clearing a cookie and guessing an id buys nobody somebody else's week. A mode's booking can only ever be bought one at a time, because a quantity of two on a line priced for exactly one set of dates is two things sold and one delivered\n    * **Paying a deposit now and the rest later, decided by the booking and carried out by the store.** A mode says \"30% down, the rest a fortnight before arrival\" and Bookings answers core's payment-schedule contract with one figure for the whole order: its total, less what the bookings on it defer. Always a figure and never a percentage \u2014 the store applies a percentage to the **order**, so a $1,125 stay at 30% with $200 of sales tax on it would ask for $406.50 today while the guest had been quoted $337.50 for the stay, and the same stay beside a t-shirt would ask for a different number again. One rule means the same stay contributes the same amount whatever else is in the basket. A basket nobody asked to defer gets no schedule at all, the checkout page gets the same answer the payment does, and the listener never throws: a refusal is a line in the log and a normally-paid order, not a checkout that will not finish\n    * **A deposit confirms the booking, and giving it all back releases the dates.** Core announces a part payment instead of a payment, so without this a stay bought with 30% down would sit pending for ever \u2014 and refunding the whole of a deposit is, as far as the order is concerned, a *partial* refund, which used to leave a confirmed cottage held for somebody who had paid nothing. The receipt, the order email, the account tab and the manage page all say what has been paid, what is still owed and the date it is owed by, in a sentence rather than a table \u2014 *Paid so far $760.20 \u00b7 Balance $1,773.80 due by Sat 25 Jul 2026* \u2014 and the front desk's one money line grew a date with it: **Deposit paid \u2014 $1,773.80 due by 25 Jul**\n    * **A balance that goes unpaid is a person's decision, not an automatic cancellation.** When core says a balance is overdue it goes on the booking's own trail, the owner gets a high-priority message with what is owed and how late it is, and the booking's mode is asked whether it has anything to add. Nothing is cancelled, nothing is refunded and nobody's dates are given away \u2014 the shop has already asked the customer once and what happens next is theirs to choose\n    * **Cancelling asks the booking what it is worth back.** A mode with a cancellation policy is handed what the shop is holding **against that booking's own line** \u2014 so two stays in one basket is two answers rather than one offer of the whole basket \u2014 answers with a figure, and the refund request a person opens carries that figure in its own sentence. The amount goes on the booking's trail whatever anybody does with the request afterwards. A mode with no policy, an appointment and a class are exactly as they were: the request is opened with no number on it, because what a customer gets back has always been the merchant's call\n    * **Moving a booking a mode priced is refused when the new dates cost something different.** The order was taken for one figure and a guest cannot move themselves onto a more expensive week for free, so the answer is a sentence with a person in it \u2014 *Those dates come to a different amount from the ones you booked. Get in touch with us and we will sort it out for you.* Dates at the same price move as normal, and an appointment moves the way it always has\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N4: the owner's screens and the desk \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **An add-on can draw its own screens inside the Bookings section.** Two named slots, and nothing else: a panel in the service editor that appears the moment the way of booking is set to one that add-on brought, and a tab of its own in the strip along the top. The add-on declares where a file is and which custom element it defines; the section fetches it through its own authenticated route, waits for the definition, and draws it with the store's own stylesheets handed over so it looks like the rest of the admin rather than like a plugin. A script that will not load leaves one sentence naming the add-on that should have drawn there \u2014 never an empty panel, because an empty panel is a screen nobody can debug. The service editor drops the questions that mean nothing to a mode \u2014 how long it takes, start times every so often, buffers \u2014 and keeps the ones that still do: notice, how far ahead, workflow, where, hours and who or what it needs. What the panel holds rides out on Bookings' own Save, in the same transaction as the service row\n    * **A booking that runs over days is drawn as one.** A mode can now say that its bookings are runs of dates rather than times of day, and saying so is all it takes: the admin's overview gains a **Stays** view \u2014 bookables down the side, a month across, one bar per booking, drawn from the middle of the day somebody arrives to the middle of the day they leave so that a changeover day reads as one bar ending and another beginning rather than as a clash \u2014 and the front desk gains the same strip over a fortnight or a month. Nothing in either of them knows what a night is: the dates, the words and the noun all come from the booking's own mode, so a cottage counts nights, a kayak counts days and neither says the other's word. \"How full\" changes with the view, because dates taken out of dates there were is the number an owner wants and a percentage of opening hours is not \u2014 a holiday let has no opening hours\n    * **The front desk knows who is arriving and who is leaving.** Today opens on two lists above everything else, with the check-in time, the nights, the guests and what is still owed on each row, and the button on a stay says **Check in** and then **Check out** rather than Start and Complete. Troubleshoot answers over a **range** of dates for a booking of that kind, one row a date with the mode's own reason beside it, rather than asking about one day at a time. A booking's own mode puts the dates into words everywhere else too: on the list, on the record, on the panels beside an order and a customer, and on the sheet at the desk\n    * **A mode can teach Bookings its own messages.** A registered mode can add messages of its own \u2014 a note before somebody arrives, a thank-you after they leave \u2014 and they go out through Bookings' own mailer, which means the order's language, the customer lookup that respects an erasure, the audit trail, the order's timeline and the claim that stops two workers sending the same thing twice. A mode says which stays are due one by asking Bookings for the ones starting or ending in a window, so the sweeping and the timing are the mode's own business and the sending is not\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N4 \u2500\u2500 -->\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N3: the range picker \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A booking mode can bring its own picker.** The storefront picker draws appointments and classes itself, and now stops at anything else and hands the mount element to a **face** the mode's own plugin registered \u2014 `window.KahunaCartBookings.registerFace('nightly', factory)` \u2014 with everything the built-in two use: the rule and the options as the services route answered them, the mode's own translated words, how this shop writes a time of day, the availability payload, holding and booking and releasing, the countdown, the store's own Add to cart with its six line keys spelled in one place, the `--kc-*` tokens and the same five host-page events. A face never opens a route of its own, never learns a line-data key and never reaches back into the picker. A service whose mode nothing installed can draw says so in a sentence \u2014 the server's own, naming the add-on that would bring the calendar \u2014 and leaves Add to cart hidden rather than inviting somebody to press it so the shop can refuse them\n    * **A face's script and stylesheet reach the page from the server.** `onKahunaCartBookingsRegisterPickerFaces` hands a mode's plugin a registry to append a script, a stylesheet and its own translated strings to, and they go out beside the picker's own two files \u2014 through Grav's asset pipeline on the shop's routes and as tags beside the calendar anywhere else, because a page's processed content is cached and anything a shortcode enqueued is missing on the second request. Both deferred and the face's second, so a face is never run by a browser that has not got `registerFace` yet. A mode's words travel under `<mode>.<key>` and the picker takes the prefix off, so two modes may both have an `arrival` and neither can take the other's\n    * **A mode that books whole days is held by its dates rather than by a start time.** Check-in is a wall time where the property is, and a browser working out the instant for itself is how a calendar ends up a day out twice a year \u2014 so a registered mode may be posted a hold with the dates it deals in and no `start`, and computes the instant on the server in the property's own zone. An appointment and a class are still held at a start time, required by name, word for word as before\n    * **Moving one of them may change its length.** A reschedule now carries whatever else the caller posted through to the mode, under the five things Bookings decides for itself \u2014 so a guest moving a week in a cottage picks a new arrival and a new departure rather than shifting a fixed span, and a caller still cannot say which booking is being moved, who it is with or how many places it takes. The manage page hands a registered mode's booking to its own face, and prints the mode's own words for when a stay is \u2014 *Wed 21 Oct to Tue 27 Oct, 6 nights* \u2014 where it used to print a start time and a finish time with no week between them\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N3 \u2500\u2500 -->\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N7: the follow-up \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A booking that lasts days can be taken at the front desk.** Somebody rings the owner and wants the second week of June, and until now there was no way to write it down: the sheet asked for a time of day, a service booked from a date to a date offered none, and Book it never lit up. The New booking sheet now asks a service of that kind for the day they arrive, the day they leave and how many people, and prints what the mode says it comes to: the dates in its own words, the check-in and check-out lines, every row behind the price and the total, all of it exactly as the server gave it and none of it worked out on the tablet. A refusal is the mode's own sentence \u2014 *3 nights minimum*, *Arrivals on Saturdays*, *That would leave nights nobody could book* \u2014 because a counter told \"that time has just been taken\" about a minimum stay rings the owner. Everything goes through the same conflict guard every other door does, and a desk user who only works her own column is still told whose it is before anything is written\n    * **And the desk says what there is to collect.** A booking taken at the counter is the no-order path: **no payment schedule is opened, no balance job is queued and there is no order** \u2014 the whole amount is taken across the counter when the guest turns up, and the sheet and the docs say so in those words. So the quote the mode gave is written down at the moment it is given, the same way it is already written onto a hold, and the desk reads it back as **To collect $1,714.00**. It is its own word rather than \"Pay today\", which about a fortnight in June would have a receptionist asking for it in March. A booking nobody quoted \u2014 seeded into the diary, imported from an outside calendar \u2014 still says nothing at all about money, because a number that is wrong by a factor of the nights is worse than no number, and so does one whose add-on has been switched off since\n    * **How wide the picker may get is a token.** `--kc-picker-max` on anything wrapping the picker, defaulting to the 44rem it always was. It is the one dimension a page argues with: 44rem is right beside a product's price and dead space in a section of a page's own, and until now the only way round it was an override with three classes in it. Setting it to `none` also lets the panel's own 900px container query put the summary beside the months instead of under them\n    * **A mode's panel in the service editor opens on what is actually saved.** `GET setup\/services` answers each service with the mode's own fields on it, so the component mounted in the editor is handed the values the contract promises it rather than an empty object. Anything that trusted the contract was drawing its own defaults over a merchant's settings and only got away with it by fetching its own rule behind the section's back\n    * **The picker inside a buy form brings its own script and stylesheet.** On the shop's own product page they arrive through Grav's pipeline and nothing changes. On a page of your own that draws the store's buy fields itself, the markup used to land with no JavaScript behind it and simply do nothing \u2014 the only way to get them was to render an availability calendar you might not want. Whatever emits the field now makes sure the assets are there, once\n    * **The front desk can be narrowed to the part of the shop somebody works in.** A store with a salon, a lane hire and a holiday cottage on it is one diary with all of them in one flat row: six stylists, two rooms, a tub, ten lanes and a cottage, and the only way to get to the salon was to tap six names one at a time. There is now a row above the names for what kind of thing it is \u2014 People, Rooms and spaces, Equipment, Properties, the same four words People and places uses \u2014 and a row of places under it when the shop has more than one address. Each is drawn only when there is more than one to choose between, and each is drawn from the whole list rather than from the diary's answer, so narrowing to one person can never hide the way back out. What you picked is remembered with the view and the names, and somebody who only works her own column sees none of it\n    * **And the Stays view is a picture of stays again.** It was drawing a row for every bookable in the shop and a round dot with one letter in it for every appointment, nine of them stacked in a Tuesday under one stylist's name. Rows are now only the things one of the shop's own day-unit services actually needs \u2014 asked of the services and their needs, never of the word \"property\" \u2014 and bars are only bookings that last longer than a day. The Stays button is not drawn at all in a shop that lets nothing by the day. The day and week grids do the opposite and leave those things out to begin with, because a cottage booked for five nights would otherwise be one block sitting in a Tuesday column at check-in time; tap its kind, or its name, and it comes back\n    * **`seed diary` says what is missing instead of saying `skipped`.** Two of its bookings go through core's own checkout, and both of them quietly skipped on almost every store \u2014 one because deposits are switched off, which is a setting and not a fault, and one because the cart door refused the hold the seed had just minted. Now each says which: *skipped \u2014 this store takes the whole amount at checkout. Switch on checkout.deposits.enabled to see a part-paid one*, or *skipped \u2014 there is no free time in the week after next to put it in*. The refusal is fixed as well: the console counts as the shop's own staff, because somebody with a shell on the server is further inside the shop than `admin.super` is, so the seed can buy its own hold and the diary gets its paid booking back\n    * **A booking made at the counter could be sold twice on MySQL.** Found by racing a stay taken at the front desk against a week somebody was holding in a basket: on MariaDB two of the six won and the house was let twice, on SQLite and PostgreSQL one did. The guard was right and its lock was right \u2014 what was wrong was the **order of two statements**. MySQL's default isolation fixes a transaction's whole view of the world at its first plain `SELECT`, so reading how many units a booking wants *before* taking the write lock left the re-check reading the world as it was before this booking queued for the lock \u2014 blind to the claim the racer ahead of it had just committed. Both wrote. Every read in the guard now happens after the lock, a caller that looks something up before claiming takes the lock itself first, and there is a test on the order of the statements as well as the race, because a bug that only appears on one engine under load is not one a test that passes locally will ever find again. It reached anything that books without going through a basket: the front desk, a merchant booking somebody in, a booking being moved, and a place given away on a full class\n<!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N7 \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **An account that may only look is no longer shown buttons it cannot use.** Somebody with `kahunacart.bookings.view` and not `kahunacart.bookings.manage` was shown Add somebody or something, New hours, Add a place, every row's Edit, Switch off and Remove, the approve, move and cancel buttons on a booking, the register's Everybody came and Call this date off, and every editor's Save, and the server refused each one with a 403. The settings answer now says `can_manage`, worked out through the same check the writes go through, and the section leaves out everything that writes for somebody who may only look. They still read every screen, open every booking and every editor \u2014 with the boxes read-only \u2014 and export the list. The order panel's Cancel it follows the same answer on the bookings list, and a component another add-on mounts in the section is handed `canManage` on its context\n    * **A store with deposits off no longer hears from Bookings about them.** A booking that asked for a deposit used to answer core's payment schedule question even when `checkout.deposits.enabled` was off, and core, which charges the whole order with the switch off, ignored the answer and wrote a line in the log naming this plugin for every checkout page and every order. Bookings now stays quiet while deposits are off and answers as before the moment they are switched on."}}},"kahunacart-rentals":{"name":"KahunaCart Rentals","version":"1.0.5","description":"Nightly stays and day rentals for KahunaCart, on top of the Bookings add-on","description_html":"<p>Nightly stays and day rentals for KahunaCart, on top of the Bookings add-on<\/p>","description_plain":"Nightly stays and day rentals for KahunaCart, on top of the Bookings add-on","icon":"bed","author":{"name":"Trilby Media, LLC","email":"hello@trilby.media"},"dependencies":[{"name":"grav","version":">=2.0.0"},{"name":"kahunacart","version":">=1.2.11"},{"name":"kahunacart-bookings","version":">=1.0.0"}],"compatibility":{"grav":["2.0"]},"homepage":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/rentals","docs":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/docs\/rentals\/overview","keywords":"grav, premium, commerce, ecommerce, rentals, nightly, stays, holiday let, vacation rental, bnb, campsite, bookings","bugs":"https:\/\/github.com\/getgrav\/grav-premium-issues\/labels\/kahunacart-rentals","license":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/premium\/license","tag_name":"1.0.5","date":"2026-10-02T01:05:18Z","release_type":"stable","zipball_url":"https:\/\/getgrav.org\/download\/plugins\/kahunacart-rentals\/1.0.5","repository":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/add-ons\/rentals","premium":{"vendor":"KahunaCart","validator":"kahunacart","license_product":"kahunacart-rentals","checkout_url":"https:\/\/kahunacart.com\/shop\/product\/kahunacart-rentals","button":"Buy Now","filename":"grav-plugin-kahunacart-rentals"},"slug":"kahunacart-rentals","install_path":"user\/plugins\/kahunacart-rentals","content_sha256":"f41cbecf49056dd54bb988dac8c02a361991fc278d91655f0889d3e6c47d243a","changelog":{"1.0.5":{"date":"10\/01\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * Help text on the rental rates and property screens now follows the admin's Help text setting, showing below the label or in a tooltip behind an info icon\n"},"1.0.4":{"date":"09\/26\/2026","content":"1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **Seeding a rental works again with Bookings 1.1.1.** Bookings 1.1.1 gave its seed recipes an optional clock of their own, under the same name the rental recipes already used for theirs, and PHP refuses to set that property twice. So `bin\/plugin kahunacart-rentals seed` and every showcase built on a rental recipe stopped with \"Cannot modify readonly property\". The rental recipes now keep their clock under a name of their own and work with any Bookings version\n"},"1.0.3":{"date":"09\/23\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The test suite runs against KahunaCart 1.3, which bundles brick\/money and YetiSearch under its own namespace.** When it borrows the base plugin's dependencies it now also loads core's `vendor-prefixed\/autoload.php`, and Bookings is still borrowed alongside it as before. Against an older KahunaCart without that file nothing changes. Nothing in Rentals itself names brick\/money or YetiSearch, so the add-on needs no other change\n"},"1.0.2":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **A monthly rate.** A property let by the month can now say so. A new **Monthly rate** on the booking rule, under **Who it sleeps, and what they pay**, prices a stay of a month or more a whole month at a time, counted from the arrival date, and the nights after the last whole month by the rate card as before. 15 January to 20 March at $1,200 a month and $60 a night is two months and five nights, $2,700, and the receipt says \"2 months at $1,200.00 a month\" and then \"5 nights\". A month is the same date in the next month and the 31st stays the 31st where it can, so from 31 January one month is 28 February and two is 31 March. **A rate band does not reach inside a month**, because a monthly price is the price for the month whatever season it is in, and bands only change the leftover nights. A stay shorter than a month, and every property with no monthly rate, is priced exactly as it was. The cleaning fee, extra guests, the deposit, the balance date and cancellation work as they do on any stay, and the quote label says \"2 months and 5 nights\" instead of 64 nights. `monthly_minor` on the rule, the API and the MCP tools, and \"Month 1\" beside each night in `bin\/plugin kahunacart-rentals nights --quote`; migration rentals_0003. A property's longest stay still defaults to 28 nights, so raise it, or set it to 0, before letting by the month. See [Rates and seasons \u2192 A monthly rate](rates-and-seasons.md#a-monthly-rate)\n"},"1.0.1":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#improved)\n    * **The \"Deposits are off\" note points at the right tab.** KahunaCart 1.2.13 moves **Deposits and part payment** from **Settings \u2192 Security** to **Settings \u2192 Commerce**, so the note under a property's **Paying** box and the [Payments and cancellation](payments-and-cancellation.md) page now send you to **KahunaCart \u2192 Settings \u2192 Commerce**. On an older KahunaCart the switch is still under Security\n"},"1.0.0":{"date":"09\/21\/2026","content":"1. [](#new)\n    * **KahunaCart Rentals, the add-on.** Nightly stays and day rentals, on top of KahunaCart Bookings. A holiday let, a cabin, a B&B room, a campsite pitch, a kayak for the afternoon and a camera kit for three days are all the same thing \u2014 something booked by the day rather than by the hour \u2014 and this is where that lives. Bookings keeps everything that is true of any booking: bookables, needs, claims, holds, the double-booking guard, orders, deposits, emails with calendar entries, the manage page and the front desk. This keeps what is only true of a stay. It installs, migrates clean, and answers \"which nights of August can the beach house be slept in, which days can a stay start or end on, and what do the 9th to the 16th cost for six people\"\n    * **A night is named by the date it starts on, in the property's own timezone.** The night of the 9th is the night you go to bed on the 9th and get up on the 10th, and a stay from the 9th to the 16th is seven nights with the 16th a departure rather than a night. Everything else falls out of saying it that way: the claim runs from check-in on the arrival date to check-out on the departure date, four in the afternoon to ten in the morning, and the six hours in between belong to nobody\n    * **The shared changeover day, which is the case home-made calendars get wrong.** One party leaves the 10th at ten in the morning and another arrives the 10th at four in the afternoon. Both hold a real claim, the two claims do not touch, and the date shows as both a departure and an arrival. It is not an edge case: on a property that is doing well it is what every Saturday in August looks like\n    * **Five states per date, not two.** A rental calendar is not a grid of free and busy squares. A date carries two facts at once \u2014 whether you could sleep there tonight and whether you could have slept there last night \u2014 and the four combinations of those are the square, the square with a diagonal one way, the square with a diagonal the other way and the filled square that every rental site in the world draws. `free`, `arrival_only`, `departure_only`, `taken`, and `blocked` for a property that is shut rather than booked\n    * **Clocks going forward and back stop being interesting.** Every conversion happens through the property's own zone on the date in question, never by adding 86,400 to yesterday's answer \u2014 so the night before the clocks go forward is seventeen hours, the night before they go back is nineteen, a calendar day is 23 or 25, and a week across either is still a week. Tested in Denver, in Berlin and in Auckland, which is east of UTC and over the date line and where the transitions happen the other way round\n    * **What a run of nights costs, band by band.** A rate card is dated bands laid over the property's own price, and they overlap on purpose: \"Winter\" across five months with \"Christmas week\" laid over the top of it is how anybody would write it down, and the higher position wins per night. A band can be a price a night or a percentage of the property's own price, can charge a different rate for Friday and Saturday nights, and can carry a minimum stay and a set of arrival days that override the property's for a stay starting inside it \u2014 which is how \"seven nights, Saturday to Saturday, in August\" is one band with two numbers on it. **A property priced one way all year needs no bands at all**: a night no band covers costs the option's own price, which is the number the merchant already typed into the shop\n    * **The orphan-night rule, on by default.** A stay that would leave a run of free nights too short for anybody to book is refused rather than sold, because a calendar full of two-night gaps under a three-night minimum is accurate and unsellable. It looks both ways, it is fine with no gap at all, and it names the night that would have been stranded \u2014 \"that would leave nights nobody could book \u2014 nobody could book the night of Wed 12 Aug\" \u2014 because \"no\" on its own is what makes an owner turn a good rule off\n    * **Airbnb, VRBO and Booking.com, both ways.** Give each of them one channel link and paste each of theirs back, and a week sold anywhere stops being offered everywhere. Nothing in this engine changed for it: Bookings reads their whole-date reservations as the nights those dates really cover, and the night states fall out of the arithmetic that was already here. What did have to be said is what a night runs between \u2014 four in the afternoon to ten in the morning, or whatever this property says \u2014 because \"the 9th to the 16th\" read literally is midnight to midnight and takes the night of the **8th** off the market, which is a night that is genuinely still for sale and which no owner would ever notice they were losing\n    * **Both changeover days stay on the market.** A channel's week from the 9th to the 16th blocks the nights of the 9th to the 15th: somebody already in the house still leaves on the 9th, and somebody else can still arrive on the 16th. Proved east and west of UTC, across the clocks going forward and back, and against a stay of our own sharing the changeover day with a channel's\n    * **`docs\/channels.md`, written for an owner**, with where to paste each link, how often each site looks, and the limit said plainly rather than buried: every iCal sync polls, so two sites can sell the same night in the gap between two looks. It is rare, it is the same with every plugin that works this way, the only cure is a channel manager and this is not one \u2014 and there is a numbered list of what to do on the day it happens to you\n    * **Seven new reasons, in the same words as Bookings' own eight.** `min_nights`, `max_nights`, `arrival_day`, `departure_day`, `guests`, `gap` and `past` are taught to Bookings' `Reason` when the mode registers, so Troubleshoot, the command line and the picker all print them beside \"Booked\" and \"Too soon\" with no second lookup table anywhere. `past` is the one that replaces a Bookings answer rather than adding to it: a night that has already started is certainly less than the notice period away, but *Too soon* reads as \"come back later\" on a date nobody can ever book again, so a guest looking at last August is told it is last August\n    * **It reads nothing of Bookings' own.** Which properties could take this service, how many of each there are, when they are open, the days the owner is away, what is already booked or held or blocked by an Airbnb feed \u2014 every one of those arrives through the narrow set Bookings hands a registered mode. Not one query in this add-on touches a table of Bookings', and there is a test that fails if one ever does. That is what keeps this engine and Bookings' own availability walk from drifting: they are the same three readers underneath\n    * **A stay goes through the same guard a haircut goes through.** The mode answers with an interval, a property and one place, and Bookings claims exactly that through the write lock, the sweep, the recheck and the unique key it has always used. Nothing this add-on returns bypasses the double-booking check and there is no way to ask it to. A stay and an hour-long booking on the same property refuse each other both ways round, which is what a venue hired by the day and also by the hour needs\n    * **Four identical doubles are one record with four units.** The calendar says \"two left\" rather than making a guest work out which of Room 1, Room 2, Room 3 and Room 4 is free, and the owner has one row to edit instead of four. A claim from an outside calendar takes the whole property rather than one unit, because there is no sensible reading of \"Airbnb has one of your four identical doubles\"\n    * **Two tables and nothing on anybody else's.** `kahunacart_rental_rules` is one row per nightly property \u2014 arrival and departure times, the shortest and longest stay, which weekdays a stay may start and end on, how many guests the rate covers, the fees, the deposit terms and the cancellation policy. `kahunacart_rental_rates` is the rate card. Nothing is added to a Bookings table and nothing to a core table, so switching this add-on off leaves every service, booking, hold and claim in the store reading exactly as it did\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-rentals nights`** prints the month as a table with what each date is for, and `--quote 2026-08-09..2026-08-16` prints a stay as a breakdown with the rate band beside every night. It is how this release is demonstrated with nothing to click, and it stays afterwards as the thing to run when an owner says the calendar is wrong \u2014 it answers out of the same engine the picker will, so it is not a second opinion\n    * **`bin\/plugin kahunacart-rentals seed rental`** writes the beach house: sleeps eight, $249 a night, $289 at weekends, a summer rate at $329 with a seven-night Saturday-to-Saturday minimum, a Christmas week, a $140 cleaning fee, $25 a night for each guest over six, a $500 damage deposit, 30% down with the rest a fortnight before arrival, a cancellation policy in three steps \u2014 and three stays already in the book, two of them sharing a changeover day. `seed bnb` writes four identical doubles as one record with breakfast as an option. Both are idempotent and both are written in the store's own timezone, and the rate bands are worked out from today so a store seeded in September still has a calendar worth looking at in March\n    * **Every time of day is written the way the rest of the shop writes it.** A store that says `6:00pm` on a class card says `4:00pm` on the stay it sold, and a store set to 24-hour time says `16:00` in both places. It goes through Bookings' one class for that rather than a second opinion here, on the receipt, in the order email, on the account tab, on the manage page and in the command line's own banner\n    * **No `composer install` and no `vendor\/`.** This add-on has no run-time package of its own: it reads KahunaCart's database through the base plugin and Bookings' engine through the mode seam, and both are installed on any store that can run it. The autoloader is four lines of PSR-4 in the plugin file, and a GPM install is a folder of files that works as it lands\n    * **No permissions of its own, and no product type of its own.** A stay is a Bookings booking and a property is a Bookings bookable, so `kahunacart.bookings.view` and `kahunacart.bookings.manage` already cover everything here \u2014 a separate one would mean an owner who ticked \"Manage Bookings and Setup\" still could not edit the rate card of the property they had just set up\n    * **What a stay costs, as a breakdown a guest can read.** The quote a property answers with is a list of rows \u2014 *7 nights at $329.00*, *2 extra guests, 4 nights*, *Cleaning* \u2014 and those rows become the cart line, the receipt and the order email, adding up to the figure the guest was shown at every step. The cleaning fee is marked as not discountable, so a 10% coupon takes 10% off the nights and nothing off the cleaning, which is what every owner means by a discount. The damage deposit is **not** in the total: it is a fact printed beside the stay, because it is money held and given back rather than money taken\n    * **30% now and the rest a fortnight before you arrive.** The property's own deposit terms go to the store through Bookings' payment-schedule seam, as a figure and in the property's own words \u2014 *Of the $2,534.00 for this stay, $760.20 is due today and the rest \u2014 $1,773.80 \u2014 by Sat 25 Jul 2026.* It says what it is a share of because it is a share of the **stay**: a hamper bought alongside the week, and any sales tax, are due in full today, so a checkout's \"Due today\" can be more than the deposit on the quote. A stay booked after the balance date is due in full today and says so rather than asking for a deposit on money that was owed last week, a property with no deposit set is paid for in one go, and a stay with nothing to pay skips all of it\n    * **A balance nobody paid does not cost anybody their holiday.** The guest is emailed a pay link three days before the date \u2014 a number an owner can change \u2014 and the owner is told the moment the date passes with the money still not in, with a sentence from the property itself naming what is owed and how much warning the guest had. The three days are there so that the owner's \"not paid\" message does not arrive on the same morning the guest was first asked. The dates stay held and the deposit stays where it is: a holiday cancelled automatically at one minute past midnight over a card that expired is how a rental business loses a guest it already had\n    * **The cancellation policy is read when somebody cancels, and it answers with a figure.** The three-step policy the property was set up with \u2014 full refund far enough out, half inside that, nothing at the last minute \u2014 is applied against what the guest has actually paid **for that stay**, so cancelling one of two weeks in one basket offers back one of them rather than the basket, and the amount lands on the refund request a person opens. **The policy is read as it stands today rather than as it stood when the stay was sold**, which is written down on the settings, in the docs and in the code, because there is nowhere on a booking to freeze terms and an owner who rewrites their policy mid-season should know which stays it now covers\n    * **A stay with nothing to pay, and a stay paid for on arrival.** A free night, a comp, a room the owner takes payment for at the door \u2014 each of them goes straight into the diary with no basket and no checkout, the same as any other booking with no money on it, and the quote still prints the breakdown so everybody can see what is being given away\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N4: the owner's screens \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The owner sets a property up from the Bookings section, on two screens.** Setting the way of booking to **Nightly stay** draws this add-on's own panels straight into the service editor: when people can arrive and leave and on which weekdays, the shortest and longest stay, who it sleeps and what an extra guest costs, the cleaning fee, the damage deposit, the deposit and when the balance falls due, the cancellation policy as a repeater that reads itself back in a sentence \u2014 *100% up to 30 days before, then 50% up to 14 days before, then 0% after that* \u2014 and the orphan-night rule. It saves on Bookings' own Save button, in the same write as the service\n    * **A Rates tab that draws the year.** A month ruler, a line across the top showing what a night actually costs, and one lane per band underneath drawn to scale in the order they are laid down \u2014 so a gap in March, a band that runs past new year and two bands over the same week are all things you can see rather than things you work out. Arrows on each lane decide which band wins where two of them overlap, a band opens into an editor for its dates, its price or its percentage, its weekend rate, its own minimum stay and its own arrival days, and underneath it all is a box that prices a real stay through the same engine a guest's calendar uses \u2014 pick two dates, press **Price it**, and read the nights, the extra guests and the cleaning with the deposit due today under them. Nothing is held and nothing is booked\n    * **Two messages a stay sends of its own.** A few days before somebody arrives they get the note the owner wrote for that property \u2014 directions, parking, the door code \u2014 with their dates, their check-in and check-out times and the damage deposit beside it; a day after they leave they get a thank-you with a link back to the property. Both have a switch of their own on the store's messages screen and both are ordinary Twig templates a theme can override by copying one file. Neither is sent for a stay that was cancelled, and the note is not sent to somebody whose balance fell due and was never paid. **The note is the only place a door code is ever printed**: it is not on the receipt, not in the confirmation, not in the calendar entry, not on the front desk and not in any calendar the shop publishes, and there is a test for every one of those clauses\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N4 \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N3: the range picker \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The calendar a guest actually uses.** Two months side by side where there is room and one where there is not, an arrival, a departure, and the nights between them drawn as one band. It is registered as a **face** on Bookings' picker rather than being a storefront of its own, so it arrives inside the store's own buy form, on whatever theme the shop already uses, in the store's own `--kc-*` colours, and the store's own quantity box and Add to cart button appear underneath once the dates are held \u2014 the same ones a jar of shampoo goes in with\n    * **Five states per date on the page, not two.** The states the engine has always answered with are finally drawn: a free night tinted, a booked night filled, a night that has gone struck through, and a **changeover day** split on the diagonal with a line through the middle \u2014 half of it spoken for and half of it not. On a property that is doing well it is what every Saturday in August looks like, and it is the one thing a home-made calendar never has\n    * **A date that cannot be chosen says why, in a sentence.** *3 nights minimum*, *Arrivals on Saturdays*, *Booked*, *That date has been and gone* \u2014 on the line under the calendar when a pointer crosses the date, and on the date itself for anybody reading with a keyboard or a screen reader. The minimum stay is enforced while the range is being drawn, so a date that would make too short a stay stops being pressable rather than being refused after somebody has pressed it. A refused date is `aria-disabled` rather than disabled, because a date nobody can explain is worse than a date nobody can book\n    * **Every figure on the summary is the shop's own answer.** Each rate band as its own row, the extra guests, the cleaning fee, the total, what is due today, when the balance falls due, the damage deposit as a stated amount and the cancellation policy in sentences \u2014 all of it comes back from the same quote the hold, the cart line and the receipt are built from. **Nothing in the page adds two numbers together**, so there is no version of this where the calendar and the checkout disagree, and a rate card edited this morning cannot be stale in a browser left open since yesterday\n    * **The dates are what is held, and the instant is worked out where the property is.** The browser posts *the 21st to the 27th* and never an instant, because check-in is four in the afternoon where the cottage is and a browser in another timezone doing that arithmetic is how a calendar ends up a day out twice a year. Dates are read and written as plain `YYYY-MM-DD` throughout the script for the same reason\n    * **It works with a keyboard, and it says what it is doing.** Arrow keys a day at a time, Page Up and Page Down a month at a time, Home and End to the ends of a week, Enter to choose and Escape to start again, with one tab stop into the grid and the focus kept on the same date when the calendar redraws. The chosen range, the nights, the price and every refusal are announced through a live region, so the summary is not something you have to go and look at\n    * **One month or two, decided by the space it has rather than by the size of the window.** The picker measures its own container, so the same calendar is two months wide in a page's main column, one month in a sidebar and one month in a phone's width, and a theme that puts it in a narrow box gets a calendar that fits rather than one that scrolls sideways. It is measured again when the box changes size, and the months are added and removed rather than hidden, so nothing keyboard-reachable is ever drawn where it cannot be seen\n    * **Moving a stay, from a guest's own link.** The manage page opens the same range picker with the guest's **own** booking left out of the reckoning, so the week they are moving off does not read as taken by themselves. The booking is named to the availability route by the token the guest was emailed and never by its id, so there is nothing in that request a stranger could guess\n    * **A read-only calendar for a listing page.** `[kahunacart-availability product=\"beach-house\" months=\"2\" \/]` and `{{ kahunacart_availability('beach-house') }}` draw a month or two of free and taken nights anywhere on the site with nothing to press. It is the same script reading the same public route the picker does, so a listing page and a booking page can never disagree about which week in August has gone\n    * **Every word on it is a language key with English behind it.** Fifty-odd of them, sent to the browser by the server as this mode's own strings \u2014 so a store with no translation reads properly, a store with one reads in its own language with no JavaScript changed, and there is a test that fails if the script ever draws a word the server does not send or the server sends one the script has no English for\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N3 \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N6: the docs \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **Eleven pages of documentation, written for somebody with a cottage to let.** Two getting-started walkthroughs that end with a page a guest can book on \u2014 one for a whole property, one for four rooms sold as one record with breakfast as an option, each with a `seed` command that writes the same thing in one line \u2014 then what a night is and what each state on the calendar means, the rate card with the warning that a band's percentage is a multiplier and not an uplift, the deposit and the balance and what a cancellation actually gives back, Airbnb and VRBO and Booking.com with the polling limit said plainly, and a whole page of why a night is not on offer with every reason code, its sentence and what to do about it. The limits are in the prose where an owner meets them rather than in a list at the end: channels poll, an outside calendar on a property with four units blocks all four, a minimum stay that applies only at weekends cannot be written down, and a stay cannot be booked at the front desk\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N6 \u2500\u2500 -->\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 nightly N7: the follow-up \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **A stay can be taken at the front desk.** Somebody rings and wants the second week of June. Open `\/desk`, tap New booking, tap the property, and the sheet asks for the day they arrive, the day they leave and how many guests \u2014 then prints the same rows and the same total the guest would have read on the property's own page: the dates written out, check in and check out, every rate band as its own row, the extra guests, the cleaning and the total. Not the deposit, the due-today figure or the balance date, because none of those happens on this path. If those days cannot be had it says why in the same words the picker uses, so you can read the reason down the telephone. **Nothing is taken and nothing is scheduled**: a booking made at the counter has no order behind it, so there is no deposit, no balance date and no reminder, and the desk says *To collect $1,714.00* until you have. Put it through the property's own page instead if you want the deposit taken now\n    * **A seeded stay can have somebody in it.** `stay()` takes an optional customer \u2014 an id, or a name and an email to find or make one from \u2014 so a recipe writing a real property's book gets a diary worth looking at rather than a calendar of anonymous blocks. Neither of the two shipped recipes passes one; it is there for the recipes other people write, which had to copy the whole method to add one key\n    * **The read-only calendar measures itself, like the picker beside it.** `months` on `[kahunacart-availability]` is the **most** it will draw rather than the number it must, so a phone gets one month with seven readable columns instead of two squeezed ones \u2014 and two calendars in the same column stop disagreeing about how many months fit, which reads as a broken page rather than as two different controls\n    * **A refusal no longer sits under a range somebody has chosen.** Crossing a night the property is shut on wrote that night's reason onto the line under the calendar and left it there, because the line was only cleared while a range was half drawn. A guest who had just picked a perfectly good week read *Closed* under it. Once both dates are chosen the line says the range and nothing else\n    * **A stay with no dates on it is refused rather than priced from 1970.** Both doors into the nightly mode send a start of nought where nobody named one, and reading that as an instant put the arrival on the first of January 1970 \u2014 so the quote walked fifty-six years of rate card before anybody was told they had forgotten a date\n    * **How wide the calendar may get is a token here too.** `--kc-picker-max`, set on whatever wraps the picker, now lifts this face's own two caps as well as the panel's \u2014 so a page whose whole middle is the calendar says so once rather than reaching past three stylesheets with a selector long enough to beat them. The defaults are unchanged: 58rem for the picker, 44rem for the read-only calendar\n    * **A stay taken at the desk races a stay in somebody's basket, on all three engines.** Six processes going for two overlapping weeks in one house, half of them through the storefront's hold and half of them straight off the front desk, all jumping at the same wall-clock moment. Exactly one of them gets the week. It is what found the ordering bug in Bookings' own guard that let MariaDB sell the house twice\n    <!-- \u2500\u2500 end nightly N7 \u2500\u2500 -->\n    * **The Rates tab and the stay rules panel respect an account that may only look.** Somebody with `kahunacart.bookings.view` and not `kahunacart.bookings.manage` reads the whole year, opens a band to see its weekend rate and arrival days, and prices a stay with the try-it box, but is not offered Add a band, the reorder arrows, or a band's Save and Remove, all of which the server refuses. The stay rules inside Bookings' service editor draw read-only for them, with no cancellation step to add or take off. The properties list, the rate card and the rule each carry `can_manage`, worked out through the same check the writes go through\n\n1. [](#bugfix)\n    * **A property only asks for a deposit when the store takes deposits.** A property with a deposit percentage on a store where `checkout.deposits.enabled` is off still quoted \"30% due today and the rest by \u2026\" in the picker, the Rates preview and the command line, while the checkout took the whole stay. Every quote now follows the store switch: with deposits off the whole stay is due when it is booked, with no deposit line, no balance date and nothing handed to Bookings to schedule. The percentage stays saved and editable, and the Paying box under the property's settings says deposits are off for this store and where to switch them on. The damage deposit is the owner's own and is still stated either way."}}}}
";